window.DEEP_STORIES["automobile"] = {"slug":"automobile","title":"The Automobile","emoji":"🚗","tagline":"From a clanking steam wagon to cars that drive themselves","intro":"Buckle up! For almost all of human history, the fastest way to travel over land was on the back of a horse. Then, in just a couple of hundred years, people built machines that could carry a whole family hundreds of kilometres in a day, without a single oat. In these 20 parts you will ride along with a French army engineer and his huge steam wagon, a German inventor named Karl Benz, and the brave Bertha Benz, who took his car on the world's first long road trip. You'll see Henry Ford's assembly line spit out a new car every few minutes, peek inside an engine to see how tiny explosions turn wheels, and watch roads, traffic lights, racing and seatbelts change the world. Then we'll face the problems too: crashes, smog and oil. And finally we'll zoom into the future of electric and self-driving cars. Start your engines!","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"Before the Car: Hooves, Sails and Daydreams","when":"about 3500 BCE – 1700s"},{"n":2,"title":"Cugnot's Steam Monster and the Red Flag","when":"1769 – 1896"},{"n":3,"title":"The Engine That Burns Inside","when":"1860 – 1885"},{"n":4,"title":"Karl Benz and the Patent-Motorwagen","when":"1844 – 1888"},{"n":5,"title":"Bertha Benz's Daring Drive","when":"August 1888"},{"n":6,"title":"Daimler, Maybach and the First Mercedes","when":"1882 – 1926"},{"n":7,"title":"Steam, Electric or Gasoline? The Great Race of 1900","when":"about 1880 – 1915"},{"n":8,"title":"Inside the Engine: Tiny Explosions, Big Power","when":"science (developed 1876 – today)"},{"n":9,"title":"From Engine to Wheels: Gears, Tires, Steering and Brakes","when":"science (1818 – today)"},{"n":10,"title":"America Catches Car Fever","when":"1893 – 1908"},{"n":11,"title":"The Model T and the Moving Assembly Line","when":"1908 – 1927"},{"n":12,"title":"A Car for Every Purse: GM, the Beetle and the World's Carmakers","when":"1908 – 1980s"},{"n":13,"title":"Roads, Rules and Traffic Lights","when":"1820s – 1970s"},{"n":14,"title":"How the Car Changed Everyday Life","when":"1900s – 1970s"},{"n":15,"title":"Racing: The Need for Speed","when":"1894 – today"},{"n":16,"title":"Crashes and the Fight for Safety","when":"1869 – today"},{"n":17,"title":"Smog, Lead and Oil: The Car's Hidden Costs","when":"1921 – today"},{"n":18,"title":"The Electric Comeback","when":"1990s – today"},{"n":19,"title":"Cars That Drive Themselves","when":"1925 – today"},{"n":20,"title":"The Road So Far, and the Road Ahead","when":"1769 – the future"}],"people":[["Leonardo da Vinci (1452–1519)","Italian artist-engineer who sketched a spring-driven self-moving cart (never built in his time)"],["Simon Stevin (1548–1620)","Flemish-Dutch engineer who built a wind-powered sailing chariot around 1600"],["Ferdinand Verbiest (1623–1688)","Flemish Jesuit missionary in China reported to have built a small steam-powered toy cart for the Kangxi Emperor"],["Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot (1725–1804)","French army engineer who built the steam 'fardier', the first full-size self-propelled road vehicle (1769–1770)"],["Richard Trevithick (1771–1833)","Cornish engineer who built high-pressure steam road carriages (1801, 1803) and an early railway locomotive"],["Goldsworthy Gurney (1793–1875)","British inventor who ran steam carriages in the 1820s–1830s"],["Walter Hancock (1799–1852)","British engineer who ran steam omnibus services in London in the 1830s"],["Amédée Bollée (1844–1917)","French bell-founder who built the steam carriage L'Obéissante (1873)"],["Étienne Lenoir (1822–1900)","Belgian-French engineer who built the first commercially successful internal combustion engine (1860)"],["Alphonse Beau de Rochas (1815–1893)","French engineer who described the four-stroke principle in 1862"],["Nicolaus August Otto (1832–1891)","German inventor who built the first practical four-stroke engine (1876)"],["Eugen Langen (1833–1895)","German engineer and Otto's business partner at Deutz"],["Siegfried Marcus (1831–1898)","Vienna-based inventor of early petrol vehicles whose dates and priority are debated"],["Karl Benz (1844–1929)","German engineer who built and patented the Benz Patent-Motorwagen (1886)"],["Bertha Benz (1849–1944)","Karl's wife, financial partner and the first person to make a long-distance car journey (1888)"],["Eugen Benz (1873–1958) and Richard Benz (1874–1955)","Bertha's teenage sons who rode with her in 1888"],["Gottlieb Daimler (1834–1900)","German engineer who, with Maybach, built fast light engines, the Reitwagen and a motor carriage"],["Wilhelm Maybach (1846–1929)","German designer of engines, the spray-nozzle carburetor and the 1901 Mercedes"],["Emil Jellinek (1853–1918)","businessman and racer who ordered the first Mercedes and named it after his daughter"],["Mercedes Jellinek (1889–1929)","the girl whose name became the Mercedes brand"],["René Panhard (1841–1908) and Émile Levassor (1843–1897)","French carmakers who created the front-engine 'Système Panhard'; Levassor won Paris–Bordeaux–Paris in 1895"],["Armand Peugeot (1849–1915)","French industrialist who turned his family firm to making cars"],["Gustave Trouvé (1839–1902)","French electrical engineer who built an electric tricycle in 1881"],["Thomas Parker (1843–1915)","English engineer who built early electric vehicles in the 1880s"],["Camille Jenatzy (1868–1913)","Belgian driver whose electric car first passed 100 km/h (1899)"],["Francis E. Stanley (1849–1918) and Freelan O. Stanley (1849–1940)","American twins who built Stanley steam cars"],["Ferdinand Porsche (1875–1951)","Austrian-German engineer of the Lohner-Porsche hybrid and the Volkswagen Beetle; he worked for the Nazi regime"],["Charles F. Kettering (1876–1958)","American engineer who developed the electric self-starter (1912 Cadillac)"],["Rudolf Diesel (1858–1913)","German engineer who invented the compression-ignition diesel engine"],["Robert Bosch (1861–1942)","German industrialist whose company made magnetos and spark plugs"],["Gottlob Honold (1876–1923)","Bosch engineer who developed the high-voltage magneto ignition with spark plug (1902)"],["James Watt (1736–1819)","Scottish engineer whose 'horsepower' unit is still used for engines"],["Onésiphore Pecqueur (1792–1852)","French engineer who patented the differential gear for vehicles (1827)"],["Rudolph Ackermann (1764–1834) and Georg Lankensperger (1779–1855)","Ackermann patented Lankensperger's steering design in Britain (1818)"],["Robert William Thomson (1822–1873)","Scottish inventor who patented the first air-filled tire (1845)"],["John Boyd Dunlop (1840–1921)","Scottish vet in Belfast who developed a practical pneumatic tire (1888)"],["André Michelin (1853–1931) and Édouard Michelin (1859–1940)","French brothers who put air-filled tires on cars"],["Charles Duryea (1861–1938) and Frank Duryea (1869–1967)","brothers who built early American gasoline cars"],["Henry Ford (1863–1947)","American industrialist whose company built the Model T and the moving assembly line; also known for antisemitic publications in the 1920s"],["Charles Sorensen (1881–1968)","Ford production chief who helped develop the moving assembly line"],["Ransom E. Olds (1864–1950)","American carmaker of the Curved Dash Oldsmobile"],["George B. Selden (1846–1922)","patent lawyer whose broad car patent was challenged by Ford"],["Horatio Nelson Jackson (1872–1955)","doctor who made the first US cross-country car trip (1903)"],["Alice Ramsey (1886–1983)","first woman to drive across the United States (1909)"],["William C. Durant (1861–1947)","founder of General Motors"],["Alfred P. Sloan (1875–1966)","GM leader who introduced model ranges and yearly model changes"],["Harley Earl (1893–1969)","GM's first head of car styling"],["Ivan Hirst (1916–2000)","British Army officer who restarted Volkswagen production after World War II"],["Alec Issigonis (1906–1988)","designer of the Mini"],["Kiichiro Toyoda (1894–1952)","founder of Toyota's car business"],["Taiichi Ohno (1912–1990)","Toyota engineer who shaped the Toyota Production System"],["John Loudon McAdam (1756–1836)","Scottish engineer of 'macadam' road-building"],["Edgar Purnell Hooley (1860–1942)","Welsh surveyor who patented Tarmac (1902)"],["Carl G. Fisher (1874–1939)","promoter of the Lincoln Highway and the Indianapolis Motor Speedway"],["Dwight D. Eisenhower (1890–1969)","US president who signed the 1956 Interstate highway law"],["John Peake Knight (1828–1886)","railway engineer behind London's 1868 traffic signal"],["Garrett Morgan (1877–1963)","African American inventor who patented an improved traffic signal (1923)"],["Walter Arnold (dates uncertain)","English driver who received the first recorded speeding fine (1896)"],["Victor Hugo Green (1892–1960)","creator of the Negro Motorist Green Book"],["Richard Hollingshead (1900–1975)","opened the first drive-in movie theatre (1933)"],["Marcel Renault (1872–1903)","co-founder of Renault, killed in the 1903 Paris–Madrid race"],["Louis Renault (1877–1944)","French carmaker and engineer, credited with early drum brake and gearbox work"],["Ray Harroun (1879–1968)","winner of the first Indianapolis 500 (1911)"],["Giuseppe Farina (1906–1966)","first Formula One World Champion (1950)"],["Malcolm Campbell (1885–1948)","British driver who set many land speed records in his Bluebird cars"],["Andy Green (born 1962)","RAF pilot who drove ThrustSSC through the sound barrier (1997)"],["Mary Ward (1827–1869)","Irish scientist and artist, the first known person killed by a motor vehicle"],["Bridget Driscoll (about 1851–1896)","first pedestrian in Britain killed by a petrol car"],["Mary Anderson (1866–1953)","American who patented a windshield wiper (1903)"],["Béla Barényi (1907–1997)","Mercedes-Benz engineer who invented the crumple zone"],["Nils Bohlin (1920–2002)","Volvo engineer who designed the modern three-point seatbelt"],["Ralph Nader (born 1934)","American consumer advocate who wrote 'Unsafe at Any Speed'"],["Thomas Midgley Jr. (1889–1944)","chemist who introduced leaded gasoline (and later CFCs)"],["Clair Patterson (1922–1995)","geochemist who measured Earth's age and campaigned against lead pollution"],["Arie Haagen-Smit (1900–1977)","Dutch-American chemist who discovered how car exhaust makes smog"],["John B. Goodenough (1922–2023), M. Stanley Whittingham (born 1941) and Akira Yoshino (born 1948)","Nobel-winning lithium-ion battery pioneers"],["Martin Eberhard (born 1960) and Marc Tarpenning (born 1964)","co-founders of Tesla (2003)"],["Elon Musk (born 1971)","early Tesla investor and chairman (2004), later CEO"],["Norman Bel Geddes (1893–1958)","designer of GM's 1939 Futurama exhibit"],["Ernst Dickmanns (born 1936)","German engineer who pioneered camera-guided self-driving cars"],["Sebastian Thrun (born 1967)","led Stanford's Stanley team and later Google's self-driving project"]],"glossary":[["external combustion engine","an engine where fuel burns outside the cylinder, like a steam engine heating water in a boiler"],["internal combustion engine","an engine where fuel burns inside the cylinder, pushing a piston directly"],["piston","a plug that slides up and down inside the cylinder, pushed by the burning gases"],["crankshaft","a bent shaft that turns the piston's up-and-down motion into spinning motion"],["four-stroke cycle","the four steps of most car engines: intake, compression, power and exhaust"],["spark plug","a part that makes a tiny electric spark to light the fuel-air mixture"],["carburetor","a device that mixes fuel with air before it enters the engine (mostly replaced by fuel injection)"],["diesel engine","an engine that ignites fuel by squeezing air until it is very hot, with no spark"],["horsepower","a unit of power, about 746 watts, first used by James Watt to compare engines with horses"],["gearbox (transmission)","a set of gears that lets the engine turn wheels slowly with lots of force or fast with less force"],["clutch","a part that connects or disconnects the engine from the wheels so you can change gears"],["differential","gears that let the left and right wheels turn at different speeds on a curve"],["pneumatic tire","a tire filled with air to cushion bumps"],["assembly line","a way of building things where the product moves past workers who each do one job"],["crumple zone","front and rear parts of a car designed to crush in a crash, absorbing energy to protect people inside"],["catalytic converter","a device in the exhaust that uses special metals to turn harmful gases into less harmful ones"],["hybrid","a car with both a fuel engine and an electric motor with a battery"],["regenerative braking","when an electric car's motor works as a generator while slowing down, recharging the battery"],["lidar","a sensor that uses laser pulses to measure distances and make a 3D map of the surroundings"],["levels of driving automation","the SAE scale from 0 (no automation) to 5 (the car can drive itself everywhere with no human)"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"Before the Car: Hooves, Sails and Daydreams","when":"about 3500 BCE – 1700s","summary":"Long ago, if you wanted to go somewhere, you walked, rode a horse or bumped along in a cart pulled by animals. Clever people dreamed of carts that could move all by themselves, powered by springs, wind or even steam. But one big piece was still missing: a small, strong engine.","sections":[{"heading":"The Greatest Round Idea","paragraphs":["Close your eyes and imagine a world without a single engine. No cars, no buses, no trains, no bicycles even. If you wanted to visit a town 50 kilometres away, you had three choices: walk for two days, ride an animal, or sit in a creaking cart while an ox plodded along at the speed of a slow stroll. For almost all of human history, that was simply how life worked.","Every car you have ever seen rolls on wheels, so our story starts with the wheel itself. Surprisingly, the wheel is much younger than farming or pottery. People had been growing crops for thousands of years before anyone put a round disc on an axle and used it to carry loads.","The oldest clues all appear at about the same time, roughly 3500 BCE, scattered across a wide area. In the city of Uruk, in Mesopotamia (the land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, in today's Iraq), scribes pressed little pictures of wheeled wagons into clay tablets dated to about 3500 to 3350 BCE. In southern Poland, archaeologists found a clay pot from about the same period, now called the Bronocice pot, scratched with what looks like a simple four-wheeled wagon. In northern Germany there are tracks of wheels from around 3400 BCE.","The oldest actual wooden wheel ever found was pulled out of a peat bog near Ljubljana, the capital of Slovenia. It was made of ash planks, together with an oak axle, and tree-ring and radiocarbon dating puts it somewhere around 3340 to 3030 BCE. It survived more than 5,000 years because the wet, airless mud stopped it from rotting.","Those first wheels were solid: heavy discs made of planks fastened together. They were strong but clumsy. Around 2000 BCE, people on the grassy plains east of the Ural Mountains, in what archaeologists call the Sintashta culture, began making wheels with spokes. A spoked wheel is much lighter, and a light wheel can turn fast. Hitch two fast horses to a light cart with spoked wheels and you have a chariot, the speedy war-machine of the ancient world, which spread from Egypt to China.","Here is the important thing to notice. The wheel made carrying things much easier, because rolling is far less effort than dragging. But the wheel does not move by itself. Every wagon, cart and chariot still needed a muscle-powered animal or person in front of it. For the next five thousand years, the speed and range of land travel was limited by how fast, how far and how long an ox, a donkey, a mule or a horse could keep going."]},{"heading":"Roads Built for Legions","paragraphs":["Wheels are only as good as the ground under them. A cart on soft mud sinks; a cart on a rocky track shakes itself to pieces. The ancient people who understood this best were the Romans.","In 312 BCE a Roman official named Appius Claudius Caecus started building a military road heading south from Rome. It became the Via Appia, the Appian Way, and it was later stretched all the way to Brindisi on the heel of Italy, about 540 kilometres from Rome. The poet Statius called it the 'queen of roads'. Parts of it still exist today, lined with ancient tombs, and some stretches near Rome are still used by cars. In 2024 UNESCO added it to its World Heritage List.","The Romans built roads mainly for their armies. A legion could march quickly along a straight, firm road to wherever trouble broke out, and supply wagons could follow. Trade travelled the same routes: grain, wine, olive oil, pottery, letters and news. Over the centuries the empire built a gigantic network. Historians estimate that the total of all Roman roads was more than 400,000 kilometres, and that the main paved highways added up to roughly 80,000 kilometres, enough to wrap around the Earth twice.","A good Roman road was a layer cake. Workers dug a trench down to firm ground, then filled it with packed rubble and stones, then layers of coarse and fine concrete-like material, and finally a top surface of tightly fitted paving stones. The surface was curved, higher in the middle, so rain ran off into ditches on each side. The Greek historian Procopius later wrote that the stones of the Appian Way fitted so well that they seemed to have grown together.","Along the roads stood milestones, stone columns about one and a half metres tall. A Roman mile was a thousand paces, about 1,480 metres, which is where our word 'mile' comes from. The government also ran a messenger system with relay stations where tired horses could be swapped for fresh ones. Using these relays, a message could cover about 80 kilometres in a day, and the future emperor Tiberius is said to have once raced about 296 kilometres in 24 hours. Ordinary horse-drawn carts were much slower, managing about 40 to 50 kilometres in a day.","When the Western Roman Empire broke apart in the 400s CE, no central government was left to repair the network. Stones were taken away for building, drains clogged, and grass and mud slowly swallowed many routes. For more than a thousand years, most roads in Europe were little better than dirt tracks: dusty in summer, deep mud in winter, and full of ruts and potholes all year. In England, local parishes were supposed to keep their own roads in order, but they rarely had the money or the will to fix busy routes used by strangers.","Things only began to improve in the late 1600s and 1700s. In 1663 the English Parliament allowed tolls to be collected on part of the Great North Road to pay for repairs, and from this grew the 'turnpike trusts', groups that charged travellers at gates (the turnpikes) and used the money to maintain the road. By the 1830s about 1,000 trusts looked after roughly 30,000 miles (48,000 kilometres) of road in England and Wales, with nearly 8,000 toll gates. Engineers such as John Metcalf, Thomas Telford and John Loudon McAdam showed how to build dry, firm road surfaces again. You will meet McAdam properly in a later part of this story."]},{"heading":"Life at the Speed of a Horse","paragraphs":["For the people of the 1600s and 1700s, the fastest way across the land was the horse. A horse walking with a rider moves at about the same speed as a brisk human walker, around 6 kilometres per hour. It can trot or gallop much faster, but only for a short time before it gets tired. A rider who wanted to travel far in one day had to pace the animal carefully, or swap horses along the way.","That is how the stagecoach worked. A stagecoach was a big covered carriage that carried paying passengers on a regular timetable, pulled by a team of usually four horses. The trip was split into 'stages'. At the end of each stage, at an inn or post station, grooms quickly unhitched the sweaty, tired team and hitched up fresh horses. In Britain the first recorded stagecoach service ran from Edinburgh to the nearby port of Leith in 1610, and by the late 1600s coaches ran along England's main roads.","Early coaches were painfully slow by our standards. Until the late 1700s they averaged roughly 5 miles per hour (about 8 kilometres per hour), covering perhaps 60 to 70 miles in a long day. In 1754 a 'Flying Coach' from Manchester to London proudly advertised that it would arrive in four and a half days. Better roads and better coaches made a big difference: the journey from Cambridge to London fell from about two days around 1750 to under seven hours by 1820.","In 1784 a theatre owner named John Palmer persuaded the British government to try carrying the mail by fast coach instead of by riders on horseback. His test coach left Bristol at four in the afternoon on 2 August 1784 and reached London about 16 hours later, while the old post had sometimes taken up to 38 hours. Within about 13 years, mail coaches were running on 42 routes.","Inside, passengers were often squeezed shoulder to shoulder on hard seats, bounced by every rut, frozen in winter and choked with dust in summer. The cheapest tickets were for seats on the roof, out in the wind and rain. Highway robbers sometimes held up coaches on lonely stretches of road, and coaches could overturn on steep or icy hills.","Horses also had to be fed, housed and cared for, and that cost a fortune. A large horse eats roughly 10 kilograms of hay and grain every day and drinks dozens of litres of water. It needs a stable, a groom, a blacksmith for its shoes, and rest. To keep a single busy coach route running, a company needed a whole army of horses waiting at the inns along the way. One route between London and Newcastle, about 278 miles long, needed around 250 horses. A horse working hard pulling coaches might only last a few years before it was worn out.","And then there was the mess. Every town with lots of horses also had lots of manure, flies and smell. Street sweepers worked hard to clear crossings so that people could walk across without ruining their shoes. You will hear much more about horse-filled cities later in this story, but for now just picture a busy market street around 1750: carts, coaches, riders and pack animals all crowded together, and the ground underfoot not exactly clean.","So people had wheels, and in some places they had decent roads. What they did not have was a way to make a vehicle move without an animal pulling it. Some very clever people had been daydreaming about exactly that."]},{"heading":"Leonardo's Clockwork Cart","paragraphs":["Imagine leafing through the private notebooks of Leonardo da Vinci, the Italian painter of the Mona Lisa, who was also an engineer, an inventor and a non-stop sketcher of ideas. Among drawings of flying machines, war machines and water pumps, there is a page that has puzzled and delighted engineers for a long time. It shows a squat, square wooden cart with three wheels and a jumble of gears and springs. It is now kept in a huge collection of his papers called the Codex Atlanticus, as sheet 812 recto, and scholars usually date it to around 1478, when Leonardo was in his mid-twenties and working in Florence.","For centuries nobody quite understood the drawing. Then, in the late twentieth century, researchers studying the sheet realised that it showed a self-propelled cart: a vehicle meant to move on its own without being pushed or pulled. The power came from big coiled springs, like the spring in a wind-up toy or an old clock, housed inside drum-shaped cases. You would wind the springs up tightly; as they slowly unwound, they turned the gears that drove the wheels.","Leonardo also seems to have solved some clever problems. A spring pushes hard when it is tightly wound and weakly when it is nearly unwound, which would make the cart jerk forward and then crawl. The design includes a balance wheel, the kind of regulator used in clocks, to keep the motion steady. There also appears to be a mechanism that could be set in advance so the cart would follow a planned route, turning at certain moments. In a way, it was a tiny programmable robot, more than 500 years old.","What was it for? It certainly was not a family car. It was far too small to carry a driver and would have run for only a short distance on each winding. Many historians think it was meant as a marvel for a festival or a theatrical show, where a cart that rolled across the stage by itself would make the audience gasp. Rich Italian rulers of the time loved such wonders.","It is very important to be clear about one thing: Leonardo did not build a car. As far as we know, the cart existed only on paper. But modern engineers have built working models from his drawing. A team in Florence is reported to have built one in 2004 that rolled along by itself, and a replica stands at Clos Lucé, the manor house in Amboise, France, where Leonardo spent his last years. These models show his idea could work, but they also show its limits. A spring can only store a small amount of energy. Once it runs down, someone has to wind it up again by hand. No spring could ever carry a family from one town to another."]},{"heading":"Sailing on Sand: Simon Stevin's Wind Chariot","paragraphs":["If springs were too weak, what about the wind? The wind pushes sailing ships across whole oceans, and it is completely free. In the summer of 1600, a brilliant Flemish-Dutch engineer named Simon Stevin decided to put a sail on wheels.","Stevin was one of the great thinkers of his age. In 1585 he wrote a little booklet called De Thiende ('The Tenth') that taught ordinary people to calculate with decimal fractions instead of awkward ones like three-sevenths, which helped make decimals popular in Europe. He studied how water pushes on dams and how weights balance on slopes, and he worked as an engineer and teacher for Prince Maurice of Orange, the military leader of the young Dutch Republic.","For Prince Maurice, Stevin built a 'sailing chariot', in Dutch a zeilwagen. Picture a wooden wagon a bit like a small boat on four wheels, with a mast and sails, and a rudder-like steering arrangement. Its playground was the wide, flat, firm sand of the Dutch coast at low tide, between the fishing village of Scheveningen, near The Hague, and the village of Petten further north.","According to the accounts of the time, on a test run around 1600 the chariot carried Stevin, Prince Maurice and about two dozen other passengers, some 28 people in all, racing along the beach faster than horses could run. Prince Maurice used it to entertain and impress important guests. Stories of the journey spread across Europe, and artists made engravings of the sail-powered wagon skimming along the shore. Stevin may even have been inspired by travellers' reports from China: in 1585 a Spanish writer described Chinese carts fitted with sails, and Chinese writings mention wind-driven carriages centuries earlier.","The original sailing chariot was lost long ago, though a model of it was kept at Scheveningen until 1802. Today, people still race 'land yachts' on wide beaches and dry lake beds, and they are the direct descendants of Stevin's idea.","But think about the problem. A sailing chariot only works where the ground is flat, wide and smooth, and only when the wind is blowing the right way. On a narrow, muddy, winding road between hedges, on a calm day, or with the wind in your face, it would go nowhere. The wind is free, but you cannot control it, so it was never going to replace the horse."]},{"heading":"A Steam Toy for an Emperor","paragraphs":["Now travel more than 7,000 kilometres east, to Beijing in the 1670s, to the court of the Kangxi Emperor of China, one of the longest-reigning and most curious rulers in Chinese history. Among his advisers was a European priest called Ferdinand Verbiest, known in China as Nan Huairen.","Verbiest was born in 1623 in Pittem, a village in Flanders (in today's Belgium). He became a Jesuit, a member of a Catholic religious order famous for its schools and scholars, and sailed to China, arriving in 1658. The Jesuits in Beijing were respected for their skill in astronomy and mathematics, because predicting eclipses and making accurate calendars mattered a great deal to the imperial government.","Verbiest's life was not always easy. In the 1660s he and other Jesuits were thrown into prison after their enemies at court accused them. But in 1669 he won a public contest in astronomy, predicting the length of shadows, the positions of the planets and an eclipse more accurately than his rival. The emperor put him in charge of the Imperial Observatory. Verbiest rebuilt it in 1673 with six large bronze instruments for measuring the sky, some of which you can still see on the old observatory platform in Beijing today. He also taught the young emperor geometry and even cast 132 cannons for the imperial army.","Around 1672, according to Verbiest's own writings, he designed a little self-moving cart as a toy to amuse the emperor. It was about 65 centimetres long, roughly the size of a large toy truck, far too small to carry a person or any cargo. On it sat a round metal container of water, heated by burning coals underneath. When the water boiled, steam rushed out of a narrow pipe and blew against a small wheel with blades, rather like wind blowing on a pinwheel. That bladed wheel was geared to the cart's wheels, so as it spun, the cart rolled forward. A tiller allowed it to be steered, and it is said it could run for over an hour.","Verbiest described this machine in a book called Astronomia Europaea ('European Astronomy'), finished in 1681 and printed in Germany in 1687, the year before he died after a fall from a horse. Because of that description, his little cart is often called the earliest known design for a steam-powered vehicle. Still, we have to be careful. No original drawing of it survives, and we only know about it from his writing, so historians cannot be completely sure exactly how it looked or how well it worked. And it was a toy, not a car: it never carried anyone anywhere.","Interestingly, it suggested a real idea. In 2021, students in Ingolstadt, Germany, built a replica based on Verbiest's description, and it worked well enough to show that his concept was possible. The power of boiling water, which people had played with since ancient Greek inventors spun little steam toys, could actually push a vehicle along."]},{"heading":"The Missing Piece","paragraphs":["Let us line up our three daydreams side by side. Leonardo's spring cart could move by itself, but its spring stored so little energy that it could only creep a short way. Stevin's sailing chariot could carry a crowd at amazing speed, but only on a wide beach with the right wind. Verbiest's steam cart used a fuel that could be carried along, but the tiny jet of steam could only push a toy.","Each inventor had found part of the answer. What none of them had was an engine: a machine small enough to fit on a vehicle, strong enough to move people and cargo, and able to keep running for hours on fuel that you carry with you. Horses were, in a sense, the best 'engines' anyone had. A horse turns hay into pulling power, can climb hills, steer around holes and keep going all day, and it even makes more horses.","In the early 1700s, real steam engines did finally appear in Britain. In 1712 an ironmonger named Thomas Newcomen built a steam engine to pump water out of coal mines. But Newcomen's engines were gigantic. Each filled a building several storeys high, ran slowly, and burned huge amounts of coal. Nobody could put a machine like that on wheels and drive it down a road.","Engineers kept improving steam engines through the 1700s, making them stronger and more efficient. And slowly a bold question began to form in the minds of a few people. If a steam engine could pump water and turn the machines of a factory, could a smaller, tougher one be made to move itself along the ground?","The first person to answer that question with a real, full-size machine was not a toy-maker, a showman or an emperor's astronomer. He was a soldier-engineer in the French army. He had a very practical and very heavy problem to solve: how to drag enormous bronze cannons into position without needing teams of horses. His answer would be a giant, clanking, puffing steam wagon, the first self-moving vehicle big enough to carry real loads, and it is waiting for us in the next part."]}],"deepDive":"Where and when was the wheel invented? The honest answer is that historians are not sure, and the debate is lively. Evidence for wheeled vehicles appears almost at the same moment, around 3500 to 3300 BCE, in places thousands of kilometres apart: pictographs from Uruk in Mesopotamia, the Bronocice pot in Poland, wheel ruts in northern Germany, clay model wagons in the steppes north of the Black Sea, and the Ljubljana Marshes wheel in Slovenia. Some archaeologists think the idea was invented once and spread very quickly along trade routes; others suspect separate inventions. Interestingly, the potter's wheel is even older in some places: one from the Cucuteni-Trypillia culture of Ukraine dates to the middle of the fifth millennium BCE. A key insight is that the hard part was not the round wheel but the precise fit between wheel and axle. If the hole in the wheel is too tight, it jams; too loose, it wobbles and wears out. That needed good tools and careful carpentry. Roman road numbers also need care. Figures such as '80,000 km of paved road' and '400,000 km in total' come from ancient lists, inscriptions and modern mapping projects, and they depend on what you count as a 'road'. Similarly, the stories of Stevin's sailing chariot come from accounts written by admirers, and modern writers repeat differing figures for its speed and distance, so it is wise to say only that it was reported to be faster than horses. Leonardo's cart is another case where interpretation matters. The drawing on Codex Atlanticus folio 812r was long thought to show something else; only careful study in the late twentieth century suggested it was a spring-driven, pre-programmable cart. Working models built since then are modern reconstructions, so they include some guesses about missing details. Finally, Verbiest's steam cart is known only from his own text. It used something like a steam turbine, steam blowing on a bladed wheel, rather than a piston engine. That is a reminder that turbines and piston engines are two quite different ways of turning steam into motion, and it was the piston engine that would power the first full-size vehicles.","funFact":"The oldest wooden wheel ever found, from the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia, is more than 5,000 years old and survived because it was buried in wet, airless mud.","check":{"q":"Why couldn't Leonardo da Vinci's self-propelled cart have become a real family car?","a":"Its springs stored only a little energy, so it could only go a short way before someone had to wind it up again (and it was only a design on paper)","wrong":["It was powered by a steam boiler that kept exploding","It needed a team of four horses to start it","It could only move when the wind was blowing"]},"sources":[{"title":"Wheel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheel"},{"title":"Roman roads","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_roads"},{"title":"Appian Way","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appian_Way"},{"title":"Stagecoach","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stagecoach"},{"title":"Turnpike trusts","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turnpike_trusts"},{"title":"Leonardo's self-propelled cart","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonardo%27s_self-propelled_cart"},{"title":"Simon Stevin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_Stevin"},{"title":"Ferdinand Verbiest","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_Verbiest"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/01-1.jpg","alt":"The Ljubljana Marshes Wheel and its axle, the oldest wooden wheel yet found, in a museum case in Slovenia.","file":"Ljubljana Marshes Wheel with axle (oldest wooden wheel yet discovered).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_(oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered).jpg","author":"Petar Milošević","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/01-2.jpg","alt":"An old print of Simon Stevin's sailing chariot of 1602, a ship on wheels rolling along the beach.","file":"De zeilwagen van Simon Stevin, 1602, RP-P-OB-80.577.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_zeilwagen_van_Simon_Stevin,_1602,_RP-P-OB-80.577.jpg","author":"Rijksmuseum","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/01-3.jpg","alt":"Ancient basalt paving stones of the Appian Way outside Rome, still showing grooves worn by cart wheels.","file":"Rome Lazio Via Appia Antica.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_Lazio_Via_Appia_Antica.jpg","author":"Nicholas Hartmann","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3698},{"n":2,"title":"Cugnot's Steam Monster and the Red Flag","when":"1769 – 1896","summary":"In 1770 a French army engineer built a giant three-wheeled steam wagon to pull cannons, but it was slow and very hard to steer. In Britain, inventors later ran steam buses on real streets, until strict laws made every slow road engine follow a person walking in front. In 1896 the rule was scrapped, and drivers celebrated with a famous run to the seaside.","sections":[{"heading":"A Soldier with a Heavy Problem","paragraphs":["Picture a muddy parade ground near Paris around 1770. Soldiers and officers stand in a nervous half-circle. In front of them hisses something nobody has ever seen before: a long, heavy wooden wagon on three iron-rimmed wheels, with a huge, round copper boiler hanging off the very front like the nose of a monster. Smoke curls from its fire. With a clank and a puff of steam, the whole contraption lurches forward, all by itself, without a single horse.","The man responsible was Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot. He was born on 26 February 1725 in the village of Void, in Lorraine in north-eastern France, and he trained as a military engineer. Armies at that time had a serious problem. Their most powerful weapons were heavy bronze cannons, some weighing several tonnes, and moving them took big teams of horses. Horses had to be fed, got tired, panicked under fire and could be killed. If a machine could haul guns instead, an army could move faster and more reliably.","From about 1765 Cugnot began experimenting with steam-powered vehicles for the French army. Steam engines worked on a simple idea: burn fuel to boil water in a closed container called a boiler, and let the expanding steam push a piston, a plug that slides in a tube called a cylinder. (Invention Lab has a whole separate Deep Dive about steam engines, so we will not go deeper here.) The tricky part was making a steam engine compact enough to ride on a vehicle and to push it along a road.","Cugnot called his machine a fardier à vapeur. A fardier was a heavy horse-drawn cart used for carrying large loads such as cannon barrels, so the name simply means 'steam dray' or 'steam heavy wagon'. In 1769 he built and tested a smaller version, and its trials were promising enough that a full-size fardier was built in 1770. That 1770 machine is the oldest surviving full-size self-propelled road vehicle in the world."]},{"heading":"Inside the Steam Fardier","paragraphs":["The 1770 fardier was enormous. It measured about 7.25 metres long, as long as a big modern van plus a car, and about 2.19 metres wide. Empty, it weighed around 2.5 tonnes. The army's specification asked it to carry a load of about four tonnes, the weight of a heavy cannon, and to travel about 2 French leagues (roughly 7.8 kilometres) in an hour.","Its layout looks strange to us. It had three wheels: two at the back under the long wooden frame where the load would sit, and a single wheel at the front. Hanging over that front wheel was the big copper boiler with its firebox, and just behind it two large metal cylinders, one on each side. Engine, boiler and front wheel were all bolted together as one unit, and the driver steered by turning that whole heavy front assembly with a pair of handles.","The pistons moved up and down, but a wheel has to spin. How do you turn a back-and-forth push into turning? Cugnot used a ratchet, like the clicking mechanism on a socket wrench or a bicycle freewheel. Each push of a piston turned the front wheel a little way round; the ratchet stopped it slipping back; then the other piston took its turn. Cugnot was one of the first people to use such an arrangement to make a steam engine drive a wheel round and round.","Now try to imagine steering it. Several tonnes of boiler, water and iron all sat on that one front wheel. Turning it was hard work. And because so much weight was out in front, the wagon was unstable, especially on rough ground or slopes. It was like trying to steer a wheelbarrow with a fridge balanced on the front."]},{"heading":"Slower Than a Walk","paragraphs":["The fardier did move by itself, which was a historic achievement. But it was painfully slow. Its real speed in tests was only about 3.6 to 4 kilometres per hour, between 2 and 3 miles per hour, slower than a person walking briskly. It never reached the speed or performance the army had asked for.","The worst problem was the boiler. It could not make steam fast enough to keep up with the engine. After roughly 10 to 15 minutes of running, the steam pressure would fall so low that the fardier had to stop. The crew then had to stoke the fire, wait for the water to boil hard again and build up pressure before the next short dash. So a trip consisted of a few minutes of clanking forward, followed by a long wait, again and again.","Why was the boiler such a weak point? A boiler can only make steam as fast as its fire can heat water. To make lots of steam quickly, you need a large heating surface, with hot gases touching as much water as possible. Cugnot's boiler was essentially a big copper pot with a fire underneath. The engine used steam faster than the fire could replace it. Later engineers solved this with boilers full of tubes, which let hot gases pass through the water many times, but those ideas were still decades away.","Tests were held between Paris and Vincennes, and at Meudon, just outside Paris. Army officials watched carefully, but they were not convinced. A cannon-hauler that crawled, stopped every quarter of an hour and could barely be steered was no better than horses. After the trials, the project was dropped, and the fardier was stored in the arsenal.","You may have read a dramatic story that during one test in 1771 Cugnot's machine smashed into a wall, making it the first car accident in history, and that he was even arrested for dangerous driving. This is a legend. No written record from the time mentions it; the story first appeared in 1804, some 33 years later. It may have happened, but historians cannot confirm it, so it is best treated as an entertaining tale rather than a fact.","Cugnot himself did not get rich. In 1772 King Louis XV granted him a pension of 600 livres a year for his work. But during the French Revolution, in 1789, the pension was stopped, and he lived in poverty in Brussels for some years. Shortly before his death, Napoleon Bonaparte restored his pension, and Cugnot returned to Paris, where he died on 2 October 1804.","His machine survived him. In 1800 the 1770 fardier was moved to the Conservatoire national des arts et métiers in Paris, and it is still on show today in the Musée des Arts et Métiers, a museum of science and technology. Visitors can stand next to the great copper boiler and see just how huge the first self-moving road vehicle was. In 2010, students from French engineering schools worked with Cugnot's home village to build a working replica, which puffed along at the Paris Motor Show."]},{"heading":"Christmas Eve in Camborne","paragraphs":["Our next stop is Cornwall, the rocky south-western tip of England, famous for its tin and copper mines. Deep mines flood with water, so Cornwall was full of steam pumping engines, and full of people who knew how to build and repair them. One of the most gifted was Richard Trevithick, born on 13 April 1771 at Tregajorran, near Camborne, the son of a mine manager. He was a giant of a man, about 1.88 metres tall, famous for his strength and a champion at Cornish wrestling.","Older steam engines, like those of James Watt, worked at low pressure and needed a separate part called a condenser to cool the steam back into water. Trevithick pioneered engines that used high-pressure steam, which people called 'strong steam'. Because the steam pushed much harder, the engine could be smaller and lighter, and it could do without the bulky condenser. A small, powerful engine was exactly what a road vehicle needed.","Trevithick built a steam road vehicle that the locals nicknamed the 'Puffing Devil'. On Christmas Eve 1801, as darkness fell, he fired it up in Camborne. With his cousin and partner Andrew Vivian steering, and six passengers riding on board, it puffed up Fore Street and Camborne Hill towards the village of Beacon. Imagine the townspeople, out for the holiday, watching a smoke-breathing machine carry their neighbours uphill without a horse in sight. It was one of the very first times human passengers had ridden on a self-propelled road vehicle.","The triumph did not last long. A few days later, the Puffing Devil broke down during another run. The team pushed it into a shelter, left the fire burning, and went off to an inn for a meal and a drink. While they were away, the water in the boiler boiled away completely. With no water to soak up the heat, the iron overheated and the whole machine was destroyed by fire. It was a mistake by its operators, not a fault of Trevithick's design, but it was the end of the Puffing Devil. The event lives on in a Cornish folk song, 'Camborne Hill', and in 2001 the Trevithick Society built a working replica that still leads parades on Camborne's yearly Trevithick Day.","The Puffing Devil was not a passenger service, just a bold experiment, and Trevithick was not even the only person trying. In the 1780s William Murdoch, an engineer working for the famous firm of Boulton and Watt, had already built a small model steam carriage that ran on its own. But Murdoch's employers were not keen on the idea, and he set it aside. Trevithick was the one who scaled it up and put people on board."]},{"heading":"A Steam Carriage in London","paragraphs":["Trevithick and Vivian did not give up. In 1802 they patented a design for a steam carriage, and in 1803 they built a new vehicle meant to impress the richest and most important city in Britain: London. The engine parts were made in Cornwall and shipped to London, and a coachmaker named William Felton, with a workshop on Leather Lane, built the passenger body. Felton charged £207 for the coach alone.","The London Steam Carriage looked like a tall stagecoach perched on top of gigantic driving wheels about 2.4 metres (8 feet) across, taller than a grown-up standing on another's shoulders. The huge wheels were meant to roll smoothly over London's rough, potholed streets. It carried seven or eight passengers at speeds between about 6 and 14 kilometres per hour (4 to 9 mph), and on one run it travelled roughly 16 kilometres, through the streets to Paddington and back by way of Islington.","Sadly, nobody wanted to buy one. On a later run it crashed into the railings in front of a house, the demonstrations had eaten up the inventors' money, and no investor came forward. The carriage was eventually scrapped. Trevithick turned to rails instead. In February 1804 his locomotive at Penydarren in South Wales pulled about 10 tonnes of iron and around 70 people along a tramway, the first time a steam locomotive had hauled a load on rails, but that is a story for the railway history books. Trevithick died poor in Dartford, Kent, in 1833."]},{"heading":"Steam Buses on Real Roads","paragraphs":["By the 1820s and 1830s, Britain went through a burst of steam-carriage excitement. Better roads built by the turnpike trusts, and better engines, made inventors think steam coaches could take passengers between towns faster and more cheaply than horses.","One of the boldest was Goldsworthy Gurney, a Cornish surgeon turned inventor. On 27 July 1829, a procession of four vehicles led by Gurney's steam carriage set off from Cranford Bridge, near Hounslow west of London, heading for the elegant spa city of Bath. It was the first long-distance journey at speed by a steam road vehicle. Not everyone was happy to see it. At the town of Melksham, a crowd, perhaps including people who feared for their jobs with horses, threw stones; the stoker was hurt and the carriage damaged. It had to be escorted under guard to Bath for repairs. On the way back, Gurney said, his carriage covered the 84 miles (about 135 kilometres) from Melksham to Hounslow in about 10 hours, including stops.","Gurney's carriages also ran a real passenger service. Starting in February 1831, Sir Charles Dance used them on the 9-mile (about 14-kilometre) road between Gloucester and Cheltenham, running twice a day. In four months they made 396 trips, averaging about 55 minutes a journey, a little faster than the hour taken by horse coaches, and fares dropped to half the old price. But then the local turnpike trustees heaped thick layers of loose new stone onto the road. Struggling across it, one carriage broke its crankshaft, and the service ended.","In London, Walter Hancock ran what were, in effect, the world's first steam buses. His 10-seat 'Infant' of 1829 was enlarged to 16 seats and from 1831 ran a regular service between Stratford, in east London, and the city centre. In 1833 his 'Enterprise' began a regular service between London Wall and Paddington via Islington. His best machine, the 22-seat 'Automaton' of 1836, made more than 700 journeys, usually rolling along at 12 to 15 mph (about 19 to 24 kilometres per hour), and could top 20 mph. By Hancock's own count his vehicles carried 12,761 passengers over about 4,200 miles. For a few years, Londoners could hop on a steam bus.","What was it like to ride one? Passengers sat in a carriage body much like a horse bus, while a crew at the front and back tended the fire and the engine. Hancock's Enterprise, for example, needed three people to run it. There was heat, hissing, the clatter of iron tyres on stone and the smell of burning fuel, but there was no horse to tire out, and the bus could keep a steady pace up hills that would have slowed a team to a walk. In October 1832 Hancock even drove his improved Infant all the way from London to Brighton on the south coast as an experiment.","Gurney also invented a very bright theatre and street light called the Bude-Light, and later a stove still used in some cathedrals. Hancock, though, had no such fallback; road steam was his life's work. By 1840 he had given up."]},{"heading":"Why the Steam Coaches Disappeared","paragraphs":["If steam buses worked, why did they vanish? There were several reasons, all pushing in the same direction.","First came tolls. The turnpike trusts that ran the main roads could charge whatever their local laws allowed, and many set enormous fees for steam vehicles. On one road a steam carriage might be charged £2 for a trip when a horse coach paid about 2 shillings, which is twenty times less. Trustees argued that heavy machines wrecked their roads. Supporters of steam replied that the wide wheels of a steam carriage did less damage than the sharp hooves of a team of horses. In 1831 a committee of the House of Commons investigated, heard evidence from Gurney and others, decided that steam carriages were safe, fast and cheap, and recommended removing the unfair tolls. The Commons passed a bill, but the House of Lords threw it out.","Second, powerful groups felt threatened. Coach companies, horse dealers, innkeepers and turnpike trusts all made money from horses. Historians still argue about how much these groups and, later, railway companies deliberately worked against road steam, but they certainly did not welcome it.","Third, and most important, came the railway. The Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened in 1830, and over the next twenty years iron rails spread across Britain. A train on smooth rails could carry hundreds of people and tonnes of goods far faster and more cheaply than any steam carriage bumping along a road. Money and engineering talent poured into railways. Even the stagecoaches began to disappear; within a few years of a railway opening, the long-distance coaches along its route usually went out of business.","And steam itself had drawbacks on the road. Boilers were heavy. The crew had to carry water and stop to refill. Starting from cold, a steam vehicle needed time, often a long time in those days, to light the fire and raise steam before it could move. A boiler needed constant attention, and a careless crew could ruin it, as the Puffing Devil showed."]},{"heading":"The Man with the Red Flag","paragraphs":["Steam did not disappear from British roads completely. By the 1850s and 1860s, big, slow 'traction engines' were working on farms, pulling ploughs, threshing machines and heavy wagons along country lanes. People worried that they frightened horses, blocked narrow lanes and damaged roads and bridges. Parliament responded with a series of laws called the Locomotive Acts.","The Locomotive Act of 1861 set speed limits of 10 mph in the country and 5 mph in towns. Then came the strictest one of all, the Locomotive Act of 1865, nicknamed the 'Red Flag Act'. It cut the speed limits to just 4 mph (about 6 km/h) on country roads and 2 mph (about 3 km/h) in towns, slower than a quick walk. Every road locomotive needed a crew of at least three. And one of them had to walk at least 60 yards (about 55 metres) ahead, carrying a red flag to warn riders and carriage drivers and to signal the driver to stop if needed. Breaking the speed limit could bring a fine of up to £10, a large sum at the time.","In 1878 the law was changed. The red flag was no longer required, and the person walking ahead only had to keep at least 20 yards (about 18 metres) in front. But the low speed limits stayed, and some counties made their own local rules, even demanding the red flag anyway. Meanwhile, the first petrol-powered cars were being built in Germany and France from the mid-1880s, but under British law they counted as 'locomotives' too. A British motorist had to crawl along with a walker in front. No wonder British carmaking got off to a slow start.","Car fans, led by people like the businessman Harry J. Lawson and Sir David Salomons of the Self-Propelled Traffic Association, campaigned hard for change. They won. The Locomotives on Highways Act of 1896 freed light vehicles weighing under 3 tons from the old rules. No walker was needed at all, and the speed limit was raised to 14 mph (about 22 km/h), though officials soon set 12 mph as the usual limit. The new law came into force on 14 November 1896.","To celebrate, Lawson organised a grand 'Emancipation Run' from London to Brighton on that very day. Emancipation means being set free. Before the start, at a breakfast in London, the Earl of Winchilsea tore a red flag in two to show that the old rule was dead. Then 33 motor vehicles set off in grey, wet November weather for the seaside town of Brighton, about 54 miles (87 kilometres) away. Only 17 are reported to have made it, a sign of how unreliable early cars still were.","The run is still remembered. Since 1927 it has been re-enacted almost every year, on the first Sunday in November, as the London to Brighton Veteran Car Run. Only cars built before 1905 may take part, and hundreds of brass-lamped, open-topped machines chug out of Hyde Park at dawn. It is called the world's longest-running motoring event."]},{"heading":"Steam's Last Bright Spark in France","paragraphs":["While British steam was held back, French inventors kept experimenting. In the city of Le Mans, Amédée Bollée ran his family's bell foundry, casting church bells, and in his spare time he built steam vehicles. At the end of 1873 he finished L'Obéissante, French for 'The Obedient One', a name that boasted it would go exactly where it was steered.","L'Obéissante carried 12 passengers and weighed just under 5 tonnes. Instead of one engine, it had two, one for each rear wheel, which helped it turn corners smoothly. On long trips it averaged around 20 kilometres per hour and on level roads it could briefly reach 40, and its front wheels had springs that moved separately, an early form of independent suspension. In October 1875 Bollée drove it about 230 kilometres from Le Mans to Paris in about 18 hours, including stops for water and meals, causing a sensation. Like Cugnot's fardier, the original now stands in the Musée des Arts et Métiers in Paris. His next steam car, La Mancelle of 1878, was built in a batch of about 50, making it one of the first vehicles of any kind produced in series.","Bollée's machines proved that steam vehicles could be fast, comfortable and reliable. But they also showed steam's stubborn weaknesses: a heavy boiler, tanks of water, coal or other fuel to shovel, and a long warm-up before you could set off. Every steam vehicle was really two machines, a furnace and boiler to make steam and an engine to use it.","So engineers began to wonder about a radical shortcut. What if, instead of burning fuel in a fire outside the engine to boil water in a heavy boiler, you could burn the fuel right inside the engine itself, so that the burning gases pushed the piston directly?"]}],"deepDive":"Cugnot's fardier is often called 'the first automobile', but historians debate what that label should mean. It was the first full-size, self-propelled road vehicle that we know was actually built and run, and the 1770 machine survives in Paris. Yet it was never practical: its boiler lacked capacity, so steam had to be raised again about every quarter of an hour, and its real speed, about 3.6 to 4 km/h, fell well short of the army's target of about 7.8 km/h with a four-tonne load. The famous 1771 wall crash appears only in an account from 1804, so it remains unproven. The British story raises a different debate: why did a country that led the world in steam technology fall behind in road vehicles? The traditional explanation blames hostile 'vested interests' such as horse-coach operators and railway companies. More recent historians stress plainer economics and engineering. Turnpike tolls were set by many separate local trusts, and some charged steam vehicles many times the horse rate. Railways, from 1830, offered far cheaper transport for both people and freight. And the heavy traction engines that later used roads really did frighten horses and strain old bridges, so the Locomotive Acts were partly a reasonable safety response, even if they became absurd for light motor cars. The speed numbers are a good example of how laws changed: 1861 allowed 10 mph in the country and 5 mph in towns; 1865 cut that to 4 mph and 2 mph with a flag-carrier 60 yards ahead; 1878 dropped the flag and allowed 20 yards; and 1896 freed vehicles under 3 tons, with a legal maximum of 14 mph that officials soon set at 12 mph. Another debate concerns the 1896 Emancipation Run itself: stories circulated that some 'finishers' had actually travelled part of the way by train, so the official count of 17 arrivals has long been treated with some caution. Finally, comparing energy sources helps explain what came next. A steam engine is an external combustion engine: fuel burns in a firebox, heats water, and only then does steam push a piston. Energy is lost at each step, and the boiler and water add great weight, which is why compact internal combustion engines would win on the road.","funFact":"Richard Trevithick's Puffing Devil was destroyed just days after its famous Christmas Eve 1801 run because its crew left the fire burning while they went to an inn, and the boiler boiled dry.","check":{"q":"Under Britain's 1865 'Red Flag Act', what did one member of a road locomotive's crew have to do?","a":"Walk at least 60 yards ahead carrying a red flag to warn other road users","wrong":["Ride a horse beside the vehicle ringing a bell","Sit on the roof blowing a whistle every minute","Run ahead and pay the toll at every gate"]},"sources":[{"title":"Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolas-Joseph_Cugnot"},{"title":"Fardier à vapeur","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fardier_%C3%A0_vapeur"},{"title":"Puffing Devil","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puffing_Devil"},{"title":"London Steam Carriage","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Steam_Carriage"},{"title":"Goldsworthy Gurney","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldsworthy_Gurney"},{"title":"Walter Hancock","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Hancock"},{"title":"Locomotive Acts","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Locomotive_Acts"},{"title":"London to Brighton Veteran Car Run","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_to_Brighton_Veteran_Car_Run"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/02-1.jpg","alt":"Cugnot's steam fardier in the Musée des Arts et Métiers, Paris, with its big round boiler hanging out front.","file":"Musée des Arts et Métiers - Fardier de Cugnot (23725801128).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mus%C3%A9e_des_Arts_et_M%C3%A9tiers_-_Fardier_de_Cugnot_(23725801128).jpg","author":"Frédéric BISSON from Rouen, France","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/02-2.jpg","alt":"The working replica of Trevithick's Puffing Devil steam carriage on a street in Camborne, Cornwall, on Trevithick Day 2024.","file":"Puffing Devil replica, Trevithick Day, Basset Street, Camborne, Cornwall - April 2024.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puffing_Devil_replica,_Trevithick_Day,_Basset_Street,_Camborne,_Cornwall_-_April_2024.jpg","author":"Mutney","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/02-3.jpg","alt":"A veteran Napier car arriving in Brighton during the 2019 London to Brighton Veteran Car Run.","file":"Napier, HE 1, London to Brighton Veteran Car Run 2019 - geograph.org.uk - 6317306.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napier,_HE_1,_London_to_Brighton_Veteran_Car_Run_2019_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6317306.jpg","author":"Peter Trimming","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3873},{"n":3,"title":"The Engine That Burns Inside","when":"1860 – 1885","summary":"Steam engines had to boil water in a big, heavy boiler before they could do anything. Then inventors in France and Germany had a cleverer idea: why not burn the fuel right inside the engine? Their new engines puffed and thumped in factories all over Europe, and one of them, the 'Silent Otto', became the great-great-grandparent of the engine in almost every car today.","sections":[{"heading":"Fire Outside, Fire Inside","paragraphs":["Imagine you want to push a toy car across the floor using a fire. With a steam engine, you would light the fire under a kettle, wait for the water to boil, pipe the steam into a cylinder, and let the steam shove a piston. The fire is outside the engine, in a separate firebox, and all the hard work is passed along through hot water and steam. That is why engineers call a steam engine an external combustion engine: combustion, the scientific word for burning, happens outside the part that actually moves.","Now imagine a very different idea. Instead of heating water, you squirt a little burnable gas and air straight into the cylinder, close it up, and light it. The hot gases expand in a flash and push the piston themselves. There is no boiler, no water to wait for and no separate fire to tend. This is an internal combustion engine, an engine where the fuel burns inside the cylinder and pushes the piston directly.","That one change sounds small, but it solved the biggest problems you met in Part 2. Cugnot's fardier and the British steam coaches had to carry heavy boilers, tons of water and coal, and they needed time to build up pressure before they could move at all. An engine that burned its fuel inside could, in theory, be much smaller and lighter, and it could start working almost as soon as it was lit. Part 8 will take an engine apart piece by piece; for now, the only thing you need to remember is this: steam engines burn outside, internal combustion engines burn inside.","The idea was not brand new in the 1860s. People had been trying it for well over half a century. In 1807 two French brothers, Nicéphore and Claude Niépce, built a strange engine they called the Pyréolophore, which used little explosions of burning dust to push a boat along the river Saône. (Nicéphore Niépce is better known today for taking one of the first photographs.) In the same years a Swiss inventor named François Isaac de Rivaz built an engine that burned hydrogen gas, set off by an electric spark, and around 1808 he fitted it to a crude wooden cart that could lurch a short distance. In 1823 the Englishman Samuel Brown patented a gas engine that was actually used for pumping water, and in the 1850s two Italians, Eugenio Barsanti and Felice Matteucci, patented an engine driven by gas explosions.","None of these early machines caught on. Some were too weak, some kept breaking, some were too expensive to run, and none could compete with the steam engine, which by then was powering mills, ships and railways across the world. What was needed was an internal combustion engine that a factory owner could actually buy, install and rely on every day. In 1860, in Paris, someone finally built one."]},{"heading":"Monsieur Lenoir's Gas Engine","paragraphs":["Jean Joseph Étienne Lenoir was born on 12 January 1822 in the village of Mussy-la-Ville, in a region that today is part of Belgium. As a young man he moved to Paris, a city buzzing with new inventions, where he became a self-taught engineer with a talent for electrical gadgets. He worked on electroplating and on telegraph machines. But the invention that made his name was an engine.","In 1860 Lenoir received a patent for an engine that ran on coal gas. Coal gas, also called town gas or illuminating gas, was made by heating coal in closed ovens, and in the 1800s it was piped under the streets of big cities to light lamps in houses, shops and street corners. That meant a factory in Paris could simply connect Lenoir's engine to the gas main, the way you might plug a machine into a wall socket today. No coal shovelling, no boiler, no stoker.","Lenoir's engine looked a lot like a small steam engine lying on its side, and in fact it borrowed much of its design from steam engines. Inside, a piston slid back and forth in a cylinder. As the piston moved along, it drew in a mixture of gas and air. Partway through its movement, an electric spark from a device called a Ruhmkorff coil lit the mixture. The burning gas expanded and pushed the piston the rest of the way. Then the same thing happened on the other side of the piston, so the engine got a push in each direction, just like a double-acting steam engine. A heavy flywheel kept everything turning smoothly between pushes.","Notice something missing: Lenoir's engine did not squeeze the gas-and-air mixture before lighting it. It simply drew it in at ordinary pressure and set it alight. As you will see, this turned out to be its great weakness. Without squeezing, the burning mixture gave only a gentle shove, and most of the energy in the gas escaped as heat. Historians usually estimate that Lenoir's engine turned only about 4 percent of the fuel's energy into useful work. The other 96 percent or so warmed up the engine, the cooling water and the room.","It also gobbled gas. Lenoir's advertisements promised that his engine would be cheap to run, but when real engines were measured, they turned out to use several times more gas than claimed. The engines got so hot that they needed a steady flow of cooling water, and if that ran out they could seize up. Even so, Lenoir's engine had real advantages for a small workshop. It was fairly quiet, it started quickly, and it did not need a licensed boiler or a full-time engine driver.","That was enough to make it the first commercially successful internal combustion engine, meaning the first one that was sold in real numbers to real customers. By 1865 about 143 had been sold in Paris alone, and in total probably a few hundred were built (fewer than 500), including some made under licence in Germany. They were modest machines by factory standards, and they ran printing presses, lathes, pumps and other workshop machines. For the first time, people who had never owned a steam engine could buy mechanical power."]},{"heading":"The Hippomobile Goes for a Drive","paragraphs":["Lenoir was not only thinking about factories. If his engine could turn a lathe, why not a wheel? Around 1863 he built a three-wheeled vehicle powered by a one-cylinder version of his gas engine, rated at only about 1.5 horsepower. It became known as the Hippomobile, a playful name built from the Greek word for horse, hippos, as if to say it was a horse that moved by itself.","Picture a day in 1863 on the dusty roads east of Paris. A clattering cart with an engine thumping away on its frame sets off from the city toward Joinville-le-Pont, a small town on the river Marne. The engine is slow and gas-hungry, the road is rough, and the passengers bounce along on hard seats. Accounts of the journey disagree about the exact distance, giving figures from about 9 kilometres to nearly 18, but most agree on one thing: the trip took around three hours. At best, that is about the speed of a person strolling, and at worst rather slower.","So was the Hippomobile the first car? It certainly deserves a place in the story. It was a road vehicle that carried people using an internal combustion engine, more than twenty years before Karl Benz's famous machine. Near the end of Lenoir's life, in July 1900, the Automobile Club de France honoured him with a silver-gilt plaque as an inventor of the gas engine and a builder of an early motor vehicle.","But the Hippomobile was more of an experiment than a useful vehicle. Its engine was designed to run on gas from city pipes, so carrying enough fuel for a long trip was a real problem. It was heavy for its power and painfully slow. Lenoir did not go on to build and sell cars. He sold his gas-engine patents in 1863, turned back to electrical inventions such as an automatic telegraph, which was used during the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 to 1871, and later experimented with engines for boats. He became a French citizen in 1870 and received the Legion of Honour in 1881, yet like many inventors he did not grow rich from his engine and was poor in his later years. He died in 1900.","The Hippomobile showed that an internal combustion engine could move a vehicle. It also showed, very clearly, that the engines of 1860 were not good enough yet. Something important was missing, and a French engineer had already worked out what it was."]},{"heading":"An Idea on Paper: Beau de Rochas","paragraphs":["Alphonse Eugène Beau de Rochas was born on 9 April 1815 in Digne, a small town in the mountains of south-eastern France. He was an engineer who worked on railways and many other projects, and he was full of ideas. Some of his ideas he built. One of the most important ones he never built at all.","In 1862, just two years after Lenoir's patent, Beau de Rochas filed a French patent that described a better way to run an engine that burned fuel inside a cylinder. Instead of letting each push of the piston do everything at once, he split the job into four separate movements of the piston. First, the piston moves outward and draws in the fuel and air. Second, it moves back and squeezes, or compresses, the mixture into a small space. Third, the squeezed mixture is lit, and the burning gases drive the piston outward with great force. Fourth, the piston moves back again and pushes the burnt gases out. Then the whole cycle starts over.","Those four steps are what we now call the four-stroke cycle: intake, compression, power and exhaust, or, as mechanics sometimes say, suck, squeeze, bang, blow. Part 8 will look at each stroke in detail. The key new step was the second one, compression. Squeezing the mixture before lighting it makes it burn hotter and push much harder, so far more of the fuel's energy ends up turning the engine instead of just heating it. Beau de Rochas understood this, and he also described other conditions for a good engine, such as making the cylinder's shape keep heat losses small and letting the gases expand as much as possible.","There was one big gap. Beau de Rochas never built a working engine to test his idea. He did not have the money or the workshop, and his patent was printed as a short booklet that few people read. For about fourteen years, his clear description of the four-stroke principle sat quietly in the French patent records while other inventors struggled on.","It is a pattern you will see again and again in the history of invention. Having an idea and writing it down is important, and it can earn you a patent. Turning that idea into a machine that works reliably, day after day, in someone else's factory is a different and often much harder job. In this story, that second job was done in Germany, by a former grocery salesman with no engineering degree at all."]},{"heading":"A Salesman, a Sugar-Maker and a Factory at Deutz","paragraphs":["Nicolaus August Otto was born in 1832 in the small German village of Holzhausen. He left school early and became a travelling salesman, selling coffee, tea, sugar and other colonial goods to shops in the Rhineland. He had no university training in engineering. What he did have was curiosity, patience and a stubborn streak.","Around 1860 Otto read newspaper reports about Lenoir's gas engine in Paris and was fascinated. With his brother, he built a copy of it and began experimenting. He wanted an engine that could run on liquid fuel, so that it would not depend on city gas pipes, but his patent application for that idea was turned down. In 1861 he noticed something important: when the gas mixture was squeezed before it was lit, the explosion was far more powerful. He tried to build an engine using compression in 1862, but the violent explosions wrecked it after only a few minutes of running. Compression clearly worked, but he did not yet know how to control it.","In 1864 Otto met Eugen Langen, a businessman and engineer from Cologne. Langen's family ran a sugar refinery, and Langen himself had invented a way of making sugar cubes, a method he later sold to the English sugar maker Henry Tate. Langen could see that Otto's engine had a future, and he had money and technical knowledge to offer. On 31 March 1864 the two men founded a company called N. A. Otto and Cie. It is often described as the first factory in the world devoted entirely to building internal combustion engines.","Their first big success was not a four-stroke engine. It was a tall, odd-looking machine called the atmospheric engine. Inside a tall vertical cylinder, a small explosion of gas threw a heavy piston upward. As the gases cooled, the pressure under the piston dropped, and the weight of the air above, the atmosphere, pushed the piston back down. On the way down, the piston engaged a toothed rack and a gear that turned the engine's shaft. It was clattery and very loud, but it used much less gas than Lenoir's engine. Its efficiency was about 12 percent, three times better.","At the 1867 World Exhibition in Paris, Otto and Langen's engine won a gold medal, beating Lenoir's design on its home ground. Orders poured in. By the mid-1870s the company was building more than 600 atmospheric engines a year, and by the time production stopped in 1882 about 2,650 had been made. In 1869 the factory moved across the river Rhine from Cologne to the industrial suburb of Deutz, and the business was reorganised as the Gasmotoren-Fabrik Deutz, the Deutz Gas Engine Factory. The company still exists today as Deutz AG.","In 1872 the company hired two men who would later become world-famous. Gottlieb Daimler, an experienced engineer who had trained as a gunsmith, studied mechanical engineering and worked in factories in England, was put in charge of running the factory. He brought with him a young designer named Wilhelm Maybach, whom he had met years earlier and who had grown up as an orphan in a children's home. Daimler organised the factory; Maybach improved the engines. For the next decade the Deutz works would be the best school for engine builders anywhere in the world."]},{"heading":"1876: The Silent Otto","paragraphs":["The atmospheric engine sold well, but it had clear limits. It was tall, noisy and could not be made very powerful; in its whole fifteen-year life it never went beyond about 3 horsepower. Otto kept returning to the idea that had wrecked his engine back in 1862: compression. If only he could tame it.","On 9 May 1876 Otto completed a new engine that did just that. It worked on the four-stroke cycle: intake, compression, power, exhaust. Each cycle took two turns of the crankshaft, and only one stroke in four actually pushed, so the engine needed a heavy flywheel to keep spinning steadily through the other three. To stop the compressed mixture from exploding too violently, Otto used a clever trick he called a stratified, or layered, charge. He arranged for the mixture to be weak near the piston and richer near the place where it was lit, so it burned progressively, more like a quick, strong shove than a sudden bang. The engine ran on town gas and was lit by a small gas flame that was let into the cylinder at just the right moment through a sliding valve. Engineers at Deutz, including Franz Rings and Hermann Schumm, helped turn Otto's experimental engine into a product that could be built and sold.","Compared with the banging atmospheric engine, the new machine ran so smoothly and quietly that it went on sale in late 1876 as the Otto Silent Engine. People soon called it the Silent Otto. It used far less gas for each unit of work, and, unlike the atmospheric engine, it could be scaled up to give much more power. Otto applied for a German patent, which was granted in 1877, and the four-stroke cycle is still often called the Otto cycle in his honour.","The Silent Otto was a huge success. In the seventeen years after it appeared, Deutz and companies licensed by Deutz built more than 50,000 engines of Otto's type. They drove machinery in workshops, small factories, printing works, flour mills, breweries and water pumping stations across Europe and America. For a small business, a gas engine was a wonderful thing: you could start it in a few minutes, stop it when you did not need it, and you never had to keep a boiler fire going all night.","But look closely at a Silent Otto in a museum and you will see why nobody put one in a carriage. These engines were heavy iron machines bolted to stone or concrete floors. They turned slowly, often around 150 to 200 revolutions per minute, and they were connected by pipes to the town gas supply. Their size and weight were fine in a factory, where the engine never had to move. For a vehicle, which has to carry its own engine and its own fuel up hills and over bumps, they were hopeless."]},{"heading":"Who Gets the Credit? The Patent Battle","paragraphs":["Otto's 1877 patent was worth a fortune. In theory, anyone who wanted to build a four-stroke engine in Germany had to buy a licence from Deutz or face a lawsuit. Other engine makers were not happy about this, and some went looking for a way around it.","They found one in the old French patent of Alphonse Beau de Rochas. His 1862 description of intake, compression, power and exhaust had been published fourteen years before Otto's engine. If the idea had already been described in public, the challengers argued, then Otto could not claim to have invented it, no matter how well his engine worked.","The case was fought out in German courts and the patent office during the 1880s. In 1886 the German patent office decided against Deutz and cancelled the key part of Otto's patent, which would otherwise have lasted until 1891. Deutz had argued that Otto's layered charge was different from what Beau de Rochas had described, but it could not convince the judges. Once the protection was gone, anyone was free to build four-stroke engines. By 1889 more than 50 companies were making engines of the Otto type.","So who really deserves the credit? The fairest answer is both of them, for different things. Beau de Rochas was the first to describe the four-stroke principle clearly, in writing, in 1862. Otto, working on his own and apparently without knowing about the French patent, built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876, solved the problems of making it run smoothly, and turned it into a machine that sold by the tens of thousands. Many historians also point out that the work of engineers at Deutz, including Daimler, Maybach, Rings and Schumm, was part of the success.","Otto was deeply hurt by the decision. He felt that his life's work had been taken away from him. Even so, his reputation survived. In 1882 the University of Würzburg had already given him an honorary doctorate, and when he died in Cologne in 1891 his engines were running all over the world. Langen, meanwhile, went on to other big projects, including the famous hanging railway, the Schwebebahn, in the German city of Wuppertal, which he started working on in the 1890s and which still carries passengers today."]},{"heading":"Small, Fast and Ready to Move","paragraphs":["Inside the Deutz works, there was a growing disagreement. Gottlieb Daimler believed the future lay in small, light engines that turned quickly and could power vehicles: carriages, boats, perhaps even flying machines one day. Otto and the company's directors were more interested in the big, profitable stationary engines they were already selling. The two men also clashed personally. Daimler felt he was not given proper credit, and Otto kept Daimler's name off his engine patents.","In the early 1880s the quarrel came to a head, and Daimler left the company (some accounts say he was dismissed). He received a large payment in Deutz shares, worth about 112,000 gold marks, as compensation for patents he and Maybach had contributed. In the summer of 1882 he used the money to buy a villa with a large garden in Cannstatt, near Stuttgart, in southern Germany. Wilhelm Maybach followed him there that autumn. In a summer house at the bottom of the garden, the two men set out to build exactly the kind of engine Deutz did not want: small, light and fast enough to move itself. Their story belongs to Part 6, but keep them in mind, because they were working at the same time as the hero of Part 4.","Meanwhile, in Vienna, another inventor was also experimenting with petrol-powered vehicles. Siegfried Marcus was born in 1831 to a Jewish family in Malchin, in northern Germany, trained as a mechanic, and moved to Vienna in 1852. He was a remarkable inventor who held more than 130 patents in 16 countries, including devices for setting off explosive charges and ignition systems for engines. He also designed a petrol engine and a clever device that mixed petrol with air, called a carburettor.","Marcus built at least two vehicles. The first, around 1870, was a simple handcart with a petrol engine, which he later took apart. The second, a four-wheeled car, still exists and is kept at the Technical Museum in Vienna. For many years people claimed it had been built as early as 1864 or 1875, which would make it older than Benz's car. Most historians today think those early dates are a myth, and that the surviving Marcus car was built around 1888 or 1889. His claim to an early place in the story is real, but exactly how early is still debated.","Marcus's story has a sad twist. After the Nazis took over Austria in 1938, they wanted to erase the achievements of Jewish people from history. His memorial in Vienna was removed, and in 1940 the German propaganda ministry ordered that his name be taken out of encyclopedias and replaced with the names of Daimler and Benz. Museum staff hid his car to protect it, and it survived the war. Today his work is remembered again, a reminder that history books can be changed for bad reasons, and that historians have to check the evidence carefully.","By the early 1880s, then, the pieces were nearly in place. Thanks to Lenoir, Beau de Rochas, Otto, Langen and the engineers of Deutz, the world had a reliable engine that burned its fuel inside. Tens of thousands of them were thumping away in workshops. But they were heavy, slow-turning machines, chained by pipes to the gas supply and bolted to factory floors. Someone needed to make one light enough to carry itself, its fuel and its passengers down a road. And in the city of Mannheim, a young engineer who had been dreaming of horseless carriages since his student days was about to try."]}],"deepDive":"Why did compression make such a difference? An engine is a machine for turning heat into motion, and the amount of work it can squeeze out depends on how much the hot gases expand while they are pushing the piston. In Lenoir's engine, the gas-air mixture was drawn in at normal atmospheric pressure, lit partway through the stroke, and then had only the rest of that stroke to expand. The burnt gases left the cylinder still very hot, carrying most of their energy away. That is why estimates of his engine's efficiency are only about 4 percent. Otto and Langen's atmospheric engine of 1867 did better, roughly 12 percent, by letting the gases expand a long way up a tall cylinder and then using the pressure of the air to do the work on the return. The four-stroke engine went further still. By squeezing the mixture into a small space before lighting it, the engine starts the power stroke with much higher pressure and temperature, and the gases then expand over a large ratio as the piston moves outward. Physicists later showed with the idealised 'Otto cycle' that the theoretical efficiency rises as the compression ratio rises. Real engines fall well short of the ideal because of friction, heat leaking into the cylinder walls and incomplete burning, but the principle explains why every modern petrol engine compresses its charge. Compression also has a limit: squeeze a fuel-air mixture too hard and it can ignite by itself or burn violently in uncontrolled pockets, a problem called knocking, which Part 8 explores. Otto's layered charge was his attempt to control this. Historians still debate how much credit each person deserves. Beau de Rochas clearly described the four-stroke sequence and the value of compression first, in 1862, and the 1886 decision against Deutz rested on that earlier publication. Yet a patent description is not a working engine, and there is little evidence that his booklet influenced Otto, who reached his design through years of experiments. Others had also compressed charges earlier; the Englishman William Barnett described compression in an 1838 patent. The story of the internal combustion engine is therefore a good example of how inventions usually happen: through overlapping ideas, partial successes and practical engineering, rather than a single flash of genius by one person.","funFact":"Eugen Langen, who helped pay for the first engine factory, had already made his mark on breakfast tables: he invented a way of making sugar cubes and sold it to the English sugar maker Henry Tate.","check":{"q":"Which French engineer described the four-stroke principle in a patent in 1862 but never built an engine?","a":"Alphonse Beau de Rochas","wrong":["Étienne Lenoir","Nicolaus Otto","Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot"]},"sources":[{"title":"Étienne Lenoir","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Étienne_Lenoir"},{"title":"Hippomobile","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippomobile"},{"title":"Alphonse Beau de Rochas","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphonse_Beau_de_Rochas"},{"title":"Nicolaus Otto","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolaus_Otto"},{"title":"Otto engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_engine"},{"title":"Eugen Langen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugen_Langen"},{"title":"Gottlieb Daimler","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gottlieb_Daimler"},{"title":"Siegfried Marcus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siegfried_Marcus"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/03-1.jpg","alt":"A Lenoir gas engine with its two big flywheels in the Deutsches Museum, Munich. It burned gas without compressing it.","file":"Lenoir engine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenoir_engine.jpg","author":"Tiia Monto","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/03-2.jpg","alt":"An early horizontal Otto gas engine with a large flywheel, on display in the Deutsches Museum, Munich.","file":"Otto A engine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_A_engine.jpg","author":"Tiia Monto","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/03-3.jpg","alt":"Siegfried Marcus's second car, a wooden four-wheeled cart with a petrol engine, in the Technical Museum Vienna.","file":"TMW Marcuswagen links 2018-07-21.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TMW_Marcuswagen_links_2018-07-21.jpg","author":"Herbert Ortner","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":4227},{"n":4,"title":"Karl Benz and the Patent-Motorwagen","when":"1844 – 1888","summary":"Karl Benz grew up poor, the son of a train driver who died when he was a baby. After years of money troubles, he built a light, three-wheeled machine with a little petrol engine at the back, and in 1886 he got a patent for it. When he first drove it through the streets of Mannheim, people stared, laughed or ran away, but it was the start of the automobile age.","sections":[{"heading":"A Train Driver's Son in Karlsruhe","paragraphs":["On 25 November 1844, in Mühlburg, a village that is now part of the city of Karlsruhe in south-west Germany, a baby boy was born to a young woman named Josephine Vaillant. He was named Karl Friedrich Michael Vaillant. A few months later his mother married the boy's father, Johann Georg Benz, and the child took his father's name. The world would come to know him as Karl Benz (his name is also often spelled Carl).","Johann Georg Benz was a locomotive driver on the railways of the Grand Duchy of Baden. It is easy to imagine little Karl hearing stories of hissing engines, coal smoke and the thrill of speeding along the tracks. But he never got to know his father well. When Karl was only two years old, Johann Georg died; family accounts say he caught pneumonia after working out in the cold and wet. Josephine was left alone to raise her son with very little money.","She was determined that Karl would have the best education she could manage, even though the family lived close to poverty. Money was tight, and she made sacrifices so that he could stay in school instead of going out to work. Karl repaid her by doing very well. At the Lyceum, a demanding secondary school in Karlsruhe, he was a top student, especially good at science and mathematics. He loved locks, clocks and machines, and he is said to have earned pocket money by repairing watches and making photographs, both very modern hobbies for the 1850s.","At the age of just 15, Karl passed the entrance exam for the Polytechnic school in Karlsruhe, one of the best engineering colleges in Germany, and began studying mechanical engineering. He graduated in 1864, aged 19. Years later, when he wrote his life story, Benz said that even as a student he had dreamed about a vehicle that could move without horses and without rails. At the time, the only machines that could pull loads by themselves were steam locomotives like the ones his father had driven, and those needed tracks."]},{"heading":"Wandering Years and an Iron Workshop","paragraphs":["Graduating was one thing. Finding a good job was another. For about seven years after college Benz moved from firm to firm, gaining practical experience but never quite settling. He worked as a fitter and draughtsman for engineering firms in Karlsruhe, Mannheim and Pforzheim, and briefly in Vienna. He learned how iron was cast, cut, shaped and bolted together, and how a drawing on paper became a real machine. These were exactly the skills he would need later.","In 1871, aged 26, he decided to work for himself. Together with a partner named August Ritter, he started an iron foundry and mechanical workshop in Mannheim, a busy trading city on the river Rhine. It went badly almost from the start. Ritter turned out to be unreliable, orders were scarce, and before long the authorities seized the company's tools because it could not pay its debts. For a young engineer with big dreams, it was a disaster.","Help came from Pforzheim. Benz had met Bertha Ringer, the daughter of a wealthy master builder and carpenter in that town. Bertha was clever, practical and very interested in technology. Before they were married, she used part of her dowry, the money a bride's family provided when she married, to buy out Ritter's share of the business. That move saved the workshop and put Karl back in control.","Karl and Bertha were married on 20 July 1872. Under German law at the time, once a woman married she could no longer act on her own as a business investor; her money and legal rights were counted as her husband's. Bertha's support, though, never stopped. She kept the household going through years of worry, ran the home, raised their five children, Eugen, Richard, Clara, Thilde and Ellen, and stood behind Karl's experiments when others thought he was wasting his time. Part 5 tells the story of her most famous day; in this part, remember that without her money and determination, there might have been no car at all.","The 1870s were hard years in Germany. A financial crash in 1873 hit businesses all over the country, and Benz's little workshop struggled to find customers for its sheet-metal and machine work. He needed a product that would actually sell. He decided it should be an engine."]},{"heading":"New Year's Eve, 1879","paragraphs":["Otto's four-stroke engine was protected by a patent, as you saw in Part 3, so Benz could not simply copy it. Instead he worked on a different design called a two-stroke engine, which makes a power stroke every time the crankshaft goes round once, instead of every second time. He hoped that selling stationary engines to workshops would finally bring in a steady income and pay for his bigger dream.","The work dragged on for months. According to the story Benz later told, on New Year's Eve 1879, after supper, he and Bertha went to the workshop to try the engine one more time. This time it started, and it kept running, with a steady beat that Benz later compared to music. Whether or not every detail of the tale is exact, the date is well recorded: the engine was finished at the end of 1879 and patented in 1880.","Building the engine forced Benz to solve many practical problems, and he patented a number of the parts he developed for it and for his later engines, including his own designs for the spark plug, the carburettor that mixes fuel with air, the clutch and the radiator. Each of these would be needed in a car.","Even so, money was still a problem. The banks that lent to Benz insisted that his business become a company with shareholders. In 1882 it was reorganised as the Gasmotoren-Fabrik Mannheim, the Mannheim Gas Engine Factory. But Benz was left owning only about five percent of the shares, and the other owners were not interested in his ideas for vehicles. Frustrated, he left after only about a year.","In 1883 he tried again. With two new partners, Max Rose and Friedrich Wilhelm Esslinger, who ran a bicycle repair business, he founded a new firm called Benz and Cie., the Rheinische Gasmotorenfabrik in Mannheim. At first it made stationary gas engines, which sold well enough that the company grew quickly. For the first time in years, Karl Benz had a steady business, a workshop, skilled workers and a little time. He used it to build the vehicle he had dreamed about since his student days."]},{"heading":"Not a Carriage With an Engine, but a Whole New Machine","paragraphs":["Many inventors before Benz had taken an ordinary horse carriage, bolted an engine onto it and hoped for the best. Benz did something different. He started with a blank sheet of paper and designed the engine, the frame, the wheels, the steering and the drive as parts of one single machine, each made to suit the others. That is one big reason historians treat his vehicle as the first true automobile powered by an internal combustion engine.","Look at the Patent-Motorwagen, which means patent motor carriage, from the side and it looks almost like a giant tricycle with a bench seat. Its frame was made of light steel tubes, the same kind of construction used for bicycles, which were becoming popular in the 1880s. It ran on three large wheels with thin wire spokes: two at the back and one at the front. Why only three? Steering two front wheels at once was a tricky problem in those days, because on a curve the inner and outer wheels need to turn by different amounts. Benz was not happy with the steering methods used on carriages, so for his first cars he avoided the problem by using a single front wheel. (Part 9 explains how engineers later solved two-wheel steering properly.) He steered it with a small crank handle on a vertical post, connected to the front wheel by a little toothed rack and gear.","Behind the bench seat, low down between the rear wheels, sat the engine. It was a single-cylinder, four-stroke engine running on the same principle as Otto's, but it was far smaller and lighter than the big iron factory engines of Deutz. Its cylinder held about 954 cubic centimetres, a little less than a one-litre carton of milk, and later measurements show it turning at about 400 revolutions per minute, about twice as fast as a typical stationary Otto engine of the time. It produced roughly three-quarters of a horsepower, about 0.55 kilowatts. That is less power than a modern ride-on lawn mower, and less than the horse it was meant to replace.","Lying flat above the engine was a big horizontal flywheel, a heavy spinning wheel that stored energy between the engine's power strokes and kept it turning smoothly. Laying it flat, rather than upright like on a factory engine, kept the machine low and compact, and it is often said that Benz chose this layout so that the spinning wheel would not disturb the steering.","The fuel was ligroin, a light, easily evaporating petroleum liquid similar to the white spirit or naphtha sold as a cleaning solvent. It was fed into a simple surface carburettor, a container in which air passed over the fuel and picked up vapour, making a burnable mixture. To light that mixture, Benz did not use Otto's little gas flame. He used electric ignition: a battery and a trembler coil produced a high-voltage spark inside the cylinder. Electric ignition was more reliable and better suited to a moving vehicle, and Benz had worked hard on it.","An engine that burns fuel inside gets very hot, so it must be cooled. Benz surrounded the cylinder with a water jacket. As the engine worked, the water heated up, boiled and simply escaped as steam, so the car had to stop now and then for its water to be topped up. This is called evaporative cooling. It was simple, but it meant that water, as much as fuel, limited how far the car could go.","Finally, the power had to reach the wheels. From the engine, a leather belt drove a cross-shaft under the seat. Moving the belt from a loose pulley to a fixed one worked like a simple clutch, letting the driver start and stop the wheels without stopping the engine. The cross-shaft contained a differential, so that the two rear wheels could turn at different speeds on a bend, and from each end of it a chain, like a bicycle chain, drove one of the rear wheels. There was only one speed and no reverse gear. To stop, the driver pulled a hand lever that pressed a brake against the drive. The whole machine was much lighter than any steam carriage and could be started in a few minutes. Starting the engine meant heaving on the flywheel by hand."]},{"heading":"How Do You Drive a Machine Nobody Has Driven Before?","paragraphs":["Today, starting a car usually means pressing a button. In 1886 it was a small adventure, and there were no driving instructors, because nobody except Benz and his workers had ever done it. Let us imagine climbing aboard a Motorwagen and going through the steps, based on how the machine was built.","First come the checks. Is there enough water in the cooling jacket? If not, the engine will soon overheat. Is there ligroin in the fuel container that feeds the carburettor? The car carried only a few litres, so it could not go very far between fill-ups, and in 1886 there were no filling stations anywhere in the world. Is the battery for the ignition connected and charged? Without its spark, the engine is just a lump of metal.","Next, the engine has to be started, and there is no electric starter motor to help. The driver stands beside the car and turns the big horizontal flywheel by hand, giving it a strong swing so that the piston is pulled through its intake and compression strokes. If the fuel mixture is right and the spark arrives at the right moment, the engine catches with a bang and then settles into a steady chuffing rhythm. If not, the driver tries again, and again.","Now the driver climbs onto the bench seat. The engine is running, but the car is still standing still, because the drive belt is on its loose pulley, spinning freely. Pushing a lever slides the belt across onto the fixed pulley, and the car begins to creep forward as the belt grips. Steering is done with the small crank on top of the vertical column, turning the single front wheel left or right. There is no steering wheel yet, no speedometer, no lights, no windscreen and no roof. On a cold or rainy day, driver and passenger simply got cold and wet.","Stopping takes planning too. Pulling the lever back slips the belt onto the loose pulley again, disconnecting the engine from the wheels, and a hand brake slows the car. The brakes were weak, the wheels were thin and hard, and every bump in the cobbled streets came straight up into the seat, because the earliest version had very little in the way of springs. Going downhill was nerve-racking. Going uphill was often worse, because with less than one horsepower and a single gear the car could slow to a crawl or stop altogether on a steep slope.","Compare that with a horse. A strong horse can work harder than this whole engine for a short time, it can find its own way home, and it does not need a mechanic. But a horse must be fed, watered, groomed and stabled every single day, even on days when it does no work at all, and it gets tired. The Motorwagen used fuel only while it was running and, in theory, could keep going as long as its fuel and water lasted. That was the promise that made Benz keep going: a tireless horse made of steel. All it needed now was more power, better gears, stronger brakes and someone brave enough to take it far from home."]},{"heading":"The Birth Certificate of the Automobile","paragraphs":["On 29 January 1886 Karl Benz applied to the Imperial Patent Office in Berlin for a patent on his invention. He described it as a vehicle with gas engine operation, in German Fahrzeug mit Gasmotorenbetrieb. (In those days, the word gas engine was often used for any engine that burned a vapour or gas, including petrol vapour.) The patent was granted on 2 November 1886 and given the number DRP 37435. DRP stands for Deutsches Reichspatent, German Imperial Patent.","The patent document is short: a few pages of text and a sheet of careful technical drawings showing the three-wheeled vehicle from the side and from above. It does not claim to have invented the engine, or the wheel, or the carriage. Its claim is for a whole vehicle driven by its own small engine, with the engine and vehicle designed to work together. Because of that, many people call DRP 37435 the birth certificate of the automobile.","The document has become so famous that in 2011 it was added to UNESCO's Memory of the World Register, a list of documents considered especially important to the history of all humanity. It sits there alongside treasures such as the Gutenberg Bible and Beethoven's Ninth Symphony.","A patent, though, does not make anyone buy a car. Benz still had to show that his strange machine actually worked, and he had to convince people that they wanted one."]},{"heading":"Laughs and Shrieks on the Ringstrasse","paragraphs":["Benz's first test drives took place in and around his workshop yard in Mannheim during 1885 and early 1886. They were short, nervous trips. The engine stalled, parts broke and had to be redesigned, and one early test reportedly ended with the machine running into a wall. Each time, Benz went back to the workshop, made improvements and tried again.","On 3 July 1886 he drove the Motorwagen in public on the Ringstrasse, the ring road around the centre of Mannheim. Imagine the scene. A summer street full of horse-drawn wagons, people walking, children playing, dogs and the occasional cyclist on a high-wheeled bicycle. Suddenly, with a rattling, popping, puffing sound and a smell of burning fuel, a carriage comes along with no horse in front of it at all, steered by a serious man in a hat gripping a little crank handle.","People's reactions were not what an inventor would hope for. Some stared in astonishment. Some laughed at the odd, spindly contraption and made fun of it. Horses shied, and some passers-by were frightened, thinking that a carriage moving by itself must be dangerous or even unnatural. The local newspapers mentioned it, but few people seemed to understand that they had just seen the beginning of something enormous. Many simply assumed it was a curiosity that would soon be forgotten, like so many other inventions they had read about.","Benz also faced practical limits. Local rules did not yet have any category for a motor vehicle, so he needed permission from the authorities for his drives, and the car's range was limited by how much fuel and water it could carry. Its top speed was about 16 kilometres per hour, roughly 10 miles per hour. That is faster than walking, about the speed of a jogging adult or a gently pedalled bicycle, but slower than a horse at full gallop.","He kept improving the design. Improved versions followed, the Model II and the Model III, with sturdier bodies, larger engines and more power, and later a second speed. Sales began in 1888, and one of the very first buyers was Émile Roger, a Paris bicycle maker who also sold Benz engines and cars in France. In 1889 a Benz Model III was shown at the great World's Fair in Paris, the same exhibition for which the Eiffel Tower was built, but it found very few buyers. Only a handful of Patent-Motorwagens were made in the first few years.","Today the vehicle has a quieter life. A Patent-Motorwagen rebuilt in the 1890s from Benz's first car has been on display in the Deutsches Museum in Munich since 1906. An early production car can be seen in the Science Museum in London, and many replicas have been built so that people can see, hear and even ride in this little machine that started so much."]},{"heading":"Fair Credit: Many Hands on the Wheel","paragraphs":["So did Karl Benz invent the car? It is better to say this: Karl Benz built and patented the first practical automobile powered by an internal combustion engine, designed from the beginning as one complete vehicle. That is a huge achievement. But he was not working alone in an empty world.","Think back over the story so far. Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot's steam fardier had moved under its own power in 1770, and British steam coaches had carried passengers on real roads decades before Benz was born. François Isaac de Rivaz had fitted a hydrogen engine to a crude cart around 1808. Étienne Lenoir had driven his Hippomobile to Joinville-le-Pont in about 1863. Benz's engine worked on the four-stroke principle first described by Alphonse Beau de Rochas and first built as a practical engine by Nicolaus Otto. In Vienna, Siegfried Marcus was experimenting with petrol-powered carts, though the dates of his surviving car are still debated.","Most strikingly, only about 100 kilometres away in Cannstatt, Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach were working in their garden workshop at the very same time. In 1885 and 1886 they built their own small, fast engine, mounted it on a wooden two-wheeler and then on a four-wheeled carriage. As far as we know, the two teams worked separately, and Daimler and Benz probably never met. You will meet their machines in Part 6. Inventions often happen like this: when the right parts and ideas become available, several people in different places race toward the same goal at once.","What made Benz's work special was the way it combined everything: a light, fast-running petrol engine, electric ignition, a carburettor, water cooling, a differential and chain drive, all built into a frame designed for them. He also had the patience to keep improving it, and a growing company to build and sell it. Later, Benz and Cie. would become one of the largest carmakers in the world, and in 1926 it merged with Daimler's company to form Daimler-Benz. Karl Benz lived until 1929, long enough to see motor cars filling the streets that had once laughed at him.","But in 1888 all of that was still in the future. Sales were tiny. Newspapers were unimpressed. Karl, who was a careful, sometimes anxious man, was not at all sure the world would ever want his car, and he is said to have hesitated to take it out on long trips in case something went wrong in front of everyone. Someone needed to show the public that this machine was not just a toy for driving around a workshop yard, but something that could carry real people on a real journey. And at home in Mannheim, his wife Bertha was quietly making a plan to prove exactly that."]}],"deepDive":"Why do historians argue about the first car, and why does Benz usually win? It depends on the definition. If a car is any self-propelled road vehicle, Cugnot's steam fardier of 1769 to 1770 came first. If it must have an internal combustion engine, de Rivaz's hydrogen cart around 1808 and Lenoir's Hippomobile around 1863 have claims, and supporters of Siegfried Marcus once argued for an even earlier date, though most historians now date his surviving car to about 1888 or 1889. Benz's claim rests on a combination of features: a light, purpose-built vehicle; a compact four-stroke engine running on liquid petroleum fuel, which could be carried on board rather than piped from a gas main; electric ignition; a patent clearly describing the whole concept; and, crucially, the start of production and sales. That last point matters. Many earlier machines were one-off experiments; Benz's design founded a continuous line of development that leads directly to modern cars. The numbers show how modest the start was. The engine's swept volume was about 954 cubic centimetres, yet it produced only around 0.75 horsepower at about 400 rpm. Modern tests on reconstructions have given figures between roughly 0.7 and 0.9 horsepower, so the exact output depends on the engine's condition and fuel. A modern small car engine of similar size might produce 50 to 70 kilowatts, about a hundred times more, thanks to higher compression, faster running speeds, better fuel mixing and precise electronic ignition. Benz's top speed of about 16 km/h was limited not just by power but by the single gear: the belt and chain ratios had to be low enough for the engine to move the car at all, so the car could not go faster without the engine racing beyond its safe speed. This is exactly the problem that multi-speed gearboxes later solved, and one Bertha would discover first-hand on the hills on the road to Pforzheim. Finally, it is worth noticing whose name is not on the patent. Bertha Benz helped fund the work, but married women in Germany at the time had very limited legal rights over property and business, so the patent is in Karl's name alone. Historians today increasingly describe the Motorwagen as a family achievement.","funFact":"Karl Benz's 1886 patent, DRP 37435, is listed in UNESCO's Memory of the World Register, which means the paperwork for his three-wheeled car is officially treated as a treasure of world heritage.","check":{"q":"On what date did Karl Benz apply for his patent DRP 37435, often called the birth certificate of the automobile?","a":"29 January 1886","wrong":["3 July 1886","31 December 1879","1 October 1908"]},"sources":[{"title":"Carl Benz","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Benz"},{"title":"Benz Patent-Motorwagen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benz_Patent-Motorwagen"},{"title":"Bertha Benz","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha_Benz"},{"title":"Gottlieb Daimler","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gottlieb_Daimler"},{"title":"Siegfried Marcus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siegfried_Marcus"},{"title":"Internal combustion engine (history)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internal_combustion_engine"},{"title":"Benz Patent-Motorwagen Nummer 1","publisher":"Wikipedia (German)","url":"https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benz_Patent-Motorwagen_Nummer_1"},{"title":"Liste des Weltdokumentenerbes (UNESCO Memory of the World list)","publisher":"Wikipedia (German)","url":"https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liste_des_Weltdokumentenerbes"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/04-1.jpg","alt":"A replica of the 1886 Benz Patent-Motorwagen: three thin wire wheels, a bench seat and the engine at the back.","file":"1886 Benz Patent Motorwagen (29744182207).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1886_Benz_Patent_Motorwagen_(29744182207).jpg","author":"Sicnag","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/04-2.jpg","alt":"Karl (Carl) Benz around 1920, many years after he built his first motor car.","file":"Carl Benz circa 1920.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Benz_circa_1920.jpg","author":"Unknown (same photo credited to various stock photo agencies. The copyright…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/04-3.jpg","alt":"Drawing of the first Benz automobile as shown in his patent of 29 January 1886.","file":"Abbildung Patentschrift.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbildung_Patentschrift.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3873},{"n":5,"title":"Bertha Benz's Daring Drive","when":"August 1888","summary":"One summer morning in 1888, Bertha Benz and her two teenage sons sneaked off in her husband's brand-new car and drove it all the way to Grandma's house, more than 100 kilometres away. Along the way she bought fuel at a pharmacy, fixed the car with a hatpin and a garter, and had a shoemaker mend the brakes. Her adventure showed the world that cars really could go places!","sections":[{"heading":"The Girl from Pforzheim Who Bet on a Dream","paragraphs":["Every famous invention has a person standing just outside the spotlight, and for the automobile that person is Bertha Benz. She was born Cäcilie Bertha Ringer on 3 May 1849 in Pforzheim, a busy town on the northern edge of the Black Forest in the Grand Duchy of Baden, one of the small states that would soon join together to form Germany. Pforzheim was famous for its jewellery and watch workshops. Bertha was the third of nine children, so the Ringer house was a lively, crowded place.","Bertha was clever and curious about machines, but in her time a girl's choices were narrow. Universities in Baden did not accept women as students, so she could not study engineering even if she had wanted to. What she could do was choose whom to trust, and she chose a serious, stubborn young engineer named Karl Benz. You met him in Part 4: the railway driver's son from Karlsruhe who dreamed of a carriage that moved by itself.","In 1870, before they were even married, Bertha used part of her dowry (the money and property a bride's family gave when she married) to invest in Karl's struggling iron-construction business in Mannheim. It was a brave and risky choice. Karl's business partner turned out to be unreliable, money was always short, and creditors were often knocking at the door. Bertha and Karl married on 20 July 1872, and over the next years they had five children: Eugen (born 1873), Richard (1874), Clara (1876), Thilde (1882) and Ellen (1890).","Here is the unfair twist. Once Bertha married, the law of the time took away much of her power to act on her own in business. Her money was now treated as her husband's. When the Patent-Motorwagen was finished, Karl applied for German patent number 37435 in his own name alone. Bertha could not legally be named as an inventor or owner beside him, even though her dowry had kept the workshop alive through the hungry years. Historians today describe her not just as 'the inventor's wife' but as his investor and partner, and the story of August 1888 shows why."]},{"heading":"Summer 1888: A Car Nobody Wanted","paragraphs":["By the summer of 1888 Karl Benz had a problem that had nothing to do with engineering. He had a working car, but hardly anyone wanted to buy it. Since his first public drive on Mannheim's Ringstrasse in 1886, he had built improved versions: the Model II and then the Model III. Only a handful of Patent-Motorwagens had been made in total; one estimate is that about six existed by 1888, counting prototypes, and only around 25 were built in all between 1886 and 1893. A French bicycle maker named Émile Roger had bought one and become Benz's agent in Paris, but in Germany people mostly stared, laughed or worried that the noisy thing would scare their horses.","Part of the trouble was Karl himself. He was a perfectionist who preferred tinkering in the workshop to showing off. He would test his car on short trips around Mannheim, often at night or in quiet places, and then return to the workshop to improve it again. Nobody outside the family had seen a Motorwagen travel a really long way, so nobody knew whether it could. Would it break down after ten kilometres? Could it climb a hill? Where would you even get fuel for it?","Abroad, things were only a little better. Émile Roger had bought the first Benz car ever sold, in 1887, in what was really a semi-private deal, and before March 1888 he had sold another Motorwagen to a French engineer named Émile Levassor, a man you will meet again in Part 6. So a few people in Paris were paying attention. But one or two sales a year could not pay the wages of a workshop. Benz & Cie. still earned most of its money from stationary gas engines for factories, and Karl's business partners were reportedly not keen on spending money on the car.","The car Bertha took is usually described as a Model III (some historians think it was actually a Model II). It looked like a large tricycle built for two adults, with a small bench seat high up, one wheel in front for steering with a tiller (a lever, not a steering wheel) and two large wheels at the back. Behind the seat sat the single-cylinder engine and a big flywheel. Power went to the rear wheels through leather belts and chains. There was no fuel tank as we know it: the car carried only about 4.5 litres of fuel in its carburetor. There was no proper cooling radiator either. Instead the engine was cooled by evaporation: water around the cylinder simply boiled away as steam and had to be topped up again and again.","The brakes were wooden blocks pressed against the iron rims of the rear wheels by a hand lever, rather like the brakes on a farm cart. There was no roof, no windscreen and no lights. Early versions had only two gears, and the lower one was still not low enough for steep hills. In other words, the Model III was a brilliant machine for a smooth, flat street in Mannheim, and a very doubtful one for a real journey into the countryside. Bertha decided to find out which it was."]},{"heading":"How to Drive a Car in 1888","paragraphs":["Before we follow Bertha onto the road, imagine you are taking a driving lesson in a Motorwagen. Forget everything you know about cars. There is no key, no starter button, no pedals for speed, and no steering wheel. Instead, you climb up onto a padded bench, quite high above the road, with nothing in front of you but a small upright lever called a tiller. Pushing the tiller left or right turns the single front wheel, a bit like steering a boat with a rudder.","To start the engine, someone has to turn the big flywheel by hand. The flywheel is a heavy wheel that keeps spinning once it gets going, smoothing out the engine's jerky pushes. Inside the single cylinder, each turn of the flywheel helps draw in a mixture of air and fuel vapour from the carburetor. An electric spark, made by a battery and an induction coil, lights the mixture. Bang, and the piston is shoved along its cylinder. With luck, after a few heaves the engine catches and settles into a steady, thumping rhythm, and you are ready to go.","Now for the clever part. The engine is running, but the car is not moving yet. That is because the leather drive belt is sitting on a 'loose' pulley, a wheel that spins freely without driving anything. When you move a hand lever, the belt slides across onto a 'fixed' pulley that is connected through gears and chains to the rear wheels. The car lurches forward. This sliding belt does the job that a clutch does in a modern car: it connects and disconnects the engine from the wheels. To stop, you slide the belt back and pull the brake lever, which presses the wooden blocks against the rear wheels.","Top speed on a good road was around 16 kilometres per hour, about as fast as a fit person can run for a short time, or a horse at a steady trot. On rough tracks and hills it was much slower. It was also loud, smelly and bumpy, and it needed constant attention: watching the water, listening to the engine, checking the chains and belts. Driving a Motorwagen was less like driving a car today and more like nursing a temperamental machine across the countryside.","It is worth remembering one more thing. In 1888 Mannheim and Pforzheim were already connected by railway, so Bertha could have made the same journey much faster and more comfortably by train. That is exactly the point. She was not travelling because she needed to get to Pforzheim; she was travelling to prove that the car could get her there. The long, difficult road was the experiment."]},{"heading":"Slipping Out at Dawn","paragraphs":["The trip is usually dated to 5 August 1888. According to the family's account, Bertha did not tell Karl what she was planning. Very early in the morning, while he was still asleep (so the story goes), she and her two oldest sons, Eugen, who was 15, and Richard, who was 13 or 14 (accounts differ slightly), quietly pushed the Motorwagen out of the workshop so the noise would not wake him. Only when they were a safe distance from the house did they start the engine, which was done by heaving the heavy flywheel round by hand.","Officially, Bertha was going to visit her mother in Pforzheim, about 106 kilometres (roughly 65 miles) to the south. That was true, but it was not the whole truth. She wanted to show the world, and especially her worried, doubtful husband, that the car could make a real journey, and she wanted people to see it. A car rattling through a dozen towns would be far better advertising than one hidden in a Mannheim workshop. She also had no special permission to make such a long drive on public roads, so the journey carried a little risk of trouble with the authorities as well as with the machine.","Picture the scene. There are no road signs for cars, because there are no cars. There are no maps meant for drivers. Bertha follows the routes she knows: the roads and wagon tracks used by horse carts and coaches, dusty in summer and full of ruts and stones. The car bounces along on its wooden wheels with iron rims and no air-filled tyres, so every bump goes straight up through the seat. The engine chugs and clatters behind them. The boys take turns helping, and some retellings say Eugen took a turn at the tiller, though Bertha is the one remembered as the driver.","Their route led south from Mannheim to Heidelberg, the old university town with its famous ruined castle above the river Neckar, then on to Wiesloch, Bruchsal and Grötzingen near Karlsruhe, and finally up into the hills to Pforzheim. Farmers stopped working in the fields to stare. Children ran alongside. Some were curious, some were frightened, and horses shied at the strange clattering noise. For most of them, it was the first motor car they had ever seen."]},{"heading":"The World's First Filling Station","paragraphs":["The first big problem arrived quickly: fuel. The Motorwagen did not run on petrol from a pump, because petrol pumps did not exist. It burned ligroin, a light, quick-evaporating liquid made from petroleum and sold as a cleaning solvent, rather like the spirit people used to remove grease spots from clothes. Where could you buy a cleaning fluid in 1888? At the pharmacy, of course, alongside medicines, soaps and chemicals.","With her small supply running low, Bertha stopped in the town of Wiesloch, south of Heidelberg, and went into the Stadt-Apotheke, the town pharmacy. The pharmacist, Willi Ockel, sold her ligroin, and the Motorwagen was ready to roll again. Because of that purchase, the Wiesloch town pharmacy is proudly called the world's first filling station. It still stands, and a memorial in Wiesloch remembers Bertha's stop. Of course, nobody in the shop that day knew they were making history; to them she was simply a surprising customer who wanted rather a lot of cleaning fluid.","Fuel was not the only thing the car drank. Remember that the engine was cooled by letting water boil away. On a long, hot summer drive that meant the car needed water constantly. Whenever they could, Bertha and the boys refilled it at inns, wells, streams and even roadside ditches. A few years later, in 1894, a careful driver of a Benz car measured that it used up to about 150 litres of water for every 100 kilometres, compared with about 21 litres of fuel. That shows how thirsty these early cooling systems were. The family's journey was really a long chain of small stops: fuel here, water there, a quick check of the chains, then onward.","This is why Bertha's drive was such a good test. It was not only a test of the car; it was a test of whether ordinary towns could support a car at all. The answer was yes, with some creativity. Pharmacies could sell fuel, inns could supply water, and local craftsmen could make repairs. That is a small version of what would later become the whole network of filling stations, garages and mechanics that cars depend on today."]},{"heading":"Hatpins, Garters and Leather: Fixing a Car with No Garages","paragraphs":["Today, if a car breaks down, you call a mechanic or a breakdown truck. In 1888 there were no mechanics who had ever repaired a car, because there were hardly any cars to repair. If something went wrong, Bertha and the boys had to fix it themselves with whatever they had. The family's story of the trip is famous for three clever repairs. They have been told so many times that small details vary, but they fit everything we know about the car's weak points.","First, the fuel line became blocked. A tiny bit of dirt in a thin pipe was enough to starve the engine of fuel, and the car stopped. Bertha took the long hatpin from her hat, the sharp pin women used to hold their hats on, and poked it through the pipe until the blockage was cleared. Fuel flowed again and the engine came back to life.","Second, there was trouble with the ignition, the electrical system that made the spark to light the fuel. A wire had lost its insulation, the covering that stops electricity escaping where it should not go. Bertha used her garter, an elastic band worn to hold up a stocking, to wrap the bare wire and insulate it. It sounds funny, but it was exactly the right idea: cover the wire with something that does not conduct electricity.","Third, and most important for the future of cars, the brakes began to fail. The Model III's brakes were wooden blocks pressed against the wheel rims. On the long downhill stretches the wood was rubbed away fast. In one village Bertha found a shoemaker and asked him to nail pieces of leather onto the brake blocks. Leather gripped better and wore down more slowly than bare wood. Without knowing it, the shoemaker had made one of the first brake linings, the replaceable, hard-wearing layer that still sits on the brake pads and shoes of every car today, although modern linings are made from special tough materials rather than leather.","There were other problems too. A drive chain stretched and needed repair, and a blacksmith helped fix it. And then there were the hills. With only two gears, the little engine simply did not have enough pulling force to climb the steepest slopes with three people on board. On those hills Eugen and Richard jumped down and pushed, sometimes with Bertha steering and sometimes with all three heaving together. It must have been hot, dusty and exhausting work in the August sun, and it gave Bertha a very clear message to bring home: this car needs a lower gear."]},{"heading":"Dusk in Pforzheim and a Telegram Home","paragraphs":["The family had left Mannheim at dawn. Hour after hour they bumped south, stopping for fuel, water and repairs, pushing up hills and riding the brakes down them. Darkness was falling by the time the Motorwagen rattled into Pforzheim, and the car had no headlights. They arrived somewhat after dusk, tired and dusty but triumphant. Counting all the stops, the trip of about 106 kilometres had taken most of a long summer day, which works out at an average speed slower than a gently trotting horse. Still, nothing about the car had stopped them for good.","Bertha did one more important thing that evening: she sent a telegram to Karl in Mannheim to tell him they had arrived safely. A telegram was a short message sent as electric signals along wires and then delivered on paper, the fastest way to send news over long distances in 1888. We can only imagine Karl's feelings when he discovered his car, his wife and two of his sons were all missing, and then learned that they had driven it all the way to Pforzheim.","Bertha spent a few days with her mother, and the car was the talk of the town. Then the family drove home. For the return trip Bertha chose a different route, partly to avoid the steepest hills. The modern memorial route follows a northern path back through Bretten, Bruchsal, Hockenheim and Schwetzingen, partly along the plain of the river Rhine. Once again the Motorwagen made it, and once again people along the way saw a car with their own eyes for the very first time.","By the time they rolled back into Mannheim, Bertha, Eugen and Richard had driven roughly 200 kilometres in total. Nobody had done anything like it before in a car with an internal combustion engine. The machine that many people had laughed at had crossed a large part of Baden, climbed (with help) into the hills, found fuel in a pharmacy and survived repairs made with a hatpin, a garter and some leather."]},{"heading":"Why One Road Trip Changed Everything","paragraphs":["Bertha's journey is considered the first long-distance trip ever made by automobile. Be careful with what that means: it was not the first time a car had been driven, since Karl and others had already made short drives. It was the first time a car proved it could carry people a real distance, between two cities, over ordinary roads, with ordinary towns supplying what it needed. That was the question every possible buyer was asking, and Bertha answered it.","The trip also worked like a road test, the kind every carmaker still does today. Bertha came home with a list of practical improvements. The car needed an extra, lower gear for climbing hills, and Karl added one. The brakes needed proper linings, and later Benz cars had leather-covered brake blocks. Karl also enlarged the engine to give it more power. In a way, Bertha was not only the first long-distance driver but also one of the first test drivers and product testers in automotive history.","It mattered, too, that the driver was a woman. In 1888 many people assumed that machines were a man's business, and that a woman could hardly be trusted with one. Bertha drove the car, navigated with no maps, bought fuel, organised repairs and solved breakdowns with her own hands and ideas, all while looking after two teenagers. Her drive has become a symbol of women's place in the history of technology. It was retold in a German television film, Carl & Bertha, in 2011, and Mercedes-Benz used the story in an advertisement for International Women's Day in 2019. More than 130 years later, it still makes a great adventure story because it really was one.","Then there was publicity. News of the woman who drove a horseless carriage from Mannheim to Pforzheim spread from town to town. The next year, 1889, the Model III was shown at the great World's Fair in Paris, the same fair that opened the Eiffel Tower, though sales there were disappointing. Benz's French agent, Émile Roger, proved much better at selling than Karl was, and slowly orders grew. In 1894 Benz launched the cheaper, lighter Velo, which sold more than 1,200 cars by 1902. Benz & Cie. grew from about 50 workers in 1889 to 430 in 1899, when it built 572 vehicles and was the largest car company in the world.","Bertha lived to see the car go from a laughed-at oddity to an everyday machine. In 1906 the family moved to the small town of Ladenburg near Mannheim, where Karl died in 1929. Bertha lived on there quietly. On her 95th birthday, 3 May 1944, the Technical University of Karlsruhe made her an honorary senator, and she died two days later, on 5 May 1944. By then millions of cars were on the roads of the world.","Today her journey is remembered on the ground. In 2008 the Bertha Benz Memorial Route was approved as a signposted tourist route, and it is part of the European Route of Industrial Heritage. Its signs lead drivers about 104 kilometres from Mannheim through Heidelberg and Wiesloch to Pforzheim, and about 90 kilometres back by the northern route, roughly 194 kilometres in all. Since 2011 a Bertha Benz Challenge for clean, modern vehicles has been held along the route. In 2016 Bertha was inducted into the Automotive Hall of Fame in the United States, 32 years after Karl.","News of her drive spread, and one question hung in the air: if a car could cross Baden, what else could it do? Meanwhile, only about 100 kilometres away in Cannstatt, near Stuttgart, two other German engineers had been working in a garden workshop on engines and vehicles of their own. Their names were Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach, and they were in a race that Karl Benz did not even know he was running."]}],"deepDive":"Historians treat Bertha Benz's 1888 drive as a turning point, but they are careful about what it proves. It was not the first time a car moved under its own power, and it was not a race or an official trial. Its importance is that it was the first documented long-distance journey by a car with an internal combustion engine, made on ordinary roads between two cities, about 104 to 106 kilometres each way. Much of what we know about the details, such as the hatpin, the garter and the exact moment of departure, comes from family accounts told and retold in later years. That is why careful writers describe the repairs as 'the family's story'. They are entirely believable, because they match the Motorwagen's known weak points: thin fuel lines, exposed ignition wiring, and wooden brake blocks that wore away on long descents. The numbers tell their own tale. The car carried only about 4.5 litres of fuel in its carburetor, so it had to be refuelled on the way, and its evaporative cooling meant water was a bigger problem than fuel: a Benz car measured in 1894 used up to about 150 litres of water per 100 kilometres against about 21 litres of fuel. Closed cooling systems with radiators, developed largely through Wilhelm Maybach's patents from 1897 onward, eventually solved this. Bertha's legal position is another topic historians discuss. Her dowry money funded Karl's business from 1870, yet as a married woman in the German states of the time she could not be named on the 1886 patent, and her contribution was long described in terms of loyalty rather than investment. Many modern writers now describe her as Karl's investor and partner, not just his supporter. Finally, the drive was also a piece of marketing. Benz & Cie. sold very few cars before 1890, but within a decade it was building hundreds a year (572 in 1899) and was the world's largest carmaker. The drive alone did not cause that growth, but it helped turn a curiosity into a product people could imagine using.","funFact":"The pharmacy in Wiesloch where Bertha Benz bought her fuel in 1888 is still known today as the world's first filling station.","check":{"q":"Where did Bertha Benz buy fuel for the car during her 1888 trip?","a":"At the town pharmacy in Wiesloch","wrong":["At a petrol station in Heidelberg","From a blacksmith in Bruchsal","At her mother's house in Pforzheim"]},"sources":[{"title":"Bertha Benz","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha_Benz"},{"title":"Bertha Benz Memorial Route","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha_Benz_Memorial_Route"},{"title":"Carl Benz","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Benz"},{"title":"Benz Patent-Motorwagen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benz_Patent-Motorwagen"},{"title":"Wiesloch","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiesloch"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/05-1.jpg","alt":"Bertha Benz as a young woman, around 1870.","file":"Berthabenzportrait.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berthabenzportrait.jpg","author":"Bühler, Mannheim","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/05-2.jpg","alt":"The Stadt-Apotheke (town pharmacy) in Wiesloch, where Bertha Benz bought fuel in 1888: the world's first filling station.","file":"Wiesloch-Stadtapotheke-Okt2012.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiesloch-Stadtapotheke-Okt2012.jpg","author":"Peter Schmelzle","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/05-3.jpg","alt":"A brown signpost for the Bertha Benz Memorial Route in Nußloch, with a little picture of her motor car.","file":"Bertha Benz Memorial Route in Nußloch.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertha_Benz_Memorial_Route_in_Nu%C3%9Floch.JPG","author":"4028mdk09","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":3840},{"n":6,"title":"Daimler, Maybach and the First Mercedes","when":"1882 – 1926","summary":"In a garden summer house near Stuttgart, two inventors worked so secretly that the neighbours thought they were making fake money! Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach built a small, fast engine, a wooden motorbike and a carriage with no horse. Later a racing fan named his new super-fast car after his daughter, who was only about 11: Mercedes.","sections":[{"heading":"The Gunsmith and the Orphan","paragraphs":["Our story now moves about 100 kilometres south-east of Mannheim, to the kingdom of Württemberg and its capital, Stuttgart. Here lived two men who, in the very same years as Karl Benz, were trying to build a light, fast engine that could move a vehicle. They were Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach, and their partnership is one of the great double acts of engineering history.","Gottlieb Daimler was born on 17 March 1834 in Schorndorf, a small town east of Stuttgart, the son of a baker. He trained first as a gunsmith, passing his trade exam in 1852, which taught him to work metal with great precision. Then he studied engineering at technical schools in Stuttgart and worked in factories in Alsace. From 1861 to 1863 he lived in England, which was then the world's leading industrial country, and worked for engineering companies including Beyer, Peacock and Company, a famous builder of railway locomotives in Manchester. He came home full of ideas about machines and mass production.","Wilhelm Maybach had a much harder start. He was born on 9 February 1846 in Heilbronn, the son of a carpenter. His mother died when he was eight and his father two years later, leaving him an orphan. He was taken in by the Bruderhaus in Reutlingen, a charitable home founded by the pastor Gustav Werner that cared for poor children and trained them in workshops. There Maybach learned technical drawing and design, and there, in the 1860s, he met Daimler, who was working for the Bruderhaus's engineering works. Daimler spotted the quiet teenager's talent, and from then on the two worked together for most of their lives.","In 1872 Daimler became factory manager of Gasmotoren-Fabrik Deutz near Cologne, the company of Nicolaus Otto and Eugen Langen that you met in Part 3, and he brought Maybach with him as chief designer. They helped turn Otto's four-stroke engine into a reliable product. But Daimler and Otto clashed. Otto's company wanted big, slow engines for factories, while Daimler dreamed of small engines light enough to power vehicles. By 1882 Daimler had left Deutz, taking a large payment as compensation, and Maybach soon followed him."]},{"heading":"The Secret Workshop in the Garden","paragraphs":["Daimler bought a villa in Cannstatt, a spa town on the river Neckar just outside Stuttgart. In the garden stood a roomy summer house with glass windows, the kind of greenhouse-like building where a wealthy family might drink tea on a sunny afternoon. Daimler added a brick extension and turned it into a workshop. There, from 1882, he and Maybach worked in great secrecy, often late into the night.","All that hammering, clanking and late-night lamplight made the neighbours suspicious. According to Daimler's biographers, they reported the two men to the police as possible counterfeiters, people who make fake money. One day, while Daimler and Maybach were away, the police borrowed a key from the gardener and searched the workshop. Instead of printing presses and false coins, they found only engine parts. The story shows how strange this new kind of work looked to ordinary people in the 1880s.","Daimler and Maybach's goal was simple to say and hard to do: make the engine faster, smaller and lighter. A big part of the problem was ignition. Lighting the fuel at exactly the right moment, over and over, was tricky. Otto's engines used a flame and sliding valves, which limited how fast they could run. Daimler and Maybach used a 'hot tube': a small metal tube, closed at one end, that poked into the cylinder and was kept glowing hot from the outside by a little burner. When the piston squeezed the fuel-air mixture into the hot tube, it caught fire. It was a crude system, and burners could blow out or set things alight, but it allowed the engine to run much faster.","In 1885 they finished an engine that was small enough to carry. It stood upright, with a single cylinder, inside a tall casing, and because of its shape people nicknamed it the 'Standuhr', which means grandfather clock. It ran on petroleum spirit and turned at about 600 revolutions per minute. That might not sound fast compared with a modern car engine, but earlier gas engines typically ran at only about 120 to 180 revolutions per minute. Faster running meant more power from a smaller, lighter engine. The grandfather clock engine is often seen as an ancestor of the modern petrol engine."]},{"heading":"A Riding Car and a Carriage Without a Horse","paragraphs":["To test the new engine, Daimler and Maybach built a vehicle around it in 1885. They called it the Reitwagen, which means riding car. It was made mostly of wood, with a wooden frame, wooden wheels with iron tyres, and no springs at all. The engine sat in the middle, beneath the leather saddle. Its single cylinder had a volume of 264 cubic centimetres and produced about half a horsepower, driving the back wheel with a belt. Because the riders were worried about balance, it had two small spring-loaded side wheels, like the training wheels on a child's bicycle.","In November 1885, Daimler's 16-year-old son Paul rode the Reitwagen a few kilometres from Cannstatt to Untertürkheim, reaching around 11 to 12 kilometres per hour. It was not a comfortable ride. The hot tube ignition sat right under the seat, and accounts say the seat caught fire. Even so, the Reitwagen is often called the first motorcycle, or at least the first motorcycle with a petrol-burning internal combustion engine. Some historians question the title, since it had those extra side wheels and steam-powered two-wheelers had been tried earlier. Daimler himself saw it only as a test bed and soon moved on. The original was destroyed in a fire in 1903, but replicas can be seen today in the Mercedes-Benz Museum in Stuttgart, the Deutsches Museum in Munich and elsewhere.","Next, in 1886, Daimler bought a four-wheeled horse carriage made by the coachbuilders Wilhelm Wimpff and Son. To avoid gossip, he told the neighbours it was a birthday present for his wife. Then he and Maybach removed the shafts where a horse would be harnessed, strengthened the carriage and fitted a larger version of their engine. The result looked exactly like what it was: a carriage with the horse missing. It is reported to have been the first four-wheeled vehicle to reach about 16 kilometres per hour.","Notice the difference between Daimler's approach and Benz's. Karl Benz, as you saw in Part 4, designed his Patent-Motorwagen as one complete new machine. Daimler and Maybach, at first, were mainly interested in the engine. They put it into almost anything that could move: the Reitwagen, a carriage, a motorboat and even an early airship. Their idea was that the engine itself was the invention, and that it could power the world. In 1889 they did build a more car-like vehicle with steel wheels and a light frame influenced by bicycles, the Stahlradwagen, and showed it in Paris. It was not produced in Germany, but it was licensed to be built in France, which turned out to be very important.","Here is one of the oddest facts in the history of the car. Benz in Mannheim and Daimler in Cannstatt were working on the same idea at the same time, only about 100 kilometres apart, and yet they apparently never met. There is no good evidence that they knew each other during the years of invention. Even when their companies fought a patent case in Mannheim in 1896, the two men are said not to have met. Their firms were rivals for 40 years, and they would only come together long after Daimler's death."]},{"heading":"German Engines, French Cars","paragraphs":["In the early 1890s the place where the car business really took off was not Germany but France. French roads were generally good, many wealthy people loved new gadgets, and Paris was full of engineers and bicycle makers looking for the next big thing. Daimler's engines reached France through a Belgian lawyer named Edouard Sarazin, who represented Daimler's interests there. When Sarazin died in 1887, Daimler asked his widow, Louise Sarazin, to carry on the agency, and she did so with great skill. She arranged for the French engineering firm Panhard et Levassor to build Daimler engines under licence. A licence is permission, usually paid for, to use someone else's patented invention. In 1890 Louise Sarazin married Émile Levassor, one of the firm's two partners.","Panhard et Levassor, run by René Panhard and Émile Levassor, made woodworking machines in Paris. Levassor first tried Daimler engines in vehicles of various shapes, and the company sold its first car in 1890. Then, in 1891, Levassor made a decision that shaped almost every car for the next hundred years. He put the engine at the front of the car, under a bonnet, with a clutch behind it, then a gearbox with sliding gears, and then a drive to the rear wheels. The driver sat behind the engine.","This arrangement became known as the Système Panhard, the Panhard system. Why was it so good? Putting the heavy engine at the front placed weight over the steering wheels, which made the car steadier. It left room for passengers behind, made it easy to reach the engine for repairs, and allowed a longer, roomier body. The gearbox let the driver choose different gears for hills and flat roads. Levassor is said to have described his first sliding-gear gearbox as 'brutal but it works', a remark that shows how rough the early versions were. Over the following decades, nearly every carmaker in the world adopted the same basic layout, and many cars still use a front engine with rear-wheel drive today.","The second French company to use Daimler engines was Peugeot. The Peugeot family had been making things in eastern France since 1810: first saws and steel tools, then coffee grinders, pepper mills, steel ribs for the hooped crinoline skirts of the 1850s, and, from around 1880, bicycles. Armand Peugeot was fascinated by vehicles. In 1889 Peugeot built a three-wheeled steam car designed by Léon Serpollet, but only four were made. In 1890 Peugeot switched to a four-wheeled petrol car using a Daimler-type engine built by Panhard. Six years later, in 1896, Peugeot began making its own engines, and Armand founded a separate car company. Today Peugeot is one of the oldest car brands in the world.","So in a strange way, Germany invented the engines and France turned them into a car industry. By the late 1890s France was the world's leading car-making country, and its races, which you will read about in Part 15, made Panhard and Peugeot famous."]},{"heading":"Troubles at Daimler's Company","paragraphs":["Back in Cannstatt, Daimler needed money to grow. In 1890 he founded the Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft, or DMG, meaning Daimler Motor Company, with Maybach as chief designer. To raise money, the company took on powerful investors, especially the industrialists Max von Duttenhofer and Wilhelm Lorenz, who had made their fortunes in gunpowder and ammunition. Daimler disliked turning his firm into a company run by shareholders, and he was soon outvoted.","The new bosses wanted to make money quickly by selling stationary engines, while Daimler and Maybach wanted to develop vehicles. Maybach was refused a seat on the board and left in 1891, though Daimler secretly kept paying him to continue designing. Working in a hotel ballroom in Cannstatt, Maybach produced two of his most important inventions. In 1893 he patented the spray-nozzle carburetor, which used the rush of air into the engine to suck fuel through a tiny jet and break it into a fine mist, rather like a perfume spray. That kind of carburetor would be used in cars for most of the twentieth century. In 1894 Daimler, Maybach and Daimler's son Paul designed the Phoenix engine, which became the company's main product.","Daimler himself was squeezed out in 1893, selling his shares. Then help came from an unexpected direction. Frederick Simms, a German-born friend of Daimler's who had set up a Daimler business in Britain, agreed to pay a large sum for the British rights to Daimler's patents, but only on condition that Daimler be brought back into DMG. In 1894 and 1895 both Daimler and Maybach returned. It shows how much the new industry depended on a few gifted people and on the friendships between them."]},{"heading":"Monsieur Mercedes","paragraphs":["Now we need to travel to the sunny coast of the Mediterranean, to the city of Nice in the south of France, where rich people from all over Europe spent the winter. Here lived Emil Jellinek, born in 1853 in Leipzig, the son of Adolf Jellinek, a famous rabbi in Vienna. Emil had been a diplomat for Austria-Hungary in North Africa and had worked in insurance, and he had become wealthy. In Nice he sold cars to rich clients, and in 1896 he bought his first Daimler car. He soon became one of DMG's most important customers and dealers.","Jellinek loved speed. He entered races along the Riviera, and like many racers of the time he used a nickname instead of his real name. The name he chose was Mercedes, after his daughter, Mercédès Jellinek, who was born in Vienna on 16 September 1889. Mercedes is a Spanish girl's name that means 'mercies' or 'mercy'. His racing team became known by the same name.","Jellinek was not shy about telling DMG what he wanted. The early Daimler cars were tall and top-heavy, more like motorized carriages, and they were dangerous at speed. After a fatal crash involving a Daimler car in a hill climb race near Nice in 1900, Jellinek pushed for something completely different: a car that was lower, longer, lighter and more powerful. In 1900 he made DMG an offer that was hard to refuse. He reportedly agreed to buy 36 cars for around half a million marks, an enormous sum, if the cars were built to his ideas and sold under the name Mercedes in France and some other countries.","So, just to be clear: the famous name Mercedes does not come from Daimler or Benz or anyone in their families. It comes from a girl of about 11, the daughter of a car fan in Nice. Funnily enough, the real Mercédès never shared her father's passion for cars. She was a musician with a fine singing voice, and she is said never to have owned a car herself."]},{"heading":"The First Modern Car: the Mercedes 35 hp","paragraphs":["Wilhelm Maybach, with help from Paul Daimler, designed the new car. The first one was delivered to Jellinek in Nice in December 1900, and it became known as the Mercedes 35 hp, after its engine power of 35 PS (German horsepower, almost exactly the same as 35 horsepower). It looked completely different from the horseless carriages of a few years earlier.","First, it was low. The frame was made of pressed steel, steel sheets stamped into strong U-shaped beams, instead of wood or steel tubes. The engine sat low down over the front axle, and the car's centre of gravity was much lower, so it was far less likely to tip over in corners. The wheelbase, the distance between front and rear wheels, was long, which made it more stable at speed.","Second, it was powerful. The engine had four cylinders and a capacity of about 5.9 litres, more than six times the size of the engine in Benz's first Motorwagen. It produced its 35 horsepower at about 950 revolutions per minute. Maybach used light metals: the crankcase was made of aluminium. Instead of the old hot tubes, the engine used a magneto, a small generator that made its own electric current for the ignition sparks, so there were no flames to blow out.","Third, it could keep cool. Maybach's honeycomb radiator, which he had patented in 1897, had about 8,000 thin tubes through which air could flow, giving a huge surface for heat to escape from the cooling water. Water went round and round in a closed system instead of boiling away. Compare this with the evaporative cooling of Bertha Benz's Motorwagen, which had to be refilled every few kilometres. The radiator sat right at the front, giving the Mercedes its famous face.","Fourth, it was easy to drive fast. A gear lever moved in a metal 'gate' with slots for four forward gears and reverse, a design that made changing gear more precise. The car weighed about 1,200 kilograms, and the racing versions could reach about 86 kilometres per hour.","Success did not come instantly. In January 1901 the new cars were entered in a race at Pau in south-western France, and technical problems put them out after only a few laps. Maybach and the DMG engineers went back to work on the faults, and a few weeks later they were ready for a much bigger test.","In March 1901, at the 'Nice Week' of motor races, the Mercedes cars, partly driven by Wilhelm Werner, astonished everyone. They won race after race, and in the hill climb from Nice to La Turbie they set a record average speed of about 51 kilometres per hour, reaching about 86 kilometres per hour at top speed. A French motoring writer, Paul Meyan, is reported to have said: 'We have entered the Mercedes era.' Other carmakers quickly began copying its design. That is why many historians call the 1901 Mercedes 35 hp the first modern car: its low pressed-steel frame, front engine, honeycomb radiator and gate gearchange set the pattern for decades to come.","Just think how far things had moved in only fifteen years. Benz's first Motorwagen of 1886 had about three-quarters of a horsepower and a top speed of about 16 kilometres per hour. The 1901 Mercedes had around 35 horsepower, nearly fifty times as much, and could go five or six times as fast. The Motorwagen looked like a tricycle; the Mercedes looked like a car. The horseless carriage had become a machine with a shape of its own, and once people had seen it, there was no going back."]},{"heading":"From Mercedes to Mercedes-Benz","paragraphs":["Sadly, Gottlieb Daimler never saw this triumph. He had suffered from heart trouble for years, and he died in Cannstatt on 6 March 1900, aged 65, before the first Mercedes was finished. His son Paul and Wilhelm Maybach carried on the work. In June 1902 DMG registered Mercedes as its trademark for cars. In 1903 Emil Jellinek legally changed his own name to Jellinek-Mercedes and joked that this was probably the first time a father had taken his daughter's name.","The company went through dramatic times. On 10 June 1903 a great fire burned down the old Cannstatt factory, destroying machines and 93 finished Mercedes cars, and production moved to a big new factory in Untertürkheim nearby. Maybach finally left DMG in 1907 after the company gave him an unimportant role. He went on to build engines for Zeppelin airships and, after World War I, luxury cars under his own name. He died in 1929. In 1909 DMG adopted its famous three-pointed star as a trademark. According to the company's story, it went back to Gottlieb Daimler's dream of engines for land, water and air.","After World War I Germany suffered a terrible economic crisis, with money losing its value almost by the hour. DMG built only 1,020 vehicles in 1923 and even made bicycles and typewriters to survive. Its old rival, Benz & Cie. in Mannheim, was struggling too. In 1924 the two firms signed an agreement to work together, and on 28 June 1926 they merged to form Daimler-Benz. Their cars were given a new brand that joined the two histories: Mercedes-Benz. Karl Benz, by then in his eighties, sat on the board of the new company until his death in 1929. The rivals who had never met were now one company.","Mercédès Jellinek died in Vienna in February 1929 at the age of 39. The girl who never owned a car had given her name to one of the most famous car brands on Earth.","Yet if you had travelled back to the streets of New York, Paris or London around 1900, just as the first Mercedes was being built, you might have been surprised by what you saw. Many of the motor cars gliding past were not petrol cars at all. Some hummed along almost silently, powered by electric batteries. Others puffed clouds of white vapour, because they ran on steam. In many cities, those quiet electric and steam cars were the most popular kind of all. The great race between steam, electricity and petrol was only just beginning."]}],"deepDive":"Who deserves credit for the modern car? The Daimler-Maybach story shows why historians avoid naming a single inventor. Benz designed the first complete, patented petrol car in 1886. Daimler and Maybach, working independently in Cannstatt, focused on a light, high-speed engine: the 1885 'grandfather clock' engine ran at about 600 revolutions per minute, several times faster than the roughly 120 to 180 rpm of earlier gas engines. Because power is roughly the force on the piston multiplied by how often it fires, a faster engine can give more power from less weight, and that power-to-weight ratio is what made engines practical for vehicles, boats and, later, aircraft. Their hot-tube ignition was a stepping stone; by 1901 the Mercedes used a low-voltage magneto, and in 1902 Bosch's high-voltage magneto and spark plug (Part 8) set the standard. Many key steps were French. Panhard et Levassor's 1891 layout, with the engine in front driving the rear wheels through a clutch and sliding-gear gearbox, became the template for most cars for the next century. Historians also emphasise Louise Sarazin, who negotiated the French licences after her husband's death in 1887 and is one of several women, like Bertha Benz, whose business roles were long overlooked. The claim that the 1901 Mercedes 35 hp was 'the first modern car' is a judgement, not a measurable fact. Supporters point to its combination of features: a pressed-steel frame, a low centre of gravity, a 5.9-litre four-cylinder engine with an aluminium crankcase, a honeycomb radiator with thousands of tubes in a closed cooling system and a gate gearchange. Critics note that each feature had appeared somewhere before; what was new was putting them together in one well-balanced, fast and reliable package. The business story matters too. Emil Jellinek's large order, reportedly 36 cars for around half a million marks, gave DMG the money and confidence to build a radical design, an early example of a customer shaping a product. The 1926 merger that created Daimler-Benz was driven by the post-war economic crisis rather than friendship: both firms were weak, and combining design, production and sales helped them survive.","funFact":"The first person to ride Daimler's 1885 Reitwagen, often called the first motorcycle, was his 16-year-old son Paul, and the seat reportedly caught fire because the hot ignition tube sat right under it.","check":{"q":"Whose name did the Mercedes car get?","a":"Mercédès Jellinek, the daughter of Emil Jellinek","wrong":["Gottlieb Daimler's wife","Karl Benz's mother","A town near Stuttgart"]},"sources":[{"title":"Gottlieb Daimler","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gottlieb_Daimler"},{"title":"Wilhelm Maybach","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilhelm_Maybach"},{"title":"Daimler Reitwagen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daimler_Reitwagen"},{"title":"Panhard","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panhard"},{"title":"Peugeot","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peugeot"},{"title":"Mercedes 35 hp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercedes_35_hp"},{"title":"Emil Jellinek","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emil_Jellinek"},{"title":"Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/06-1.jpg","alt":"Daimler's 1885 Reitwagen, a wooden motorcycle with small side wheels, at the Mercedes-Benz Museum in Stuttgart.","file":"Daimler Reitwagen.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daimler_Reitwagen.JPG","author":"Wladyslaw","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 de"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/06-2.jpg","alt":"The 1901 Mercedes racing car designed by Wilhelm Maybach, the first car named Mercedes, in a factory photo.","file":"Mercedes 35hp by Wilhelm Maybach.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercedes_35hp_by_Wilhelm_Maybach.jpg","author":"Mercedes-Benz-Werkphoto","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/06-3.jpg","alt":"Emil Jellinek with his young daughter Mercedes sitting on his lap; the car brand was named after her.","file":"Emil and mercedes jellinek.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emil_and_mercedes_jellinek.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3751},{"n":7,"title":"Steam, Electric or Gasoline? The Great Race of 1900","when":"about 1880 – 1915","summary":"Around 1900, nobody knew what kind of engine cars would use. Some cars hummed quietly on batteries, some hissed with steam, and some banged and smoked with gasoline. One electric car even became the first to zoom faster than 100 km/h!","sections":[{"heading":"Three Kinds of Car on One Street","paragraphs":["Imagine standing on a busy street in New York City in the year 1900. Horses still clop past pulling wagons and cabs, but every now and then something strange rolls by with no horse at all. First comes a cab that makes almost no sound, just a soft whirr and the crunch of its wheels. A few minutes later a little buggy glides past with a faint hiss and a puff of white vapour, like a kettle on wheels. Then a third machine comes rattling around the corner, popping and banging, leaving a blue cloud of smelly smoke behind it. People cover their noses. A horse shies away.","Those three machines were the three teams in one of the most important contests in the history of technology. The first was an electric car, running on batteries. The second was a steam car, which burned fuel to boil water. The third was a gasoline car, with the new internal combustion engine you met in Parts 3 to 6. In 1900 nobody knew which one would win. Many sensible people would have bet against the noisy gasoline car.","In fact, around 1900 gasoline cars were a minority on American roads. Only a few thousand cars were built in the United States that year, and steam and electric cars together made up most of them. Sources disagree on the exact numbers. One often-quoted estimate says roughly 40 percent of American cars were steam, about 38 percent electric and only about 22 percent gasoline; the US Department of Energy says that electric cars alone were roughly a third of the vehicles on the road. Whichever count you use, the picture is the same: the gasoline car was the underdog.","So how did the underdog win? To find out, we need to meet the teams, their inventors and their heroes, and look at what each kind of car was good at and bad at. Along the way we will see the first car ever to break 100 kilometres per hour, a steam car that flew through the air on a Florida beach, and a clever starter motor that changed everything."]},{"heading":"Team Electric: Batteries on Wheels","paragraphs":["Electric cars are often talked about as something brand new, but they are actually older than most gasoline cars. The key ingredient was the rechargeable battery. In 1859 the French scientist Gaston Planté invented the lead-acid battery, a box of lead plates in acid that could be charged up with electricity, then give that electricity back later. It is the same basic type of battery that still starts most gasoline cars today. Better versions appeared around 1881, and suddenly inventors had a way to carry stored electricity around.","One of the first to try was Gustave Trouvé, a brilliant Parisian electrical engineer who invented dozens of gadgets, from a tool for finding bullets inside wounded soldiers to an early electric outboard motor for boats. In 1881 he took a tricycle made by the English bicycle maker James Starley, fitted it with a small electric motor that he had improved from a Siemens design, and added rechargeable batteries. On 19 April 1881 he rode it along the Rue de Valois in the middle of Paris. It was one of the first electric vehicles ever to carry a person on a city street. Trouvé never managed to patent it, which is one reason his name is not as famous as it deserves to be.","Across the Channel in England, the engineer Thomas Parker was also busy. Parker worked in Wolverhampton, in the industrial Midlands, and he helped bring electric power and trams to British towns. He is reported to have built an electric car around 1884, although the earliest surviving photograph of one of his vehicles dates from 1895. His company, Elwell-Parker, built early electric carriages in the 1890s.","In the United States, a chemist named William Morrison of Des Moines, Iowa, built an electric carriage around 1890 that could carry six people at about 14 miles per hour (roughly 23 km/h). It was really an ordinary wagon with a motor and a heavy load of batteries, but crowds came to see it, and it got Americans excited about horseless carriages.","Then came the first electric taxis. Two engineers in Philadelphia, Henry Morris and Pedro Salom, built electric carriages they called Electrobats. In 1897 their company began running a fleet of electric cabs in New York City, starting with about a dozen and growing to more than 60. The business was soon taken over by the Electric Vehicle Company, which tried to build hundreds more. It even set up stations where a cab's heavy, nearly empty battery could be swapped for a fully charged one, a clever idea that some electric car companies are still experimenting with today. That same year, 1897, London got its own electric cabs designed by Walter Bersey. Londoners nicknamed them Hummingbirds because of the humming noise they made."]},{"heading":"The Never Satisfied: Breaking 100 km/h","paragraphs":["Now for the most thrilling chapter in Team Electric's story. In 1899, the fastest car in the world was not a gasoline car at all. It was an electric car shaped like a bullet, and its name was La Jamais Contente, which is French for 'The Never Satisfied'.","Its driver was Camille Jenatzy, a Belgian engineer born in 1868 in Schaerbeek, near Brussels. Jenatzy built electric vehicles, and he had a bushy red beard that earned him the nickname 'Le Diable Rouge', the Red Devil. He also had a rival: a French nobleman named Count Gaston de Chasseloup-Laubat, who drove an electric car made by the Jeantaud company. Their contest was one of the first speed duels in motoring history.","The battleground was a straight road at Achères, a small town just west of Paris. On 17 January 1899 Jenatzy covered a timed kilometre at about 66.7 km/h, a new record. The very same day Chasseloup-Laubat went faster. Ten days later Jenatzy came back and reached about 80 km/h. Chasseloup-Laubat answered again, this time with a car given a simple pointed nose to help it slip through the air. Back and forth the record went, like a tennis rally.","Jenatzy decided to end the argument with a car built for nothing but speed. La Jamais Contente was long and narrow, with a body shaped like a torpedo or a cigar. The body was made of partinium, a light alloy containing aluminium, tungsten and magnesium. Underneath were two electric motors, each one driving the rear wheels by chain, and a heavy load of batteries. Together the motors could produce around 68 horsepower for a short burst, an enormous amount for 1899, when Karl Benz's first car had produced less than one. The whole machine weighed about 1,450 kilograms, much of it batteries. It rolled on air-filled Michelin tyres.","The design was not perfect. The driver sat high up, with his head, shoulders and chest sticking out above the smooth body, which spoiled some of the streamlining. The wheels and the chassis underneath were exposed to the wind too. But it was the first car ever built specially to break the land speed record.","On 29 April 1899 (some sources say 1 May), Jenatzy climbed aboard at Achères and switched on the power. The car hummed down the road, faster and faster, and the timekeepers clicked their watches. The result: about 105.9 kilometres per hour, or 65.8 miles per hour. For the first time in history, a car had gone faster than 100 km/h. A person on that road was moving faster than a galloping racehorse and faster than many trains of the day.","La Jamais Contente held the record for about three years, until 1902, when the Frenchman Léon Serpollet beat it in a steam car. Jenatzy himself went on to race gasoline cars and won the important Gordon Bennett Cup race in Ireland in 1903 driving a Mercedes. The bullet-shaped electric car still survives. You can see it today at the National Car and Tourism Museum in the Château de Compiègne, north of Paris."]},{"heading":"Team Steam: Kettles That Could Fly","paragraphs":["If you think a steam car must have been slow and clumsy, like Cugnot's great wagon in Part 2, the Stanley twins would like a word with you. Francis Edgar Stanley and Freelan Oscar Stanley were identical twins born in 1849 in Kingfield, Maine. They looked so alike that people often could not tell them apart. Before cars, they made a fortune with photographic plates, and they sold that business to the Eastman Kodak company. With the money, they turned to building steam cars.","Their first car ran in 1897. Within a couple of years they had sold more than 200 cars, which made them one of the biggest carmakers in America at the time. In 1902 they founded the Stanley Motor Carriage Company, with its factory in Watertown, Massachusetts. Their cars became known everywhere as Stanley Steamers.","Here is how a Stanley worked, in simple terms. Under the seat sat a round boiler, a strong metal drum full of water threaded with many small tubes. Beneath it was a burner that burned gasoline, or later kerosene, a bit like a giant camping stove. The flames heated the water, the water turned to high-pressure steam, and the steam pushed the pistons of a small two-cylinder engine connected directly to the rear axle. To make the boiler strong, the Stanleys wound piano wire around its outer shell. There was no gearbox at all. The more steam you let in, the faster you went, smoothly and almost silently.","And they could go fast. In January 1906 a Stanley racing car, a low, upturned-canoe-shaped machine nicknamed the Rocket, came to the hard, flat sand of Ormond-Daytona Beach in Florida. Its driver was Fred Marriott, a mechanic from the Stanley factory. According to the company's story, he got the job partly because he was the only bachelor in the team, so if something went wrong there would be no widow left behind. Marriott raced over a measured mile in about 28.2 seconds. That works out to about 127 miles per hour, or roughly 205 km/h. It was the fastest any car had ever gone, and it was several years before any car of any kind went faster.","The next year, 1907, Marriott tried to go even faster in an improved Rocket. The car hit a bump in the sand at an estimated 140 to 150 miles per hour, lifted into the air like a plane taking off, and broke apart when it landed. Marriott was badly hurt but survived. He never tried for the record again, and the Stanleys decided not to risk any more racing.","Marriott's steam car record turned out to be amazingly tough. It stood for more than a hundred years. Only in August 2009 did a British team beat it, at Edwards Air Force Base in California, with a modern steam car called Inspiration. Driver Charles Burnett III averaged about 139.8 miles per hour over a measured mile, and a day later Don Wales pushed the car to about 148 miles per hour over a kilometre.","The Stanleys were not alone. The White company of Cleveland, Ohio, a firm that also made sewing machines, built thousands of steam cars between 1900 and 1911. Rollin White designed a clever boiler made of stacked coils of tube that made steam quickly. White steam cars were tough and comfortable, and one was even used by the US Secret Service, driving along behind President Theodore Roosevelt's horse-drawn carriage. When President William Howard Taft turned the White House stables into a garage in 1909, White cars were among the first vehicles kept there."]},{"heading":"Report Cards for Three Racers","paragraphs":["If you were buying a car in 1900, which would you have chosen? Let us give each team a report card.","Electric cars scored top marks for being quiet, clean and easy. There was no smoke, no smell and no hot, dangerous fire anywhere. You did not have to change gears or wrestle with a crank. You simply flicked a switch, moved a lever, and off you went. That made them popular with doctors making house calls in town, with city families, and especially with wealthy women, who were the target of many electric car advertisements. Clara Ford, the wife of Henry Ford himself, drove an electric car made by the Detroit Electric company rather than one of her husband's gasoline cars. But electrics had serious weaknesses. Their heavy lead-acid batteries stored only a small amount of energy for their weight. A typical electric car of the time could manage only about 30 to 40 miles (50 to 65 km) before it needed recharging, and its top speed was often only about 15 to 20 miles per hour. Recharging took hours, and outside big cities hardly any homes had electricity at all. The inventor Thomas Edison worked for years on a better nickel-iron battery to fix these problems, but even his improved battery could not close the gap.","Steam cars got high marks for power and smoothness. They could climb steep hills, glide along without changing gear, and, as Fred Marriott showed, go astonishingly fast. Their weak points were time and water. On early steam cars, getting the boiler hot enough could take many minutes before you could set off, which was annoying if you just wanted to pop to the shops. They also gulped water, so drivers had to stop to refill often, and in freezing winters the water could freeze. Lighting a burner and watching the pressure gauges took skill. Many people also feared that a boiler might explode, even though Stanley boilers were very strongly made.","Gasoline cars had the worst manners of the three. Their engines were noisy, rattly and smelly. They had to be started with a hand crank, a metal handle stuck into the front of the engine that you had to spin hard with your arm. If the engine fired at the wrong moment, the crank could kick back violently, and broken wrists and arms were common. Changing gear on early gasoline cars was tricky, with grinding and crunching if you got it wrong. But gasoline had one gigantic advantage: it packs an enormous amount of energy into a small, light tank. A gasoline car could travel far, then fill up again in a few minutes from a can. On a long trip into the countryside, that mattered more than anything else.","So in 1900 the race looked close. Electric was best in town, steam was the speed king, and gasoline was best for long journeys. The winner would depend on what changed next: fuel prices, roads, factory methods and one very clever invention."]},{"heading":"Why Not Both? Porsche's Early Hybrid","paragraphs":["Some inventors asked a different question: why choose? A young Austrian engineer named Ferdinand Porsche, who would later become very famous (and whose story continues in Part 12), came up with a remarkable answer around 1900.","Porsche was working for Jacob Lohner and Company, a Vienna firm that built fine horse-drawn carriages for royalty and the rich. Together they created the Lohner-Porsche. Instead of one big motor connected to the wheels by chains or shafts, Porsche put an electric motor right inside the hub of each front wheel. Each motor turned its own wheel directly, with no gears or chains in between, so very little energy was wasted. These are called hub motors, and the idea is still used in some electric vehicles today. One battery-powered Lohner-Porsche was shown at the great Paris Exposition of 1900, where millions of visitors came to see the newest wonders of science.","The problem, as always, was the battery. One special version, a coach with motors in all four wheels built for a British customer, needed about 1.8 tonnes of batteries. So Porsche tried something cleverer. In his 'Mixte' cars, a gasoline engine did not drive the wheels at all. Instead it turned a generator, which made electricity, which then powered the motors in the wheels. A battery could store extra energy too. A car with both a fuel engine and an electric motor is called a hybrid, and Porsche's Mixte is one of the earliest examples in the world. It also had four-wheel drive in some versions, decades before that became common.","Lohner-Porsche cars were expensive and heavy, and only a few hundred were sold in total. But the idea did not die. Almost a hundred years later, in 1997, Toyota began selling the Prius hybrid in Japan, using a computer-controlled version of the same basic idea, as you will see in Part 18."]},{"heading":"How the Race Was Won","paragraphs":["Between about 1900 and 1915, the contest swung decisively toward gasoline. No single event did it. Instead, four big changes piled up, each one adding weight on the gasoline side of the scale.","The first change gushed out of the ground in Texas. On 10 January 1901, workers drilling on a low hill called Spindletop, near the town of Beaumont, heard a rumble. Suddenly mud, then gas, then oil came roaring out of the hole and shot more than 150 feet (about 45 metres) into the air. The Lucas gusher, as it was called, sprayed an estimated 100,000 barrels of oil a day, and it took workers nine days to bring it under control. Spindletop was just the start. Huge new oil fields were found in Texas, Oklahoma and California, and the United States became the world's leading oil producer. Gasoline, which had once been an almost useless leftover from making lamp oil, became cheap and plentiful.","The second change was roads. As more people bought cars, they wanted to drive between towns, not just around them. Roads between cities slowly improved, and drivers wanted cars that could go 100 miles or more without stopping for hours. An electric car could not do that, and there were no charging points in the countryside. A gasoline car could, and soon there were places to buy fuel in almost every town. Long range was the gasoline car's superpower.","The third change was price. In 1908 Henry Ford launched the Model T, and his factories found ways to build it faster and cheaper every year (you will see how in Part 11). According to the US Department of Energy, by 1912 a gasoline car could cost about 650 dollars, while an electric roadster cost about 1,750 dollars, almost three times as much. For most families, the choice was easy.","The fourth change solved the gasoline car's worst problem: the dangerous hand crank. A famous story says that in 1910 a car builder named Byron Carter stopped to help a woman whose car had stalled, and the crank kicked back and smashed his jaw, leading to his death. Historians are not sure the story is true in every detail, but it is often told as the reason the boss of Cadillac, Henry Leland, decided cranking had to go. Leland turned to Charles Kettering, an inventive engineer from Ohio whose company was called Delco, short for Dayton Engineering Laboratories Company.","Kettering's solution was brilliant. He fitted the car with a battery and a small but very powerful electric motor. When the driver pressed a button or pedal, the battery sent a strong burst of current to the motor, and the motor turned the engine over through gears until it fired and started running on its own. Then the clever part: once the engine was running, it turned a generator that recharged the battery, ready for the next start. The same electrical system also powered the spark for the engine and the car's electric lights. Electric lights were a big improvement in themselves, since many early cars used gas or oil lamps.","Kettering had a working model by February 1911, and Cadillac put his electric self-starter on its 1912 cars, ordering 12,000 of them. The invention won Cadillac a famous British engineering award, the Dewar Trophy, in 1913. Other carmakers quickly copied the idea. Now anyone could start a gasoline car with the press of a button, without any risk of a broken arm. One of the electric car's biggest selling points, its easy starting, had disappeared overnight."]},{"heading":"The Winner Takes the Road","paragraphs":["Team Electric and Team Steam did not vanish at once. Electric car sales in the United States peaked in the early 1910s, and more than 300 different American companies built electric vehicles at one time or another. Electric delivery vans, milk floats and factory trucks carried on working for decades, especially in Britain, where battery-powered milk floats hummed through the streets in the early morning until quite recently. But as private cars, electrics slowly faded, and by the 1930s they had nearly disappeared.","Steam cars faded too. White built its last steam car in January 1911 and switched to gasoline cars and trucks. The Stanley company carried on making steam cars, but after Francis Stanley died in a road accident in 1918 the business lost its way, and it closed in 1924. Altogether the Stanleys built about 11,000 steam cars. A few enthusiasts, such as the Doble company in California in the 1920s, built astonishingly advanced steam cars that could start in under a minute, but they were far too expensive for ordinary buyers.","By about 1915, the gasoline car had clearly won the great race. It was not the quietest, the cleanest or, at first, the fastest. It won because it could go far on cheap fuel, because factories learned to build it cheaply, and because clever engineers fixed its worst faults one by one. That is an important lesson about inventions: the winner is not always the one that looks best on day one, but the one that gets improved the fastest.","There is a twist in this tale, of course. A hundred years later, worries about pollution and climate would bring electric cars roaring back, as you will discover in Part 18. But for most of the twentieth century, the road belonged to the gasoline engine. So what exactly was going on inside that noisy metal box under the hood? Let us lift the hood, peer inside, and find out exactly how tiny explosions make a car move."]}],"deepDive":"Why did gasoline beat batteries in the early 1900s, and why are batteries winning now? Much of the answer lies in a number engineers call energy density: how much energy a fuel or battery stores per kilogram. Gasoline holds roughly 12 to 13 kilowatt-hours of chemical energy per kilogram. The lead-acid batteries of 1900 stored only a few tens of watt-hours per kilogram, hundreds of times less. Even allowing for the fact that an electric motor turns most of its energy into motion while an early gasoline engine wasted the large majority of its fuel as heat, a gasoline car could carry many times more useful energy for the same weight. That is why electric cars of the era were limited to roughly 30 to 40 miles of range and a top speed of 15 to 20 mph, while gasoline cars could cross whole states. Refuelling also mattered: pouring liquid fuel transfers energy far faster than recharging a battery from a weak early electricity supply, and in 1910 most rural American homes had no electricity at all. Historians also argue about how much the result was decided by technology and how much by business and timing. The Electric Vehicle Company, which ran New York's electric cabs, collapsed partly through poor management and scandals over vehicle performance, not just battery physics. Cheap Texas oil after Spindletop in 1901, Ford's falling prices and the self-starter of 1912 all arrived within about a decade, creating a snowball effect: more gasoline cars led to more filling stations and repair shops, which made gasoline cars even more convenient. Economists call this kind of self-reinforcing advantage a network effect or lock-in. Steam's decline is a similar story. Stanley cars were technically impressive, but the company built only about 11,000 in nearly three decades, while Ford was soon building that many in days. Statistics from this period are uncertain: production and registration figures for 1900 vary from source to source, so the often-quoted split of about 40 percent steam, 38 percent electric and 22 percent gasoline should be treated as a rough estimate rather than a precise count.","funFact":"The first car to travel faster than 100 km/h, La Jamais Contente in 1899, was an electric car, not a gasoline one.","check":{"q":"Which invention, first offered on the 1912 Cadillac, ended the need to start gasoline cars with a hand crank?","a":"Charles Kettering's electric self-starter","wrong":["The spray-nozzle carburetor","The pneumatic tire","The steam flash boiler"]},"sources":[{"title":"La Jamais Contente","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Jamais_Contente"},{"title":"Camille Jenatzy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camille_Jenatzy"},{"title":"The History of the Electric Car","publisher":"US Department of Energy","url":"https://www.energy.gov/articles/history-electric-car"},{"title":"History of the electric vehicle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_electric_vehicle"},{"title":"Stanley Motor Carriage Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanley_Motor_Carriage_Company"},{"title":"Fred Marriott","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Marriott"},{"title":"Lohner–Porsche","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lohner%E2%80%93Porsche"},{"title":"Charles F. Kettering","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_F._Kettering"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/07-1.jpg","alt":"Camille Jenatzy in the flower-decorated, bullet-shaped electric car La Jamais Contente, the first car to reach 100 km/h (1899).","file":"Jamais contente parade.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamais_contente_parade.jpg","author":"Jules Beau","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/07-2.jpg","alt":"Fred Marriott in the Stanley steam racing car \"Rocket\" at Daytona Beach, around 1906.","file":"N041921.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N041921.jpg","author":"LeSesne, Richard H.","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/07-3.jpg","alt":"A Lohner-Porsche electric car in 1902, with the big motor hub in its front wheel, carrying Archduke Franz Ferdinand.","file":"Lohner-Porsche (1902).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lohner-Porsche_(1902).jpg","author":"Das interessante Blatt","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4014},{"n":8,"title":"Inside the Engine: Tiny Explosions, Big Power","when":"science (developed 1876 – today)","summary":"A car engine works by making thousands of tiny, controlled bursts of fire every minute. Each burst pushes a metal plug called a piston, and clever parts turn all that pushing into spinning. It is suck, squeeze, bang, blow, over and over again!","sections":[{"heading":"Lifting the Hood","paragraphs":["Pop open the hood of an ordinary gasoline car and you will see a tangle of hoses, wires, plastic covers and metal. Somewhere in the middle, usually under a cover, sits the engine block: a heavy lump of cast iron or aluminium about the size of a large suitcase. It looks calm and still. But when the engine is running, something amazing is happening inside it. Thousands of times every minute, a tiny cloud of fuel and air is squeezed, set on fire and allowed to burst outward. Each burst lasts only a few thousandths of a second, but together they can push a car weighing more than a tonne up a mountain road.","This kind of engine is called an internal combustion engine, because the fuel burns inside the engine itself, rather than in a separate fire under a boiler as in a steam engine. You met its inventors in Parts 3 to 6: Étienne Lenoir, Alphonse Beau de Rochas, Nicolaus Otto, Gottlieb Daimler, Wilhelm Maybach and Karl Benz. In this part we are going to stop telling the story of people for a while and look at the machine itself. How does burning a liquid make wheels turn?","Here is the big idea in one sentence. When fuel burns, it turns into very hot gases, and hot gases want to expand, so if you trap them in a closed tube with one end that can move, they will shove that end along with tremendous force. The tube is called the cylinder, and the movable end is called the piston. Everything else in the engine exists to feed the cylinder with fuel and air, light the fire at exactly the right moment, clear away the waste, stop everything from melting, and turn the piston's pushes into spinning motion.","Let us start with the heart of it all, the four-stroke cycle, the rhythm that Nicolaus Otto first made work in his engine of 1876 and which still beats inside most of the world's cars today."]},{"heading":"Suck, Squeeze, Bang, Blow","paragraphs":["Picture a single cylinder standing upright. It is a smooth, round tube, closed at the top except for two small doors called valves. One valve, the intake valve, lets fresh fuel and air in. The other, the exhaust valve, lets burnt gases out. At the top there is also a spark plug, poking into the cylinder like a tiny lightning rod. Inside the tube is the piston, a short, solid metal plug that fits so snugly it can slide up and down without letting gas leak past. Springy metal rings around the piston, called piston rings, help seal the gap.","Each trip of the piston up or down the cylinder is called a stroke. A four-stroke engine needs four strokes, two down and two up, to complete one full cycle. Mechanics have a famous way to remember them: suck, squeeze, bang, blow.","Stroke one is intake, or suck. The intake valve opens and the piston slides down the cylinder. As it moves down, it leaves an empty space behind it, which lowers the pressure, so air and fuel rush in to fill it, just as air rushes into a syringe when you pull back the plunger. At the bottom, the intake valve closes.","Stroke two is compression, or squeeze. Now both valves are shut tight, and the piston moves back up the cylinder, squeezing the fuel and air into a small space at the top. In a typical car engine the mixture is crushed to about a tenth of its starting volume. Squeezing it makes it hotter and packs the fuel molecules close together, so that when it burns it will release its energy quickly and push much harder.","Stroke three is power, or bang. Just before the piston reaches the top, the spark plug fires. A tiny electric spark jumps across a gap and lights the mixture. It is not really an explosion like a firework; it is a very fast, controlled burn, with a flame spreading outward from the spark in a few thousandths of a second. The burning gases heat up enormously, their pressure soars, and they slam the piston down the cylinder. This is the only one of the four strokes that actually produces power. The other three are the engine's preparation and tidying-up.","Stroke four is exhaust, or blow. Near the bottom of the power stroke, the exhaust valve opens. The piston moves back up and pushes the burnt gases out of the cylinder, into the exhaust pipe and out of the tailpipe. Then the exhaust valve closes, the intake valve opens, and the whole thing starts again.","Here is a surprising fact. In a four-stroke engine, each cylinder only fires once for every two full turns of the crankshaft, the spinning shaft at the bottom of the engine. If an engine is running at 3,000 revolutions per minute, a normal speed on a motorway, each cylinder fires 1,500 times a minute. That is 25 tiny bursts of fire every second in every single cylinder. A typical modern gasoline car engine turns its crankshaft about 700 times a minute while idling at traffic lights and can spin to 6,000 times a minute or more when you press hard on the pedal."]},{"heading":"From Push to Spin: The Crankshaft and Friends","paragraphs":["A piston going up and down is not much use to a car. Wheels need to spin. So the engine needs a way to turn straight-line pushing into round-and-round turning. The answer is an ancient and beautiful piece of engineering: the crank.","You already use a crank every time you ride a bicycle. Your legs push up and down, but the pedals are fixed to cranks that go round in a circle, so your up-and-down pushing becomes spinning. An engine does the same thing. Each piston is joined to a metal arm called a connecting rod. The bottom of the connecting rod is attached to a bent, offset part of the crankshaft, called a crankpin. As the piston is shoved down, the connecting rod pushes the crankpin round, and the whole crankshaft turns. As the crankshaft keeps turning, it pulls the piston back up again for the next stroke. In this way the crankshaft is both the engine's output and its timekeeper.","There is a problem, though. In a one-cylinder engine, only one stroke in four is a power stroke. During the other three, the engine is not pushing at all; in fact it has to do work to squeeze the mixture. So how does it keep turning? The answer is the flywheel, a heavy metal disc bolted to the end of the crankshaft. Once a flywheel is spinning, its weight keeps it spinning, like a merry-go-round that keeps going round after you stop pushing it. The flywheel stores energy from each power stroke and gives it back during the other three, smoothing out the jerky pushes. Karl Benz's first car had a big flywheel lying flat at the back, which you can see in photos of it.","Next, the valves. They have to open and close at exactly the right moments, thousands of times a minute, without ever getting out of step. This job belongs to the camshaft, a rod with egg-shaped bumps along it called cams. As the camshaft turns, each bump pushes a valve open, then lets a spring snap it shut again. The camshaft is linked to the crankshaft by a toothed belt, a chain or gears, and it turns at exactly half the crankshaft's speed. That makes sense when you think about it: each valve needs to open only once in every four strokes, which is every two turns of the crank.","If the timing belt breaks while the engine is running, the valves can stop moving while the pistons keep racing up and down, and in some engines the pistons then smash into the open valves. That is why mechanics replace timing belts on a schedule, long before they wear out."]},{"heading":"Feeding the Fire: Carburetors and Fuel Injection","paragraphs":["Fire needs fuel and oxygen, and an engine is very fussy about the mixture. For gasoline to burn completely and cleanly, it needs roughly 14.7 kilograms of air for every kilogram of fuel. Too much fuel and the engine wastes it, making black smoke and soot; too little and the engine runs hot, coughs and loses power. Engineers call a mixture with extra fuel rich and one with extra air lean.","The earliest gasoline engines used crude devices that simply blew air across the surface of a bowl of fuel, picking up vapour. They worked badly, especially on cold days. Wilhelm Maybach found a better way. In 1892 he designed a carburetor that sprayed fuel through a tiny nozzle, called a jet, into a stream of air; it was patented in 1893. His design used a trick of physics. The air passed through a narrow, waisted section of tube called a venturi. When air is forced through a narrow space it speeds up, and fast-moving air has lower pressure. That low pressure sucked fuel up out of the jet and broke it into a fine mist, like a perfume sprayer. A small float in the fuel bowl, like the float valve in a toilet cistern, kept the fuel at just the right level.","The driver controlled the engine's speed with a flap called the throttle. Pressing the accelerator pedal opened the throttle wider, letting more air through, which in turn pulled in more fuel. More fuel and air meant bigger bangs and more power. For about ninety years, almost every gasoline car in the world used some kind of carburetor built on Maybach's idea.","But carburetors were not very precise. They could not adjust perfectly to changes in temperature, altitude or how hard the engine was working. That wasted fuel and caused pollution. The answer was fuel injection, which squirts an exact dose of fuel through a nozzle called an injector, timed for each cylinder. The first cars known to use electronic fuel injection were some 1958 Chrysler-family models with a system called the Bendix Electrojector. In 1967 the German company Bosch introduced its electronic D-Jetronic system on the Volkswagen 1600. By the early 1990s, electronic fuel injection had replaced carburetors in most new gasoline cars in rich countries.","Today the fuel is controlled by a small computer called the engine control unit. It reads signals from sensors all over the engine: how much air is coming in, how fast the crankshaft is turning, how warm the engine is, how far the driver has pressed the pedal, and how much oxygen is left in the exhaust. Then it calculates exactly how much fuel to inject, and when, many times every second. Many modern engines even spray the fuel directly into the cylinder at very high pressure. A modern family car carries many such little computers, and together they are far more powerful than the guidance computer that flew aboard the Apollo spacecraft to the Moon in 1969."]},{"heading":"Lightning in a Bottle: The Spark Plug","paragraphs":["Lighting the fuel at the right instant sounds simple, but early engineers found it fiendishly hard. Otto's first engines used a real flame that was let into the cylinder through a sliding gate. Daimler and Maybach used a hot tube, a little tube of metal kept glowing red-hot by a burner on the outside of the engine, which lit the mixture when it was squeezed into it. Hot tubes worked, but they were dangerous, you could not easily change when they fired, and a gust of wind could blow out the burner. Karl Benz used electric ignition, with sparks from a battery and coil, but early electrical parts were unreliable.","The solution came from a workshop in Stuttgart, Germany, run by Robert Bosch. Bosch's company made a device called a magneto, a small generator that uses spinning magnets to make electricity, so it needs no battery. Early magnetos made only low-voltage sparks, produced by snapping apart two contacts inside the cylinder, which needed delicate moving parts in the hottest part of the engine. In 1901 Bosch gave a young engineer named Gottlob Honold a challenge: make a hot, long-lasting spark between electrodes that did not move. Honold, who had started at Bosch as an apprentice at the age of about fourteen or fifteen, rose to it. In 1902 he produced a high-voltage magneto ignition together with a new spark plug, described as the first commercially successful high-voltage spark plug. Car makers quickly adopted it, and it helped turn Bosch into one of the world's biggest car-parts companies.","A spark plug is a clever little object. It screws into the top of the cylinder. Down its middle runs a metal rod, the central electrode, surrounded by a white ceramic insulator so the electricity cannot escape sideways. At the bottom, a small bent metal tab, the ground electrode, sits just a millimetre or so away from the tip. When the ignition sends a surge of electricity, typically somewhere around 12,000 to 25,000 volts, down the central electrode, the electricity leaps across that tiny gap as a spark, a miniature bolt of lightning. Mechanics set the gap carefully; in car engines it is usually somewhere between about 0.6 and 1.8 millimetres.","Timing matters enormously. The spark has to happen a fraction of a second before the piston reaches the top, because the flame needs a moment to spread. At high engine speeds, the spark has to come earlier. Old cars did this with spinning weights and springs; modern cars do it with the engine computer.","Sometimes things go wrong. If the fuel and air are squeezed too hard or get too hot, part of the mixture can catch fire by itself before the flame from the spark plug reaches it. Two flame fronts then crash into each other and the pressure inside the cylinder jumps sharply, making a metallic rattling or pinging sound. Mechanics call this knocking. Gentle knocking wastes power; severe knocking can crack pistons and wreck an engine.","That is where the octane rating on a fuel pump comes from. It measures how well a fuel resists knocking. Chemists compare each fuel against two reference liquids: iso-octane, which resists knocking very well and is given a score of 100, and normal heptane, which knocks very easily and scores 0. A fuel rated 95 behaves like a mixture of 95 percent iso-octane and 5 percent heptane. Higher-octane fuel lets engineers build engines with higher compression, which squeeze harder and get more power from each drop. Many modern engines have knock sensors, tiny microphones bolted to the engine, that listen for knocking and tell the computer to delay the spark. For much of the twentieth century, one way to raise octane was to add a lead chemical to gasoline, which turned out to be a serious health problem, a story told in Part 17."]},{"heading":"Keeping Cool and Slippery","paragraphs":["Each bang inside a cylinder is fiercely hot. For an instant, the burning gases can be well over 2,000 degrees Celsius, hotter than the melting point of the aluminium many engines are made of. If nothing carried that heat away, the engine would seize up and destroy itself within minutes. So every engine needs a cooling system.","Most car engines are cooled by liquid. The engine block is full of hollow passages, called water jackets, surrounding the cylinders. A water pump, driven by the engine, pushes coolant through these passages. Coolant is mostly water mixed with antifreeze, a chemical that stops it freezing in winter and boiling in summer, and protects the metal from rust. The coolant soaks up heat from the hot metal and flows to the radiator at the front of the car. A radiator is a flat honeycomb of thin metal tubes and fins with a huge surface area. Air rushing through it as the car moves, helped by a fan when the car is standing still, carries the heat away. The cooled liquid then flows back to the engine to do the job again. A valve called a thermostat keeps coolant away from the radiator until the engine has warmed up, so the engine reaches its best working temperature, usually around 90 degrees Celsius, quickly. Remember the honeycomb radiator of the 1901 Mercedes from Part 6? It was one of the first good versions of this idea.","Some engines, especially in motorcycles, lawnmowers and older cars like the original Volkswagen Beetle, are cooled by air instead, with metal fins around the cylinders that shed heat like a radiator.","The other great enemy of an engine is friction. Inside, metal parts slide and spin against one another thousands of times a minute. Without help, they would rub, overheat and weld themselves together. The answer is oil. An oil pump pushes oil through tiny channels to every bearing and moving surface, where it forms a film thinner than a hair. The moving parts actually float on this film and never quite touch. The oil also carries away heat and washes away tiny bits of dirt. That is why drivers check the oil and change it regularly. Run an engine without oil and it can be ruined in a few minutes.","All this cooling shows something important about how engines work: they are surprisingly wasteful. A typical gasoline engine turns only about 20 to 35 percent of the energy in its fuel into useful motion, and that is at its best. Most of the rest escapes as heat, through the radiator and out of the exhaust pipe. According to the US government's fuel economy experts, once you add up all the losses from heat, friction, pumping air, idling and driving pumps and the gearbox, only about 12 to 30 percent of the energy in the fuel tank actually ends up turning the wheels. That waste of energy is one reason engineers have kept working on better engines, and one reason electric cars, as Part 18 will show, have made such a comeback."]},{"heading":"Rudolf Diesel's Engine Without a Spark","paragraphs":["One inventor was so bothered by wasted energy that he set out to design a completely different kind of engine. Rudolf Diesel was born in Paris in 1858 to German parents. As a student at the Technical University in Munich, he attended lectures by the refrigeration pioneer Carl von Linde about the science of heat, and learned how poorly the steam engines of the day used their fuel. He dreamed of an engine that would waste far less.","Diesel's idea was daring. You may have noticed that a bicycle pump gets warm when you pump hard. Squeezing air heats it up. Diesel realised that if you squeezed air hard enough, it would become hot enough to set fuel on fire all by itself, without any spark. So his engine sucks in only air, not fuel. On the compression stroke it crushes that air to something like a fifteenth to a twenty-third of its original volume, much harder than a gasoline engine does, until it is hot enough to ignite fuel, typically several hundred degrees Celsius. At the top of the stroke, an injector sprays fuel into the scorching air, and it bursts into flame instantly. Engineers call this compression ignition. There is no spark plug at all.","Diesel was granted a German patent in 1892 and published a book about his theory in 1893. Turning the idea into a working machine was much harder. He spent years at Maschinenfabrik Augsburg (the company later became part of MAN), building and testing experimental engines that kept failing. Finally, in 1897, an engine that worked properly was officially tested. It had a single tall cylinder, produced around 20 horsepower, and turned about 26 percent of its fuel's energy into work, very high for the time, when steam engines often managed only around 10 percent or less. Diesel became famous and rich.","Diesel engines are heavier than gasoline engines, because they have to be strong enough to cope with such high pressures. But they are more efficient and very good at producing strong turning force at low speeds, ideal for pulling heavy loads. Diesel engines began driving river boats and submarines in the early 1900s and ocean-going ships from 1912, and the first diesel locomotive ran on a Swiss railway in 1912. The first mass-produced diesel passenger car, the Mercedes-Benz 260 D, appeared in 1936. Today diesel engines power most of the world's big lorries, buses, tractors, ships and many trains. Huge ship engines, as tall as a house, can turn more than half their fuel's energy into motion, making them among the most efficient engines ever built.","Rudolf Diesel's own story ended in mystery. In September 1913 he boarded a steamship crossing from Antwerp in Belgium to England. During the night he vanished, and his body was later found in the sea. Nobody knows for certain what happened to him."]},{"heading":"How Many Horses? Power and Cylinders","paragraphs":["Car adverts love to boast about horsepower. Where does that odd word come from? In the 1780s the Scottish engineer James Watt was trying to sell his improved steam engines to mine owners and brewers who used horses to turn their machinery. To explain how strong his engines were, he needed a unit everyone understood. Watt measured horses working at mills and decided that one horse could do about 33,000 foot-pounds of work per minute, meaning it could lift 33,000 pounds by one foot, or 330 pounds by 100 feet, in one minute. That became one horsepower. A popular legend says Watt worked it out after a brewer challenged him with his strongest horse, but historians treat that story as just a story.","In modern units, one horsepower is about 746 watts, enough to light several old-fashioned light bulbs. In Europe, engines are often measured in metric horsepower, called PS in Germany, which is slightly smaller, about 735.5 watts. Karl Benz's first car of 1886 produced less than one horsepower. A typical family car today produces somewhere around 100 to 200 horsepower, and some sports cars produce more than 600. Power is how fast an engine can do work, while torque is how hard it can twist the crankshaft. A diesel tractor has lots of torque for pulling, while a racing engine has high power at very high speeds.","Engines get more power in three main ways: making each cylinder bigger, making the engine spin faster, or adding more cylinders. Adding cylinders also has a big extra benefit: smoothness. A one-cylinder engine delivers one hard kick every two turns, which makes it shake and vibrate. With four cylinders, the power strokes are spread out so that one of them is always pushing. The pistons are arranged so that as some go up, others come down, balancing each other out. The order in which the cylinders fire, called the firing order, is carefully planned for the smoothest running.","Car engines come in many shapes. The most common is the inline four, with four cylinders standing in a straight row. Bigger engines often arrange their cylinders in two slanted rows in a V shape, giving V6 and V8 engines, which are shorter and fit neatly under the hood. Some engines, like those in the original Volkswagen Beetle and in Porsches and Subarus, lay the cylinders flat on opposite sides, pushing outward like boxers punching, which is why they are called boxer engines. Luxury and racing cars have used V12 engines and even more cylinders.","So now you know what is happening under the hood: air and fuel rushing in, pistons squeezing, sparks flashing, flames pushing, valves snapping open and shut, oil and coolant flowing, and a crankshaft spinning furiously thousands of times a minute. But a spinning shaft is not yet a moving car. How does all that whirling power reach the wheels? How do you make the car go slowly up a hill or fast on a straight road, steer it around a corner, and, most importantly of all, stop it? Those are the puzzles of the next part."]}],"deepDive":"Why are engines so wasteful? The answer lies in thermodynamics, the science of heat and work. An engine is a heat engine: it takes heat from a hot source (the burning gas) and turns part of it into work, dumping the rest into a cooler place (the air outside). In 1824 the French engineer Sadi Carnot showed that no heat engine can turn all its heat into work, and that the best possible efficiency depends on the difference between the hottest and coldest temperatures. For the idealised Otto cycle that models a gasoline engine, theory says efficiency rises with the compression ratio: squeezing the mixture harder means the gas expands further on the power stroke and gives up more of its energy. That is why engineers keep pushing compression ratios up, from roughly 4:1 to 6:1 in many early engines to around 10:1 to 14:1 in modern gasoline engines. The limit is knocking, which is why fuel octane and engine design are tied together. Diesel engines avoid that limit because they compress only air, so ratios of 15:1 to 23:1 are normal, and they also run without a throttle plate, avoiding the pumping losses a gasoline engine suffers when part-open. Real engines fall far short of theory: heat leaks into the cylinder walls, friction steals energy, and gas must be pumped in and out. Best-case figures are roughly 35 to 40 percent for modern gasoline engines, about 40 to 45 percent for truck diesels, and around 50 to 55 percent for giant two-stroke ship diesels, while Formula One power units have been reported to exceed 50 percent. In everyday driving the average is much lower, because engines spend much of their time at light load or idling. Historians also debate credit for the engine's key ideas. Beau de Rochas described the four-stroke cycle in 1862 but never built one; Otto built the first practical version; and Diesel's 1892 patent described a theory that he himself had to revise once he discovered it would not work as first written. Like the car itself, the engine was improved by many hands over many decades.","funFact":"In a car engine running at 3,000 revolutions per minute, each cylinder fires about 25 times every second.","check":{"q":"In a diesel engine, what sets the fuel on fire?","a":"Air squeezed so hard that it becomes hot enough to ignite the fuel","wrong":["A spark plug","A glowing hot tube heated by an outside burner","A small flame let in through a sliding gate"]},"sources":[{"title":"Four-stroke engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-stroke_engine"},{"title":"Carburetor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carburetor"},{"title":"Fuel injection","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuel_injection"},{"title":"Spark plug","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spark_plug"},{"title":"Gottlob Honold","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gottlob_Honold"},{"title":"Octane rating","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Octane_rating"},{"title":"Where the Energy Goes: Gasoline Vehicles","publisher":"US Department of Energy, fueleconomy.gov","url":"https://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/atv.shtml"},{"title":"Rudolf Diesel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rudolf_Diesel"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/08-1.jpg","alt":"A glass lantern slide from about 1910 showing the four strokes of the Otto cycle: suction, compression, power and exhaust.","file":"Lantern Slide - Tangyes Ltd, Otto Four-Stroke Cycle Diagram , circa 1910.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lantern_Slide_-_Tangyes_Ltd,_Otto_Four-Stroke_Cycle_Diagram_,_circa_1910.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/08-2.jpg","alt":"Rudolf Diesel's 1897 prototype engine, built in Augsburg, on display at the Deutsches Museum in Munich.","file":"Stationärer Dieselmotor, Prototyp 1897, Deutsches Museum-L1001401.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Station%C3%A4rer_Dieselmotor,_Prototyp_1897,_Deutsches_Museum-L1001401.jpg","author":"Frank Schulenburg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/08-3.jpg","alt":"A 1935 cutaway drawing of a spark plug, showing the central electrode, insulator and the spark gap at the bottom.","file":"KLG sparkplug, sectioned (Autocar Handbook, 13th ed, 1935).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KLG_sparkplug,_sectioned_(Autocar_Handbook,_13th_ed,_1935).jpg","author":"Andy Dingley (scanner)","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4316},{"n":9,"title":"From Engine to Wheels: Gears, Tires, Steering and Brakes","when":"science (1818 – today)","summary":"An engine can spin, but how does that spin reach the wheels? Meet the clever parts that make a car go, turn and stop: gears, a box of wheels that let tires 'disagree', air-filled tires first made for a little boy's tricycle, and brakes that use liquid to multiply the push of your foot.","sections":[{"heading":"A Spinning Shaft and the Clutch","paragraphs":["Imagine you have just built a perfect engine. It sits on a workbench, roaring away, its crankshaft spinning a couple of thousand times every minute. You feel proud. Then you push it towards the door and realise something awkward: it does not go anywhere. All that power is trapped in one whirling steel shaft. To make a car, you need a whole chain of clever parts that carry the power from that shaft to four rubber wheels on a bumpy road, and then let a human being point those wheels in the right direction and stop them safely.","Engineers call this chain the drivetrain. It begins at the engine and runs through the clutch, the gearbox, a long spinning driveshaft (in many cars), the differential and finally the axles that turn the wheels. Around it sit the other systems that make a car drivable: steering, tires, brakes and suspension. None of these was invented in a single flash. Some were borrowed from horse carriages, some from bicycles, some from clocks and some from aircraft workshops. A few of the key ideas are much older than the car itself, going back to the 1810s and 1820s, long before anyone had an engine small enough to fit under a seat.","Let us start with the first problem. An internal combustion engine cannot start itself from a standstill while it is connected to heavy wheels, and it stalls if it is forced to turn too slowly. A steam engine can push hard even when it is barely moving, but a gasoline engine needs to keep spinning to make power. So the very first thing a car needs is a way to let the engine run freely while the wheels stay still, and then to join the two together smoothly.","That joining job belongs to the clutch, a part that connects or disconnects the engine from the wheels. In the most common kind, the dry friction clutch, a round friction disc faces the heavy flywheel at the end of the crankshaft. Strong springs squeeze the disc against the spinning flywheel, the surfaces grip, and power flows on to the gearbox. When the driver presses the clutch pedal, levers pull the springs back, the disc slides away and the engine can keep spinning while the wheels stand still.","The clever bit comes when you let the pedal up slowly. For a second or two the disc touches the flywheel but slips against it, a bit like rubbing your hands together. That slipping lets the car's speed catch up gently with the engine's speed, so the car rolls forward instead of leaping or stalling. Every learner driver knows this moment, called finding the 'biting point'. Slip too long and the friction material wears away and gets hot. Let go too quickly and the car jerks and the engine dies.","Early cars tried many styles of clutch. A popular one was the cone clutch, often faced with leather, which wedged into a matching hollow cone in the flywheel; it gripped strongly but could grab suddenly. Later designs used flat discs, and racing cars and motorcycles often stack several thin plates together."]},{"heading":"The Gearbox: Strong and Slow or Fast and Light","paragraphs":["Once the engine is connected, a second problem appears. A gasoline engine works best within a certain range of speeds. Too slow and it chugs and stalls; too fast and it screams and wears out. But the wheels of a car need to turn very slowly when you are crawling away from a standstill or climbing a steep hill, and very quickly when you are cruising along a motorway. The gearbox, also called the transmission, solves this by letting the engine turn the wheels slowly with lots of force, or fast with less force.","You already know how this works if you ride a bicycle with gears. When you pedal up a steep hill in a low gear, your legs spin round many times while the back wheel turns only a little, but every push feels easy. In a high gear on a flat road, one turn of the pedals sends the wheel spinning a long way, but each push is harder. A car gearbox does exactly the same. In first gear, a small gear on the engine's side drives a much bigger gear, so the output turns perhaps three or four times more slowly than the engine but with three or four times more twisting force, which engineers call torque. In top gear, the gears might be equal in size, a ratio of 1 to 1, called direct drive, or the output may even spin faster than the engine, called overdrive.","The early Benz cars had hardly any gearing choice at all, which is why, in 1888, Bertha Benz and her sons had to push their car up the hills on the road to Pforzheim, and why Bertha suggested to Karl afterwards that the car needed a lower gear for climbing.","The French firm Panhard et Levassor fitted a three-speed gearbox to its cars in 1891, part of the layout described in Part 6 that most cars copied for decades. In these early gearboxes the driver actually slid toothed gears along a shaft until they meshed with other spinning gears. If the two gears were turning at different speeds, their teeth smashed together with a horrible grinding noise. Drivers nicknamed them 'crash boxes', and changing gear smoothly took real skill: you had to listen to the engine, judge its speed and sometimes press the clutch twice for a single change, a trick called double-declutching.","The cure was synchromesh. In a synchromesh gearbox, small cone-shaped friction rings gently rub against the gear you are about to choose and bring it to the same speed as its partner before the teeth lock together, so they slide in quietly. The American engineer Earl Avery Thompson invented the idea in 1919, and Cadillac put it into production cars at the end of the 1920s (sources give 1928 or the 1929 model year). By the middle of the 1930s most American carmakers had synchronised at least the higher gears, and in the 1950s Porsche made gearboxes with synchromesh on every forward gear.","Gearboxes also gained more gears. For most of the twentieth century three or four forward gears were normal; five-speed boxes spread in the 1980s, and today many cars have six, eight or even ten, letting the engine stay near its most efficient speed and save fuel."]},{"heading":"The Differential: Why Wheels Need to Disagree","paragraphs":["Here is a puzzle. Draw two circles on the playground, one inside the other, like a running track with two lanes. Ask two friends to walk around them, side by side, keeping exactly level the whole way. The friend on the outside lane has to take bigger or faster steps, because the outer circle is longer. A car going round a bend has the same problem. Its outer wheels travel a longer path than its inner wheels in exactly the same time, so they must spin faster.","Let us put in some numbers. Suppose a car's left and right wheels are 1.5 metres apart and the car drives once around a circle where the inner wheel follows a path 10 metres from the centre. The inner wheel travels about 63 metres. The outer wheel, 11.5 metres from the centre, travels about 72 metres, roughly 15 percent further. If both driven wheels were fixed to one solid axle, one of them would have to skid or scrub along the ground the whole way round. The tires would wear out quickly, the car would fight the driver on every corner and on slippery ground it could slide.","The answer is a cluster of gears called the differential. It sits in the middle of the driven axle, in that rounded lump you can see under the back of many older rear-wheel-drive cars and trucks. Power from the engine turns a large ring gear, which carries a small cage holding little gears called planet gears or spider gears. These mesh with gears on the ends of the left and right axles. When the car goes straight, both wheels push back equally, the little gears do not spin on their own and both axles turn together. On a bend, the inner wheel meets a little more resistance; now the small gears start to roll, letting the inner wheel slow down while the outer wheel speeds up by exactly the same amount. The average speed of the two wheels always matches the speed of the ring gear.","The idea of gears that add and subtract motions is older than cars: a clock made by Joseph Williamson around 1720 used one, and so did cotton-spinning machines in the 1820s.","For vehicles, the key name is Onésiphore Pecqueur, a French watchmaker and engineer who worked at the Conservatoire des Arts et Métiers in Paris, the same institution whose museum now keeps Cugnot's steam wagon. In 1827 Pecqueur patented a steam vehicle design that included a differential on the driven axle, letting the two wheels turn at different speeds. His experience with clock gears that turn at different rates helped him see the solution. Later, in 1876, the English inventor James Starley patented a differential for a pedal-powered quadricycle, and when Karl Benz built his Patent-Motorwagen, he included a differential too.","The simple 'open' differential has one weakness you may have seen on a snowy day. Because it sends the same twisting force to both wheels, if one wheel sits on ice and can barely push, the other wheel gets almost nothing either. The slippery wheel spins madly while the car stays stuck. Engineers invented limited-slip differentials, locking differentials and, more recently, electronic traction control that gently brakes the spinning wheel, all to deal with this problem."]},{"heading":"Steering: A Carriage-Maker's Clever Trapezium","paragraphs":["Turning corners causes trouble at the front of a car, too. For thousands of years, carts and carriages were steered by pivoting the whole front axle on one central pin. This turntable steering works, but when the axle swings round, the front wheels move in under the body, so the carriage becomes narrower and wobblier exactly when it is turning, and a sharp turn could tip it over.","In 1816 a German carriage builder in Munich, Georg Lankensperger, came up with a better system. Instead of one big pivot, each front wheel got its own small pivot right next to the wheel, so the axle could stay fixed while the wheels turned. Each wheel's pivot had a short steering arm, and a rod called a tie rod linked the two arms together.","The genius was in the angles. If you look at the axle, the two steering arms and the tie rod from above, they form a trapezium, a four-sided shape with the tie rod a little shorter than the axle. Because of this shape, when the driver turns, the inner wheel automatically turns more sharply than the outer wheel. That matters because, as we saw with the differential, the inner wheel follows a smaller circle. With the angles set just right, lines drawn through the centres of all four wheels meet at a single point, the centre of the turn, and every tire rolls cleanly around its own circle without being dragged sideways.","Lankensperger's agent in England was Rudolph Ackermann, a German-born businessman who ran a famous print and book shop in London. In 1818 Ackermann took out a British patent for the design, which is why the idea is still called Ackermann steering geometry today, even though the inventor was Lankensperger. To be fair to everyone, there is an even earlier claim: the English doctor and inventor Erasmus Darwin, grandfather of Charles Darwin, seems to have sketched a similar idea around 1758, reportedly after being hurt when his own carriage overturned.","Ackermann geometry was designed for horse carriages, but it was waiting for the car. Early motor vehicles were often steered with a tiller, like a small boat, before the steering wheel took over. Modern cars do not use perfect Ackermann angles, because tires flex and slip slightly at speed, but every car still steers its front wheels on separate pivots linked by tie rods, just as Lankensperger proposed more than 200 years ago."]},{"heading":"Riding on Air: The Story of the Pneumatic Tire","paragraphs":["The first cars bounced along on wheels much like those of a carriage: wooden spokes and rims, sometimes wrapped with a band of iron or solid rubber. On the rutted, stony roads of the 1880s and 1890s, that meant a jolting, rattling ride that shook passengers and broke parts. The solution was a tire filled with air, called a pneumatic tire, which works like a cushion. When the wheel hits a small stone, the soft air-filled tube squashes around it instead of the whole car being thrown upward.","The first person to patent such a tire was Robert William Thomson, a Scottish engineer from Stonehaven. In 1845, aged about 23, he patented his 'Aerial Wheels' in Britain, followed by France and the United States. They used a hollow rubber tube filled with air, protected by a leather cover bolted to the wheel. In March 1847 he demonstrated them on horse-drawn carriages in Regent's Park, London, and one set reportedly ran about 1,200 miles without showing wear. Yet the idea did not catch on. Rubber was expensive, there were no bicycles or cars yet that badly needed such tires, and Thomson went on to other inventions, including a fountain pen.","About forty years later the idea was reinvented by someone who had probably never heard of Thomson. John Boyd Dunlop was a Scottish vet working in Belfast. In late 1887 his young son complained that his tricycle gave him a hard, bumpy ride on the city's cobbles. Dunlop made a tube of thin rubber sheet, pumped it full of air, wrapped it in canvas and fixed it around a wheel. The ride was dramatically smoother and faster. He was granted a patent in December 1888, and in 1889 a cyclist named Willie Hume won races in Ireland and England on Dunlop's tires, beating riders on solid tires and convincing the cycling world.","There was a twist. About two years after his patent, Dunlop learned that it was invalid, because Thomson's earlier patent had already described the same basic idea. Even so, Dunlop deserves real credit: he showed that rubber could survive the wear of a tire while keeping its springiness, and with the businessman Harvey du Cros he helped start the company that made pneumatic tires a worldwide industry. His name is still on tires today.","Cars were heavier and faster than bicycles. In France, the brothers André and Édouard Michelin took over a rubber company in Clermont-Ferrand in 1889. They designed a removable bicycle tire that could be changed far more quickly than the glued-on kind, patented it in 1891, and the cyclist Charles Terront rode on it to win the first Paris–Brest–Paris race that year.","In June 1895 the Michelins went further. They entered a car named L'Éclair ('Lightning') in the Paris–Bordeaux–Paris race, a gruelling round trip of about 1,178 kilometres, and fitted it with air-filled tires, something almost nobody had tried on a car in a major race. The tires punctured again and again, and the brothers spent hours by the roadside making repairs. L'Éclair did complete the course, though too slowly to count within the official time limit. That might sound like a failure, but it proved that a car could travel on air, and within a few years pneumatic tires became standard on cars.","Tires kept improving. In 1946 Michelin patented the radial tire, whose inner cords run straight across from side to side; radials grip better, run cooler and last longer, and by the 1980s they were on almost every car in North America."]},{"heading":"Brakes: Turning Speed into Heat","paragraphs":["Going is only half of driving. Stopping is the other half, and for the first cars it was the scary half. A moving car carries a lot of energy, called kinetic energy, and to stop it that energy has to go somewhere. Brakes work by friction: they rub something against a spinning part of the wheel and turn the energy of motion into heat. Here is a sense of how much. A family car weighing 1,500 kilograms travelling at 100 kilometres per hour carries roughly 580,000 joules of energy. Stopping it turns all of that into heat in a few seconds, enough heat, in theory, to bring about one and a half litres of cold water to the boil.","The earliest cars borrowed their brakes from horse carriages. A lever pressed a wooden block or a metal 'spoon' against the tire or the rim, or against a drum on the axle. That was fine at 15 kilometres per hour, but the wood wore away quickly. During Bertha Benz's famous drive in 1888, the family story says she asked a shoemaker to nail leather onto the brake blocks, an early brake lining. As cars got faster and heavier, they needed something far stronger.","The drum brake was the next big step. A round metal drum, shaped like a short, wide can, is fixed to the wheel and spins with it. Inside sit two curved 'shoes' lined with friction material. When the driver presses the pedal, the shoes are pushed outward against the inside of the drum, and the rubbing slows the wheel. When the pedal is released, springs pull the shoes back. A car built by Wilhelm Maybach's team is reported to have used an early drum brake in 1900, and the French carmaker Louis Renault patented the principle in 1902. Renault lined his brake shoes with woven asbestos, a mineral fibre that could survive great heat. Much later, people discovered that breathing asbestos dust causes serious lung diseases, so modern brakes use other materials.","Early drum brakes were worked by rods, levers and cables, which stretched and bent, so it was hard to make every wheel brake equally. If one wheel gripped harder than the others, the car could swerve."]},{"heading":"Liquid Muscle: Hydraulic and Disc Brakes","paragraphs":["The breakthrough came from a simple fact about liquids: you cannot squeeze them into a smaller space. Fill a sealed tube with oil and push on one end, and the push travels instantly to the other end, and to every other part of the liquid, with the same pressure. This principle was described by the French scientist Blaise Pascal in the 1600s, and it is the secret of hydraulic brakes.","In a hydraulic brake, the brake pedal pushes a small piston in a part called the master cylinder, which is full of brake fluid. Thin metal pipes carry that fluid to a cylinder at each wheel, where larger pistons push the brake shoes or pads. Because the pressure is the same everywhere, a bigger piston feels a bigger force. If a wheel piston has four times the area of the master piston, a push of 10 newtons at the pedal end becomes a push of about 40 newtons at the wheel. A driver's foot can produce a huge squeezing force at all four wheels at once, and the pressure is shared evenly, so the car stops straight.","Several inventors experimented with hydraulic brakes in the early 1900s. The design that caught on came from an American, Malcolm Loughead, who patented his hydraulic brakes in 1917. The racing-car builder Fred Duesenberg tried Loughead's brakes, and in 1921 the Duesenberg Model A became one of the first production cars sold with hydraulic brakes on all four wheels. Other carmakers followed through the 1920s and 1930s. Malcolm and his brother Allan later changed the spelling of their surname to match its sound, and that is why one of the world's most famous aircraft companies is called Lockheed.","Drum brakes have one serious weakness. Because the friction happens inside a closed drum, the heat has trouble escaping. On a long downhill road or in a race, the drums get so hot that the brakes grow weaker, a frightening effect called brake fade. The answer was the disc brake. Here a flat metal disc spins with the wheel, out in the open air, and a clamp called a caliper squeezes friction pads against both sides of it, the way you might pinch a spinning coin between finger and thumb.","The English engineer Frederick Lanchester patented a disc brake for cars in 1902, but it used copper pads that wore out quickly on the rough roads of the time. The real proof came in motor racing: in 1953 Jaguar won the 24 Hours of Le Mans with disc brakes, a story told in Part 15. Two years later the Citroën DS became the first car built in large numbers with modern disc brakes. Today almost every car has discs at the front, where most of the stopping happens, and many have them at the back too."]},{"heading":"Smoothing the Bumps and Doing Away with the Clutch Pedal","paragraphs":["There is one more problem every car has to solve: the road is never flat. Potholes, stones, cobbles and ruts would shake passengers and parts to pieces if the wheels were bolted directly to the body. So between the wheels and the body sits the suspension, made of springs and shock absorbers.","The first car springs came straight from carriages. Leaf springs are stacks of long, curved steel strips clamped together; when a wheel hits a bump, the stack bends and soaks up the blow. Coil springs, like giant pen springs, are found on most cars today.","Springs have a problem of their own: once squashed, they bounce back, and then keep bouncing. A car on springs alone would bob up and down like a boat after every bump. That is the job of the shock absorber, more correctly called a damper. A modern damper is a tube of oil with a piston inside. As the wheel moves, the piston is forced through the oil, which can only squeeze through tiny holes and valves. This slows the movement and turns the energy of the bouncing into a little heat, so the car settles after one or two gentle movements.","Early dampers used friction plates and tended to stick. The French engineer Maurice Houdaille patented a lever-arm hydraulic damper in 1908 and 1909, and dampers of this type came into wide use after World War I; Ford fitted them as standard on its new Model A at the end of the 1920s.","The last great change in this part made driving easier for millions of people. Changing gear by hand, with a clutch pedal and a crash box, was tiring, especially in city traffic. Engineers dreamed of a gearbox that would choose gears by itself. In May 1939 General Motors began making the Hydra-Matic, the first fully automatic transmission produced in large numbers for passenger cars, and offered it on its 1940 Oldsmobiles. It had four forward gears, chosen automatically by oil pressure and the speed of the car, and instead of a clutch pedal it used a fluid coupling: two fan-like wheels facing each other in a bath of oil, so that one spinning wheel stirs the oil and the oil turns the other. At first it cost an extra 57 dollars. Almost 200,000 had been sold by early 1942, when American car factories switched to war production, and Hydra-Matic gearboxes were then fitted to military vehicles such as the M5 Stuart and M24 Chaffee light tanks.","Step back and look at what we have assembled: a clutch to make a gentle start, a gearbox to match engine and road, a differential so the wheels can disagree on corners, steering that lets each front wheel follow its own circle, air-filled tires, powerful brakes and springy suspension. By the early 1900s, the best European cars had many of these parts, and they were wonderful machines. But there was a catch. Each was built largely by hand, by skilled craftsmen filing and fitting each piece, and that made them very expensive, toys for the rich. Meanwhile, on the far side of the Atlantic Ocean, in a country of huge distances, muddy roads and restless inventors, a new kind of car fever was about to break out."]}],"deepDive":"Much of a car's drivetrain is applied physics. A gearbox trades speed for torque: if an engine making 150 newton-metres turns a gear pair with a ratio of 3.5 to 1, the output shaft turns 3.5 times more slowly but, ignoring small friction losses, delivers about 525 newton-metres. The final drive in the differential multiplies torque again, often by another 3 to 4 times, which is why a modest engine can heave a tonne and a half of car up a steep hill in first gear. Power, which is torque multiplied by turning speed, stays roughly constant through the gears; the gearbox only changes how it is packaged. The differential is a mechanical averaging machine: the ring gear's speed always equals the average of the two wheel speeds, so if one wheel stops entirely the other spins at twice the normal rate. That same property explains its weakness: because an open differential delivers equal torque to both sides, the wheel with the least grip limits the push of both. Hydraulic brakes use Pascal's principle. Pressure equals force divided by area, and pressure in a sealed fluid is the same everywhere, so a wheel piston with several times the area of the master cylinder piston produces several times the force, at the cost of moving a shorter distance. Modern cars add a vacuum or electric booster so the pedal feels light. Historians also argue about credit. Ackermann steering is named after the man who held the 1818 British patent, not the carriage builder Georg Lankensperger who designed it, and Erasmus Darwin may have had the idea around 1758. Dunlop's 1888 tire patent was ruled invalid because Robert William Thomson had patented a pneumatic tire in 1845, yet Dunlop's practical tire and business partners created the industry. Drum brakes are credited both to a Maybach-built car of 1900 and to Louis Renault's 1902 patent. These cases show a common pattern in invention: the first person to describe an idea, the first to patent it and the first to make it succeed are often different people.","funFact":"The Lockheed aircraft company got its name from brothers Malcolm and Allan Loughead, and Malcolm patented hydraulic car brakes in 1917, before the brothers changed the spelling of their name.","check":{"q":"Why does a car need a differential?","a":"Because on a bend the outside wheel travels farther than the inside wheel, so the two wheels must turn at different speeds.","wrong":["To change the engine from forward to reverse","To cool the brakes on long hills","To pump air into the tires while driving"]},"sources":[{"title":"Differential (mechanical device)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_(mechanical_device)"},{"title":"Ackermann steering geometry","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ackermann_steering_geometry"},{"title":"Tire","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tire"},{"title":"John Boyd Dunlop","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Boyd_Dunlop"},{"title":"Robert William Thomson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_William_Thomson"},{"title":"Hydraulic brake","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydraulic_brake"},{"title":"Disc brake","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disc_brake"},{"title":"Hydramatic","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydramatic"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/09-1.jpg","alt":"Diagram of a car differential: the big ring gear (1), small pinion gears (2), drive shaft and pinion (3, 4), and axles.","file":"Differential gear (PSF).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Differential_gear_(PSF).png","author":"Pearson Scott Foresman","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/09-2.jpg","alt":"Ackermann steering: steering arms and a tie rod turn the front wheels so all wheels circle the same centre point.","file":"Ackermann New.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ackermann_New.jpg","author":"User GRAHAMUK on en.wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/09-3.jpg","alt":"John Boyd Dunlop, the Scottish vet who made the first practical air-filled (pneumatic) tyre in 1888.","file":"John Boyd Dunlop.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Boyd_Dunlop.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4338},{"n":10,"title":"America Catches Car Fever","when":"1893 – 1908","summary":"In the 1890s, American tinkerers began building cars in sheds and bicycle shops. Two brothers won a race through the snow, a young engineer named Henry Ford built a car with no reverse gear, and a doctor, a mechanic and a bulldog wearing goggles drove all the way across the United States.","sections":[{"heading":"A Huge Country with Muddy Roads","paragraphs":["In the early 1890s, the newest motor cars were rattling along the roads of Germany and France. In the United States, things looked very different. America was enormous, about 4,500 kilometres from coast to coast, and most of it was linked by railways, not good roads. Outside the cities, roads were mostly dirt tracks that turned into deep mud after rain and clouds of dust in summer. Farmers lived miles from the nearest town and travelled by horse and wagon. Most Americans had never seen a car.","Yet the country had some big advantages. It had plenty of iron, wood, oil and coal. It had busy machine shops, bicycle factories and a huge, growing population eager for new gadgets. Bicycles had become a craze in the 1890s, and bicycle makers already knew how to build light frames, chains, wire wheels and air-filled tires. Many of America's first car builders started out in bicycle shops.","In 1900 there were only about 8,000 cars registered in the whole United States, a tiny number for a country of about 76 million people. But in the space of roughly fifteen years, a group of tinkerers, mechanics and businessmen would turn the car from a strange toy into the start of a giant industry. This part tells how America caught car fever."]},{"heading":"The Duryea Brothers of Springfield","paragraphs":["Our story begins in Springfield, Massachusetts, with two brothers, Charles Duryea and his younger brother Frank. Charles, born in 1861, had worked in the bicycle business. Frank, born in 1869, was a skilled toolmaker and mechanic. In the early 1890s they set out to build a gasoline-powered carriage, fitting a small engine to a horse buggy in a Springfield workshop.","On 21 September 1893, the Duryea motor wagon made its first test run on Taylor Street in Springfield. It was not pretty, and it was not fast, but it moved under its own power. Many historians call it one of the first successful gasoline cars built in the United States. Other Americans had experimented earlier, and some may have run petrol vehicles a little before the Duryeas, so it is fairest to say the brothers were among the first rather than definitely the very first.","What really set the Duryeas apart is what came next. In 1895 they founded the Duryea Motor Wagon Company in Springfield, often described as the first American company formed to build and sell gasoline cars. By the end of 1896 it had sold 13 cars, all built to the same design, which made them some of the first automobiles ever sold in the United States. Thirteen cars sounds like nothing today, but it was a start: a business making cars to sell, not just one inventor's private machine.","Sadly, the brothers fell out. They argued over money and plans, including whether to move the business to Detroit, and they split up before 1900. For the rest of their long lives, Charles and Frank each told the story in a way that gave himself more of the credit. Many historians believe Frank did much of the hands-on engineering that made the cars work, while Charles was the promoter and organiser, but the quarrel means some details of who did what are still uncertain."]},{"heading":"Racing Through the Snow: Chicago, Thanksgiving 1895","paragraphs":["In 1895 a Chicago newspaper, the Chicago Times-Herald, decided to hold America's first big motor car contest. The paper offered 5,000 dollars in prizes to encourage the new industry, and it hoped the race would sell lots of newspapers too. Eighty-three vehicles were entered. But building a car that actually worked was much harder than filling in an entry form. The race was postponed once because so few cars were ready, and when the day finally came, only six turned up at the start line: four gasoline cars and two electric ones.","The race was held on Thanksgiving Day, 28 November 1895. It was a terrible day for driving. A snowstorm had hit Chicago, and the course, about 54 miles (87 kilometres) from Jackson Park on the city's south side north to Evanston and back, was covered in slush, snow and mud. Imagine standing in the cold at dawn, wrapped in a heavy coat, as the strange machines coughed, popped and slithered away from the start, while horse-drawn sleighs glided by much more easily.","Frank Duryea drove the Duryea brothers' car. It was not a smooth ride. He had to stop for repairs, including a visit to a blacksmith's forge to fix a broken part, and he took a wrong turn at one point. But his car had one great advantage: air-filled tires, which gripped the slushy roads better than solid ones. After 10 hours and 23 minutes, Frank crossed the finish line in first place. That is an average of only about 5 miles per hour over the whole day, about the speed of a brisk walk, but he had finished, which most entrants could not do.","Second place went to a German-built Benz entered by Oscar Mueller of Decatur, Illinois. Near the end, Mueller passed out from the cold, and the race umpire riding with him, a young Detroit engineer named Charles Brady King, took the controls and drove the car across the line. The other cars, including both electrics, did not finish.","The prizes were a bit confusing. The judges gave a gold medal to the electric car built by Morris and Salom, based mostly on separate tests of design and performance. But the Duryea won the 2,000 dollar first prize, and Mueller's Benz won 1,500 dollars. Newspapers across the country reported the race, and suddenly thousands of Americans were talking about horseless carriages. The race also helped to show that pneumatic tires were the way forward."]},{"heading":"Henry Ford: From Farm Boy to Night-Time Tinkerer","paragraphs":["One of the people who read about the new motor cars with great excitement was a young engineer in Detroit, Michigan, named Henry Ford. He was born on 30 July 1863 on his family's farm in Greenfield Township, near Dearborn, just outside Detroit. His father, William, was a farmer who had come from Ireland. His mother, Mary, died in 1876, when Henry was only about 12, a loss he felt deeply.","Henry did not love farm work, but he loved machines. When he was about 12, his father gave him a pocket watch, and he soon became known for taking watches apart and putting them back together. By 15 he was repairing watches for friends and neighbours. He went to a small one-room school until about the eighth grade and never went to high school.","In 1879, aged 16, Henry walked off to Detroit to become an apprentice machinist, learning to cut and shape metal parts. He returned home for a while, operated and repaired portable steam engines for farmers, and was hired by the Westinghouse company to service its steam engines in the area. In 1891 he took a job as an engineer at the Edison Illuminating Company, which ran one of Detroit's electric power stations. He did well, and by 1893 he was chief engineer.","His job had one useful feature: he was on call at the power station at odd hours, which left time for experiments. In a brick shed behind his rented home on Bagley Avenue in Detroit, Henry, helped by friends, spent his nights and spare hours building a gasoline engine and then a whole small vehicle around it. His wife, Clara, supported him so strongly that he later called her 'the Believer'.","On 4 June 1896, in the early hours of the morning, the machine was ready. Ford called it the Quadricycle, because it ran on four bicycle-style wire wheels. It had a two-cylinder engine of about 4 horsepower that drove the back wheels with a chain, a seat for the driver, a tiller instead of a steering wheel and a small fuel tank under the seat. It weighed about 500 pounds (about 230 kilograms). The gearbox had only two forward speeds, and there was no reverse gear at all: to back up, you had to get out and push.","According to a famous story, the finished Quadricycle was too wide to fit through the shed's door, and Ford had to knock out part of the brick wall to get it out. Whether every detail of that tale is true or not, the Quadricycle worked. Ford drove it around the dark streets of Detroit, and later that year he sold it for 200 dollars, using the money to keep building. Years later he bought it back for 60 dollars. The original Quadricycle is now displayed at The Henry Ford museum in Dearborn, Michigan.","Ford was not the first person to drive a car in Detroit: Charles Brady King, the same engineer who finished the Chicago race at the wheel of Mueller's Benz, drove his own motor car on Detroit's streets a few months earlier, in March 1896. In 1896 Ford also met his famous boss, Thomas Edison, at a company meeting. When Ford described his gasoline car, Edison encouraged him to keep going, and Ford never forgot it."]},{"heading":"Two Failures and a Third Try","paragraphs":["Building one car is not the same as running a car company. Ford found that out the hard way.","In August 1899, backed by a group of Detroit investors, Ford left the Edison company and became chief engineer of the new Detroit Automobile Company. It was a disaster. Ford kept trying to perfect his designs, and the few vehicles the company made were expensive and not very good. In January 1901 the company was dissolved.","Ford decided that racing would prove his skill and attract new investors. In October 1901 he drove a racing car he had built himself and beat Alexander Winton, one of America's best-known carmakers, in a race at Grosse Pointe, near Detroit. The win brought fresh backers, and in November 1901 the Henry Ford Company was formed. Yet within a few months Ford quarrelled with his investors and left. They brought in a skilled precision engineer, Henry Leland, and renamed the firm after the founder of Detroit, Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac. The Cadillac company went on to great success without Ford.","Ford kept racing. He built an enormous, roaring racing car called the '999', and in October 1902 the daring cyclist-turned-driver Barney Oldfield drove it to victory, becoming one of America's first racing stars. The publicity helped once more.","On 16 June 1903, Ford and a new group of investors, led by a Detroit coal dealer named Alexander Malcomson, founded the Ford Motor Company with 28,000 dollars in cash. Among the partners were the brothers John and Horace Dodge, whose machine shop built much of the running gear for Ford's first car, the 1903 Ford Model A. The company started small, in a rented building, with a handful of workers putting together parts made by other firms. This time, it worked. The Ford Motor Company sold cars and made money from its first year, and it is still one of the world's biggest carmakers today."]},{"heading":"Ransom Olds and the Curved Dash","paragraphs":["While Ford was struggling, another Michigan carmaker had already shown that many people would buy a car if the price was right. Ransom Eli Olds was born on 3 June 1864 in Geneva, Ohio, and grew up working in his father's engine and machine shop in Lansing, Michigan. In August 1897 he founded the Olds Motor Vehicle Company in Lansing. Two years later a rich copper and timber businessman, Samuel L. Smith, put money into the firm, which became the Olds Motor Works and moved its main factory to Detroit.","Olds built several experimental models. Then, on 9 March 1901, the Detroit factory caught fire and was badly damaged. According to the often-told story, only one prototype was saved: a small, light runabout with a front that curved up like the toe of a sleigh. Whether or not it happened quite that way, the company put this little car into production, and it became famous as the Curved Dash Oldsmobile.","The Curved Dash was simple and cheap for its time. It sat two people, had a one-cylinder engine of about 5 horsepower under the seat, was steered with a tiller and could reach about 20 miles per hour (32 km/h). It cost 650 dollars, still a lot of money when a factory worker might earn roughly 400 to 500 dollars in a whole year, but far less than the big hand-built cars of the rich.","To make many cars quickly, Olds bought many parts, such as engines and gearboxes, from outside machine shops, and organised his factory so that cars were put together step by step from those parts. Historians often call this an early assembly line, although it was not the moving, conveyor-driven kind that Ford's team would build later: the cars mostly stood still while workers and parts came to them.","The results were dramatic. Olds built about 425 Curved Dash cars in 1901, roughly 2,000 to 2,500 in 1902, and several thousand a year by 1903 and 1904, making it the best-selling car in America and one of the first cars built in large numbers anywhere. In all, about 19,000 were made. The car even inspired a hit song, 'In My Merry Oldsmobile', in 1905. Olds himself left the company in 1904 after disagreements with the Smith family and started another firm, using his initials: REO."]},{"heading":"The Patent That Claimed Every Car","paragraphs":["As American carmakers multiplied, they ran into a strange legal roadblock. It came from a man who had never built and sold a single car: George B. Selden, a patent lawyer from Rochester, New York.","Back in 1879, Selden had filed a US patent application for a 'road engine', a carriage powered by a light, liquid-fuelled internal combustion engine based on an American engine design by George Brayton. A patent gives an inventor the right to stop others from using an invention for a set number of years, usually counted from the day it is granted. Selden cleverly kept his application waiting, making changes and amendments for sixteen years, while the car industry grew up around him. Only in November 1895 was US Patent 549,160 finally granted. Because the clock started then, his patent would last until 1912, and its wording was so broad that it seemed to cover almost any gasoline car.","In 1899 Selden sold the rights to a group of investors connected with the Electric Vehicle Company, who set out to make carmakers pay. In 1903 many of the leading companies formed the Association of Licensed Automobile Manufacturers. Its members agreed to pay a small fee on every car they sold, shared between the patent owners and the association, and in return they could advertise that their cars were 'licensed'.","Henry Ford refused to pay. His new company was turned down when it asked to join, and he decided to fight. The patent owners sued Ford and several other independent carmakers, and the legal battle lasted about eight years. To make sure buyers were not scared away, Ford promised in advertisements to protect any customer who might be sued. In 1909 a judge ruled in favour of Selden, and it looked as if Ford had lost.","Ford appealed. In January 1911 the appeals court decided that Selden's patent was valid, but only for cars using the Brayton-type engine that Selden had described. Almost every real car used an engine based on Nicolaus Otto's four-stroke design instead, so they did not infringe. The patent, which had only about a year left to run, became almost worthless overnight. Ford became a hero to many Americans as the little company that had beaten a big monopoly, and the case helped convince carmakers to share patents more freely with each other in the years that followed."]},{"heading":"Coast to Coast: The Doctor, the Mechanic, the Dog and Alice","paragraphs":["By 1903 cars could putter around a city, but could one cross the whole continent? Many people thought it was impossible. There were almost no paved roads between cities, no road signs, few bridges designed for cars and no filling stations. Then came a bet.","In May 1903, at the University Club in San Francisco, a 31-year-old doctor from Vermont named Horatio Nelson Jackson accepted a 50 dollar wager that he could cross the United States by car in less than 90 days. He had no car, very little driving experience and no maps. He hired a young mechanic and driver, Sewall K. Crocker, and bought a slightly used Winton touring car with a two-cylinder, 20-horsepower engine. He named it the Vermont, after his home state.","Jackson and Crocker set off from San Francisco on 23 May 1903. The trip was a long list of troubles. A tire burst early in the journey. They lost spare parts, money and even Jackson's glasses along the way. In Wyoming they went about 36 hours without food. When the car's wheel bearings broke, they made do with bearings borrowed from a farm mowing machine, and they spent days waiting for spare parts to arrive by train. Sometimes they used ropes and a block and tackle to haul the car out of mud or up steep slopes.","In Idaho, near the town of Caldwell, the travellers gained a third crew member: a young bulldog named Bud. Newspaper stories gave different accounts of how they got him, but in a letter to his wife Jackson said he had paid 15 dollars. The car had no roof and no windshield, and the dust bothered Bud's eyes, so Jackson gave him his own pair of driving goggles. Bud became a celebrity, and crowds came out to see the dog in goggles.","The Vermont rolled into New York City in the early hours of 26 July 1903, after 63 days, 12 hours and 30 minutes. Jackson won his bet, although the trip reportedly cost him thousands of dollars, far more than the 50 he won. They were the first people to cross the United States by car. A motorcyclist, George A. Wyman, had reached New York a few weeks earlier on a motorised bicycle, so Jackson's record is specifically for the automobile. In 1944 Jackson gave the Vermont to the Smithsonian Institution, and it is still kept at the National Museum of American History in Washington, D.C.","Six years later, another driver made a crossing in the opposite direction, and this time the driver was a woman. Alice Huyler Ramsey, a 22-year-old from Hackensack, New Jersey, left New York City on 9 June 1909 in a green Maxwell touring car. The Maxwell-Briscoe company sponsored the trip, partly as publicity: it wanted to show that its cars were easy and reliable enough for anyone, including women, to drive. Ramsey brought three passengers: two older sisters-in-law and a 19-year-old friend, Hermine Jahns. None of them could drive, so Alice did all of it herself.","The journey was about 3,800 miles (around 6,100 kilometres) long. Only a tiny fraction of the route, about 152 miles, was paved. Ramsey changed flat tires, cleaned spark plugs and coped with breakdowns, sometimes waiting in small towns while parts were fixed or sent. She steered the heavy car through mud, sand, swollen streams and mountain passes. With only rough maps, the group sometimes, according to Ramsey's own account, found their way by following telephone poles, because the lines with the most wires usually led to the next town.","On 7 August 1909, after 59 days, Alice Ramsey drove into San Francisco. She was the first woman to drive across the United States. Decades later she wrote a book about the trip, 'Veil, Duster, and Tire Iron' (1961), and in 2000 she became the first woman inducted into the Automotive Hall of Fame.","Jackson's and Ramsey's journeys showed something important. Cars were no longer just toys for city streets; with enough grit, they could tie a whole continent together. But at the start of the 1900s, most cars were still too costly for ordinary families. Henry Ford had a bold idea about that. As he later put it, 'I will build a motor car for the great multitude'. He meant a car simple and cheap enough for a farmer, a factory worker or a shopkeeper to own, and the car he built to keep that promise would change the world."]}],"deepDive":"Who built America's first car? The honest answer is that it depends on your definition. Several inventors ran self-propelled vehicles in the United States before the Duryeas, including steam carriages, and a few people may have run small gasoline machines in the early 1890s. The Duryea brothers' 1893 test in Springfield is usually counted as one of the first successful American gasoline cars, and their 1895 company as the first American firm set up to build and sell them; by the end of 1896 it had sold 13 identical cars. Even within the family, credit was disputed: Charles and Frank spent decades disagreeing about who had done the real work. Detroit had its own priority question. Charles Brady King drove a motor car there in March 1896, about three months before Henry Ford's Quadricycle, which is a reminder that Ford's fame came from what he did later, not from being first. The Selden patent is a classic case study in how patent law can be used. By delaying his 1879 application for sixteen years with amendments, Selden made sure his patent was granted in 1895, just as the industry was taking off, so it would last until 1912. The 1911 appeals court decision did not declare the patent invalid; instead it ruled that it covered only cars with Brayton-type engines, while nearly all real cars used Otto-type four-stroke engines. Economic historians point out that the case changed industry behaviour: afterwards, American carmakers formed a cross-licensing arrangement in which members shared most patents with each other without fees, which lowered legal costs and may have sped up innovation. Numbers also help explain the boom. In 1900 there were only about 8,000 registered cars in the US, but the Curved Dash Oldsmobile showed that a light car costing 650 dollars could sell by the thousands, roughly 425 in 1901 and several thousand a year by 1903 and 1904. The idea that price, not just engineering, would decide the car's future set the stage for Ford's Model T.","funFact":"Bud, the bulldog who rode across America with Horatio Nelson Jackson in 1903, wore his own pair of driving goggles to keep the dust out of his eyes.","check":{"q":"Who won the Chicago Times-Herald race on Thanksgiving Day 1895?","a":"Frank Duryea","wrong":["Henry Ford","Ransom E. Olds","Alice Ramsey"]},"sources":[{"title":"Duryea Motor Wagon Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duryea_Motor_Wagon_Company"},{"title":"Chicago Times-Herald race","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago_Times-Herald_race"},{"title":"Ford Quadricycle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Quadricycle"},{"title":"Henry Ford","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Ford"},{"title":"Oldsmobile Curved Dash","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oldsmobile_Curved_Dash"},{"title":"George B. Selden","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_B._Selden"},{"title":"Horatio's Drive: America's First Road Trip","publisher":"PBS","url":"https://www.pbs.org/horatiosdrive/"},{"title":"Alice Huyler Ramsey","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alice_Huyler_Ramsey"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/10-1.jpg","alt":"Henry Ford sitting in his Quadricycle, the first car he built (1896), with its four bicycle-style wheels.","file":"FordQuadricycle.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FordQuadricycle.jpg","author":"Spooner & Wells, Inc.","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/10-2.jpg","alt":"A 1902 Oldsmobile Curved Dash runabout in a museum, with its sleigh-like curved front.","file":"Oldsmobile Curved Dash Runabout 1902.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oldsmobile_Curved_Dash_Runabout_1902.jpg","author":"Lars-Göran Lindgren Sweden","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/10-3.jpg","alt":"The mud-covered Winton car \"Vermont\" with Jackson, Crocker and goggle-wearing Bud the dog on the first drive across America, 1903.","file":"Horatio Nelson Jackson on his famous drive across America in 1903.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horatio_Nelson_Jackson_on_his_famous_drive_across_America_in_1903.png","author":"Horatio Nelson jackson","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3681},{"n":11,"title":"The Model T and the Moving Assembly Line","when":"1908 – 1927","summary":"In 1908 Ford's company built a tough, simple car called the Model T, nicknamed the Tin Lizzie. Then Ford's engineers made the cars move past the workers on a long line, so a car could be put together in about an hour and a half instead of half a day. Prices fell so low that millions of ordinary families could finally buy a car.","sections":[{"heading":"A Car for the Great Multitude","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part, Henry Ford made a big promise: to build a motor car for the great multitude. In 1908 that sounded almost silly. Cars were toys for rich people. They broke down often, many cost more than an ordinary worker earned in two or three years, and you usually needed a paid driver who was also a mechanic. Most Americans still lived on farms or in small towns, connected by dirt roads that turned into mud every spring.","On 1 October 1908 the Ford Motor Company introduced the car that was supposed to change all that. It was called the Model T. The name simply meant that it came after a long list of earlier Ford models with letters such as A, B, C, F, K, N, R and S. The first production cars were built at Ford's brick factory on Piquette Avenue in Detroit, and orders flooded in. Within days of the announcement, Ford had thousands of orders, so many that the company could not keep up.","The Model T was not the work of Henry Ford alone. He described what he wanted, but the detailed design came from a small team working in a locked room at the Piquette plant. Childe Harold Wills was a skilled engineer and metal expert. Joseph Galamb and Eugene Farkas were young engineers who had moved to America from Hungary. Galamb drew much of the car, and Farkas worked on parts such as the axles and frame. Together they turned Ford's wishes into thousands of exact drawings.","What did Ford want? First, the car had to be simple. Anyone with a wrench and a bit of common sense should be able to fix it. Second, it had to be tough enough for terrible country roads. Third, it had to be light, because a lighter car needs a smaller engine and less fuel. And fourth, it had to be cheap to build, which meant using the same design year after year instead of changing it all the time.","The result looked tall and a little awkward, like a carriage on stilts. That was on purpose. The Model T sat high off the ground, so it could roll over ruts, rocks and tree roots that would scrape the bottom of a lower car. Its springs were arranged so that the wheels could twist and bounce over bumps while the body stayed fairly level. Farmers loved that. A car that got stuck in the first muddy field was no use to them.","Under the hood was a four-cylinder engine of about 2.9 litres that made about 20 horsepower. That may not sound like much today, when ordinary family cars often have more than 100 horsepower, but the Model T was light, so 20 horsepower was enough. Its top speed was roughly 65 to 70 km/h (about 40 to 45 mph), which was plenty on roads full of potholes, horses and chickens.","One secret ingredient was a special metal: vanadium steel. Ordinary steel of the time could be brittle, and a broken axle in the middle of nowhere was a disaster. Adding a small amount of the element vanadium made steel much stronger and tougher, so parts could be thinner and lighter without snapping. Ford's team used vanadium steel for important parts such as the front axle. Ford liked to tell a story about examining a French racing car wreck and finding a strong vanadium part in it; whether or not every detail of that story is exact, the metal really did help make the Model T both light and strong.","People soon gave the car nicknames. The most famous was the Tin Lizzie. Nobody is completely sure where it came from. Some say Lizzie was a common, friendly name for a hard-working horse or helper, and the car was the farmer's new dependable workhorse. Whatever its origin, the name stuck, and the Model T was also called the flivver, a slang word for a cheap little car."]},{"heading":"Driving a Tin Lizzie: Pedals, Levers and a Crank","paragraphs":["If you climbed into a Model T today, you might be very confused. There is a steering wheel, yes, but the rest of the controls look nothing like a modern car. Learning them was part of the adventure for millions of first-time drivers.","On the floor were three pedals. The left pedal controlled the two forward gears: pushed all the way down, it put the car in low gear for starting and climbing hills; let all the way up, it put the car in high gear for cruising. The middle pedal was for reverse. The right pedal was the brake, which worked on the transmission rather than directly on the front wheels. A tall hand lever beside the driver worked as a parking brake and also helped hold the car in neutral.","The speed was not controlled by a pedal at all. Instead there were two small levers just under the steering wheel. One was the throttle, which let more fuel and air into the engine. The other adjusted the timing of the spark, meaning exactly when the spark plugs fired. Drivers had to learn to move the spark lever to the right place before starting, or the engine could kick back.","That kick was the most dangerous part of driving an early Model T. Most early cars were started with a hand crank: a metal handle that stuck out at the front. You turned it to spin the engine until it caught and started running. If the spark was set wrong, the engine could fire too early and whip the crank backward, sometimes hard enough to break a thumb or a wrist. Drivers learned to hold the crank in a special way, with the thumb on the same side as the fingers, to stay safe. As Part 7 told, Charles Kettering's electric self-starter appeared on the 1912 Cadillac. Ford was slower to add one, and an electric starter became available on the Model T from about 1919.","The gearbox itself was clever. It used a planetary gear system, in which small gears circle around a central gear like planets around the Sun. Bands tightened around drums inside the gearbox to select low gear, high gear or reverse. Ford advertised it as a three-speed system, but since one of those speeds was reverse, people today usually describe it as having two forward speeds. Because there was no tricky gear lever to move, many people found it easier to learn than other cars of the time.","The Model T also had a small trick for its electrics. Instead of a separate battery and generator in early versions, it used a flywheel magneto: a ring of magnets spinning with the engine's flywheel that produced electricity for the spark plugs and, from about 1915, for the electric headlights too (the earliest cars used gas lamps). The faster the engine ran, the brighter the lights could glow, which meant headlights dimmed when you slowed down. Remember the magneto, because it plays a starring role in the story of the assembly line.","Repairs were part of everyday life. Because the same design was built in such huge numbers, spare parts were cheap and easy to find. Hardware stores and mail-order catalogues sold Model T parts and add-ons, from extra seats to tools. Owners swapped tips, jokes and repair tricks. The car became a kind of shared hobby for an entire country."]},{"heading":"Borrowed Ideas: Guns, Pigs and Stopwatches","paragraphs":["The Model T was a good design. But a good design alone does not make a car cheap. In its early years, Ford built the Model T much like other carmakers built their cars: the car stayed in one spot while groups of workers walked around it, fetching parts and fitting them on. To build hundreds of thousands of cars, Ford's team would need a whole new way of working. They did not invent it out of nothing. They borrowed ideas from many places and put them together in a new way.","The first big idea was interchangeable parts. In the past, skilled craftsmen made each part slightly differently, then filed and fitted the pieces by hand so they matched. In the early 1800s, United States government armouries such as Springfield in Massachusetts and Harpers Ferry in Virginia worked for decades to make gun parts so exact that any trigger would fit any gun of the same model. They used special measuring tools called gauges and holding frames called jigs and fixtures. This became known as the American system of manufacturing. Ford's factory relied on it completely: if every part was exactly the same, workers did not need to fiddle and file, they just bolted things together.","Making exact parts quickly required powerful machine tools. Ford's factories eventually used tens of thousands of them, including drills with many spindles that could bore all the holes on one side of an engine block in a single operation, and milling machines that could shape a whole row of engine blocks at once. The machines were arranged in the order the work was done, rather than grouped by type, so parts flowed from one machine to the next.","The second big idea came from a surprising place: slaughterhouses. In Chicago meatpacking plants, from the 1860s onward, animal carcasses hung from hooks on overhead rails and moved past a row of workers. Each worker made one cut, again and again, as the carcass slid by. This was really a disassembly line, taking things apart instead of putting them together. A Ford worker named William Klann visited a Chicago slaughterhouse belonging to Swift and Company and came back with the thought: what if we did this in reverse, and built cars as they moved past us?","Other industries had also used moving lines and conveyors. Flour mills moved grain with belts and screw conveyors. Breweries and canneries moved bottles and cans. Ransom Olds, as Part 10 told, had already organised his factory so that Curved Dash Oldsmobiles were assembled in a planned order in 1901, though the cars did not yet move on a powered line.","The third idea was careful timing. An engineer named Frederick Winslow Taylor had become famous for studying workers with a stopwatch, breaking each job into tiny steps and finding the quickest way to do each one. This is often called time study, and a related method that studies each body movement is called motion study. Henry Ford later played down Taylor's influence, but Ford's engineers certainly timed jobs, measured movements and rearranged work so that no one wasted a step.","Finally, there were the people. Historians credit a whole group at Ford for the moving assembly line. Peter E. Martin ran production. Charles Sorensen, nicknamed Cast-Iron Charlie because of his work on the foundry and patterns, was his second in command. Clarence Avery planned and timed many of the experiments. C. Harold Wills, Joseph Galamb and others contributed, and William Klann brought the slaughterhouse idea. Henry Ford pushed hard for lower costs and gave his team the freedom to experiment. So the honest answer to who invented the moving assembly line for cars is: a team, building on ideas that had been floating around for a century."]},{"heading":"Highland Park: The Line Starts to Move","paragraphs":["The Piquette Avenue plant was soon far too small. So Ford built a gigantic new factory in Highland Park, a town surrounded by the city of Detroit. It was designed by the architect Albert Kahn and opened in 1910. Its walls were filled with so many large windows that people nicknamed it the Crystal Palace. All that glass let daylight pour onto the factory floor, which mattered in an age of dim electric bulbs. The building had several floors, and parts could travel downward through chutes and openings from one level to the next, so gravity did some of the carrying.","Imagine walking onto the Highland Park floor in early 1913. The air smells of hot oil and metal shavings. Leather belts slap and hum overhead, carrying power from spinning shafts to rows of machines. Trolleys rumble past loaded with castings. Even before the moving line, this was one of the most organised factories in the world. By one widely used count, Ford built about 170,000 Model Ts in 1912 (sources differ a little, partly because Ford often counted by business year rather than calendar year). But Ford's engineers believed they could do far better.","Their first real experiment, in the spring of 1913, was with the flywheel magneto, the electricity-making ring of magnets you met earlier. Until then, one skilled worker assembled a whole magneto at a bench, which took about 20 minutes. The engineers split the job into many small steps and lined up the workers along a waist-high rail. Each person did one small task, then slid the magneto along to the next person. Historians who studied Ford's records report that the time per magneto dropped to about 13 minutes, and then, after the rail was raised to a better height and the work was set to move at a steady pace, to about 5 minutes. The same number of people were now making about four times as many magnetos.","That was the moment the idea clicked. If it worked for magnetos, why not for engines, transmissions and axles? Over the next months, Ford's team set up moving lines for one part after another. Then they tackled the biggest challenge of all: the whole chassis, meaning the frame with its axles, springs, wheels, engine and transmission.","Charles Sorensen later wrote that, years earlier, he and a colleague named Charles Lewis had tested the idea one Sunday by dragging a chassis across the old Piquette factory floor with a rope while workers added parts. Historians treat the details as his own account. What is certain is that in 1913 the Highland Park team began pulling chassis along with a rope and a winch, then with a powered chain running along the floor.","On 7 October 1913, the moving chassis line began running. Before the line, building one Model T chassis had taken a team about 12 and a half hours, or roughly 728 minutes. Within a couple of months the time fell to about 2 hours and 40 minutes. By early 1914, after more experiments with the speed of the line, the height of the work and the order of the jobs, the time had dropped to about 93 minutes, roughly an hour and a half. The work was divided into dozens of steps, around 45 by one count, and a finished chassis could roll off the end of the line every few minutes.","The rule of the line was simple: bring the work to the worker, not the worker to the work. Parts arrived by conveyor or slide exactly where they were needed. The worker stayed in one place, did one job, and the moving chassis brought the next one along. Nobody had to walk around looking for bolts.","The results were astonishing. Ford built about 202,000 Model Ts in 1913, about 308,000 in 1914 and about 501,000 in 1915. By around 1914, Ford was by far the biggest carmaker in the world. By 1918, about half of all the cars in the United States were Model Ts. And because the company now built cars so quickly, it could sell them for less, which made even more people buy them, which let the company build even more. Factories everywhere took notice."]},{"heading":"Five Dollars a Day","paragraphs":["There was a problem. The moving line was fast, but it was also boring and exhausting. Imagine tightening the same few nuts, hundreds of times a day, while the chassis keeps sliding toward you whether you are ready or not. If you were slow, the next worker could not do their job. If you needed a break, someone had to cover for you. Workers complained about the relentless pace, and many simply quit and went to work somewhere else.","In 1913 the turnover, meaning how many workers left and had to be replaced, was enormous. Some departments had to hire around 300 people over a year just to keep 100 jobs filled. Every new worker had to be found, hired and trained, which wasted time and money. A line only works if everyone on it is there and knows what they are doing.","On 5 January 1914, Ford Motor Company made a startling announcement. Its basic pay for qualifying workers would jump from about $2.34 for a nine-hour day to $5 for an eight-hour day. It was more than double what most factory workers in Detroit earned. Ford also moved to three eight-hour shifts, so the factory could run around the clock.","Newspapers around the world reported it. Other business leaders were shocked and some predicted Ford would go broke. Instead, thousands of job-seekers rushed to Highland Park. Reports describe huge crowds standing outside the gates in freezing January weather, hoping to be hired. Turnover fell dramatically, and the company claimed it had dropped from about 32 percent in 1913 to under 2 percent in 1915. The best mechanics in Detroit wanted to work for Ford.","There was also a clever business side to it. Workers earning $5 a day could, after saving for a while, afford to buy the very car they were building. Every lower price and every higher wage widened the circle of people who could own a car. Later, economists used the word Fordism for this combination of mass production, high output and higher wages that let workers become customers.","But the $5 day came with strings attached. Much of the extra money was called profit-sharing, and to get it a worker usually had to have worked at Ford for at least six months and had to live in a way the company approved of. Ford set up what it called the Sociological Department, with dozens of investigators, about 50 according to one account, who visited workers' homes. They checked whether workers drank heavily, gambled, kept their homes clean, saved money, or looked after their children. Workers who did not meet the rules could lose the bonus until they changed. Many immigrant workers were also expected to attend company English classes.","Some families welcomed the help and advice. Others felt it was nosy and controlling: a company had no business telling grown-ups how to live in their own homes. Within a few years the department became smaller and less intrusive, and Ford himself later described that kind of home inspection as out of date.","And the work itself stayed hard. The line set the pace, not the person. Many workers felt like parts of the machine. That tension, between amazing productivity and dull, tiring work, has followed assembly lines ever since, and later factories, as you will see in the next part, tried new ways to give workers more say."]},{"heading":"Cheaper Every Year, and Any Color So Long As It Is Black","paragraphs":["Ford's plan was to make the Model T a little cheaper almost every year. In 1908 the touring car, the version with seats for about five people, cost about $850. By 1916 the touring car had fallen to about $360. By 1925 the runabout, a two-seater, could be bought new for about $260. Even allowing for changes in the value of money, that was an enormous drop. At the same time, the car got small improvements: electric headlights, an optional electric starter, and later closed bodies with windows that kept out the rain.","By around 1920 Ford was building roughly a million Model Ts a year. Ford factories and assembly plants opened in other countries too, including one at Trafford Park near Manchester in England from 1911, and plants in Canada, Ireland, Denmark and elsewhere. The Model T became a world car.","On farms, the Tin Lizzie became much more than transport. Farmers jacked up a rear wheel and wrapped a belt around it, so the spinning wheel could drive a saw, a water pump, a corn sheller or a washing machine. Companies sold kits that turned a Model T into a small tractor for pulling ploughs. A Model T could carry milk to town, take children to school and bring a doctor to a sick neighbour. Some were turned into trucks, fire engines, snow vehicles with skis, and even little railway cars. Henry Ford also built a real tractor, the Fordson, but for many families the Model T itself was the farm's handiest machine.","Now for the most famous story about the Model T: the color. In his 1922 book My Life and Work, Henry Ford wrote that any customer could have a car painted any colour he wanted, \"so long as it is black.\" This line is often repeated to suggest that every Model T was black. That is not true.","In the early years, from 1908 to about 1913, Model Ts came in several colors, including grey, green, blue and red, depending on the body style and year. Then, from about 1914 to 1925, black really was the only color offered. Why? Because paint takes time to dry, and on a fast assembly line every minute counted. The black enamel Ford used, sometimes called Japan black, dried faster than other colors available at the time, so cars could keep moving. Ford actually used over 30 different kinds of black paint on different parts of the car, each chosen for how it would be applied and dried.","Then, in 1926, colors came back. New quick-drying lacquer paints, such as the Duco lacquer developed by the chemical company DuPont, made bright colors practical on a fast line. Late Model Ts came in shades with names like green, maroon and grey. So the true story is: colors, then black only, then colors again."]},{"heading":"The End of the Tin Lizzie","paragraphs":["By the mid-1920s the world had changed, and the Model T had not changed nearly enough. Roads were getting better, so people cared less about high ground clearance. Buyers wanted cars that were faster, smoother, more comfortable and better looking. They wanted closed bodies, a normal gear lever, more power, modern brakes and a choice of colors and styles. Many families already owned a car and wanted something newer and nicer, not just another copy of the same old Tin Lizzie.","Other companies were happy to offer exactly that. Some let customers pay in instalments, a little each month, instead of saving up the whole price first. Henry Ford disliked buying on credit and was stubborn about his car. Some of his managers, and reportedly his son Edsel, urged him to design a successor, but for years he refused, until falling sales finally pushed him to approve a new car in 1926. Sales of the Model T began to slide.","Finally, in 1927, Ford gave in. On 26 May 1927, the company celebrated what it called its 15 millionth Model T. Soon afterwards the Model T assembly lines stopped. Including cars built in other countries, more than 15 million Model Ts had been made since 1908, a record for a single car model that would last for about 45 years.","Then Ford's factories went quiet for months while they were retooled, meaning old machines were replaced and new ones set up, for an entirely new car. During that pause, thousands of Ford workers were laid off and Ford dealers had almost nothing new to sell. In December 1927 the new car was unveiled. Ford called it the Model A, starting the alphabet again to show it was a fresh beginning. It had a more powerful engine, a normal three-speed gearbox, four-wheel brakes and a choice of colors. Crowds lined up to see it, and more than four million were built in the following few years.","The Model T had done what Ford promised. It had put the world on wheels. But it had also taught a hard lesson: making the same thing cheaper and cheaper only works while people still want that thing. While Ford's factories stood silent in 1927, a rival company had been quietly gaining ground, by offering buyers something the Model T never did: choice."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of technology such as David Hounshell have argued that Ford's achievement was less a single invention than a system. Interchangeable parts had been pursued in American armouries for decades; continuous-flow processing existed in milling, brewing, canning and meatpacking; Olds and others had organised car assembly in sequence. Highland Park combined all of these: highly accurate single-purpose machine tools, a single unchanging product, machines arranged in the order of the work, gravity slides and conveyors, and finally powered lines that set the pace. The numbers show why this mattered. Chassis assembly fell from about 728 labour-minutes to about 93 in roughly six months, and annual output rose from about 170,000 cars in 1912 (by one count) to roughly a million around 1920. Lower costs let Ford cut prices, which expanded demand, which justified even more specialised machines: a feedback loop sometimes described as economies of scale. The costs were real too. Deskilling turned many craft jobs into repetitive tasks, and the extreme turnover of 1913 shows workers voting with their feet. The $5 day is still debated. Some economists describe it as an early example of efficiency wages, where paying above the market rate reduces quitting and absenteeism enough to pay for itself; others stress its paternalism, since the Sociological Department attached moral conditions to the profit-sharing portion. Henry Ford's personal legacy is also complicated: alongside his role in mass production, his newspaper, the Dearborn Independent, published antisemitic articles in the 1920s, for which he later issued a public apology. Finally, the Model T's long life illustrates the danger of over-specialisation. Machines built to make one design extremely efficiently made change slow and expensive, which is why the switch to the Model A required a shutdown lasting months. Later manufacturers, especially Toyota, would look for ways to keep the speed of mass production while making factories more flexible.","funFact":"Ford used more than 30 different kinds of black paint on the Model T, each chosen to suit a different part and the way it was painted and dried.","check":{"q":"Why was black the only Model T color from about 1914 to 1925?","a":"Because black paint dried fastest, keeping the fast assembly line moving","wrong":["Because Henry Ford thought every other color was unlucky","Because a law in the United States banned colored cars","Because black cars could drive faster than colored ones"]},"sources":[{"title":"Ford Model T","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Model_T"},{"title":"Highland Park Ford Plant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highland_Park_Ford_Plant"},{"title":"The Moving Assembly Line and the Five-Dollar Workday","publisher":"Ford Motor Company","url":"https://corporate.ford.com/articles/history/moving-assembly-line.html"},{"title":"Assembly line","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_line"},{"title":"Henry Ford","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Ford"},{"title":"Charles E. Sorensen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_E._Sorensen"},{"title":"History of Ford Motor Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Ford_Motor_Company"},{"title":"Mass production","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_production"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/11-1.jpg","alt":"A black 1916 Ford Model T touring car with brass radiator, shown at a classic car show.","file":"1916 Ford Model T touring car.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1916_Ford_Model_T_touring_car.JPG","author":"Stephen Foskett (Wikipedia User: sfoskett)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/11-2.jpg","alt":"Ford workers in 1913 each add parts as flywheel assemblies slide past along a long line.","file":"Ford assembly line - 1913 (restored).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ford_assembly_line_-_1913_(restored).jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/11-3.jpg","alt":"Ford's Highland Park plant in Michigan, where the moving assembly line began, seen in winter.","file":"Highland Park Ford plant.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Highland_Park_Ford_plant.jpg","author":"Andrew Jameson (talk) at en.wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4297},{"n":12,"title":"A Car for Every Purse: GM, the Beetle and the World's Carmakers","when":"1908 – 1980s","summary":"General Motors beat Ford by letting people choose: cheap cars, fancy cars, new styles every year, and paying a little each month. Around the world, clever designers made tiny, cheerful cars like the Mini, the 2CV and the Volkswagen Beetle. Then a Japanese company called Toyota found a smarter way to build cars, and its Corolla became the best-selling car name of all time.","sections":[{"heading":"Billy Durant's Car Collection","paragraphs":["While Henry Ford was perfecting one car, another man in Michigan was collecting whole car companies the way some people collect trading cards. His name was William C. Durant, and everybody called him Billy.","Durant did not start with cars at all. In 1886, in the town of Flint, Michigan, he and a partner named Josiah Dallas Dort founded a company that built horse-drawn road carts. By the early 1900s their Durant-Dort Carriage Company had become one of the biggest makers of horse-drawn vehicles in the United States. Durant was a brilliant salesman who understood that people bought vehicles through dealers, on trust, and on dreams.","In 1904 he took control of a struggling young carmaker called Buick and used his carriage company's money, factories and dealer network to turn it around. Within a few years Buick was one of the best-selling cars in America. Durant had a bigger idea. Instead of betting everything on one car, why not own many car companies, so that if one model failed, the others would carry on?","On 16 September 1908, just a couple of weeks before Ford introduced the Model T, Durant founded General Motors. Within about a year GM had bought Oldsmobile, Cadillac and the Oakland Motor Car Company (whose cars were later sold as Pontiac), along with many smaller firms making parts. Durant was a dreamer, though, not a careful accountant. He spent so fast that GM ran short of cash, and in 1910 the bankers who lent it money pushed him out.","Durant bounced back. In 1911 he teamed up with Louis Chevrolet, a Swiss-born racing driver, to start a new car company bearing Chevrolet's name. Chevrolet the man soon left after disagreements, but Chevrolet the car became a hit. Durant used its success to buy up GM shares and, amazingly, regained control of General Motors in 1916, and in 1918 Chevrolet officially joined the GM family. Then, in 1920, during a sudden business slump, he lost control again, this time for good, to the du Pont family and other big investors.","Durant's later life is a reminder that business can be a roller coaster. He started more companies, lost most of his fortune in the stock market crash and the Great Depression, and in his old age ran a bowling alley with a small restaurant in Flint. But the giant he had assembled was about to be reorganised by a very different kind of leader."]},{"heading":"Alfred Sloan's Ladder of Cars","paragraphs":["Alfred P. Sloan was an engineer from New York who had run a company making roller bearings, the small hardened steel balls and rollers that let wheels and shafts spin smoothly. When his firm became part of the GM group in the 1910s, he joined General Motors, and in 1923 he became its president. Where Durant was a gambler, Sloan was an organiser. He loved charts, numbers and clear rules.","Sloan looked at GM's messy family of brands, several of which were competing against one another for the same buyers, and sorted them into a neat ladder of prices. At the bottom was Chevrolet, a low-priced car meant to fight the Model T. Above it came Pontiac, then Oldsmobile, then Buick, and at the very top the luxurious Cadillac. GM described the idea as offering a car for every purse and purpose, meaning a car for every budget and every need.","The ladder worked like a staircase through life. A young couple might buy a Chevrolet. As they earned more, they could step up to a Pontiac or Oldsmobile, then to a Buick, and perhaps one day a Cadillac, without ever leaving GM. Compare that with Ford's approach: for years, Ford essentially offered one car, in one basic design, eventually in one color.","Sloan's second big idea was the yearly model change. Each year, GM cars received new styling, new trim and small improvements, so that last year's car looked a little old-fashioned. Supporters called this progress; critics later called it planned obsolescence, meaning deliberately making products seem out of date so people buy new ones sooner. Either way, it got customers excited every autumn when the new models arrived.","The third idea was buying on credit. In 1919 GM set up the General Motors Acceptance Corporation, known as GMAC, which lent money to customers so they could drive a car home after paying only part of the price, then pay the rest in monthly instalments. Henry Ford disliked debt and was slow to follow. For millions of families, being able to pay bit by bit made a new car possible years sooner.","The fourth idea was style. In 1927 a young Californian designer named Harley Earl, who had built flashy custom car bodies for Hollywood stars, designed the elegant LaSalle for GM's Cadillac division. It was such a success that Sloan created a special department, the Art and Colour Section, and made Earl its first leader. It was one of the first in-house design departments at any big carmaker. Earl pioneered shaping new car designs in modelling clay, so designers could see and change the curves in three dimensions. His team built the Buick Y-Job around 1938 to 1939, often called the first concept car, a show car built to test the public's reaction. Later, GM's 1948 Cadillac carried small tailfins inspired by a fighter plane, the Lockheed P-38 Lightning, starting a fashion that grew wilder through the 1950s.","Earl also had a secret project for a sporty two-seater. Chevrolet's managers liked it, and in 1953 the public met the Chevrolet Corvette, a low, open sports car with a body moulded from fibreglass, a lightweight material made of glass fibres set in plastic resin. It showed that even the company famous for sensible family cars could sell excitement, and the Corvette is still being made today, more than seventy years later.","In 1927, the year Ford shut down to switch from the Model T to the Model A, Chevrolet sold more cars in the United States than Ford did, by most counts. Ford fought back, and the two brands traded first place for decades, but General Motors as a whole grew into the largest industrial company the world had ever seen. Sloan's ladder, yearly models, credit and styling became the standard way to sell cars almost everywhere."]},{"heading":"Small Wonders: Europe's Tiny Cars","paragraphs":["In Europe, life was different. Roads were often narrow and twisty, cities were old and crowded, fuel was expensive, and many governments taxed cars by engine size. Most families had far less money than American families. So European designers became experts at something difficult: making a car as small, light and cheap as possible while still fitting a family inside.","In Britain, Herbert Austin, with a young draughtsman named Stanley Edge helping on the detailed drawings, created the Austin 7, announced in July 1922. Its four-cylinder engine was tiny, only 696 cc at first and 747 cc from 1923, smaller than many of today's motorcycle engines, and made only about 10 horsepower. Yet it was a proper little car with four wheels, real brakes and room for a small family. People called it the Baby Austin. About 290,000 were built in Britain until 1939, and the design was built under licence or copied abroad: in Germany as the Dixi, the first car made by BMW, in France by Rosengart and in the United States as the American Austin. It also helped end the craze for flimsy, half-motorcycle cyclecars.","In France, Pierre-Jules Boulanger, a leader of the Citroën company, asked his engineers in the 1930s for a car that would bring motoring to French farmers. According to the famous brief, it should be like an umbrella on four wheels, carry four people and about 50 kg of farm goods, and drive across a freshly ploughed field without breaking a basket of eggs. André Lefèbvre led the engineering, and others including Flaminio Bertoni and Walter Becchia shaped its body and engine. When Germany invaded France during World War II, Citroën staff hid the prototypes from the occupying forces; some were buried or destroyed so they could not be used.","The car finally appeared at the Paris Motor Show on 7 October 1948. It was the Citroën 2CV, short for deux chevaux, two horses, which referred to its tiny rating in the French tax system. Its first engine was a 375 cc air-cooled twin of about 9 horsepower. Its soft, bouncy suspension let it lean dramatically in corners, and the roof was a roll-back canvas sheet. Critics laughed, but buyers queued for years. Production lasted until 1990, and more than 3.8 million 2CVs were built.","In Italy, Fiat engineer Dante Giacosa designed the Nuova 500, launched in July 1957. It was under 3 metres long, about 2.97 m, with a 479 cc two-cylinder air-cooled engine at the back. Italians nicknamed it the Cinquecento, meaning five hundred. It squeezed families into narrow medieval streets and parked in gaps where bigger cars could not fit. Nearly 3.9 million were made before production ended in 1975.","Britain's most famous small car came out of a fuel crisis. In 1956 the Suez Crisis disrupted oil supplies, and Britain brought in petrol rationing. Tiny German bubble cars suddenly sold well. The British Motor Corporation asked its designer Alec Issigonis to create a proper small car that used little fuel. His answer, announced on 26 August 1959, was the Mini.","Issigonis's trick was to turn the engine sideways. Instead of pointing front to back, the four-cylinder engine sat across the car, a layout called transverse, with the gearbox tucked underneath it in the oil sump, driving the front wheels. With no long engine in front and no driveshaft running to the back, about 80 percent of the floor space could be used for people and luggage, even though the car was only about 3.05 metres long. Tiny 10-inch wheels sat right at the corners. The Mini went around corners like a go-kart, and sporty Mini Coopers won the famous Monte Carlo Rally in 1964, 1965 and 1967. About 5.4 million original Minis were built before production ended in 2000. Today, most small family cars in the world use the same idea of a sideways engine driving the front wheels."]},{"heading":"The Beetle: A People's Car with a Dark Beginning","paragraphs":["The most famous small car of all has a complicated story, and it is worth telling honestly. In the 1930s, Germany was ruled by the Nazi government led by Adolf Hitler. The government wanted a cheap Volkswagen, which simply means people's car in German, to win popularity with ordinary Germans, and it hired the engineer Ferdinand Porsche, whom you met in Part 7, to develop it. Hitler did not design the car. Porsche and his engineering team did, and historians note that other designers in Europe had been sketching similar small, rounded, rear-engined cars before them, so exactly who deserves credit for which idea is still debated.","The design was unusual. The engine sat at the back, not the front. It was a flat-four, meaning four cylinders lying flat, two on each side, and it was cooled by air blown over metal fins instead of by water and a radiator, so there was no water to freeze in winter or boil over in summer. The body had a rounded shape that helped it slip through the air, and the car was built to cruise steadily on Germany's new motorways.","Ordinary Germans were encouraged to pay into a savings scheme, a few marks every week, toward their own car. A huge new factory was built from 1938 near the village of Fallersleben, beside a new town later named Wolfsburg. But World War II began in 1939, and the factory switched to building military vehicles, such as the Kübelwagen, an army version of the car, instead. More than 330,000 people who had paid into the savings scheme never received a car. During the war, as in many German factories, much of the work was done by forced labourers and prisoners brought from occupied countries, a painful chapter that Volkswagen has since acknowledged and studied.","By the end of the war in 1945 the factory was heavily damaged by bombing, and it lay in the zone of Germany controlled by the British army. It might easily have been taken apart. Instead, a young British officer named Major Ivan Hirst, an engineer in the Royal Electrical and Mechanical Engineers, arrived in the summer of 1945 and saw that much of the machinery had survived. The occupying forces badly needed vehicles, so Hirst persuaded the British military to order 20,000 cars from the factory. Working with German staff in a half-ruined building, often short of parts and materials, the factory was producing about 1,000 cars a month by 1946.","British carmakers who inspected the car at the time were not impressed and thought it had no future. They were spectacularly wrong. In 1948 Heinrich Nordhoff, a German manager, took charge, and in 1949 control passed to the new West German government. Volkswagen began exporting all over the world. In the United States, an advertising campaign from 1959 with the slogan Think Small turned the car's tiny size into a joke that made people like it more. People everywhere called it the Beetle, the Bug, the Käfer in German, or the Fusca in Brazil.","The Beetle kept its basic shape for decades while being improved underneath. On 17 February 1972, it passed the Ford Model T's record of about 15 million cars to become the most-produced car model in the world up to that time. Production moved between factories in Germany, Brazil, Mexico and elsewhere. The very last original Beetle rolled off the line in Puebla, Mexico, on 30 July 2003. In total, about 21.5 million were made, a car born in one of the darkest periods of the twentieth century that ended up becoming a friendly symbol of peace and fun."]},{"heading":"Car Factories Go to War","paragraphs":["During World War II, car factories in many countries were turned to military work, because they were the best places on Earth for making complicated machines in huge numbers. In the United States, which joined the war at the end of 1941, the government ordered carmakers to stop building cars for ordinary customers in early 1942. For more than three years, almost no new family cars were built in America.","Instead, the assembly lines built trucks, tanks, aircraft engines, guns and shells. General Motors, Ford and Chrysler became some of the biggest military suppliers in the country. Chrysler ran an enormous tank factory near Detroit. Other companies built engines for fighter planes. The small, tough Jeep, built by Willys-Overland and by Ford, became one of the war's best-known vehicles.","The most dramatic example was Ford's Willow Run plant near Ypsilanti, Michigan. It was designed by Albert Kahn, the same architect who had designed Highland Park, and Charles Sorensen led the effort to plan it. Willow Run was gigantic, with roughly 3.5 million square feet of floor space, more than 300,000 square metres, and at its peak about 42,000 people worked there. Its job was to build the B-24 Liberator, a big four-engine bomber, far more complicated than any car.","At first, Willow Run struggled. Airplanes were far more complicated than cars, and the designs kept changing. Critics joked about it. But by 1944 the plant was finishing a bomber roughly every hour, about one every 63 minutes at its best, and in a single month it produced more than 400. By the end of the war Ford had built about 7,000 complete B-24s there and supplied kits for nearly 1,900 more. Willow Run became a symbol of how mass-production ideas from the Model T era could be applied to almost anything.","When the war ended in 1945, the factories switched back to cars, and Americans who had waited years for a new car rushed to buy one. In Europe and Japan, by contrast, many factories had been damaged or destroyed. Rebuilding them would take years, and in Japan it would lead to a very different way of building cars."]},{"heading":"Toyota and the Smarter Factory","paragraphs":["Toyota's story begins not with cars but with weaving looms, the machines that turn thread into cloth. Sakichi Toyoda was a Japanese inventor who designed clever automatic looms, including one that stopped by itself when a thread broke, so that faulty cloth was not produced and one worker could watch many machines. His son, Kiichiro Toyoda, was an engineer who dreamed of building cars.","In 1933 Kiichiro set up an automobile department inside the family's loom company. His team studied American and European cars, taking them apart to learn how they worked, and completed their first passenger car prototype, the Model A1, in May 1935. In 1937 the car business became a separate company, the Toyota Motor Co. The family name is Toyoda, but the company chose the spelling Toyota, reportedly partly because it was considered luckier and sounded clearer.","Japan after World War II was poor, and Toyota nearly went bankrupt around 1950. Kiichiro Toyoda resigned as president that year during a labour dispute, and he died in 1952. Toyota could not copy Ford's approach of building one model in vast numbers with mountains of spare parts piled up in warehouses. It did not have the money, the space or the customers. It needed to build many different vehicles, in smaller numbers, without wasting anything.","The engineer who worked out how was Taiichi Ohno, together with Eiji Toyoda, a cousin of Kiichiro. Over about three decades, from the late 1940s to the 1970s, they developed what became known as the Toyota Production System. One of its two main ideas was just-in-time: making only what is needed, only when it is needed, and only in the amount needed. Ohno was inspired partly by American supermarkets, where shelves are refilled only as customers take things away. In a Toyota factory, each station takes the parts it needs from the station before it, and that station then makes just enough to replace them. Simple cards called kanban travel with the parts to signal what to make next.","The other main idea came straight from Sakichi's looms. It is called jidoka, often translated as automation with a human touch. If something goes wrong, the line stops so the problem can be found and fixed at once, instead of letting faulty cars pile up at the end of the line to be repaired later. Workers could signal a problem, and a lighted board called an andon showed supervisors exactly where help was needed. In Ford's old system, stopping the line was almost unthinkable. In Toyota's, it was part of the job.","Toyota also encouraged workers to suggest small improvements every day, a habit called kaizen, which means continuous improvement. Over time, these ideas made Toyota's cars famous for reliability. Researchers in the West later studied the system in detail and gave it the name lean manufacturing, and today hospitals, offices and software companies borrow its ideas.","In November 1966 Toyota launched a small family car called the Corolla, with a 1.1-litre engine. It was not flashy. It was simply comfortable, affordable and very hard to break. When the oil crisis of 1973, which you will read about in Part 17, made fuel expensive, small, efficient and reliable Japanese cars like the Corolla won buyers all over the world. In 1997 the Corolla passed the Beetle's total, and in 2021 Toyota announced that more than 50 million Corollas had been sold across twelve generations. That makes it the best-selling car name in history."]},{"heading":"A World Full of Carmakers","paragraphs":["Look back over this part and you can see a pattern. Ford showed that a car could be cheap if you built one design in enormous numbers. General Motors showed that people also wanted choice, style and easy payments. European designers showed that clever engineering could fit a family into a very small, thrifty car. Volkswagen showed that a simple design, improved patiently, could last for generations. And Toyota showed that a factory could be fast, flexible and careful about quality all at once.","By the 1980s, cars were being built on every inhabited continent. Germany, France, Italy, Britain, Sweden, Japan and the United States had big car industries, and countries such as South Korea, Brazil and Mexico were building more and more. Factories that once made a few hundred cars a year now made hundreds of thousands. The number of cars on the world's roads climbed into the hundreds of millions.","The competitors also learned from each other. Ford eventually adopted yearly models, a range of brands and credit for buyers. General Motors and other Western companies later studied Toyota's methods closely, and some even ran joint factories with Toyota to learn them first-hand. Japanese and European firms, in turn, borrowed American marketing and styling ideas. No single company or country had all the answers; the modern car industry grew out of this constant copying, competing and improving, just as the car itself had been built from the ideas of many inventors.","Every one of those cars needed somewhere to go. Each one wanted a smooth surface, signposts, rules about who goes first, places to fill up with fuel and places to park. So with millions of new cars pouring out of factories every year, a big question loomed: where on earth were they all going to drive?"]}],"deepDive":"Business historians often present the Ford–GM rivalry of the 1920s as a contest between two philosophies. Ford's strategy was cost leadership through a single standardised product: highly specialised machinery drove the cost of a Model T down, but made change expensive. Sloan's GM used a multidivisional structure, in which brands such as Chevrolet and Cadillac ran their own operations while a central office set financial targets and coordinated the price ladder. This model, analysed by the historian Alfred Chandler, became a template for large corporations in many industries. Yearly model changes and consumer credit are still debated: they made cars accessible and fuelled innovation in styling and engineering, but they also encouraged debt and quick replacement of products that still worked. The Volkswagen story raises different questions about memory and responsibility. The car was promoted by a dictatorship, the savings scheme failed its subscribers, and the wartime factory relied on forced labour; yet the postwar company, revived under British supervision, became a central part of West Germany's economic recovery. Historians commissioned by Volkswagen in the 1990s documented the wartime forced labour in detail. Toyota's contribution is now one of the most studied topics in management. Researchers at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology compared car plants around the world in the 1980s and concluded that lean Japanese plants could build a car with far fewer labour-hours and fewer defects than many Western mass-production plants, which helped spread just-in-time and quality methods. Critics point out that just-in-time systems are vulnerable when supply chains break, as the global shortage of computer chips showed in 2021, when carmakers around the world had to halt production because a few small parts were missing. Numbers also need care: production totals for the Beetle, the Mini and other long-lived models vary slightly between sources, depending on whether foreign assembly and close variants are counted.","funFact":"Citroën's design brief for the 2CV asked that it be able to drive across a ploughed field carrying a basket of eggs without breaking any.","check":{"q":"Which car passed the Model T's production record in 1972?","a":"The Volkswagen Beetle","wrong":["The Mini","The Toyota Corolla","The Citroën 2CV"]},"sources":[{"title":"Alfred P. Sloan","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_P._Sloan"},{"title":"Volkswagen Beetle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volkswagen_Beetle"},{"title":"Ivan Hirst","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Hirst"},{"title":"Mini","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mini"},{"title":"Citroën 2CV","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citro%C3%ABn_2CV"},{"title":"Willow Run","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow_Run"},{"title":"Toyota Production System","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toyota_Production_System"},{"title":"Toyota Corolla","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toyota_Corolla"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/12-1.jpg","alt":"A cream-coloured 1967 Volkswagen Beetle on display at a classic car show.","file":"1967 VW Beetle (8207335760).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1967_VW_Beetle_(8207335760).jpg","author":"Thomas's Pics","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/12-2.jpg","alt":"621 AOK, the first 1959 Morris Mini-Minor off the production line, designed by Alec Issigonis.","file":"Morris Mini-Minor 1959 (621 AOK).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morris_Mini-Minor_1959_(621_AOK).jpg","author":"DeFacto","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/12-3.jpg","alt":"B-24 Liberator bombers lined up on the assembly line at Ford's Willow Run plant during World War II.","file":"Looking up one of the assembly lines at Ford's big Willow Run plant, where B-24E (Liberator) bombers are being made... - NARA - 196389.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Looking_up_one_of_the_assembly_lines_at_Ford%27s_big_Willow_Run_plant,_where_B-24E_(Liberator)_bombers_are_being_made..._-_NARA_-_196389.jpg","author":"Howard R. Hollem","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3844},{"n":13,"title":"Roads, Rules and Traffic Lights","when":"1820s – 1970s","summary":"Early cars bumped along muddy, dusty roads built for horses. People learned to glue roads together with tar, built highways right across whole countries, and invented traffic lights, stop signs, driving tests and number plates to keep everyone safe. They even had to invent the gas station!","sections":[{"heading":"Mud, Dust and Mr McAdam","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part, millions of new cars were rolling out of factories. But a car is only as good as the road under its wheels, and in 1900 most roads were terrible. In the countryside of Europe and America, a typical road was simply a strip of packed earth. In dry weather it was rutted and bumpy. After rain it turned into a sticky brown soup that could swallow a wheel up to the axle. Horses could plod through mud. Early cars, with their thin tires and weak engines, often could not.","The best roads of the time owed a great deal to a Scottish engineer born almost a hundred years before the first car. John Loudon McAdam (1756–1836) had made money in business in New York as a young man, then returned to Scotland and became a trustee of a turnpike, a road whose users paid a toll to keep it in repair. He was fascinated, and annoyed, by how badly roads were built. In 1816 he became surveyor of roads for the turnpike trust around Bristol in England, in charge of about 149 miles of road, and he started testing his ideas for real.","McAdam's idea was surprisingly simple. Earlier engineers, like the Romans, had built roads on thick, expensive foundations of big stones. McAdam said you did not need that. The natural soil could carry the weight, as long as you kept it dry. So he raised the road a little above the surrounding ground and gave it a gentle hump in the middle, called a camber, so that rainwater ran off into ditches at the sides. On a road 30 feet (about 9 metres) wide, the middle stood only about 3 inches (7.5 cm) higher than the edges.","On top of the soil went layers of broken stone. The lower layer was about 20 cm thick, made of stones up to about 7.5 cm across. The top layer was thinner, about 5 cm, made of small stones about 2 cm across. Size really mattered. McAdam's workers broke stones by hand with hammers, and supervisors were told to check that no stone weighed more than about 6 ounces (170 grams). A popular saying, often linked to McAdam, was that any stone too big to fit in your mouth was too big for the road. The sharp, angular pieces locked together like a jigsaw as wheels rolled over them, making a hard, smooth crust.","From about 1820 his method spread quickly, and his name became a word: a road built this way was 'macadamised', or simply 'macadam'. In 1823 the first macadam road in the United States was finished in Maryland, on a 10-mile stretch between Hagerstown and Boonsboro. Before it was rebuilt, a winter stagecoach could take five to seven hours to cover those 10 miles. That gives you an idea of how slow travel on bad roads could be.","Macadam worked brilliantly for horses and iron-rimmed carriage wheels. Then the car arrived, and something unexpected happened. A fast-moving rubber tire creates a little pocket of low air pressure behind it, and this sucks the fine dust out from between the stones. Every car that sped along a macadam road dragged a huge cloud of dust behind it. People living by the road had dust in their houses and lungs. Worse still, as the dust was pulled away, the stones lost their grip on each other and the road slowly fell apart.","The answer was to glue the stones together. In 1902 a Swiss doctor, Ernest Guglielminetti, suggested spraying tar from the gasworks in Monaco onto the roads to hold the dust down. Around the same time a Welsh-born engineer, Edgar Purnell Hooley (1860–1942), was county surveyor for Nottinghamshire in England. According to the story he later told, in 1901 he walked past a tar works and noticed a spot where a barrel of tar had spilled and someone had thrown gravel over the mess to tidy it up. That patch was smooth, firm and free of dust, while the road around it was not.","Hooley worked out a recipe: heat tar, mix it with crushed stone and slag (a stony waste left over from making iron), spread it, and roll it flat. He applied for a patent in 1902 and set up a company in 1903 called Tar Macadam (Purnell Hooley's Patent) Syndicate Limited. He was a better engineer than businessman and struggled to sell it. In 1905 an ironworks owner and Member of Parliament, Sir Alfred Hickman, took over the business and relaunched it under a short, catchy name: Tarmac. The company still exists today, and many people in Britain call any black road surface 'tarmac'.","Other surfaces joined in. Asphalt, a sticky black material made from the heavy leftovers of crude oil, was mixed with sand and stone to make a smooth, quiet road, and modern mixed asphalt paving became common in the 1920s. Concrete was harder and lasted longer, and in America it became a favourite for main highways. Most roads today are still asphalt or concrete, laid on carefully drained layers of crushed stone, just as McAdam taught."]},{"heading":"Coast to Coast: The Lincoln Highway and Route 66","paragraphs":["Smooth town streets were one thing. Crossing a whole continent was another. In 1912 the United States had no proper road from coast to coast. Beyond the big eastern cities, drivers found a maze of farm tracks, river crossings and desert trails, and often navigated by following telephone poles.","Carl G. Fisher (1874–1939) wanted to change that. Fisher was an energetic businessman from Indiana who had made a fortune selling car headlights and had helped build the Indianapolis Motor Speedway. In September 1912 he proposed a 'coast-to-coast rock highway', an improved road all the way from New York to San Francisco, and he hoped it would be ready by May 1915 for a big world's fair in San Francisco. He guessed it would cost about 10 million dollars and asked car companies and drivers to donate. Within a month friends had promised a million.","The Lincoln Highway Association was founded on 1 July 1913, with Henry B. Joy, president of the Packard car company, as its leader, and naming the road after Abraham Lincoln gave it a patriotic feel. The route ran from Times Square in New York City to Lincoln Park in San Francisco, through 13 states, and its first official length was 3,389 miles (5,454 km).","At the start, the Lincoln Highway was more of an idea than a road. Less than half of it was improved. The association's own scouting trip in 1913 took 34 days and met mud, sand drifts, overheated engines, floods and broken axles. So the association came up with a clever trick to persuade people. From 1914 it paid for 'seedling miles', short demonstration stretches of smooth concrete road in the middle of muddy countryside. The first was finished in November 1914 near Malta, Illinois. Once local drivers had felt how wonderful a mile of concrete was, the idea went, they would demand that the rest of the road be paved too. It worked: towns and states gradually improved their sections, and red, white and blue stripes painted on poles marked the route.","In 1926 the United States brought order to its growing jumble of named trails by giving main roads numbers instead. East–west routes got even numbers and north–south routes got odd numbers. Much of the Lincoln Highway became US 30. And on 11 November 1926 a brand-new number was born: US Route 66.","Route 66 ran about 2,448 miles (3,940 km) from Chicago, Illinois, to Santa Monica, California, through eight states. Unlike most highways, it did not run straight; it curved diagonally across the country, linking many small towns in Missouri, Kansas, Oklahoma, Texas, New Mexico and Arizona. A promoter from Oklahoma named Cyrus Avery pushed hard for it. In 1938 it became the first US highway to be completely paved from end to end.","Route 66 became famous for the people who travelled it. In the 1930s, during the Great Depression and the terrible dust storms of the 'Dust Bowl', tens of thousands of poor farming families from Oklahoma and nearby states drove west on it, hoping for work in California. The writer John Steinbeck called it 'the mother road' in his 1939 novel The Grapes of Wrath. After World War II it became a road of holidays and adventure, lined with diners, motels and gas stations with bright neon signs, as you will see in Part 14."]},{"heading":"Sixty-Two Days in the Mud: Eisenhower's Convoy","paragraphs":["Imagine it is the summer of 1919 and World War I has just ended. The US Army wants to know: could it move soldiers and supplies across America by truck? So it organises a test, a long line of military vehicles called a convoy, from Washington, D.C., to San Francisco.","On 7 July 1919 the convoy set off. It was huge: 81 vehicles and trailers, including many heavy cargo trucks, stretched out in a long line along the road. About 24 expeditionary officers, 15 observers from the War Department and 258 enlisted soldiers went along. One of those observers was a 28-year-old lieutenant colonel named Dwight D. Eisenhower, who joined the convoy at its first night's stop in Frederick, Maryland. He later said he went 'partly for a lark, and partly to learn.'","He learned a lot. For much of the way the convoy followed the Lincoln Highway, and the farther west it went, the worse things got. In Nebraska, Wyoming and Utah the roads were often mud or sand. Trucks sank and had to be dragged out with winches and ropes. The convoy broke dozens of old wooden bridges, 14 of them in Wyoming alone, and soldiers had to repair them before the trucks could cross. In the Utah and Nevada desert, the 'road' was sometimes little more than a track. Altogether the convoy stopped about 230 times for breakdowns, repairs, accidents and stuck vehicles.","The planners had hoped the convoy would move much faster. In fact it managed only about 5.65 mph (about 9 km/h), roughly the speed of a fast jog, over more than 3,200 miles. The trip took 62 days: 56 days of travel and six days of rest. The convoy rolled into Oakland, California, on 5 September 1919, a few days behind schedule, and crossed the bay by ferry into San Francisco the next morning, where crowds cheered.","In his official report, Eisenhower wrote plainly that long truck journeys across the middle of the country were 'impracticable until roads are improved.' He never forgot the experience. Twenty-five years later, as the top Allied commander in Europe during World War II, he saw something very different in defeated Germany: wide, fast motorways called autobahns. Those two memories, the muddy convoy and the smooth autobahn, would one day help shape the biggest road-building project in history. As he later put it, 'Germany had made me see the wisdom of broader ribbons across the land.'"]},{"heading":"Roads Only for Cars: Autobahns and Interstates","paragraphs":["Even a well-paved road had a problem once cars became common. It was shared by everyone: walkers, cyclists, horses, farm carts, children and cows, with crossroads and driveways every few metres. A fast car could not safely go fast. Engineers began to dream of a completely new kind of road: one only for motor vehicles, with no crossroads at the same level, separate lanes for each direction, and gentle curves designed for speed. Today we call these motorways, freeways or expressways.","One early experiment was the AVUS in Berlin, a 10 km road with separate carriageways, begun in 1913 and finally opened in 1921 as a racetrack and testing road. In 1926 a German group called HAFRABA began planning a motorway from Hamburg through Frankfurt to Basel in Switzerland. Its detailed plans filled 70 volumes, but it could not get the money or political support to build it.","Germany's first true section of road of this kind ran between the cities of Cologne and Bonn. It was about 18 to 20 km long, built from 1929 to 1932 partly to give work to unemployed men during a terrible economic crisis, and it was strongly supported by Cologne's mayor, Konrad Adenauer. It opened to traffic on 6 August 1932, a few months before the Nazis came to power. (Officially it was classed as a country road at first, and only got full autobahn status in 1958.)","After Adolf Hitler took power in 1933, the Nazi government took over the motorway idea and pretended it had been Hitler's own. A huge national network, the Reichsautobahn, was announced, and Hitler was filmed and photographed turning the first spade of earth near Frankfurt in September 1933. Those pictures were used endlessly in propaganda, the use of news, films and posters to persuade people, to make the regime look modern and caring. The reality was harsher. Workers were paid little, worked 11 to 12 hours a day, and suffered many accidents, and as time went on, prisoners and forced labourers were made to work on the roads. The first completed stretch, Frankfurt to Darmstadt, opened in May 1935. About 3,000 km were finished by the end of 1938, and roughly 3,800 km when work stopped in 1941. Germany had few private cars at the time, so many of these roads were nearly empty.","Now go back to Eisenhower. In 1953 he became President of the United States. America already had a few modern toll roads, such as the Pennsylvania Turnpike, opened in 1940, but there was no national network, and traffic jams and crashes were growing every year. Eisenhower pushed for a giant plan, and on 29 June 1956 he signed the Federal-Aid Highway Act. It authorised about 41,000 miles (66,000 km) of new high-speed roads, called the Interstate Highway System, to be built over roughly ten years. The first estimate of the cost was 25 billion dollars.","How do you pay for that? The law created a Highway Trust Fund, filled by taxes on gasoline, tires and new vehicles. The federal government paid 90 percent of the building costs and each state paid 10 percent, so every tank of fuel helped build the roads it was burned on.","The Interstates had no traffic lights and no level crossroads: you joined and left them only by ramps. Even the numbers followed a pattern. North–south routes got odd numbers, low in the west (I-5 on the Pacific coast) and rising towards the east (I-95 on the Atlantic). East–west routes got even numbers, low in the south (I-10) and higher in the north (I-90).","Building took much longer than ten years. The system was officially declared complete on 14 October 1992, when the last planned section of I-70 opened through the steep Glenwood Canyon in Colorado. Today the Interstate system is nearly 49,000 miles (roughly 78,700 km) long. It is a tiny fraction of America's roads, yet about a quarter of all the miles Americans drive happen on it. As Part 14 will show, though, these mighty roads also cut through cities and neighbourhoods, and not everyone was happy about where they went."]},{"heading":"Red, Amber, Green: The Story of Traffic Lights","paragraphs":["Traffic lights are older than cars. The very first one was built for horses. In the 1860s the streets around the Houses of Parliament in London were jammed with carriages, carts and people on foot, and crossing the road was dangerous. A railway engineer named John Peake Knight (1828–1886) had an idea. Railways already used signals with moving arms, called semaphores, to tell train drivers to stop or go. Why not use one on the street?","On 9 December 1868 Knight's signal was put up near Westminster Bridge, at the corner of Great George Street and Bridge Street, right outside Parliament. It was a tall pillar with arms that a police constable raised or lowered by hand. Arms straight out meant stop; arms lowered meant drivers could go with care. At night, when the arms were hard to see, the constable used a lever to show a red or green lamp, lit by gas.","For a few weeks it worked. Then, on 2 January 1869, a leak of gas caused one of the lamps to explode, injuring the policeman who was operating it. The signal was taken away, and London did not try the idea again for decades.","The electric light bulb made safe traffic signals possible. In 1912 a police officer in Salt Lake City, Utah, named Lester Wire built an electric traffic light, often described as the first. Two years later, in August 1914, the American Traffic Signal Company installed an electric signal with red and green lights at the corner of East 105th Street and Euclid Avenue in Cleveland, Ohio. It is often called the first electric traffic signal put to regular use on a public street.","There was still something missing. With only red and green, a light could suddenly switch from go to stop and give drivers no warning. In 1920 a Detroit police officer, William Potts, designed a signal with three colours for a four-way crossing, adding an amber (yellow) light between red and green to mean 'caution, the light is about to change.' In 1921 Detroit put up Potts's automatic four-way signals in 15 towers around the city. Soon automatic timers replaced many of the police officers who had worked signals by hand.","Garrett Morgan (1877–1963) is often named as the inventor of the traffic light, but that is not quite right. Morgan was a remarkable African American inventor and businessman in Cleveland. According to his own account, he saw a crash between a car and a horse-drawn carriage, and decided crossings needed a better signal. In 1923 he received US patent number 1,475,024 for a traffic signal: a hand-cranked pole with arms showing 'stop' and 'go', and an extra position that could stop traffic in every direction at once so that people could cross safely, rather like the 'all-red' moment many traffic lights still use. He later sold the rights to General Electric for 40,000 dollars. His signal was an important improvement, but earlier signals existed from 1868 and electric ones from 1914."]},{"heading":"Rules of the Road","paragraphs":["Once cars were common, everyone needed to follow the same rules. Some of the earliest rules were made in Britain, which, as Part 2 told, had very strict speed limits for road locomotives. In January 1896 those limits were still in force, and they were painfully slow: 2 mph in towns.","On 28 January 1896 a man named Walter Arnold drove his Benz car through East Peckham, a village in Kent, in southeast England. He was going about 8 mph (13 km/h), roughly four times the town limit and about as fast as a person can jog. A policeman chased him down, reportedly on a bicycle, and Arnold was taken to court. He was fined one shilling, plus costs. He is believed to be the first person ever convicted of speeding. Later that same year the law changed and the speed limit rose to 14 mph, and in 1903 a new Motor Car Act raised it to 20 mph.","Driving licences began with the very first cars. In 1888, after people in the town complained about the noise and smell of his Motorwagen, Karl Benz received written permission from the authorities of the Grand Duchy of Baden to drive on public roads. Britain made driving licences compulsory from 1 January 1904, with a minimum age of 17, but you did not have to pass a test: you simply applied. Compulsory driving tests in Britain only came with the Road Traffic Act of 1934. In the United States, New York required licences for professional drivers (chauffeurs) from 1910, and in 1913 New Jersey became the first state to make every driver pass an exam.","Number plates, or licence plates, let the police identify a car. France led the way: an order from the Paris police on 14 August 1893 is usually counted as the start of registration plates. Germany followed in 1896 and the Netherlands created the first national plates in 1898, numbered simply 1, 2, 3 and so on. In New York, from 1901, car owners had to show their initials on the back of the car, and many motorists made their own plates from leather or metal. Massachusetts became the first American state to hand out official plates, in 1903.","Road signs had to be invented too. In 1914 or 1915 a Detroit police sergeant named Harry Jackson cut the corners off a square board and painted the word STOP on it, and Michigan began using stop signs in 1915. The first ones were black letters on white, about 60 cm (24 inches) square. In 1922 American highway officials picked the eight-sided octagon shape, so drivers could recognise a stop sign even from behind or when it was covered in snow. Strangely, for years stop signs were yellow, because red paint that did not fade in sunlight was hard to make. Only in 1954 did the familiar white letters on red become the American standard, matching the red of traffic lights. Many countries agreed on similar shapes and colours at the Vienna Convention on Road Signs and Signals in 1968.","Then there is the biggest rule of all: which side of the road do you drive on? Nobody knows for sure why some places chose left and others right; popular stories about sword arms or Napoleon have no solid evidence behind them. A London law of 1756 told carriages on London Bridge to keep left, and Britain stayed on the left. Massachusetts made driving on the right law in 1821, and when Ford's Model T put the steering wheel on the left in 1908, other American carmakers soon followed. Today about 75 countries and territories, home to about a third of the world's people, drive on the left. Sweden switched from left to right on Sunday 3 September 1967, a day called Dagen H, when every driver stopped, carefully crossed to the other side and set off again."]},{"heading":"Fill Her Up! The First Filling Stations","paragraphs":["The last thing roads needed was fuel. In Part 5, Bertha Benz bought her fuel, a cleaning liquid called ligroin, from a pharmacy in Wiesloch in 1888. For years after that, drivers bought petrol in cans from pharmacies, hardware stores, bicycle shops and blacksmiths, and poured it into the tank through a funnel. It was messy, slow and dangerous: spilled fuel could easily catch fire.","The answer was a building made just for selling fuel. In 1905 one of the earliest purpose-built filling stations opened in St. Louis, Missouri, at 420 South Theresa Avenue. In 1907 the Standard Oil Company of California opened one in Seattle. Early stations were often just a shed, a storage tank and a hose, with an attendant who filled your car for you.","Soon stations became welcoming places where drivers could pull in off the street. Standard Oil of Ohio opened a drive-in station in Columbus in 1912. In December 1913 the Gulf Refining Company opened a smart brick station in Pittsburgh, designed by an architect. It sold fuel and oil, offered air for tires, had restrooms, and handed out free road maps, which became a tradition at American gas stations for decades. Other countries followed: Britain's first, run by the Automobile Association, opened at Aldermaston in 1919. Self-service, where drivers fill up their own cars, did not become common in the United States until after a station in Los Angeles tried it in 1947.","So much had to be invented besides the car itself: smooth surfaces, long highways, motorways, traffic lights, licences, number plates, stop signs and filling stations. By the 1960s and 1970s, shiny new highways stretched to the horizon. Families began loading suitcases, picnic baskets and children into the back seat to see where those roads would take them, and, as the next part shows, the car was about to change not just how people travelled, but where and how they lived."]}],"deepDive":"Road-building is a story about money and who pays. McAdam's roads were paid for by turnpike tolls, which is why early motorists and cyclists in Britain and America campaigned for public road funding in the 'Good Roads' movement. The US took a big step with the Federal Aid Road Act of 1916, which offered federal money to states that set up highway departments, and an even bigger one in 1956, when the Highway Trust Fund turned fuel tax into a dedicated road budget. The 90/10 split meant states could build huge projects for a small share of the cost, which strongly encouraged building, sometimes faster than communities could debate where roads should go. Historians still argue about how much Germany's autobahns inspired the Interstates. Eisenhower himself pointed to both his 1919 convoy and the autobahns, but American engineers had been planning toll-free 'interregional highways' since the late 1930s, and the 1956 law grew out of reports written before he took office. The Nazi myth that the autobahn was Hitler's idea has also been taken apart: the HAFRABA plans and the Cologne–Bonn road came first, and the regime's claims that autobahns ended unemployment were exaggerated, since direct employment peaked at about 125,000 workers in 1936. Traffic lights raise their own engineering questions. A simple fixed-time signal wastes time when roads are empty, so engineers added detectors buried in the road, then computers that coordinate whole corridors so cars meet a 'green wave'. Modern signals use sensors and cameras to adapt timing every few seconds. Even the colours involve physics and psychology: red has the longest wavelength of visible light, scatters least in fog and was already the railway signal for danger, so it was a natural choice for 'stop'. Today's debate is about who roads are for. Many cities are reconsidering wide urban highways, and some, like Syracuse in New York, plan to replace elevated freeways with street-level boulevards.","funFact":"For about 30 years, from 1924 to 1954, American stop signs were yellow, because red paint that would not fade in the sun was hard to make.","check":{"q":"Who was the first person known to be convicted of speeding, and how fast was he going?","a":"Walter Arnold, about 8 mph, in Kent, England, in 1896","wrong":["Henry Ford, about 60 mph, in Detroit in 1908","Bertha Benz, about 30 mph, in Germany in 1888","John Peake Knight, about 20 mph, in London in 1868"]},"sources":[{"title":"Macadam","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macadam"},{"title":"Lincoln Highway","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Highway"},{"title":"1919 Motor Transport Corps convoy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1919_Motor_Transport_Corps_convoy"},{"title":"Eisenhower's report on the 1919 convoy","publisher":"US Federal Highway Administration","url":"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/infrastructure/convoy.cfm"},{"title":"Reichsautobahn","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reichsautobahn"},{"title":"Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal-Aid_Highway_Act_of_1956"},{"title":"Traffic light","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traffic_light"},{"title":"Stop sign","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stop_sign"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/13-1.jpg","alt":"Garrett Morgan's 1923 patent drawing for a traffic signal with STOP and GO arms.","file":"US1475024-drawings-page-1.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US1475024-drawings-page-1.png","author":"Garrett Morgan","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/13-2.jpg","alt":"Army trucks of the 1919 Motor Transport Corps convoy crawl along a rough dirt road.","file":"Militor truck in 1919 Motor Transport Corps convoy.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Militor_truck_in_1919_Motor_Transport_Corps_convoy.jpg","author":"Department of Defense. Department of the Army. Office of the Chief Signal…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/13-3.jpg","alt":"An iron Lincoln Highway guide board in Morrisville, Pennsylvania, marking a guide post adopted in 1917.","file":"2014-05-12 12 20 28 Lincoln Highway post at the western end of the Calhoun Street Bridge in Morrisville, Pennsylvania.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014-05-12_12_20_28_Lincoln_Highway_post_at_the_western_end_of_the_Calhoun_Street_Bridge_in_Morrisville,_Pennsylvania.JPG","author":"Famartin","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4299},{"n":14,"title":"How the Car Changed Everyday Life","when":"1900s – 1970s","summary":"A hundred years ago, city streets were full of horses and very smelly. Cars cleaned up the streets, let families live in new suburbs, and invented whole new places like motels, drive-in movies and shopping malls. Cars gave many people freedom, but highways also cut through neighbourhoods, and not everyone could travel safely.","sections":[{"heading":"When Cities Ran on Horses","paragraphs":["Close your eyes and imagine walking down a busy street in New York, London or Chicago in about 1890. You hear the clatter of iron horseshoes and wooden wheels on stone. Horse-drawn buses, delivery wagons, cabs, brewers' carts and fire engines crowd the road. And you smell it before you see it: horses, everywhere, and everything horses leave behind.","Big cities around 1900 were home to tens of thousands of working horses, and the largest had many more. They pulled almost everything. They also produced an astonishing amount of waste. A working horse makes roughly 7 to 15 kg of manure a day, plus litres of urine. In New York City, estimates from the 1890s suggest horses left about 2.5 million pounds (more than 1,100 tonnes) of manure and about 60,000 gallons of urine on the streets every single day.","In dry weather the manure was ground into dust by wheels and blown into people's eyes, homes and food. In wet weather it became a slippery, stinking slush. Huge piles collected in vacant lots and outside stables, and they bred enormous numbers of flies, which carried germs to kitchens and markets. Doctors at the time worried that this filth helped spread diseases. When horses were worked to exhaustion or fell ill, they sometimes died right in the street, and their bodies could lie there until someone came to haul them away to be turned into glue and fertiliser.","Horses also caused traffic jams and accidents. A frightened horse could bolt, dragging a heavy wagon through a crowd, and many people were hurt by kicks, falls and runaway carts. Feeding and stabling all those animals was expensive too: horses needed hay and oats every day, whether they worked or not, and city stables took up a lot of valuable land.","New York tried hard to clean up. In 1895 the city's new street-cleaning chief, George E. Waring Jr., set up a corps of workers in white uniforms who swept the streets every day with brooms and carts. He also got a law passed saying that horses and carts had to be kept in stables overnight rather than left in the street. Manure was sold as fertiliser and dead horses went to glue factories. The streets became dramatically cleaner, and the city even held a parade in 1896 to celebrate the sweepers.","You may have heard a famous story that in 1894 a London newspaper predicted the city would be buried under nine feet of manure by 1950. It is a great story, but historians have searched for that newspaper article and never found it, so it is best treated as a modern myth. The real problem was serious enough without exaggeration.","So when the first cars appeared, many people saw them as the clean, modern answer. A car did not leave manure, did not attract flies, did not need feeding when it was parked and could not bolt in fright. An Italian newspaper in 1896 even looked forward to cars removing manure from the streets. Nobody yet worried about what came out of the exhaust pipe; that story comes in Part 17. The change happened fast. In 1890 the United States had about 13,800 companies building horse-drawn carriages. By 1920 only about 90 were left. By 1929 there were about 23 million cars registered in the United States, and the horse had almost disappeared from city streets."]},{"heading":"Moving to the Suburbs","paragraphs":["Before cars, most city workers had to live close to their jobs. If you walked to work, you could live perhaps half an hour's walk away. In the late 1800s, horse-drawn streetcars and then electric streetcars let people move a bit farther out. Developers built neighbourhoods along the streetcar lines, called streetcar suburbs, with narrow plots, houses with front porches, and shops clustered near the stops. An electric streetcar could cover in about ten minutes a distance that took half an hour to walk. Driveways and garages were rare, because hardly anyone had a car.","The car changed the shape of cities. A family with a car no longer needed to live near a streetcar line or a railway station. They could live almost anywhere a road went. From the 1920s, and especially after World War II, new suburbs were designed around the car: wide curving streets, houses with garages and driveways, and homes spread out on bigger plots of land, often far from shops and offices.","After the war, millions of soldiers came home, married and started families, and they needed houses. The US government helped them borrow money cheaply through the GI Bill and government-backed mortgages. Builders bought farmland outside cities and put up whole new towns of nearly identical houses. The most famous was Levittown, on Long Island near New York. Between 1947 and 1951 the company Levitt and Sons built more than 17,000 homes there, using assembly-line methods borrowed from car factories: teams of workers moved from house to house, each doing one job. By July 1948 they were finishing about 30 houses a day.","Levittown also shows the unfair side of this story. Its early house rules said homes could be lived in only by white people, and Black families, including war veterans, were turned away. Many other suburbs and banks of the time also kept Black Americans out, through rules and practices that historians call housing discrimination. So the dream of a suburban home with a car in the driveway was not open to everyone.","Suburbs grew enormously. In the decade after the war the United States built more than a million new homes a year, several times the rate of the Depression years before. People who lived in the suburbs drove to work, drove to the shops, and drove their children to school and to friends' houses. A second car started to seem normal.","Shopping changed too. Instead of walking to a busy downtown street, suburban families drove to shopping centres surrounded by vast car parks. On 8 October 1956 Southdale Center opened in Edina, Minnesota, near Minneapolis. Designed by the architect Victor Gruen, it was the first fully enclosed, climate-controlled shopping mall in the United States, warm in the freezing Minnesota winter and cool in summer. Its plans included about 5,200 parking spaces. Hundreds of new shopping centres opened across the United States every year in the late 1950s. Downtown shops in many cities began to struggle as customers drove out to the malls."]},{"heading":"Motels, Drive-Ins and the Great Road Trip","paragraphs":["Cars did not only change where people lived. They created brand-new kinds of places that simply could not have existed before.","Take the motel. In the early days of motoring, a family driving a long way might camp by the road or stay in a grand hotel in the middle of a city, where you had to find somewhere to park and carry your bags through a fancy lobby. In California, an architect named Arthur Heineman had a better idea. He noticed that driving from Los Angeles to San Francisco took about two days, so travellers needed a place to stop halfway. In San Luis Obispo, roughly at the midpoint, he built a roadside inn where guests could park right outside their own room. It opened on 12 December 1925 as the Milestone Mo-Tel. The name joined 'motor' and 'hotel' together, and the word 'motel' was born.","The Milestone Mo-Tel looked like a Spanish mission, with white walls, red-tiled roofs and a bell tower. Every unit had its own bathroom with a shower, and a room cost 1.25 dollars a night. Thousands of motels followed along highways like Route 66, with bright neon signs, swimming pools and names promising comfort to tired drivers. Parts of the original building, including its bell tower, still stand.","Eating changed too. In 1921 Kirby's Pig Stand opened in Dallas, Texas, and is considered the first drive-in restaurant. Instead of going inside, customers parked, and waiters called carhops came out to take orders and bring food to the car, sometimes on trays that hooked onto the car window. Drive-in restaurants were especially popular in the 1950s and 1960s. Later, drive-through windows let people order and collect food without even parking, an idea that helped fast-food chains spread across the world.","Then there were movies under the stars. Richard Hollingshead, from a family that ran a chemical company in Camden, New Jersey, tested an idea in the driveway of his home in Riverton, New Jersey, in 1932: he put a film projector on the hood of his car and nailed a screen to trees in his garden. He received a patent for his drive-in theatre in May 1933, and on 6 June 1933 he opened the first one in Pennsauken, near Camden. It had space for about 400 cars and a screen about 12 by 15 metres. Families could watch from their own car seats, and his advertising promised that the whole family was welcome, however noisy the children were. The first film shown was a comedy called Wives Beware.","Hollingshead's own theatre did not make money, and he sold it after three years. But the idea took off. Drive-in theatres peaked in the late 1950s, when there were more than 4,000 of them in the United States.","Put all of these together, and you get one of the great inventions of the car age: the family road trip. Parents piled children, suitcases and picnic baskets into the car and set off to see national parks, beaches and famous sights, eating at roadside diners and sleeping in motels along the way. Travel that once needed train tickets and timetables could now begin whenever a family wanted, from their own front door."]},{"heading":"No More Lonely Farms","paragraphs":["In 1900 most Americans, and most people around the world, lived in the countryside. For a farm family, the nearest town might be 10 or 20 kilometres away along a dirt road. With a horse and wagon, a trip to town to buy supplies, sell eggs or visit the doctor could take most of a day, so families went only when they had to. Children often could not attend school beyond the small one-room schoolhouse within walking distance. In winter, when roads were deep in snow or mud, some families hardly saw anyone else for weeks.","Mail was the first link to the wider world. In the United States, the Post Office began Rural Free Delivery in 1896, starting with routes in Jefferson County, West Virginia. Instead of travelling to a distant post office, farm families could have letters, newspapers and catalogues brought to a mailbox at the end of their lane. In 1913 the Post Office added parcel post, so families could order clothes, tools and even seeds from mail-order companies like Sears, Roebuck and Company and have them delivered. By 1930, more than 43,000 rural routes served nearly 6.9 million families. Mail carriers were some of the first country people to switch from horses to motor vehicles, and the government's interest in good routes for the mail helped persuade Congress to pay for better rural roads, starting with a federal road law in 1916.","The car, especially the cheap and tough Model T, made the biggest difference. Suddenly a 20 km trip to town took less than an hour. Farmers could drive their produce to market and bring back supplies the same morning. Families could go to church, visit relatives, attend county fairs and go to the cinema. Young people from different farms could meet much more easily, and many found friends, and husbands and wives, from farther away than their parents ever could.","Farmers also found clever ways to use their cars as machines. With the rear wheels jacked up, a belt could be run from a wheel to a saw, a water pump or a butter churn, so the car's engine did farm work. Some companies sold kits to turn a Model T into a small tractor.","Health care changed too. A country doctor with a car could make house calls to many more patients in a day and could reach an emergency much faster. Sick or injured people could be driven to a hospital in town. Over the following decades, motor buses also made it possible to close many tiny one-room schools and carry children to larger schools with more teachers, more subjects and better equipment, although some communities were sad to lose their small local school."]},{"heading":"Freedom Behind the Wheel","paragraphs":["For many people, the car meant independence. Nobody showed that more clearly than women. You have already met Bertha Benz, who made the first long-distance car journey in 1888 at a time when, as a married woman, she was not allowed to hold her husband's patent and women could not study at university in her part of Germany. In 1909, as Part 10 told, Alice Ramsey became the first woman to drive across the United States.","By the 1920s, especially after the electric self-starter (Part 7) ended the need for heavy hand-cranking, millions of women were driving. Carmakers began advertising directly to them. A woman with a car could take a job farther from home, run a business, visit friends and family when she chose, or simply go for a drive on her own. In a time when many women were still expected to stay at home, that was a big change, and it came in the same years that women in the United States and Britain won the right to vote.","But freedom on the road was not shared equally. In the United States, from the late 1800s until the 1960s, many states and towns enforced segregation, laws and customs that separated Black and white Americans and treated Black people unfairly. For Black families, a road trip could be stressful and even dangerous. Many hotels, restaurants and gas stations refused to serve Black customers. Some places were known as 'sundown towns', where Black people were warned to leave before dark. Running out of fuel or needing a room for the night in the wrong place could put a family at risk.","Victor Hugo Green (1892–1960), a Black postal worker living in Harlem in New York City, decided to help. In 1936 he published a small guidebook listing businesses that welcomed Black travellers: hotels, guest houses, restaurants, petrol stations, barber shops and more. The first edition covered only the New York area. It was so useful that it grew year after year, eventually covering much of the United States and parts of Canada, Mexico, the Caribbean and Bermuda. It was called The Negro Motorist Green Book, and later simply The Green Book.","The guide was sold by mail and through churches and Black-owned businesses, and also at Esso gas stations, one of the few big companies that supported it. At its peak about 15,000 copies were printed each year. Many families kept one in the glove compartment for every trip. In its introduction, Green wrote that he hoped 'there will be a day sometime in the near future when this guide will not have to be published.'","That day came closer when the Civil Rights Act of 1964 made it illegal for businesses like hotels and restaurants to refuse customers because of their race. After Green died in 1960, Alma Green and the staff kept the guide going, and the last edition appeared in 1966–67. Published from 1936 to 1966, the Green Book is now remembered as both a clever, practical tool and a powerful reminder that the open road was not open to everyone."]},{"heading":"Streets for Cars, Not People","paragraphs":["As the number of cars grew, cities changed to make room for them, and not always for the better. Before cars, a city street was a shared space. Children played in it, sellers set up stalls, people stopped to chat, and walkers crossed wherever they liked. As fast cars filled the roads, the street became a dangerous place to be. In the 1920s, as cars knocked down many pedestrians, especially children, cities and car groups began telling walkers to stay on the pavement and cross only at marked crossings. People who crossed carelessly were mocked as 'jaywalkers'. Little by little, the road became a place for cars, and people were pushed to its edges.","Public transport suffered too. America's streetcar systems were huge in the early 1900s, with tens of thousands of miles of track, and streetcar ridership peaked around the early 1920s. After that, as more people bought cars, ridership fell almost every year. Many companies were already in financial trouble because fares were fixed by law while costs rose. During the 1930s and 1940s, most US cities replaced their streetcars with buses or let them close.","You may hear that General Motors secretly destroyed America's streetcars. The real story is more complicated. In 1949 General Motors, Firestone, Standard Oil of California and other companies were convicted of conspiring to monopolise the sale of buses and supplies to transit companies they had invested in, and GM was fined just 5,000 dollars. But most transport historians think streetcars were already declining for many reasons, especially the popularity of the car, rising costs and fixed fares, the Great Depression, and government road building, and that GM took advantage of a decline that was already happening.","The biggest scars came from highways. When the new Interstates of Part 13 reached cities in the 1950s and 1960s, planners often ran them straight through existing neighbourhoods, knocking down homes, shops and churches. Very often the neighbourhoods chosen were poorer ones, and many were Black communities, where people had less political power to fight back. Some neighbourhoods were split in two by huge concrete roads, cutting people off from their neighbours, schools and shops.","Not everyone accepted this. In the 1960s and 1970s, protests sometimes called 'freeway revolts' stopped or changed planned highways in cities such as San Francisco, Boston and New York. In New York, the writer and activist Jane Jacobs helped lead campaigns against a planned expressway across Lower Manhattan, which was eventually abandoned. Today some cities are even removing old elevated highways and replacing them with streets, parks and homes.","Parking took up space too. Every car needs somewhere to sit when it is not moving, which is most of the time, so cities built car parks, garages and wide streets lined with parked cars. Downtown buildings were sometimes torn down to make surface parking lots. Planners still argue about how to balance the needs of drivers with those of people walking, cycling and riding buses and trains."]},{"heading":"Cars Go to War","paragraphs":["Cars and trucks changed warfare too. One of the most famous stories comes from the start of World War I. In early September 1914 the German army was advancing towards Paris. The French commander of the city's defences, General Joseph Gallieni, needed to rush extra soldiers to the front line. On the night of 7 to 8 September, about 600 Paris taxicabs, many of them small Renault cabs, were gathered at Les Invalides and sent to carry soldiers about 50 km towards the fighting near Nanteuil-le-Haudouin, during the First Battle of the Marne. The drivers kept their meters running, and the French government later paid the fares, about 70,000 francs in total.","These 'Taxis of the Marne' became a legend in France, a symbol of ordinary people helping to save their country. Historians today point out that the taxis carried only a small part of the troops and probably did not change the outcome of the battle, which was decided by much larger movements of soldiers by train and on foot. But the boost to French spirits was real, and the story showed something new: motor vehicles could move people faster than an army had ever moved them before.","In World War II, the most famous military car was the Jeep. In 1940 the US Army asked carmakers for a small, tough, four-wheel-drive scout car and gave them just 49 days to build a prototype. A small company called American Bantam, with designer Karl Probst, built the first one, and Willys-Overland and Ford developed their own. Willys's version, the MB, became the standard design, and Ford also built it as the GPW. Together they made about 640,000 Jeeps. They carried officers, pulled small guns, and served as ambulances. Nobody is quite sure where the name 'Jeep' came from; one popular idea is that it came from 'GP', short for 'general purpose', but that has never been proven. After the war, the Jeep became a civilian off-road vehicle, an ancestor of today's SUVs."]},{"heading":"A World on Wheels","paragraphs":["Step back and look at how much changed in a single lifetime. A child born in 1890 grew up in a city full of horses, with muddy streets and long walks to everywhere. By the time that child was 40, in 1930, there were about 23 million cars in the United States alone. By the time they were 70, in 1960, families lived in suburbs, shopped in malls, ate at drive-ins, slept in motels and crossed the country on highways.","The same transformation spread around the world, sometimes faster, sometimes slower. In Europe, cheap small cars after World War II, like those in Part 12, put millions of families on the road. Today there are roughly one and a half billion motor vehicles in the world, although estimates vary. Many people's daily lives, from how they get to school to where their food comes from, depend on cars and trucks.","But the car brought more than convenience. It also brought a new thrill: speed. From the very beginning, some people did not want to just get somewhere. They wanted to get there first, faster than anyone had ever gone before. And they were willing to risk everything to do it. In the next part, we head to the racetrack."]}],"deepDive":"Historians often describe what cars did to cities with the idea of a 'travel time budget'. Studies in many countries suggest people tend to spend roughly an hour a day travelling, whatever technology they use. When people walked, that hour kept cities compact, a few kilometres across. Streetcars stretched cities along their lines, and cars let them spread in every direction, which is one reason suburbs grew so fast. Car-based growth also relied on public policy, not just personal choice. In the United States, government-backed mortgages, the tax-funded Interstates and zoning rules that separated homes from shops all favoured spread-out, car-dependent suburbs. Historians have documented how mortgage maps and lending rules in the 1930s and later marked many Black neighbourhoods as risky, a practice called redlining, while suburbs like Levittown used racial covenants to exclude Black buyers; the Supreme Court ruled in 1948 (Shelley v. Kraemer) that courts could not enforce such covenants, but discrimination continued in other ways. The 'horse problem' is often used as a lesson about technology, but carefully. The urban horse really was a public-health headache, yet the famous 1894 prediction of nine feet of manure is not supported by any known newspaper. The real lesson may be that the car solved one pollution problem while creating others, such as exhaust, noise, traffic deaths and land given over to parking. Debates about the streetcar 'conspiracy' show how history can be argued. Writer Bradford Snell's 1974 testimony to the US Senate claimed a deliberate plot, but economists and transport historians such as George Hilton argued that streetcars were losing money long before GM got involved. Most scholars now see GM's actions as real but limited, affecting perhaps a tenth of US transit systems. Finally, cars changed social life in ways that are hard to measure: courtship, the teenage driving licence as a coming-of-age moment, and new kinds of freedom that were, for decades, not shared fairly.","funFact":"The world's first motel, the Milestone Mo-Tel in San Luis Obispo, California, opened in 1925 and charged 1.25 dollars a night.","check":{"q":"What was the Negro Motorist Green Book?","a":"A guidebook (1936–1966) listing places that welcomed Black travellers during segregation","wrong":["A road atlas showing all the new Interstate highways","A rulebook for the first driving tests","A catalogue for ordering cars by mail"]},"sources":[{"title":"The Negro Motorist Green Book","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Negro_Motorist_Green_Book"},{"title":"Victor Hugo Green","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Hugo_Green"},{"title":"George E. Waring Jr.","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_E._Waring_Jr."},{"title":"Great Horse Manure Crisis of 1894","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Horse_Manure_Crisis_of_1894"},{"title":"Milestone Mo-Tel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milestone_Mo-Tel"},{"title":"Drive-in theater","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drive-in_theater"},{"title":"Rural Free Delivery","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rural_Free_Delivery"},{"title":"General Motors streetcar conspiracy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Motors_streetcar_conspiracy"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/14-1.jpg","alt":"Cover of the 1940 Negro Motorist Green-Book, a guide to safe places for Black travellers.","file":"The Negro Motorist Green Book.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Negro_Motorist_Green_Book.jpg","author":"Victor Hugo Green","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/14-2.jpg","alt":"The Motel Inn (1925) in San Luis Obispo, often called the first motel, with its bell tower.","file":"Motel Inn (1925) (\"first motel\"), horizontal view, Route 101, San Luis Obispo, California LOC 24910535557.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Motel_Inn_(1925)_(%22first_motel%22),_horizontal_view,_Route_101,_San_Luis_Obispo,_California_LOC_24910535557.jpg","author":"The Library of Congress from Washington, DC, United States","license":"No restrictions"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/14-3.jpg","alt":"London Bridge around 1900, packed with horse-drawn wagons, carts and people on foot.","file":"Londonbridge1900.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Londonbridge1900.jpg","author":"John L.Stoddard (died 1931), Scan by Robert Schediwy","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3926},{"n":15,"title":"Racing: The Need for Speed","when":"1894 – today","summary":"Almost as soon as cars existed, people wanted to know which one was fastest. The first races ran along ordinary country roads from one city to another, until they became far too dangerous and moved onto special tracks. Racing drivers chased speed records all the way to a jet car that went faster than sound, and many of the tricks they learned now keep your family car safe.","sections":[{"heading":"A Newspaper Invents the Motor Race","paragraphs":["Imagine standing at the edge of a dusty road just outside Paris on the morning of 22 July 1894. Crowds line the route in their summer hats. Bicycles weave between the spectators. Then, one by one, a strange parade of machines rattles past: little petrol carriages with tall wooden wheels, a few steam vehicles hissing and puffing, and drivers in caps and goggles gripping steering tillers. This was Paris–Rouen, and it is often called the world's first motor competition.","It was not organised by a carmaker or a sports club, but by a newspaper. Le Petit Journal, one of the most popular papers in France, was always looking for exciting stories that would sell more copies. Its editor, Pierre Giffard, had already organised famous bicycle races. Now he announced a contest for 'horseless carriages' from Paris to the city of Rouen, a distance of about 126 kilometres (78 miles) along the valley of the River Seine.","The interest was astonishing for such a new invention. More than a hundred people paid the entry fee, and their ideas for powering a vehicle were wonderfully varied. Some entries were said to run on compressed air, on gravity, on levers or even on weights and pendulums. Most of those never turned up. To make sure only real working machines started, the organisers held short qualifying runs of about 50 kilometres in the days before the main event. In the end, 21 vehicles lined up for the start.","Here is an important detail: Paris–Rouen was not really meant to be a race. The main prize of 5,000 francs was to go to the vehicle that came 'closest to the ideal', which the judges defined as safe, easy to control and cheap to run. Speed mattered, but so did reliability. This made sense in 1894. Most people had never seen a car, and the big question was not 'How fast can it go?' but 'Will it get there at all?'","The first vehicle to arrive in Rouen was a big steam tractor driven by Count Jules-Albert de Dion, pulling a passenger carriage behind it like a small train on the road. It took about 6 hours and 48 minutes, an average of roughly 19 km/h. But the judges did not give de Dion the top prize. His machine needed a stoker, a second person to shovel fuel into the fire, so it did not fit their idea of a practical everyday vehicle. He received the second prize instead.","Just a few minutes behind came Albert Lemaître in a small Peugeot petrol car. The 5,000-franc prize was shared between two French companies whose cars both used engines built under licence from Gottlieb Daimler: Panhard et Levassor and the Peugeot family firm. Every one of the petrol cars that started reached Rouen, which was a big advertisement for the young petrol engine. The newspapers were full of the story, and a new sport had been born almost by accident."]},{"heading":"Levassor's Marathon: Paris to Bordeaux and Back","paragraphs":["One year later, the organisers went further. On 11 June 1895 the cars set out on Paris–Bordeaux–Paris, a giant loop of about 1,178 kilometres (732 miles) from the capital down to the port of Bordeaux in south-west France and back again. This time it really was a race: the fastest finisher would be the winner, though the prize rules still had a catch, as we will see.","The star of the event was Émile Levassor, one of the two partners of Panhard et Levassor. He was a 52-year-old engineer, and he drove a two-seater Panhard with an engine of about 1.2 litres, producing only a few horsepower. Today a small family hatchback has about twenty times more power. His car had solid rubber tyres, no windscreen and no roof. At night the road ahead was lit only by dim lamps.","Levassor drove through the day, through the night and into the next day. According to the story usually told, a relief driver had been arranged to take over partway along the route, but Levassor arrived so far ahead of schedule that the man was not ready, so he simply carried on himself. Whether every detail of that story is exact, it is clear that he did nearly all the driving alone. He crossed the finish line in Paris after about 48 hours and 48 minutes, having averaged roughly 24 km/h (15 mph). He was almost six hours ahead of the next car.","Think about what that means. Twenty-four kilometres an hour is about the speed of a fast bicycle rider today. But Levassor kept it up for two days and two nights over roads made for horses, with dust in his eyes and a vibrating, roaring engine in front of him. When he finally climbed down, he is said to have been exhausted but delighted. A monument to him and his drive still stands at the Porte Maillot in Paris.","Here comes the catch. The official rules had been written for four-seater cars, and Levassor's car had only two seats. So the judges did not give him the official first prize. It went instead to Paul Koechlin in a Peugeot, who arrived about eleven hours after Levassor. Many people felt this was unfair, and the following year's big race, Paris–Marseille–Paris in 1896, was simply won by whoever was fastest.","The 1895 race also had a famous loser. André and Édouard Michelin, two brothers who made rubber products, entered a car called L'Éclair fitted with their new air-filled tyres. They suffered puncture after puncture and did not finish within the time limit. (Part 9 tells the story of their tyres.) Still, many people noticed how much more comfortably an air-filled tyre rode over bumps, and within a few years nearly every racing car used them.","Sadly, the dangers of racing caught up with Levassor himself. In the 1896 Paris–Marseille–Paris race he was injured when his car overturned, reportedly after swerving to avoid a dog. He died the following year, in 1897, and many accounts link his death to those injuries. He had helped show the world what a car could do, but his story was an early warning about what speed could cost."]},{"heading":"Paris–Madrid: The Race That Had to Stop","paragraphs":["Over the next few years, city-to-city racing became wildly popular across Europe. Each year the cars got bigger, heavier and far more powerful. In 1894 the fastest cars averaged under 20 km/h. By 1903 the biggest racing machines could reach well over 120 km/h on a straight road, with engines many times larger than Levassor's. Organisers even introduced a weight limit of 1,000 kilograms for the largest class, so builders stripped their cars down to bare frames to fit in as big an engine as possible.","The roads, however, had not changed much. They were narrow, made of packed gravel or stone, and full of bends, bridges, railway crossings and villages. There were no crash barriers. Animals wandered across. Most dangerous of all, crowds of spectators stood right at the edge of the road, sometimes stepping out to get a better look at the next car coming through the dust.","On 24 May 1903 the most ambitious race of all set off from Versailles, near Paris. Paris–Madrid was meant to run all the way across France and Spain to the Spanish capital. Accounts differ, but somewhere between about 220 and 275 cars and motorcycles started, leaving at intervals from around a quarter to four in the morning. Hundreds of thousands of people were said to be watching along the route.","The dust thrown up by each car hung in the air and made it almost impossible for the next driver to see. Cars overtook one another blindly in thick clouds. Through the day there were a series of terrible crashes. Accounts vary, but somewhere between six and eight people died, including drivers, riding mechanics and spectators, and many more were injured.","One of those killed was Marcel Renault, who with his brothers Louis and Fernand had founded the Renault car company. His car left the road near the village of Couhé-Vérac. His brother Louis, driving another Renault, reached Bordeaux and won the class for lighter cars, only to learn the news of Marcel's accident afterwards. It was a heartbreaking moment for the young company.","The French government acted that same evening. The race was stopped at Bordeaux, the end of the first stage, and was not allowed to continue to Spain. According to many accounts, the cars were not even allowed to drive themselves back. They were pulled to the railway station by horses and sent home to Paris on trains. Fernand Gabriel, in a Mors, was declared the winner of the stage, having averaged around 105 km/h on public roads, an incredible speed for the time.","Paris–Madrid is remembered as the last of the great city-to-city races on open roads. It showed, in the saddest possible way, that fast cars and crowds of people on ordinary roads did not mix. If racing was going to survive, it needed a new home."]},{"heading":"Racing Moves Behind the Fences","paragraphs":["Some races still used closed-off public roads for a while, with the roads shut and guarded on race day. The first French Grand Prix in 1906, held on a long loop of roads near Le Mans, worked this way. But a few people had a bolder idea: why not build a special place just for fast cars?","In England, the answer came from a wealthy landowner named Hugh Locke King. British law at the time limited cars on public roads to 20 miles per hour, so British carmakers had nowhere to test their cars at full speed. Locke King decided to build a track on his own land at Brooklands, near Weybridge in Surrey. Work began in late 1906, and Brooklands opened on 17 June 1907. It was the world's first purpose-built motor racing circuit.","Brooklands was a giant concrete oval about 4.45 kilometres (2.77 miles) around, 30 metres wide, with huge curved banks at the ends that rose nearly 9 metres high in places. The idea of the banking was like a cyclist leaning into a corner: by tilting the road, the track helped the cars stay on course at high speed without skidding outwards. Painted down the middle was a line that was supposed to show where a car could go round the banking at speed almost without steering. Brooklands could hold huge crowds and also became one of Britain's first airfields; later, famous aircraft were built there. Part of the site is now the Brooklands Museum.","Across the Atlantic, the American businessman Carl G. Fisher had visited European races and wanted something similar at home. (He was the same man who later promoted the Lincoln Highway, as you saw in Part 13.) With three partners he bought farmland outside Indianapolis, Indiana, and built the Indianapolis Motor Speedway, a rectangle with rounded corners exactly 2.5 miles (about 4 kilometres) around. It opened in August 1909.","The first surface was a mix of crushed stone, gravel and tar, and it was a disaster. During the first car races that August it broke up, throwing stones and dust, and several people were killed, including drivers, riding mechanics and spectators. Fisher and his partners quickly decided to repave the whole track with bricks. About 3.2 million bricks, each weighing about 4.5 kilograms (10 pounds), were laid in just a couple of months. That is why the Speedway is still nicknamed 'the Brickyard'. Today the track is covered in asphalt, except for a strip of original bricks one yard wide across the start-finish line, which winning drivers traditionally kneel down and kiss."]},{"heading":"Three Great Races: Indy, Le Mans and Formula One","paragraphs":["On 30 May 1911 the Speedway held its first 500-mile race: 200 laps of the brick oval. An estimated 80,000 to 85,000 people came to watch, an enormous crowd for the time, and 40 cars started. In those days most racing cars carried two people. Beside the driver sat a riding mechanic, who pumped oil by hand, watched the tyres and, very importantly, turned round to warn the driver about cars coming up from behind.","Ray Harroun, a 32-year-old engineer, drove a long, pointed yellow car called the Marmon Wasp, built by the Nordyke and Marmon company of Indianapolis. His car had only one seat, and other teams complained that a driver alone could not safely see what was behind him. Harroun's answer was to fix a small mirror on a frame above the cockpit so he could glance back at the cars following him. It is believed to be one of the first rear-view mirrors ever used on a car. Harroun later admitted that on the bumpy bricks the mirror shook so much that it was not very useful, but the idea caught on, and today every car on the road has one.","Harroun drove carefully, saving his tyres, while faster rivals stopped again and again to change theirs. A relief driver, Cyrus Patschke, took the wheel for a stretch of about 35 laps in the middle of the race. Harroun won in 6 hours, 42 minutes and 8 seconds, averaging about 74.6 mph (120 km/h). The Indianapolis 500 has been held almost every year since, apart from wartime breaks, and is one of the most famous races in the world.","In France, a different kind of challenge appeared in 1923. On 26 and 27 May, cars raced around a loop of closed public roads near the town of Le Mans for a whole day and a whole night: the first 24 Hours of Le Mans. The point was not just to be fast but to be tough. Lights had to work through the dark, brakes had to last, and engines had to keep running hour after hour. The first winners were André Lagache and René Léonard in a Chenard et Walcker, sharing the driving between them.","Le Mans is still held every year, today on the Circuit de la Sarthe, which is about 13.6 kilometres long and still partly made of public roads that are closed for the race. For many years the race began with the famous 'Le Mans start': drivers stood on one side of the track, ran across when the flag dropped, jumped into their cars and roared away. Because drivers sometimes set off before fastening their safety belts, this start was dropped in 1970 and replaced the next year by a rolling start. In 2010, the winning car covered about 5,410 kilometres in 24 hours, almost as far as a flight from Paris to New York.","Grand Prix racing (from the French for 'great prize') continued through the 1920s and 1930s with fierce battles between teams from Italy, France, Germany and Britain. After the Second World War, the sport's governing body created a single set of rules, known as Formula One, and in 1950 started a World Championship for drivers. The very first championship race was the British Grand Prix at Silverstone, a former wartime airfield in the English countryside, on 13 May 1950. King George VI and the royal family came to watch.","That first race was won by the Italian driver Giuseppe 'Nino' Farina in an Alfa Romeo, averaging about 146 km/h over 70 laps. Farina went on to win the 1950 championship, becoming the first Formula One World Champion. Today Formula One cars are among the most advanced machines on Earth, packed with sensors, carbon fibre and hybrid engines, and the championship visits countries on almost every continent."]},{"heading":"Faster Than Sound: The Land Speed Record","paragraphs":["While racers competed against each other, a few daring drivers competed against the clock. The land speed record is the highest speed ever officially measured for a car. Under the rules, a car has to make two runs in opposite directions over a measured distance, usually a kilometre or a mile, within a short time limit, and the official speed is the average of the two. This cancels out any help from wind or a gentle slope.","The very first records were set in France in 1898 and 1899 by electric cars. Count Gaston de Chasseloup-Laubat set the first official record of about 63 km/h in December 1898, and in 1899 Camille Jenatzy's bullet-shaped electric car became the first to pass 100 km/h (Part 7 tells that story). Petrol cars took over from 1902, though a Stanley steam car snatched the record in 1906.","Records soon needed long, flat, open spaces. Drivers raced along beaches at low tide, such as Pendine Sands in Wales and Daytona Beach in Florida, and later on the dazzling white Bonneville Salt Flats in Utah, a dried-up lake bed so flat you can see the curve of the Earth.","The British driver Malcolm Campbell became the most famous record-breaker of the 1920s and 1930s. He named his cars Blue Bird after seeing a play called The Blue Bird by the Belgian writer Maurice Maeterlinck, and painted them blue. Between 1924 and 1935 he set nine land speed records. His first, in 1924 at Pendine Sands, was about 146 mph (235 km/h). On 3 September 1935, at Bonneville, he became the first person to drive a car faster than 300 mph (about 484 km/h). He also set speed records on water in boats called Blue Bird, and his son Donald carried on the family tradition.","After the Second World War, record cars changed completely. Wheel-driven cars with giant piston engines, like John Cobb's Railton, which reached about 394 mph in 1947, gave way to cars pushed along by jet engines from aircraft. In 1963 the American Craig Breedlove drove a three-wheeled jet car, Spirit of America, past 400 mph. In 1970 a rocket car called Blue Flame passed 1,000 km/h. In 1983 the British businessman Richard Noble drove his jet car Thrust2 to about 633 mph (1,019 km/h) in Nevada.","Noble's next dream was even bigger: a car that would break the sound barrier. The speed of sound in air at ground level is roughly 1,200 km/h, depending on the temperature. Going faster than that on land was risky in ways nobody fully understood. Shock waves might lift the car off the ground or batter it apart. The team built ThrustSSC (SSC stands for 'supersonic car'), designed by Ron Ayers, Glynne Bowsher and others.","ThrustSSC looked like a jet fighter without wings. It was about 16.5 metres long, weighed about 10.6 tonnes and was pushed by two Rolls-Royce Spey jet engines of the kind used in Phantom fighter planes, producing around 50,000 pounds of thrust at full power. At top speed it burned roughly 18 litres of fuel every second. Its driver was Andy Green, a Royal Air Force fighter pilot, chosen because he was used to thinking and reacting at enormous speeds.","On 15 October 1997, in the Black Rock Desert of Nevada, Green made two runs in opposite directions and set a record of about 1,228 km/h (763 mph). For the first time ever, a car on the ground had gone faster than sound, and observers heard the sonic boom roll across the desert. The record still stands today. ThrustSSC now rests in the Coventry Transport Museum in England. From Chasseloup-Laubat's 63 km/h to Green's 1,228 km/h, the record grew almost twentyfold in a century."]},{"heading":"From the Racetrack to Your Driveway","paragraphs":["Carmakers have long said that racing improves the breed, meaning that the hard lessons of the racetrack find their way into ordinary cars. That is not always true, but in some important cases it is.","The clearest example is the disc brake. Early cars used drum brakes, where curved shoes press outwards against the inside of a metal drum turning with the wheel. They work, but when they get very hot after repeated hard stops, they lose grip, a problem called brake fade. A disc brake instead squeezes pads against both sides of a flat metal disc, a bit like pinching a spinning coin between your fingers. The disc is out in the open air, so it cools much faster.","In 1953 Jaguar, working with the Dunlop company, raced its C-Type sports cars at Le Mans with disc brakes on all four wheels. While other drivers had to brake early and carefully to save their overheating brakes, the Jaguar drivers could brake later and harder lap after lap. Jaguar C-Types finished first, second and fourth, and the winners, Duncan Hamilton and Tony Rolt, became the first to win Le Mans at an average of over 100 mph. Other carmakers took note. The Citroën DS of 1955 had front disc brakes, and within a couple of decades they were common on everyday cars. Most new cars today have discs at least on the front wheels.","Racing also taught engineers about tyres, which had to grip at high speed without overheating or bursting, and about aerodynamics, the science of how air flows around a moving object. Racing teams used wind tunnels to make cars slipperier and, from the late 1960s, fitted wings that use the air rushing past to push the car down onto the road, a bit like an upside-down aeroplane wing. Everyday cars use the same science to save fuel and stay stable on the motorway.","The most important lessons, though, were about safety, and many were learned painfully. On 11 June 1955 at Le Mans, a car crashed into the crowd and more than 80 spectators died. It remains motor racing's worst disaster. Several countries temporarily banned racing, and tracks around the world began building stronger barriers and keeping crowds further back.","In the 1960s, the Scottish Formula One champion Jackie Stewart began campaigning hard for safety after he was trapped in his crashed car at the Belgian Grand Prix in 1966. Partly thanks to campaigners like him, racing gradually adopted full safety harnesses, fireproof suits, better helmets, crash barriers, run-off areas and on-site medical teams. Racing cars were built with strong survival cells around the driver, rather like the rigid passenger compartment you will meet in Part 16. Later came the HANS device, a collar that stops a driver's head whipping forward in a crash, required in Formula One from 2003.","In 2018 Formula One introduced the halo, a curved titanium bar above the cockpit that protects the driver's head from flying wheels and debris. Some people complained that it looked ugly. Then, at the Bahrain Grand Prix in 2020, Romain Grosjean's car hit a barrier and burst into flames. The halo pushed the metal barrier away from his head, and he climbed out of the fire with burns to his hands. Afterwards he said that without the halo he would not have been able to talk about it.","So racing is a story of courage, cleverness and showmanship, but also of risk. Every rule about barriers, belts and helmets exists because someone was hurt or killed before it was made. And as cars on ordinary roads became faster and more common, the same question that haunted racing began to haunt everyday life too."]}],"deepDive":"Why did early races matter so much? In the 1890s cars were expensive curiosities, and many people doubted they would ever be more than toys for the rich. Races were public demonstrations. When every petrol car that started Paris–Rouen in 1894 reached the finish, and when Levassor averaged about 24 km/h for 1,178 km in 1895, it proved that petrol cars could be reliable over long distances, at a time when steam and electric cars were still serious rivals. Newspapers and carmakers understood this marketing value immediately, which is why so many early races were organised by publishers and why companies such as Panhard, Peugeot, Mercedes and Renault poured money into them. The land speed record also tells a story about physics. Air resistance (drag) grows roughly with the square of speed, so the power needed to overcome it grows roughly with the cube. Doubling your speed needs around eight times the power to push through the air. That is why records crept up from about 63 km/h to about 100 km/h with modest electric motors, then needed aircraft engines of hundreds of horsepower, and finally jet engines. ThrustSSC's two Spey engines gave around 50,000 pounds of thrust, which engineers often compare to the power of about 100,000 horsepower, or roughly a thousand family cars. Near the speed of sound, air can no longer move out of the way smoothly, and shock waves form. The ThrustSSC team spent years on computer simulations and models to make sure the car would stay on the ground. Historians also debate how much racing really improved ordinary cars. Disc brakes are a strong example, though they had been used on aircraft and a few earlier cars before Jaguar's success. Other technologies, such as safety glass and seatbelts, came mainly from road-safety research rather than racing. Racing's biggest gift may be in safety culture: tragedies such as Paris–Madrid in 1903 and Le Mans in 1955 forced organisers to think systematically about crowds, barriers and the human body, and modern Formula One survival cells and the halo are the result of decades of crash investigation. Finally, a note on the rear-view mirror: Harroun's 1911 mirror is famous, but a few writers mention earlier examples, so it is safest to call it one of the first, not the very first.","funFact":"The Indianapolis Motor Speedway was paved with about 3.2 million bricks in 1909, and a one-yard strip of those original bricks still marks the start-finish line today.","check":{"q":"Why did Ray Harroun fit a mirror to his Marmon Wasp in the first Indianapolis 500 in 1911?","a":"He drove alone without a riding mechanic, so he needed it to see cars coming up behind him","wrong":["To check his hair before meeting the crowd","To reflect sunlight into the eyes of rival drivers","To look at his engine while he was driving"]},"sources":[{"title":"Paris–Rouen (motor race)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris%E2%80%93Rouen_(motor_race)"},{"title":"Paris–Bordeaux–Paris","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris%E2%80%93Bordeaux%E2%80%93Paris"},{"title":"Paris–Madrid race","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris%E2%80%93Madrid_race"},{"title":"1911 Indianapolis 500","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1911_Indianapolis_500"},{"title":"Indianapolis Motor Speedway","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indianapolis_Motor_Speedway"},{"title":"Brooklands","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooklands"},{"title":"Land speed record","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_speed_record"},{"title":"ThrustSSC","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ThrustSSC"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/15-1.jpg","alt":"Panhard et Levassor car No. 5, which Émile Levassor drove first to the finish of Paris–Bordeaux–Paris 1895.","file":"La Panhard & Levassor n°5 d'Émile Levassor et Charles d'Hostingue, victorieuse du Paris-Bordeaux-Paris 1895.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Panhard_%26_Levassor_n%C2%B05_d%27%C3%89mile_Levassor_et_Charles_d%27Hostingue,_victorieuse_du_Paris-Bordeaux-Paris_1895.jpg","author":"La Vie au Grand Air","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/15-2.jpg","alt":"Ray Harroun sits in the Marmon Wasp, No. 32, winner of the first Indianapolis 500 in 1911.","file":"The Marmon Wasp.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Marmon_Wasp.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/15-3.jpg","alt":"ThrustSSC, the jet-powered car that first broke the sound barrier on land, at Coventry Transport Museum.","file":"ThrustSSC at Coventry Transport Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThrustSSC_at_Coventry_Transport_Museum.jpg","author":"Tristan Surtel","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":4183},{"n":16,"title":"Crashes and the Fight for Safety","when":"1869 – today","summary":"Cars gave people amazing freedom, but they also brought a new danger: crashes. Clever inventors fought back with windscreen wipers, safety glass, seatbelts, crumple zones and airbags. Thanks to them, a ride in a car today is far safer than it was for your grandparents, though there is still work to do.","sections":[{"heading":"A Scientist on a Steam Carriage","paragraphs":["Our story starts not on a busy city street but on a quiet country road in Ireland, beside a castle with a giant telescope. The town was called Parsonstown at the time; today it is Birr, in County Offaly. In the grounds of Birr Castle stood the Leviathan, for decades the largest telescope in the world, built by William Parsons, the third Earl of Rosse.","The Earl's cousin, Mary Ward, was a remarkable scientist. Born Mary King in 1827, she had been collecting insects since she was about three years old. She taught herself to use a microscope, made her own glass slides from thin slices of ivory, and drew what she saw with beautiful precision. Her book Sketches with the Microscope, first printed privately in 1857 in just 250 copies, proved so popular that it was later published for everyone and reprinted again and again. She also wrote a book about telescopes and was one of only three women on the mailing list of the Royal Astronomical Society, at a time when women were barred from most universities and scientific societies.","The Parsons family loved machines. The Earl's sons, who included the young Charles Parsons, later famous for inventing the steam turbine, had built a small steam-powered road carriage. On 31 August 1869, Mary Ward was riding on it with family members along the road near the town. As it went round a bend, the carriage lurched, and she was thrown off and fell under one of its heavy iron wheels. She died almost at once. She was 42.","Mary Ward's death is often called the first known death caused by a motor vehicle. Historians add a careful note, though: steam carriages had caused accidents before. In 1834 a steam carriage boiler exploded in Scotland, near Paisley, and several passengers were killed. So it is fairer to say that Mary Ward is the first person widely recorded as being killed by a self-propelled road vehicle, and that her death was an early sign of what powerful machines on roads could do. Her microscope and books can still be seen in Ireland today.","Notice something important about this first tragedy. Nobody was being careless on purpose. The vehicle simply had no seatbelts, no doors and nothing to hold a passenger in place, and nobody had yet thought about what happens to a human body when a heavy machine stops or swerves suddenly. Working that out would take another century."]},{"heading":"Crystal Palace, 1896: A Car Meets a Pedestrian","paragraphs":["Twenty-seven years later, in the summer of 1896, petrol cars were brand new in Britain. Parliament was about to change the law to let them travel at up to 14 mph, as you read in Part 2, though the new rules would not start until that November. Crowds gathered at the Crystal Palace, a gigantic glass-and-iron exhibition building in south London, where a company called the Anglo-French Motor Carriage Company was giving people demonstration rides in its cars.","On 17 August 1896, a 44-year-old woman named Bridget Driscoll was crossing a terrace in the Crystal Palace grounds with her teenage daughter and a friend. A demonstration car driven by a young man named Arthur Edsall came towards them. Witnesses said she seemed confused about which way to step, as many people would have been when faced with a machine they had never seen before. The car struck her, and she died within minutes from a head injury.","At the inquest, the official inquiry into her death, people disagreed about how fast the car had been going. One witness described it as moving at 'a reckless pace, in fact, like a fire engine'. The driver said he was going only about 4 mph, the speed the car had been set to, and a cab driver who examined the car said it could not go faster than about 4.5 mph. After about six hours, the jury decided it was an accidental death. The coroner, Percy Morrison, said he hoped that 'such a thing would never happen again'.","Bridget Driscoll is remembered as the first pedestrian in Britain to be killed by a petrol car. The coroner's hope, sadly, was not fulfilled. As millions of cars filled the roads in the following decades, crashes became common. In many countries, more people were killed on the roads each year than by most diseases. Something had to be done, but at first people blamed drivers, not machines. The idea that cars themselves could be designed to protect people grew only slowly."]},{"heading":"Seeing Clearly: Wipers, Safety Glass and Headlights","paragraphs":["Some of the earliest safety inventions were about something very simple: being able to see. On a snowy day in New York City, around 1902, an Alabama woman named Mary Anderson rode on a streetcar, a kind of electric tram. She noticed that the driver kept having to open his window or lean out to wipe snow and sleet off the glass with his hand, letting cold air blow in on the passengers. There had to be a better way.","Back home, she designed a device with a rubber blade on the outside of the windscreen, connected to a lever inside. By swinging the lever, the driver could sweep the blade back and forth across the glass without ever opening the window. A counterweight kept the blade pressed against the glass, and the whole thing could be taken off when winter was over. On 10 November 1903 she was granted US Patent number 743,801 for a 'window cleaning device'.","Anderson tried to sell her idea to companies, but they said it had no commercial value. Her patent lasted 17 years and ran out in 1920 without making her any money. Yet hand-operated wipers became normal on cars in the following years, and in 1917 another American woman, Charlotte Bridgwood, patented an electric 'storm windshield cleaner', an early automatic wiper. In 2011 Mary Anderson was added to the US National Inventors Hall of Fame.","Another danger lurked in the windscreen itself. Ordinary glass breaks into long, sharp, dagger-like shards. In a crash, people were badly cut by flying glass. According to a well-known story, around 1903 the French chemist Édouard Bénédictus knocked a glass flask off a shelf in his laboratory and was surprised to see that it cracked but stayed in one piece. The flask had held a plastic solution called cellulose nitrate, which had dried into a thin film on the inside and held the broken glass together. Whether or not every detail of the story is true, Bénédictus did go on to patent laminated safety glass, made of a sandwich of glass and a clear plastic layer, in 1909.","Laminated glass was used for gas-mask eyepieces during the First World War and gradually found its way into cars. By the late 1920s Ford was fitting safety-glass windscreens to its Model A. Today car windscreens are made of laminated glass, so that when a stone hits them they crack like a spider's web but do not shatter into the car. Side windows are usually toughened glass, which breaks into small, blunt pebbles instead of sharp blades.","Seeing at night was another problem. The first cars used candle or oil lamps and then acetylene gas lamps, which made a bright flame but had to be lit by hand and refilled. In 1912 Cadillac offered a complete electric system, with Charles Kettering's self-starter (Part 7), a generator, a battery and electric lights. Electric headlights could be switched on with a flick, and later designs added dipped beams so drivers would not dazzle people coming the other way. Brake lights, indicators and reflectors followed, all helping drivers see and be seen."]},{"heading":"Béla Barényi and the Car That Crumples on Purpose","paragraphs":["For a long time, many people believed that the strongest, stiffest car was the safest. If a car came out of a crash with hardly a dent, surely that was good? An engineer named Béla Barényi realised this was exactly wrong.","Barényi was born on 1 March 1907 in Hirtenberg, a small town in Austria, then part of Austria-Hungary. As a student in Vienna he was already sketching ideas for safer, cleverer cars. After working for several car companies, he joined Daimler-Benz in Germany in 1939, and he spent the rest of his career there, heading a department that worked on future designs until he retired in 1972.","Here is the key idea. When a car hits a wall, it stops suddenly. The people inside keep moving forward until something stops them too. What hurts people is not speed itself but stopping too quickly, because the faster you stop, the bigger the force on your body. Think of catching a raw egg. If you hold your hands stiff, the egg smashes. If you let your hands move back as you catch it, the egg slows down gradually and survives.","Barényi's answer was to divide the car into zones. The front and rear would be crumple zones, designed to fold up in a controlled way during a crash, like a can being squashed. As the metal crumples, it soaks up the energy of the crash and stretches out the time it takes for the car to stop, by a tiny fraction of a second, which makes a huge difference to the people inside. In the middle, around the passengers, he wanted a strong, rigid passenger cell, a safety cage that would not collapse onto the people inside.","Barényi had worked on the idea since the 1930s, and in 1952 he received a German patent (number 854157) for the design. In 1959 Mercedes-Benz launched a new family of saloon cars, nicknamed the 'Fintail' because of the small fins on the rear, which was the first car built fully around his crumple-zone design. The same year, Mercedes-Benz began crashing real cars into obstacles to test them, a practice that has become standard across the industry.","Barényi had many other safety ideas, including steering columns that collapse instead of spearing into the driver's chest, and stronger roof pillars to protect people if a car rolls over. He is often called the father of passive safety, which means safety features that protect you automatically without you having to do anything. Patent experts count well over a thousand patents filed in his name around the world. Every modern car is designed to crumple at the ends and stay strong in the middle, just as he imagined."]},{"heading":"Nils Bohlin's Belt: Three Points That Saved Millions","paragraphs":["A crumple zone slows the car gently, but it only helps if you slow down with the car. Without a belt, a person in a crash keeps flying forward and hits the steering wheel, dashboard or windscreen, or is thrown out of the car altogether. Seatbelts hold you in place so that you share the car's gentler stop.","Belts themselves were not new. Aircraft had used them for years, and some early cars had them. In 1949 the American company Nash offered lap belts, which go across the hips, in its cars, but few buyers wanted them. Saab in Sweden fitted belts as standard in 1958. But lap belts and diagonal belts had problems. A lap belt alone lets the upper body fold forward violently. A belt across only the chest can let a person slide out underneath it.","In 1958 Volvo, the Swedish carmaker, hired a safety engineer named Nils Bohlin. Bohlin had spent years at the aircraft company Saab designing ejection seats, which fire pilots out of a damaged fighter jet. He knew a lot about what happens to a human body under huge forces. In about a year he designed the three-point seatbelt: one strap goes across the lap and another diagonally across the chest, joined at a single buckle beside the hip, and anchored low so it stays in place during a crash. The straps rest over the strong bones of the hips and the chest, spreading the force. It could be put on with one hand in a couple of seconds.","The first car sold with Bohlin's three-point belts was a Volvo PV544, delivered in Sweden in August 1959. Then Volvo did something unusual. Instead of keeping the design to itself, it made the patent available for other carmakers to use free of charge, because the company believed the belt was too important for safety to be kept secret. Today the three-point belt is fitted to practically every car in the world.","Bohlin also collected evidence. In 1967 he presented a study of about 28,000 crashes in Sweden. It found that, in crashes below about 60 mph where the passenger compartment stayed in one piece, no one wearing a belt had been killed. Studies since then show that wearing a seatbelt cuts the risk of death for people in the front seats by roughly half. Volvo estimates that the three-point belt has saved more than a million lives worldwide.","Of course, a belt only works if it is worn, and for years many people did not bother. Laws helped. In 1970 the state of Victoria in Australia became the first place in the world to require drivers and front-seat passengers to wear belts. Many other countries followed: the United Kingdom made belts compulsory for front-seat occupants in 1983, and in 1984 New York became the first US state with a seatbelt law. Campaigns such as 'Clunk Click Every Trip' in Britain helped make buckling up a habit."]},{"heading":"Unsafe at Any Speed: Laws, Rules and Dummies","paragraphs":["In the United States in the 1960s, around 50,000 people were dying on the roads every year. Many carmakers spent large sums changing the style of their cars each year, with new fins, chrome and colours, but much less on making them safer. The common view was that crashes were caused by 'the nut behind the wheel', meaning bad drivers, so the car itself was not to blame.","A young lawyer named Ralph Nader disagreed. In November 1965 he published a book called Unsafe at Any Speed. It argued that cars were designed with too little thought for what happens in a crash: hard dashboards, sharp knobs, steering columns that could push into a driver's chest, and confusing controls. Its best-known chapter criticised the handling of the early Chevrolet Corvair. (Later government tests disputed some of the claims about the Corvair, and historians still argue about it, but the book's wider point about safety design was very influential.)","The book became a bestseller, and things got even more dramatic when it came out that General Motors had hired private investigators to follow Nader and look for embarrassing information about him. In March 1966 the president of General Motors apologised to Nader in front of a US Senate committee. Nader later sued the company for invading his privacy and won a settlement, which he used to help fund car-safety campaigns.","The public outcry helped Congress act. On 9 September 1966, President Lyndon B. Johnson signed the National Traffic and Motor Vehicle Safety Act, which for the first time let the US government set compulsory safety standards for new cars. Soon new cars had to have seatbelts, padded dashboards, energy-absorbing steering columns, shatter-resistant windscreens and dual braking systems. In 1970 a new government agency, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, or NHTSA, was set up to write and enforce these rules and investigate crashes.","To test whether cars really protected people, engineers needed something to put inside them during crashes. Early researchers sometimes used volunteers in carefully controlled experiments. The US Air Force doctor John Paul Stapp famously rode rocket sleds that braked from over 1,000 km/h in less than a couple of seconds, to learn how much force a human body can survive. Obviously, real people could not be used in full car crashes, so engineers built crash-test dummies.","One of the first, named Sierra Sam, was built in 1949 to test aircraft ejection seats. In 1976 a dummy called Hybrid III appeared, and versions of it are still used today. A modern dummy is packed with sensors in its head, neck, chest and legs, recording tens of thousands of measurements in the tenth of a second a crash lasts. Dummies now come in families: different sizes of adults, children and, more recently, dummies designed to represent women's bodies more accurately, because for many years most dummies were modelled on an average man. Organisations such as Euro NCAP, founded in 1997, crash-test new cars and give them star ratings, so buyers can compare safety."]},{"heading":"Airbags, Smart Brakes and Child Seats","paragraphs":["A seatbelt holds your body, but in a hard crash your head can still swing forward. The airbag is a cushion that inflates in front of you faster than you can blink. An American engineer named John Hetrick patented a car airbag in 1953, after his family car swerved into a ditch. Early designs used compressed air, which turned out to be too slow. The breakthrough came in the late 1960s with an electronic crash sensor and a small chemical charge that produces a burst of gas, mostly nitrogen, filling the bag in about 20 to 30 thousandths of a second.","General Motors offered airbags on some cars in the 1970s, including the 1973 Oldsmobile Toronado, but few buyers chose them. In 1981 Mercedes-Benz offered a driver's airbag on its S-Class, designed to work together with the seatbelt. From the late 1980s and through the 1990s airbags spread quickly, and in the United States every new car built from the 1998 model year had to have front airbags for both the driver and front passenger. Side airbags and curtain airbags, which protect the head in side impacts, arrived in the 1990s. Airbags are designed to work with seatbelts, not instead of them. They are also the reason small children should not sit in a front seat with an active airbag.","Another danger was skidding. If you slam on the brakes hard, the wheels can lock and stop turning, and a car sliding on locked wheels cannot be steered. Aircraft had used anti-skid systems for years. In 1978 the German company Bosch, working with Mercedes-Benz, introduced an electronic anti-lock braking system, or ABS, on the S-Class. Sensors watch each wheel, and if one is about to lock, a computer briefly releases and reapplies the brake many times a second. The driver feels the pedal pulsing, and, most importantly, can still steer around an obstacle while braking hard.","In 1995 Bosch and Mercedes-Benz took the idea further with electronic stability control, or ESC. If sensors detect that the car is starting to skid sideways or spin, the system gently brakes individual wheels to bring the car back into line. Studies found it greatly reduces crashes in which cars slide off the road, and from the early 2010s it became compulsory on new cars in the United States and the European Union.","Children need special protection because their bodies are smaller and their neck and bones are still developing. In 1964 the Swedish professor Bertil Aldman, inspired by the way astronauts lay on their backs facing backwards during launch, developed an early rear-facing child seat that Volvo tested. A rear-facing seat spreads the crash forces across a baby's whole back and head instead of throwing the head forward. According to the World Health Organization, using proper child restraints can cut deaths among infants in crashes by about 71 percent.","Safety is not only about machines. Drinking alcohol before driving makes crashes far more likely. In 1954 the American scientist Robert Borkenstein invented the Breathalyzer, a device that measures alcohol in a person's breath, making drink-driving laws much easier to enforce. In 1980 Candace Lightner, whose 13-year-old daughter had been killed by a drunk driver, founded Mothers Against Drunk Driving in California, and campaigns like it changed public attitudes in many countries. Lower speed limits near schools, speed cameras, helmets for motorcyclists and cyclists, and better road designs such as roundabouts and safe crossings have all helped too."]},{"heading":"How Safe Are We Now?","paragraphs":["So, has all this worked? In many countries, the answer is a strong yes. In the United States in the mid-1960s, about five people died for every 100 million miles driven. In recent years the figure has been around 1.3, even though Americans now drive several times more miles in total. In Great Britain, almost 8,000 people died on the roads in 1966; in recent years the number has been around 1,600, despite far more traffic. Seatbelts, crumple zones, airbags, ABS, better roads, better emergency medicine and tougher laws all played a part.","But the world as a whole still has a very serious problem. The World Health Organization's 2023 global report estimated that about 1.19 million people die in road crashes every year, and its latest fact sheet gives a similar figure of about 1.16 million. That is roughly one person every half-minute. Between 20 and 50 million more are injured. Road injuries are the leading cause of death for children and young people aged 5 to 29 worldwide.","The danger is not shared equally. According to the WHO, about 92 percent of road deaths happen in low- and middle-income countries, even though those countries have only around 60 percent of the world's vehicles. More than half of the people killed are not inside cars at all: they are pedestrians, cyclists and motorcyclists, the people with the least protection, just like Bridget Driscoll in 1896.","The United Nations has set a goal of halving road deaths and injuries by 2030. Experts talk about a 'safe system' approach: accepting that people will always make mistakes, and designing cars, roads, speeds and rules so that those mistakes do not cost lives. Sweden's 'Vision Zero' policy, adopted in 1997, aims for a future where nobody is killed or seriously injured on the roads at all.","Today engineers are working on cars that can brake automatically if they sense a pedestrian, warn drivers who drift out of their lane, and protect people outside the car with softer bonnets and even pedestrian airbags. Yet while the fight against crashes was going on, another, quieter danger was building up. It could not be seen in a crash test, and no seatbelt could protect anyone from it. It was coming out of the tailpipe."]}],"deepDive":"The physics of car safety comes down to one idea: what injures people is not speed but sudden changes in speed. The force on a body depends on how quickly it decelerates. A person moving at 50 km/h who stops in one-hundredth of a second experiences a force roughly ten times greater than if the same stop is stretched over a tenth of a second. Crumple zones, seatbelts that stretch slightly, belt pretensioners and load limiters, and airbags that vent gas as you sink into them are all ways of lengthening that stopping time and spreading the force over the strongest parts of the body. Kinetic energy grows with the square of speed, which is why speed limits matter so much: a crash at 60 km/h carries about 44 percent more energy than one at 50 km/h. The WHO estimates that each 1 percent rise in average speed increases the risk of a fatal crash by about 4 percent, and a pedestrian hit at around 30 km/h is far more likely to survive than one hit at 50 km/h. Historians and safety researchers also debate why road deaths fell. Engineering improvements are one part. Others include seatbelt laws, drink-driving enforcement, better trauma care and ambulances, divided motorways, and more cautious driving cultures. Some economists, notably Sam Peltzman in the 1970s, argued that safety features might encourage riskier driving and cancel out some benefits, an idea called risk compensation. Later studies generally found that safety features still saved many lives overall, though the effect is not always as large as engineers expected. Another debate is about fairness: crash-test dummies were long based on an average man, and some studies suggest women have faced higher injury risks in comparable crashes, which is why new female dummies are being developed. Finally, the claims of firsts need care. Mary Ward is often called the first motor-vehicle fatality, but earlier steam-carriage accidents, such as the 1834 boiler explosion near Paisley in Scotland, killed passengers. Volvo's 'more than a million lives saved' is the company's own estimate, but independent studies agree that seatbelts are among the most effective safety devices ever invented.","funFact":"When Volvo introduced Nils Bohlin's three-point seatbelt in 1959, it let other carmakers use the patent for free, so a single design ended up protecting drivers of almost every brand of car in the world.","check":{"q":"What does a crumple zone do in a crash?","a":"It crushes in a controlled way to absorb energy and slow the car more gently, protecting the people inside","wrong":["It makes the car bounce back off whatever it hits","It pumps air into the tyres so the car stops faster","It locks the doors so nobody can get out"]},"sources":[{"title":"Mary Ward (scientist)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Ward_(scientist)"},{"title":"Death of Bridget Driscoll","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Bridget_Driscoll"},{"title":"Mary Anderson (inventor)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Anderson_(inventor)"},{"title":"Béla Barényi","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A9la_Bar%C3%A9nyi"},{"title":"Nils Bohlin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nils_Bohlin"},{"title":"Unsafe at Any Speed","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unsafe_at_Any_Speed"},{"title":"Road traffic injuries (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/road-traffic-injuries"},{"title":"Airbag","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbag"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/16-1.jpg","alt":"Mary Anderson's 1903 US patent No. 743,801 for a 'window cleaning device', the first windshield wiper","file":"Anderson Window Cleaning Device 1903.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anderson_Window_Cleaning_Device_1903.png","author":"Mary Anderson (Life time: 1866-1953)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/16-2.jpg","alt":"A 1961 Volvo PV544. This model was the first car sold with Nils Bohlin's three-point seatbelt","file":"Dülmen, Merfeld, Volvo PV 544 B18 -- 2021 -- 0075-9.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BClmen,_Merfeld,_Volvo_PV_544_B18_--_2021_--_0075-9.jpg","author":"Dietmar Rabich","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/16-3.jpg","alt":"Two Hybrid III crash test dummies sitting in a Hyundai Elantra, being prepared for a safety test","file":"V08383P339.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V08383P339.jpg","author":"Calspan Corporation, National Highway Traffic Safety Administration","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4017},{"n":17,"title":"Smog, Lead and Oil: The Car's Hidden Costs","when":"1921 – today","summary":"Cars gave people freedom, but their exhaust carried hidden dangers: poisonous lead, eye-stinging smog and invisible carbon dioxide. Brave scientists tracked down the problems, and new laws and clever inventions like the catalytic converter made the air much cleaner.","sections":[{"heading":"A Knock in the Engine","paragraphs":["Imagine riding in a shiny new car in about 1920. You press the pedal to climb a hill, and from under the hood comes a sharp, metallic rattle: ping-ping-ping, like someone tapping the engine with a tiny hammer. Drivers called it knock. It was more than an annoying sound. Knock meant the fuel inside the cylinders was exploding at the wrong moment, slamming against the piston instead of pushing it smoothly. It wasted power, overheated engines and, if it went on long enough, could crack or melt parts.","Engineers knew there was a cure for knock in theory. If engines could squeeze the fuel-air mixture harder, they would be more powerful and use less fuel. But the harder you squeezed ordinary gasoline, the more it knocked. So carmakers were stuck building low-compression engines that wasted a lot of their fuel. Whoever found a way to stop knock would unlock cars that were faster, stronger and cheaper to run.","At General Motors, the problem landed on the desk of Charles Kettering, the same inventive engineer whose electric self-starter had ended hand-cranking in 1912. Kettering's research team in Dayton, Ohio, included a young engineer named Thomas Midgley Jr., born in 1889. Midgley was not trained as a chemist, but he was curious, energetic and willing to try almost anything. For several years he and his helpers mixed one substance after another into gasoline, ran it through a test engine and listened.","Some of their attempts sound funny today. Iodine helped a little but was expensive and corroded metal. A compound containing tellurium worked quite well, but it gave off a garlic-like stink so powerful and long-lasting that it clung to the people who handled it. Nobody wanted to buy fuel that made them smell. The team kept searching through the chemical elements, looking for heavy metal compounds that might calm the explosion.","On 9 December 1921, Midgley's team tried a liquid called tetraethyl lead, often shortened to TEL. It is a compound in which a single atom of lead is attached to four small carbon-and-hydrogen groups. A tiny dose, just a few drops in a large tank of gasoline, made the knock vanish. It was cheap to make in bulk, and General Motors could patent its use. To the engineers it looked like a perfect answer.","There was one word the company did not want customers to see: lead. People already knew that lead was poisonous. Painters, plumbers and miners who worked with it often became ill. So when the new fuel went on sale in the early 1920s, it was marketed simply as 'Ethyl'. In 1924 General Motors and Standard Oil of New Jersey created a joint company, the Ethyl Gasoline Corporation, to make and sell it. Gas stations soon advertised Ethyl as the premium fuel for powerful engines."]},{"heading":"The Poison in the Pump","paragraphs":["Making tetraethyl lead turned out to be far more dangerous than adding it to a fuel tank. In 1924 a series of disasters struck the workers who produced it. At a Standard Oil refinery in New Jersey, men who handled the liquid began acting strangely. Some had terrible nightmares and hallucinations, some trembled and lost their balance, and some became violent or confused. Five workers there died and many more were badly hurt. Newspapers gave the fuel a frightening nickname: 'loony gas'.","It was not only one plant. Workers also fell sick at DuPont's factory in Deepwater, New Jersey, a major site where TEL was manufactured, and at the research labs in Ohio. Counting the different companies involved, about 17 workers died from lead poisoning in the 1920s. Several cities and states briefly banned the sale of leaded gasoline while officials argued about what to do.","Tetraethyl lead is so dangerous because it is a neurotoxin, meaning it damages the nervous system. Unlike many forms of lead, it soaks easily through skin and can cross into the brain. Doctors today know that even small amounts of lead, breathed in or swallowed over many years, can harm memory and learning, and that young children are especially at risk because their brains are still growing.","The companies fought hard to save their product. On 30 October 1924, Thomas Midgley gave a press conference meant to calm the public. In front of reporters he poured tetraethyl lead over his hands and then held a bottle of it under his nose, breathing the vapour for about a minute, to show it was safe. What he did not tell them was that he had already suffered from lead poisoning himself and had taken time off work to recover. It was a dramatic show, but it proved nothing about the long-term risks.","In 1925 the US government held a conference and set up a committee to study the question. Its report found no clear proof of danger to the public at the time, and leaded gasoline went back on sale. For the next forty years, most research on the health effects of lead was paid for by the industry itself. The leading expert, Robert Kehoe, argued that the lead people carried in their bodies was natural and normal, and that no one needed to worry unless there was clear evidence of harm.","So leaded fuel spread around the world. By the middle of the 20th century, almost every car on Earth burned it. Each time a driver pressed the accelerator, a fine mist of lead particles puffed out of the tailpipe and settled onto roads, gardens, playgrounds and into people's lungs. The poison was invisible, and it would take an unusually stubborn scientist to prove how far it had travelled."]},{"heading":"The Scientist Who Couldn't Get Clean Air in His Lab","paragraphs":["Clair Patterson, born in Iowa in 1922, was not trying to fight pollution at all. He wanted to answer one of the biggest questions in science: how old is the Earth? His plan was to measure tiny amounts of lead in very old rocks and meteorites. Some kinds of lead are slowly made when uranium breaks down, so by measuring the lead carefully you can work out how much time has passed, a bit like reading a clock that started ticking when the planet formed.","There was a maddening problem. Every sample Patterson tested seemed to contain far too much lead. His measurements kept being ruined by contamination: lead from the air, from dust, from glassware, even from his own hands. To fix it, he built one of the world's first 'clean rooms', a lab where the air was filtered, surfaces were scrubbed again and again, and scientists wore special clothing. Using lead from a meteorite that crashed in Arizona long ago, Patterson, using a method developed with his colleague George Tilton, calculated an age for the Earth of 4.55 billion years in 1956. That number has stood the test of time remarkably well.","But Patterson could not stop wondering why lead was everywhere. Where had it all come from? He began measuring lead in seawater, in deep ocean water and in snow. Later he studied ice cores, long tubes of ice drilled out of the Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets, where each layer holds snow from a particular year. The deep, old layers contained only tiny traces of lead. Then, in the layers from recent decades, lead shot upward, rising steeply after tetraethyl lead was added to gasoline.","In 1965 Patterson published a paper arguing that people living in industrial countries carried far more lead in their bodies than humans in earlier ages and that much of it came from car exhaust. This was a direct challenge to Kehoe and the lead industry, who insisted that the levels were natural. Patterson paid a price for speaking out. Several research organisations refused to give him contracts, and in 1971 he was left off a National Research Council panel on lead pollution, even though he was one of the world's leading experts.","He kept going anyway, testifying to lawmakers and collecting more evidence. Other scientists and doctors added to the case, finding links between children's blood lead levels and their health and learning. Slowly the tide turned. In 1973 the US Environmental Protection Agency issued rules to phase down lead in gasoline, starting in 1976, and on 1 January 1996 leaded fuel was banned for road vehicles in the United States. As lead fell from gasoline, the amount of lead in Americans' blood dropped dramatically.","Other countries followed. Japan was the first to ban leaded petrol completely, in 1986. The European Union and the United Kingdom removed it from sale on 1 January 2000. In 2002 the UN Environment Programme (UNEP) launched a partnership to help the remaining countries switch. The last country to sell leaded petrol for cars was Algeria, which stopped in July 2021. On 30 August 2021, UNEP declared that the era of leaded petrol for cars was over worldwide. It estimated that ending it would prevent more than a million early deaths every year. A few small aircraft with piston engines still burn leaded fuel, and regulators are working on replacing it too."]},{"heading":"The Day Los Angeles Thought It Was Under Attack","paragraphs":["On 26 July 1943, in the middle of World War II, a thick brownish haze rolled over downtown Los Angeles. People's eyes stung and streamed with tears. Their throats burned, and they could barely see a few blocks down the street. Some residents feared the city was being attacked with chemical weapons. It was not an attack. It was something new, and it would come back again and again: smog.","The word smog had been invented decades earlier by mixing 'smoke' and 'fog', to describe the dirty coal-smoke fogs of cities like London. London's worst disaster, the Great Smog of December 1952, was a winter soup of soot and sulfur from millions of coal fires, and it killed thousands of people in just a few days. But the Los Angeles haze was different. It appeared on bright, sunny days, not foggy ones, and the city did not burn much coal. Officials blamed factories, a rubber plant, backyard trash burning and oil refineries. Closing some of them did not make the haze go away.","Geography made things worse. Los Angeles sits in a wide, flat basin with the Pacific Ocean on one side and mountains on the others. A cool ocean current often creates what scientists call an inversion layer: a lid of warmer air sitting on top of cooler air near the ground. Normally warm air near the ground rises and carries pollution away. Under an inversion, the dirty air is trapped like smoke under an upside-down bowl. And by the 1940s, Los Angeles was a city built around cars, with more of them every year.","The scientist who solved the mystery was Arie Haagen-Smit, a Dutch-born chemist at the California Institute of Technology (Caltech) in Pasadena. Born in Utrecht in 1900, he was an expert on plant chemistry and on the substances that give foods their flavours and smells; he had even studied what makes a pineapple taste like a pineapple. That skill at identifying tiny amounts of chemicals in the air turned out to be exactly what the smog problem needed.","Starting in 1948, Haagen-Smit collected and studied smoggy air. He found that one of its main ingredients was ozone, a form of oxygen with three atoms instead of two, which irritates eyes and lungs and damages plants and rubber. But ozone was not coming straight out of any chimney. In the early 1950s he showed how it was being made right in the sky. Car exhaust contains nitrogen oxides, created when the heat of burning fuel makes nitrogen and oxygen from the air combine, and also unburned bits of fuel called hydrocarbons. When strong sunshine hits this mixture, it sets off a chain of chemical reactions that produce ozone and other irritating chemicals. The sunny weather that made California famous was cooking car exhaust into smog.","This kind of pollution is called photochemical smog, from 'photo', meaning light. Haagen-Smit even recreated it in the lab by shining light on a mix of exhaust-like gases and smelling and measuring what came out. Car companies and oil companies at first did not want to believe him, and some scientists hired by industry tried to prove him wrong. But his experiments held up. In 1968 Governor Ronald Reagan appointed him the first chairman of the California Air Resources Board, the state agency that would become one of the toughest car-pollution regulators in the world."]},{"heading":"Cleaning Up the Tailpipe","paragraphs":["By the late 1960s, smog was no longer just a Los Angeles problem. Cities across the United States and the world had dirty air, and millions of people were becoming worried about pollution. The first Earth Day, on 22 April 1970, brought millions of Americans out to demonstrate for a cleaner environment. Politicians noticed. On 2 December 1970 the United States created the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), and on 31 December 1970 President Richard Nixon signed a major new version of the Clean Air Act.","The 1970 law set a startling goal for the car industry: new cars had to cut the harmful gases coming out of their tailpipes by about 90 percent within a few years. Carmakers protested that it could not be done in time, and deadlines were pushed back more than once. But the pressure forced engineers to find a solution, and the solution turned out to be a clever metal box bolted into the exhaust pipe: the catalytic converter.","A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a chemical reaction without being used up itself. Inside a catalytic converter is a ceramic block shaped like a honeycomb, with thousands of tiny channels that give it a huge surface area. The walls of those channels are coated with a thin layer of rare, precious metals: platinum, palladium and rhodium. As the hot exhaust gases rush through, they touch these metals, and harmful gases are rearranged into less harmful ones. Poisonous carbon monoxide is turned into carbon dioxide. Unburned hydrocarbons become carbon dioxide and water. In later 'three-way' converters, which American and Canadian cars began using from 1981, nitrogen oxides are also split back into harmless nitrogen and oxygen.","Most new gasoline cars sold in the United States from the 1975 model year on had a catalytic converter. Converters need to be hot to work, roughly 400 degrees Celsius, which is why they are mounted close to the engine and why a car pollutes the most in its first minute or two after a cold start.","There was a catch, and it brings our story back to lead. Lead in the exhaust coats the precious metals inside the converter and ruins them, a problem engineers call 'poisoning' the catalyst. A single tank of leaded fuel could badly damage a converter. So cars with catalytic converters had to run on unleaded gasoline, and they were given narrower fuel filler pipes so the wider nozzles at leaded pumps would not fit. The need to protect converters became one of the strongest reasons to remove lead from gasoline altogether.","Governments also began checking cars already on the road. In many places, cars must pass regular emissions tests, where a machine measures what comes out of the tailpipe or reads the car's computer to make sure the pollution controls are working. Together, these rules made a big difference. According to figures reported from the EPA, since 1970 emissions of carbon monoxide from vehicles and other mobile sources in the US have fallen by more than 80 percent and nitrogen oxides by more than half, even though there are now far more cars driving far more miles. One modern side effect: because converters contain valuable metals, thieves sometimes crawl under parked cars and saw them off."]},{"heading":"Lines at the Pump: The Oil Shock of 1973","paragraphs":["Pollution was not the only hidden cost of the car. There was also the question of where the fuel came from. By the early 1970s, the United States, Western Europe and Japan were all burning enormous amounts of oil, and a growing share of it came from the Middle East. Gasoline was cheap, and big American cars with huge engines often managed only around 10 to 15 miles per gallon.","In October 1973, war broke out when Egypt and Syria attacked Israel. In response to Western support for Israel, the Arab members of OPEC, an organisation of oil-exporting countries, cut their oil production and announced an embargo: they would stop selling oil to the United States, the Netherlands and several other countries. The embargo lasted until March 1974. Within a few months the world price of oil roughly quadrupled, from about 3 dollars a barrel to nearly 12 dollars.","For ordinary drivers, the result was a shock. In the United States, the average price of a gallon of gasoline jumped from about 38.5 cents in May 1973 to about 55 cents a year later. Worse, there often was not enough. Cars lined up for blocks outside gas stations, sometimes waiting for hours, and some stations hung up signs saying they had run out. President Nixon asked gas stations not to sell fuel on Saturday nights and Sundays, some places limited sales to certain days, and people were asked to turn off Christmas lights to save energy.","In Europe, several countries banned driving on Sundays. In the Netherlands, one of the countries targeted by the embargo, the motorways stood empty on those car-free Sundays, and people walked, roller-skated and rode bicycles and even horses down the middle of the highways. Photographs of families strolling along empty motorways became one of the most famous images of the crisis.","The oil shock changed what people wanted to drive. Many buyers switched from heavy 'gas guzzlers' to smaller, more fuel-efficient cars, and the small, well-made cars from Japan and West Germany won millions of new fans, a story told in Part 12. Governments also acted. In 1975 the US Congress passed the Energy Policy and Conservation Act, which created fuel economy rules called CAFE, short for Corporate Average Fuel Economy. Each carmaker's new cars had to reach a certain average number of miles per gallon. The first target for cars was 18 miles per gallon in the 1978 model year, rising to 27.5 miles per gallon by 1985. Similar fuel economy and carbon rules now exist in Europe, Japan, China and many other places.","The 1973 crisis taught the world a lesson it has had to relearn several times since: when a country's cars depend almost entirely on one fuel that comes from far away, events on the other side of the world can empty its gas stations."]},{"heading":"The Gas You Cannot See or Smell","paragraphs":["Even a perfectly tuned car with a brand-new catalytic converter still sends one gas out of its tailpipe in huge amounts: carbon dioxide, or CO2. In fact, the catalytic converter makes more of it, because its whole job is to finish burning the leftovers. Carbon dioxide is not poisonous in the way carbon monoxide or lead are. You breathe it out every few seconds. It has no colour and no smell. For most of the car's history, nobody thought of it as pollution at all.","Here is a puzzle that surprises many people. When you burn one litre of petrol, which weighs only about three quarters of a kilogram, it produces about 2.3 kilograms of carbon dioxide, around three times heavier than the fuel itself. How can that be? The answer is that most of the weight comes from the air. Petrol is made of hydrogen and carbon atoms. When it burns, each carbon atom grabs two oxygen atoms from the air, and oxygen atoms are heavier than carbon atoms. The US Environmental Protection Agency calculates that burning one US gallon of gasoline makes about 8,887 grams of CO2.","Add that up over a year and the numbers grow fast. The EPA estimates that a typical American passenger car, driving about 11,500 miles a year at around 22 miles per gallon, puts out roughly 4.6 tonnes of carbon dioxide every year. That is about as heavy as an adult elephant, released invisibly into the sky one puff at a time. Multiply it by well over a billion vehicles worldwide and you get one of the biggest sources of carbon dioxide on the planet.","Why does that matter? Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas. It lets sunlight pass through the atmosphere but traps some of the heat that the Earth gives off, a little like a blanket. A certain amount is natural and keeps our planet warm enough to live on. But since the Industrial Revolution, burning coal, oil and gas has raised the amount of carbon dioxide in the air sharply, and the Earth's average temperature has risen along with it. Scientists link this warming to hotter heatwaves, rising seas and changing weather patterns.","Transport is one of the largest pieces of the puzzle. The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that transport produces roughly a fifth to a quarter of the world's energy-related carbon dioxide emissions, and that most of this comes from road vehicles: cars, vans, trucks and buses, rather than ships, trains or planes. Unlike smog or lead, carbon dioxide cannot be cleaned out of the exhaust by a box of precious metals. Burning fuel made of carbon always makes it. The only ways to cut it are to burn less fuel, to use fuels that do not contain fossil carbon, or to stop burning fuel at all."]},{"heading":"Dieselgate: When a Car Learned to Cheat","paragraphs":["Rules to clean up cars only work if the tests that check them are honest. In 2015 the world discovered that one of the biggest carmakers on Earth had been cheating for years.","Diesel engines, invented by Rudolf Diesel in the 1890s, are very efficient and produce less carbon dioxide per kilometre than many gasoline engines. But they tend to produce more nitrogen oxides, the same gases that help make smog and harm people's lungs. Cleaning nitrogen oxides out of diesel exhaust is difficult and can make a car slightly less powerful or less economical. In the 2000s, Volkswagen advertised its diesel cars as 'clean diesel', and they sold well in Europe and the United States.","Then a small group of researchers decided to check. The International Council on Clean Transportation, a research organisation, paid scientists at West Virginia University to measure the emissions of diesel cars while they were driven on real roads, using portable testing equipment fitted to the cars. In 2013 and 2014 the team found something strange. In the official laboratory tests, Volkswagen's diesels were clean. On ordinary roads, some were pouring out many times the legal limit of nitrogen oxides.","The explanation turned out to be hidden in the cars' computer software. The engine computer could tell when the car was being tested in a lab, for example from the way the steering wheel stayed still while the wheels turned on rollers. During a test, it switched the pollution controls fully on. Out on the road, it turned them down, letting the car emit up to about 40 times the legal limit of nitrogen oxides. A secret system like this is called a 'defeat device', because it defeats the purpose of the test.","In September 2015 the US EPA publicly accused Volkswagen of breaking the Clean Air Act, and the company admitted that about 11 million diesel cars worldwide contained the software, including about 482,000 in the United States. Its chief executive, Martin Winterkorn, resigned within days. In 2017 Volkswagen pleaded guilty to criminal charges in the United States, and several managers and engineers faced prosecution. By 2020 the scandal had cost the company more than 30 billion dollars in fines, settlements, repairs and buying back cars. The press nicknamed it 'Dieselgate'.","The scandal had big consequences. Governments in Europe and elsewhere introduced new tests that measure cars' emissions on real roads, not just in laboratories. Sales of diesel cars in Europe began a long decline. And many people began to ask a bigger question. If even modern engines with clever cleaning systems could not easily meet the rules, maybe the answer was not a cleaner engine at all, but a car with no exhaust pipe."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of science often use the leaded gasoline story as a case study in how evidence, money and regulation interact. Several things made the case hard to settle. First, lead's harm is slow and spread out: no single driver or child falls ill from one tank of fuel, but tiny doses across hundreds of millions of people add up. Second, for about four decades nearly all research on lead and health was paid for by the industry, and its leading scientist, Robert Kehoe, argued for what became known as the 'Kehoe paradigm': the burden of proof lay on critics to show clear harm, rather than on industry to show safety. Clair Patterson's ice-core and ocean measurements broke that logic by showing that modern lead levels were far above natural background. Later studies of children by researchers such as Herbert Needleman linked even modest blood lead levels to lower IQ scores and attention problems. Estimates of the total damage remain debated, but they are large: a 2011 study estimated hundreds of millions of lost IQ points worldwide each year while leaded fuel was in use, and UNEP has credited the global phase-out with more than a million fewer premature deaths a year. Some economists have proposed a 'lead-crime hypothesis', linking falling childhood lead exposure to falling violent crime about two decades later; it is an active and contested research area, not a settled fact. The chemistry of smog is also more complex than a single reaction. Sunlight splits nitrogen dioxide into nitric oxide and a free oxygen atom, which joins an oxygen molecule to make ozone. Normally nitric oxide would destroy that ozone again, but hydrocarbons interfere, converting nitric oxide back into nitrogen dioxide without using up ozone, so ozone builds up through the day. That is why smog peaks on hot, sunny afternoons, and why controlling both nitrogen oxides and hydrocarbons matters. Finally, the carbon dioxide numbers follow straightforward chemistry: carbon has an atomic mass of 12 and carbon dioxide 44, so each kilogram of carbon burned yields about 3.67 kilograms of CO2. Since petrol is roughly 85 percent carbon by mass and a litre weighs about 0.74 kg, one litre gives roughly 2.3 kg of CO2.","funFact":"Thomas Midgley Jr., the engineer who introduced leaded gasoline, later helped develop CFCs, the chemicals that damaged the ozone layer, leading one environmental historian to write that he 'had more adverse impact on the atmosphere than any other single organism in Earth's history.'","check":{"q":"Why did cars with catalytic converters need unleaded gasoline?","a":"Lead coats and ruins the precious metals inside the converter","wrong":["Lead made the engine knock louder","Unleaded fuel was the only fuel sold in 1975","Lead caused the converter to make more ozone"]},"sources":[{"title":"Tetraethyllead","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetraethyllead"},{"title":"Thomas Midgley Jr.","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Midgley_Jr."},{"title":"Clair Cameron Patterson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clair_Cameron_Patterson"},{"title":"1973 oil crisis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1973_oil_crisis"},{"title":"Arie Jan Haagen-Smit","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arie_Jan_Haagen-Smit"},{"title":"Catalytic converter","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catalytic_converter"},{"title":"Greenhouse Gas Emissions from a Typical Passenger Vehicle","publisher":"US Environmental Protection Agency","url":"https://www.epa.gov/greenvehicles/greenhouse-gas-emissions-typical-passenger-vehicle"},{"title":"Volkswagen emissions scandal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volkswagen_emissions_scandal"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/17-1.jpg","alt":"An old Atlantic gas pump with an 'Ethyl' anti-knock sign, which meant leaded gasoline (Pennsylvania, 1982)","file":"Atlantic Ethyl pump, Rt. 23, Leola, Pennsylvania LCCN2017707812.tif","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlantic_Ethyl_pump,_Rt._23,_Leola,_Pennsylvania_LCCN2017707812.tif","author":"John Margolies","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/17-2.jpg","alt":"Thick smog hides the buildings of the Los Angeles Civic Center in 1948","file":"View of part of the Los Angeles Civic Center masked by smog in 1948.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_part_of_the_Los_Angeles_Civic_Center_masked_by_smog_in_1948.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/17-3.jpg","alt":"The honeycomb core from inside a catalytic converter, on display at the Science Museum in London","file":"Catalytic Converter Interior.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catalytic_Converter_Interior.jpg","author":"Cyrogigabyte","license":"CC0"}],"words":4367},{"n":18,"title":"The Electric Comeback","when":"1990s – today","summary":"Electric cars were popular in 1900, then almost vanished. Thanks to a powerful new battery invented by three scientists, they came roaring (well, humming) back, and now millions of quiet electric cars are sold every year.","sections":[{"heading":"Parked, Not Forgotten","paragraphs":["Remember the quiet electric cars of 1900, gliding through New York and Paris while gasoline cars clattered and smoked? In Part 7 we saw them lose the race: their heavy lead-acid batteries could only carry them a short way, recharging took hours, and once cheap oil, long roads and the electric self-starter arrived, gasoline won. For most of the 20th century, electric vehicles survived only in quiet corners: milk floats delivering bottles in Britain, golf carts, forklift trucks in warehouses.","The real problem was always the battery. A battery stores energy in chemicals, and the question that matters for a car is how much energy you can pack into each kilogram. Gasoline is astonishingly good at this. A full tank holding about 50 litres, weighing less than an adult, can carry a car 600 kilometres or more. To store the same useful energy in old lead-acid batteries, you would need a pile weighing well over a tonne. An electric car of 1900, or even of 1990, simply could not carry enough energy to compete.","So for the electric car to come back, three things had to change. Batteries had to store much more energy for their weight. They had to become much cheaper. And someone had to want electric cars badly enough to build them. The push came first from the place where smog had been discovered: California.","In 1990 the California Air Resources Board, the agency Arie Haagen-Smit had once led, made a bold rule. To keep selling cars in California, the biggest carmakers would have to sell a small but growing share of zero-emission vehicles, cars with no exhaust at all. In practice, that meant electric cars. The same year, General Motors showed off a sleek electric concept car called the Impact at the Los Angeles Auto Show. The electric car was about to get a second chance."]},{"heading":"The EV1: The Car That Was Crushed","paragraphs":["The Impact became a real car: the General Motors EV1, built from 1996 to 1999. It was a low, smooth two-seater shaped like a teardrop to slip through the air with as little drag as possible. It was built specially as an electric car from the start, not a gasoline car with its engine swapped out, and it was surprisingly quick and quiet. Drivers who tried it often loved it.","But the EV1 still had the old battery problem. The first version used lead-acid batteries and could go roughly 60 to 100 miles (about 100 to 160 kilometres) on a charge. A later version used nickel-metal hydride batteries, the same type later used in many hybrid cars, and could manage roughly 75 to 150 miles. Only 1,117 EV1s were built in total. And here is the unusual part: you could not buy one. The cars were only leased, which means drivers rented them for a few years and the cars still belonged to GM.","Meanwhile, carmakers fought the California rule, arguing that customers did not want electric cars and that the technology was not ready. The rule was weakened. GM ended the EV1 program and, between late 2003 and 2004, took back nearly all the cars from their drivers. Some drivers begged to buy their EV1s and were refused. Most of the cars were crushed and shredded. About forty were given to museums and universities with their electric drive systems disabled.","The end of the EV1 made many people angry. A 2006 documentary film was even titled 'Who Killed the Electric Car?'. GM said the cars were expensive to support and that it could not keep supplying spare parts for so few vehicles. Critics said the company had given up on a promising technology too soon. Either way, the EV1 proved two things. Electric cars could be fun and practical for daily driving, and they still needed a much better battery. That battery already existed. It was hiding inside people's video cameras and mobile phones."]},{"heading":"The Rocking-Chair Battery","paragraphs":["The battery that brought electric cars back to life is the lithium-ion battery, the same kind that powers phones, laptops and cordless tools today. Its story stretches across three countries and three scientists, who shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it.","Lithium is the lightest metal in the universe, and it gives up its electrons very eagerly, which makes it perfect for storing energy. But pure lithium metal is also dangerous: it reacts violently with water and can catch fire. In the 1970s, during the oil crisis described in Part 17, a British-born chemist named M. Stanley Whittingham, working for the oil company Exxon in the United States, built an early rechargeable lithium battery. It used a material called titanium disulfide that could soak up lithium between its layers. It worked, but with a lithium metal electrode it was costly and could catch fire, and Exxon dropped the project.","In 1980 John Goodenough, an American professor working at the University of Oxford in England, found a better material for the positive side of the battery: lithium cobalt oxide. It gave a much higher voltage, about 4 volts, so the battery could store far more energy. Goodenough was already in his late fifties when he made this discovery, and he kept working on batteries into his nineties. When he received the Nobel Prize in 2019 at the age of 97, he became the oldest person ever to win one.","The last piece came from Japan. In 1985 Akira Yoshino, a chemist at the company Asahi Kasei, replaced the dangerous lithium metal on the negative side with a form of carbon made from petroleum coke. Now neither side contained pure lithium metal. Instead, lithium ions, which are lithium atoms that have given away one electron, could travel back and forth between the two sides. In 1991 Sony, working with Asahi Kasei, began selling the first commercial lithium-ion batteries, at first for a Sony video camera.","Here is how it works. A lithium-ion battery has two electrodes with a liquid called an electrolyte between them. When you use the battery, lithium ions travel through the electrolyte from the negative side to the positive side, and tuck themselves between layers of the material there, while electrons flow the long way round, through the wires of your phone or car, doing useful work. When you charge it, electricity pushes the ions back the other way. Because the ions rock back and forth between the two sides, scientists sometimes call it a 'rocking-chair battery'.","Over the following decades, lithium-ion batteries kept getting better and cheaper. In the three decades after 1991 the amount of energy they could hold in the same space roughly tripled. According to the research company BloombergNEF, the average price of a lithium-ion battery pack fell to about 115 dollars per kilowatt-hour in 2024, roughly 90 percent cheaper than in 2010. That one change, more than anything else, is what made the electric comeback possible."]},{"heading":"Hybrids, a Laptop-Battery Sports Car and the Leaf","paragraphs":["Before fully electric cars became practical, there was a halfway step: the hybrid, a car with both a fuel engine and an electric motor with a battery. As we saw in Part 7, Ferdinand Porsche had tried the idea around 1900. Almost a century later, Toyota made it a success. On 10 December 1997 the Toyota Prius went on sale in Japan, and it reached the rest of the world in 2000. Prius is a Latin word that means something like 'before' or 'first', and it was the first hybrid car built in really large numbers.","A Prius never needs to be plugged in. Its petrol engine and electric motor share the work, and a computer decides from moment to moment which to use. When the car pulls away slowly or creeps through traffic, it can run on electricity alone, silently. When it needs more power, the engine joins in. And when the driver brakes, the electric motor works backwards as a generator, turning the car's motion into electricity to recharge the battery instead of wasting it as heat. The result was a car that used far less fuel in city driving. By 2022 more than 5 million Prius cars had been sold, and hybrid systems spread to many other models.","The car that made electric cars exciting came from a small new company in California. Tesla Motors was founded on 1 July 2003 by two engineers, Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning. Elon Musk invested millions of dollars and became the company's chairman in 2004, and later its chief executive. Their idea was bold: rather than wait for special car batteries, they would wire together thousands of the small cylindrical lithium-ion cells already being mass-produced for laptop computers.","The result was the Tesla Roadster, delivered to its first customers in 2008. It was a two-seat sports car built on a chassis based on the British Lotus Elise. Under its body sat a battery pack weighing about 450 kilograms, made of 6,831 laptop-style cells, each about the size of a thick marker pen. The Roadster could go about 393 kilometres (244 miles) on a charge, the first production electric car able to travel more than 200 miles, and it could accelerate from 0 to 60 miles per hour in under 4 seconds. It was very expensive, around 100,000 dollars, and only about 2,450 were sold. But it changed people's minds. Electric cars no longer looked like slow golf carts. They looked fast and fun.","Tesla's next car, the Model S, was a large family saloon that went on sale in June 2012. It was built in Fremont, California, in a factory Tesla had bought that had once belonged to General Motors and Toyota. The battery sat flat under the floor, leaving room for luggage at both ends. Tesla also started building its own network of fast chargers, called Superchargers, in 2012. The Model S was named Motor Trend's Car of the Year for 2013, and it stayed in production until May 2026.","Big, established carmakers were moving too. In 2009 Mitsubishi began selling the small i-MiEV, one of the first modern electric cars made in series for ordinary roads. In December 2010 Nissan started delivering the Leaf, a sensible five-door hatchback designed to be an everyday family car at a much lower price than a Tesla. The first Leaf could travel about 160 kilometres on a charge, enough for most people's daily driving. It was the world's best-selling electric car in its early years, and in September 2020 Nissan built its 500,000th Leaf."]},{"heading":"China Plugs In, and the World Follows","paragraphs":["One of the biggest stories of the electric comeback started not in a car factory but in a battery factory. In 1995 a chemist named Wang Chuanfu founded a small company called BYD in Shenzhen, China, to make rechargeable batteries for mobile phones and other electronics. It grew into one of the world's largest battery makers. In 2003 BYD bought a small car company and began making cars of its own.","BYD moved quickly into electric power. In 2008 it showed the F3DM, described as the world's first production plug-in hybrid (though only small numbers were sold), and in 2009 it unveiled the e6, its first fully electric car. The company developed its own 'Blade' battery, using a chemistry called lithium iron phosphate, which contains no cobalt and is less likely to catch fire. In 2022 BYD stopped making cars that run only on petrol. Its sales rose from about 427,000 cars in 2020 to about 4.27 million in 2024, and in 2025 it overtook Tesla to become the world's largest maker of electric cars.","BYD is only one of many Chinese companies building electric cars, helped by years of government support, huge battery factories and a vast home market. In 2024 almost half of all new cars sold in China were electric or plug-in hybrid. Chinese cities also run enormous fleets of electric buses, taxis and scooters.","Around the world, the numbers have grown astonishingly fast. According to the International Energy Agency, more than 17 million electric cars, counting both fully electric cars and plug-in hybrids, were sold worldwide in 2024. That was about one in every five new cars. Ten years earlier, electric cars had been well under one percent of sales.","The country that went furthest is Norway. For years Norway made electric cars cheaper to buy by excusing them from big purchase taxes and from its 25 percent sales tax, and it let electric car drivers skip road tolls, park for free in many places and sometimes even use bus lanes. Norway set itself a goal that all new cars sold from 2025 should be zero-emission. In 2024 close to nine out of every ten new cars sold in Norway were fully electric. In the streets of Oslo today, a petrol car has become an unusual sight among new models.","Not every country is moving at the same speed. Electric cars are still a small share of sales in many parts of the world, where prices, charging networks and electricity supply are still catching up. But for the first time since 1900, the electric car is a serious contender in the race."]},{"heading":"Under the Floor: How an Electric Car Works","paragraphs":["Lift the hood of an electric car and you may find... a storage box for your bag. Many electric cars have a small boot at the front, sometimes nicknamed a 'frunk', because there is no big engine to fill the space. So where is everything?","The heart of the car is the battery pack, usually spread flat across the floor between the wheels like a giant chocolate bar. It is made of hundreds or thousands of individual lithium-ion cells, grouped into modules and wrapped in a strong, sealed case with its own cooling system, because batteries work best and last longest when they are neither too hot nor too cold. Placing this heavy pack low down gives the car a low centre of gravity, which helps it grip corners and makes it harder to tip over.","The battery stores electricity as direct current, which flows in one direction. Most car motors run on alternating current, which switches direction many times a second. So a box of electronics called an inverter converts one into the other and controls exactly how much power reaches the motor when the driver presses the pedal.","The motor itself is beautifully simple. It has a fixed outer part called the stator, wrapped in coils of copper wire, and a spinning inner part called the rotor. When electricity flows through the coils in the right pattern, it creates a magnetic field that sweeps around in a circle, dragging the rotor along with it. A gasoline engine has pistons, valves, camshafts, spark plugs and hundreds of moving parts, while an electric motor's main moving part is the rotor itself. Most electric cars do not even need a multi-speed gearbox, because a motor can spin happily from zero to well over 10,000 turns a minute. One single gear is enough.","Electric motors are also very strong from the moment they start turning. A gasoline engine has to rev up before it gives its best pulling force, called torque. An electric motor gives strong torque instantly. That is why even an ordinary electric family car can feel surprisingly quick when the traffic light turns green.","The biggest advantage is efficiency. As we saw in Part 8, a gasoline engine wastes most of its fuel as heat, and according to the US Department of Energy only about 12 to 30 percent of the energy in the fuel ends up moving the wheels. Electric motors turn roughly 85 to 90 percent or more of their electrical energy into motion, and the same agency estimates that an electric car delivers more than 77 percent of the energy it takes from the grid to its wheels, even after charging and other losses.","Electric cars also have a clever trick called regenerative braking. When the driver lifts off the pedal or brakes, the motor switches roles and becomes a generator. The car's forward motion spins it, producing electricity that flows back into the battery, and the effort of generating that electricity slows the car down. In ordinary cars, braking turns motion into wasted heat in the brake discs. In an electric car, a good part of that energy is saved for later, which is especially useful in stop-and-go city traffic. Many electric cars offer 'one-pedal driving', where simply easing off the accelerator slows the car strongly, though normal friction brakes are still there for hard stops.","Finally, charging. Most owners who have a driveway or garage charge at home overnight, plugging in like a phone. A full charge from an ordinary home charger can take anywhere from about 3 to 12 hours, depending on the car and the charger. On long trips, drivers stop at fast chargers along highways, which can fill a battery from low to about 80 percent in roughly half an hour, time for a meal and a stretch. Engineers are working to make charging faster still."]},{"heading":"Not So Simple: Mining, Money and Where the Power Comes From","paragraphs":["Electric cars are not magic, and they come with real challenges. The first that most people worry about is range: how far a car can go on one charge. Early cars like the first Leaf managed around 160 kilometres; many newer models can manage 400 kilometres or more, though cold weather, high speeds and heaters can reduce that. Charging still takes longer than filling a tank with petrol, and in some places there are not yet enough public chargers. Electric cars have also usually cost more to buy than similar petrol cars, mostly because of the battery, though prices have been falling and running costs are often lower.","Then there are the materials. Lithium-ion batteries need lithium, and many also use nickel, cobalt, manganese and graphite. All of these must be mined. In the salt flats of Chile's Atacama Desert, one of the driest places on Earth, lithium is produced by pumping up salty underground water and letting it evaporate in huge ponds, which uses large amounts of water in a region where water is scarce. Local communities have raised concerns about the effect on their land and wildlife.","Cobalt raises even harder questions. Most of the world's cobalt comes from the Democratic Republic of the Congo in central Africa. Some of it is dug by hand in small, unregulated mines where workers, sometimes including children, face dangerous conditions and toxic dust, as human-rights groups and journalists have documented. Carmakers and battery makers are under pressure to trace where their cobalt comes from, and many are switching to battery chemistries, such as lithium iron phosphate, that use no cobalt at all.","What happens to a battery when it is worn out? Car batteries last longer than many people expected: some studies of real cars have found that batteries often keep most of their capacity after many years of driving, and the US Department of Energy suggests they may last 12 to 15 years in moderate climates. Old car batteries can be given a 'second life' storing electricity for homes or power grids, and then recycled to recover valuable metals. Only a small share of old batteries used to be recycled, but large recycling plants are now being built, because recovered metals mean less new mining.","Finally, an electric car is only as clean as the electricity that charges it. A car with no exhaust pipe produces no pollution on the street, which is great for the air in cities. But if its electricity comes from a coal power station, some pollution and carbon dioxide are simply moved to the power station's chimney. Most studies find electric cars still produce less carbon dioxide over their whole lives than petrol cars in most countries, and the advantage grows as more electricity comes from wind, solar, hydro and other low-carbon sources. To get the full benefit, cleaner cars need cleaner power."]},{"heading":"Hydrogen: The Other Car With No Exhaust","paragraphs":["Batteries are not the only way to make an electric car. Another idea is to carry hydrogen gas and turn it into electricity on board, using a device called a fuel cell. In a fuel cell, hydrogen from the tank meets oxygen from the air across a thin membrane. Each hydrogen atom is split into a proton, which passes through the membrane, and an electron, which cannot, so it flows out through a circuit instead, powering an electric motor. On the far side, the protons and electrons meet oxygen and join up to make water. The only thing that comes out of the tailpipe is water vapour, sometimes dribbling out as a few drops.","The best-known hydrogen car is the Toyota Mirai, whose name means 'future' in Japanese. It went on sale in Japan on 15 December 2014 and was described as the first fuel cell car to be mass-produced and sold to the public. The Mirai stores its hydrogen in strong tanks made of carbon fibre at an enormous pressure of about 700 times normal air pressure. Filling up takes only about 3 to 5 minutes, much like a petrol car, and the second-generation Mirai can travel about 650 kilometres on a full tank. In 2021 one set a record by driving about 1,360 kilometres on a single fill.","So why aren't hydrogen cars everywhere? The biggest problem is finding somewhere to fill up. Hydrogen stations are expensive to build, and there are very few of them. Most countries have almost none, and even California, one of the main markets for hydrogen cars, had only 33 stations at the end of 2017; in 2024 Mirai owners there even sued Toyota over how hard it was to find fuel. Making hydrogen also takes a lot of energy, and today most hydrogen is made from natural gas, which releases carbon dioxide. By late 2022 only about 21,500 Mirais had been sold worldwide, a tiny number compared with millions of battery cars. Hydrogen may prove more useful for big trucks, buses, ships and factories than for family cars, but engineers are still testing many ideas.","Today, the electric car has come full circle. More than a century after electric taxis hummed through New York and La Jamais Contente zoomed past 100 kilometres per hour, electric motors are once again moving millions of people. But the biggest change of all might be still to come. Engineers are not only changing what powers the car. They are asking whether the car needs a human driver at all."]}],"deepDive":"Why did lithium-ion batteries get so much cheaper? Economists describe a 'learning curve': each time the world's total production of something doubles, its cost tends to fall by a fairly steady percentage, because factories get bigger, processes get smarter and suppliers compete. For lithium-ion cells the drop has been roughly 19 percent per doubling, and cell prices have fallen by about 99 percent since 1991. BloombergNEF's surveys put average pack prices at about 115 dollars per kilowatt-hour in 2024, roughly a tenth of their 2010 level. Many analysts have treated about 100 dollars per kilowatt-hour as the point where an electric car can cost about the same to build as a comparable petrol car, though that depends on raw-material prices, which can swing sharply, as lithium did in 2022. Battery chemistry is another active debate. Nickel-rich cells (often called NMC or NCA) store more energy per kilogram and suit long-range cars, while lithium iron phosphate (LFP) cells are cheaper, longer-lasting, safer and cobalt-free but somewhat heavier for the same energy. Researchers are also developing sodium-ion batteries, which avoid lithium altogether, and solid-state batteries, which replace the liquid electrolyte with a solid. Comparing the climate impact of electric and petrol cars requires a 'life-cycle assessment' that counts emissions from mining, manufacturing, driving and disposal. Making a battery creates extra emissions, so an electric car starts life with a larger carbon 'debt'. It pays this back over time because it emits nothing while driving and uses energy far more efficiently. How quickly it pays back depends on the electricity mix: fast in places with lots of hydro or wind power, like Norway, and more slowly where coal dominates. Most published studies find that, over a typical lifetime, battery electric cars produce substantially lower greenhouse gas emissions than similar petrol cars in most regions, and the gap is widening as grids get cleaner. Hydrogen faces an efficiency challenge: turning electricity into hydrogen, compressing it, and turning it back into electricity in a fuel cell loses much more energy than simply storing electricity in a battery, which is one reason most carmakers have focused on batteries.","funFact":"When John Goodenough received the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his work on lithium-ion batteries, he was 97 years old, the oldest person ever to win a Nobel Prize.","check":{"q":"What does regenerative braking do in an electric car?","a":"It uses the motor as a generator to recharge the battery while slowing down","wrong":["It cools the battery with water from the brakes","It switches the car to a petrol engine for stopping","It pumps hydrogen into the fuel cell"]},"sources":[{"title":"General Motors EV1","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Motors_EV1"},{"title":"Lithium-ion battery","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithium-ion_battery"},{"title":"Lithium-ion Battery Pack Prices See Largest Drop Since 2017, Falling to $115 per Kilowatt-Hour","publisher":"BloombergNEF","url":"https://about.bnef.com/insights/clean-transport/lithium-ion-battery-pack-prices-see-largest-drop-since-2017-falling-to-115-per-kilowatt-hour-bloombergnef/"},{"title":"Tesla Roadster (first generation)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesla_Roadster_(first_generation)"},{"title":"Toyota Prius","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toyota_Prius"},{"title":"BYD Auto","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BYD_Auto"},{"title":"All-Electric Vehicles","publisher":"US Department of Energy (fueleconomy.gov)","url":"https://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/evtech.shtml"},{"title":"Toyota Mirai","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toyota_Mirai"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/18-1.jpg","alt":"A red 1997 General Motors EV1 electric car with its charging paddle, at the California Automobile Museum","file":"1997 General Motors EV1.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1997_General_Motors_EV1.jpg","author":"DestinationFearFan","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/18-2.jpg","alt":"A 2008 Tesla Roadster, the first-generation electric sports car, at the AutoRAI show in Amsterdam (2009)","file":"2008 Tesla Roadster front.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2008_Tesla_Roadster_front.jpg","author":"Rutger van der Maar","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/18-3.jpg","alt":"Two plug-in hybrid cars charging at a public charging post beside a canal in Amsterdam, 2014","file":"Amsterdam car charging station, for two cars, Oudezijds Voorburgwal, 2 July 2014.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amsterdam_car_charging_station,_for_two_cars,_Oudezijds_Voorburgwal,_2_July_2014.jpg","author":"AxelBoldt","license":"CC0"}],"words":4065},{"n":19,"title":"Cars That Drive Themselves","when":"1925 – today","summary":"What if your car could drive you to school all by itself? People dreamed about it for 100 years, from a radio-controlled 'ghost car' in New York to robot vans racing across the desert. Today, in some cities, you can ride in a taxi with nobody in the driver's seat!","sections":[{"heading":"Ghost Cars and Magic Motorways","paragraphs":["Imagine standing on a busy New York sidewalk on a hot summer day in July 1925. Streetcars clang, delivery trucks rumble, and a horse-drawn milk wagon plods along beside honking automobiles. Then a shiny Chandler car rolls up Broadway, turning its wheel, beeping its horn and changing speed all by itself. You look through the window and gasp. Nobody is sitting in the driver's seat.","This was the 'American Wonder', a car shown off by a company called Houdina Radio Control. The trick was not magic, even though the name sounds a lot like the famous escape artist Harry Houdini. A second car followed close behind, carrying a radio transmitter. A person in that second car sent radio signals, and an antenna on the driverless car picked them up. Inside, small electric motors turned the signals into actions: steering left or right, speeding up, slowing down, sounding the horn.","The demonstration took place on 27 July 1925. The car drove up Broadway and down Fifth Avenue through heavy traffic. Newspaper reports said it barely missed trucks, other cars and a milk wagon, and in the end it crashed into a sedan. Nobody was badly hurt, but the message was clear. A car could be moved without a person holding the wheel, yet it still needed a human brain somewhere nearby, watching and deciding. The 'ghost car' was really a giant remote-controlled toy.","Historians are not even completely sure who 'Francis Houdina' was. His name appears in newspapers for only a few years, from 1925 to 1928, and some researchers think it may have been a made-up name. In the following years, similar 'phantom auto' shows toured American towns, thrilling crowds at fairs. They were fun, but they were not the future of transport. A radio-controlled car cannot think. Without someone steering from a distance, it would happily drive straight into a wall.","Fourteen years later, in 1939, millions of people came to the New York World's Fair to see what the future might hold. The biggest hit of all was a ride inside the General Motors building called Futurama. It was designed by Norman Bel Geddes, a stage designer and industrial designer famous for streamlined, futuristic shapes.","Visitors sat in comfortable moving chairs, 552 at a time, carried along a conveyor about one third of a mile long. The ride lasted about 18 minutes. Below them, as if they were flying in an aeroplane, spread a gigantic model landscape covering about an acre, roughly half the size of a football pitch. It held more than 500,000 tiny buildings, around a million miniature trees and about 50,000 little cars. Thousands of those model cars actually moved, gliding along a fourteen-lane superhighway that sliced across mountains, rivers and farmland.","Bel Geddes was showing America of 1960, twenty years in the future. In his imagined world, cars on the great motorways would be guided automatically, kept at safe distances and safe speeds by signals from the road itself. Drivers would be able to relax while their cars travelled quickly from city to city. In 1940 he described these ideas in a book called Magic Motorways. Business magazines reported that the show was so popular that people waited in long lines all day, with tens of thousands of visitors every day.","The real breakthrough would need something no one in 1939 had: a computer small enough and fast enough to ride inside a car, and the clever programs to let that computer see the world for itself."]},{"heading":"Ernst Dickmanns Teaches a Van to See","paragraphs":["In the 1980s, computers were still slow and bulky. A home computer of the time could barely play simple games with blocky graphics. Yet in Germany, an aerospace engineer named Ernst Dickmanns had a bold idea. Instead of burying cables in roads, why not give the car eyes? Video cameras could watch the road, and a computer could study the pictures to find the lane markings and work out where to steer.","Dickmanns and his team worked at the Bundeswehr University Munich, a university run by the German armed forces. Their first test vehicle was a large Mercedes-Benz van they named VaMoRs, packed with cameras and computers. The challenge was enormous. A camera produces a flood of data, many pictures every second, far more than computers of the 1980s could study in full. Dickmanns' clever solution was to let the computer pay attention only to the most useful parts of each picture, such as the edges of the lane, and to use what it already knew about how cars move to predict where those edges should appear next. He called this 'dynamic vision'. It is a bit like the way you do not stare at every leaf when you ride a bike; your eyes jump to the important things.","It worked. In 1986 the van drove itself on a test track, and by 1987 it was steering itself on an empty stretch of motorway at speeds of up to about 96 kilometres per hour (60 mph). For the time, this was astonishing.","Europe's car industry took notice. From 1987 to 1995 a huge European research programme called Prometheus, part of the EUREKA network and costing hundreds of millions of euros, studied how to make road traffic safer and smoother. Dickmanns' camera-based approach became one of its stars. At the programme's big final presentation in October 1994, on a motorway near Paris, two Mercedes sedans fitted with his team's systems, called VaMP and VITA-2, drove more than 1,000 kilometres in heavy traffic, at speeds of up to 130 km/h. They kept in their lanes, followed other cars and even changed lanes, with safety drivers ready to take over.","Then in 1995 came the most daring test. A specially equipped Mercedes S-Class drove from Munich to a project meeting in Denmark and back, a round trip of about 1,758 kilometres (some accounts give a slightly shorter figure). On the open German autobahn, where some stretches have no speed limit, the car reached more than 175 km/h (about 109 mph). About 95 percent of the distance was driven by the computer. The longest stretch without a human touching the controls was about 158 kilometres. A safety driver always sat behind the wheel, and people still had to help at tricky moments, but a car had steered itself across much of a continent using cameras and computer vision."]},{"heading":"No Hands Across America","paragraphs":["On the other side of the Atlantic, at Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, another team had been working on robot vehicles since 1984. Their project was called Navlab, short for Navigation Laboratory. The first Navlab, built from 1986, was a Chevrolet van crammed so full of computers, power supplies and air conditioners that researchers rode in the back like scientists in a mobile laboratory. It crawled along slowly at first, but each new Navlab got smaller, smarter and faster.","In 1988 a Carnegie Mellon researcher named Dean Pomerleau built a program called ALVINN. It used a neural network, a kind of computer program loosely inspired by the way brain cells connect. Instead of being told the rules of steering, ALVINN learned by watching. A human drove while the computer looked at the camera picture and noted which way the wheel was turned. After enough practice, the program could look at a new picture of the road and guess the right steering direction by itself. This 'learning from examples' idea is close to how much of today's artificial intelligence works.","By 1995 the team had Navlab 5, a Pontiac Trans Sport minivan carrying a portable computer, a windscreen camera and a GPS receiver. That summer, researchers Todd Jochem and Dean Pomerleau set off on a road trip they called 'No Hands Across America'. They drove from Pittsburgh all the way to San Diego, California, a journey of roughly 2,800 to 2,850 miles (about 4,500 kilometres).","There was an important catch. The computer only did the steering. The humans in the front seats still controlled the accelerator and brakes, deciding how fast to go and when to stop. Even so, the steering numbers were impressive: the computer steered about 98 percent of the way, with people taking the wheel for only around 50 miles in total, at moments when the road ahead was too confusing for the camera. Average speeds on the highway were around 60 mph.","Navlab 5 showed something that surprised many people. A car did not need a room full of expensive equipment to follow a highway. A laptop-sized computer and one camera could do much of it. But following a well-marked highway is the easy part of driving. The hard part is everything else: junctions, roadworks, cyclists, people stepping out from behind parked vans, and rough roads with no markings at all. To push the technology into that messy world, someone was about to hold a race."]},{"heading":"The DARPA Desert Races","paragraphs":["DARPA, the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, is a research agency of the United States Department of Defense. It has a long history of funding wild ideas that later changed everyday life; it helped pay for the early work that grew into the internet. In the early 2000s, the US Congress set a goal that many military supply vehicles should one day be able to drive without people, so that fewer soldiers would be put in danger. DARPA decided that instead of paying one company to try, it would hold a contest open to anyone. The prize: one million US dollars.","The first DARPA Grand Challenge took place on 13 March 2004 in the Mojave Desert of California. The route stretched about 150 miles (240 kilometres) from near Barstow, California, towards Primm on the Nevada border, along dirt tracks, over rocky hills and through narrow passes. No human could touch the vehicles once they started. About fifteen robot vehicles reached the starting line. It was a disaster, though a very educational one. Some vehicles crashed into fences, some flipped over, one got confused at the start, and others simply stopped. The best performer, Carnegie Mellon's converted Humvee named Sandstorm, managed about 7.4 miles (11.8 kilometres) before it got stuck on a rock after a sharp turn, its wheels spinning helplessly. No one won the prize.","Many people laughed. But the teams did not give up. DARPA doubled the prize to two million dollars and announced a second race. On 8 October 2005, 23 finalists set out on a new desert course of about 132 miles (212 kilometres) near the California–Nevada border, full of tunnels, sharp turns and a twisting mountain pass with steep drops beside the track. This time, five vehicles made it all the way to the end.","The winner was Stanley, built by the Stanford Racing Team from Stanford University in California and led by Sebastian Thrun, a German-born computer scientist who directed Stanford's Artificial Intelligence Laboratory. Stanley was a blue Volkswagen Touareg, a sport utility vehicle, prepared with help from Volkswagen's Electronics Research Laboratory. On its roof sat five laser scanners, plus a video camera and GPS antennas. In its boot, seven computers ran its software. Stanley's great trick was learning. The team had fed it records of human driving, and its software combined the close-range laser scans with camera pictures to work out which parts of the road ahead were safe even far beyond the lasers' reach, so it could drive faster on smooth stretches. It finished the course in 6 hours and 54 minutes. Carnegie Mellon's two vehicles, Sandstorm and H1ghlander, came second and third. Stanley now belongs to the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History in Washington, D.C.","Deserts are empty, though. Real driving happens in towns. So in 2007 DARPA held the Urban Challenge at a former air force base in Victorville, California. On 3 November 2007, robot vehicles had to complete about 60 miles (96 kilometres) of missions on the base's streets within six hours, obeying California traffic rules, stopping at junctions, giving way properly, parking and dealing with other moving cars, both robot and human-driven. The winner was Boss, a Chevrolet Tahoe built by Carnegie Mellon's Tartan Racing team, in about 4 hours and 10 minutes. Stanford's car Junior came second, and Virginia Tech's Odin came third.","The DARPA races did more than award prizes. They gathered hundreds of young engineers, many of whom went on to lead the self-driving projects of the next twenty years. In a way, those dusty desert tracks were the starting line of the whole modern industry."]},{"heading":"How a Robot Car Sees and Decides","paragraphs":["So how does a self-driving car actually work? You can think of it as doing three jobs again and again, many times every second: sense, think and act. Sensing means gathering information about the world. Thinking means understanding that information and deciding what to do. Acting means turning the steering wheel, pressing the accelerator or applying the brakes, all controlled by electric motors and computer signals.","For sensing, most self-driving cars carry several kinds of 'eyes', because each has strengths and weaknesses. Cameras see colour and detail. They can read traffic lights, signs and lane lines, and spot the difference between a plastic bag and a small animal. But cameras struggle in darkness, dazzling sunlight and heavy rain, just like human eyes.","Radar sends out radio waves and listens for their echoes. It can measure how far away something is and how fast it is moving, and it works well in fog, rain and the dark. But radar pictures are blurry; it is hard to tell a parked car from a metal sign with radar alone.","Lidar, short for 'light detection and ranging', fires many very short pulses of invisible laser light every second, often from a spinning unit on the roof. It measures how long each pulse takes to bounce back, and since light travels at a known speed, that gives an exact distance. Millions of these measurements build a 3D picture of everything around the car, called a point cloud: kerbs, poles, people, bicycles, all as a cloud of tiny dots. Waymo says its lidar can detect objects up to about 300 metres away. Some companies, including Tesla, prefer to rely on cameras alone, arguing that humans drive with vision; others believe using several types of sensor together is safer.","The car also needs to know exactly where it is. GPS satellites give a rough position, accurate to a few metres. That is fine for a phone map but not for staying inside a lane. So many robotaxis also carry extremely detailed maps of their streets, showing every lane, kerb and traffic light. By matching what its lidar sees to the map, the car can work out its position to within a few centimetres. Motion sensors called gyroscopes and accelerometers help too, by measuring every turn and change of speed.","Now comes the thinking. First the software must recognise things: this blob of dots is a cyclist, that one is a bus, this is a pedestrian standing at a crossing. Modern cars do this using artificial intelligence trained on millions of example pictures and scans. Next comes prediction, perhaps the hardest part of all. Will the cyclist keep going straight? Is the person at the crossing about to step out? Is that car going to change lanes? Human drivers make these guesses all the time without noticing. The computer must make them on purpose, giving each possibility a likelihood. Finally comes planning: choosing a safe, smooth path and speed for the next few seconds, then doing it all over again a fraction of a second later.","No single sensor or program is perfect. That is why engineers build in redundancy, which means backup systems. If one sensor is dirty or one computer fails, another can take over and bring the car safely to a stop."]},{"heading":"Level 0 to Level 5: How Self-Driving Is a Car?","paragraphs":["People use the words 'self-driving' and 'autonomous' for very different things, from a car that beeps when you drift out of your lane to a taxi with nobody in the front seat. To clear up the confusion, SAE International, an organisation of engineers that writes standards for vehicles, published a scale in 2014 in a document called J3016. It describes six levels of driving automation, numbered 0 to 5.","At Level 0, there is no driving automation. The human does all the driving, although the car may give warnings, such as a beep when something is in your blind spot, or even brake by itself in an emergency.","At Level 1, the car can help with one job at a time: either steering or speed. Adaptive cruise control, which keeps a safe gap behind the car in front, is a typical example.","At Level 2, the car can control both steering and speed at the same time in some situations, for example keeping in lane and following traffic on a motorway. This is where most of today's driver-assistance systems sit, including those sold with names like 'Autopilot' or 'Super Cruise'. The key rule at Level 2 is that the human is still the driver. They must watch the road every moment and be ready to take over instantly. The car is helping, not driving. Many crashes have happened when people trusted these systems too much, looked away or even fell asleep.","At Level 3, the car drives itself in certain conditions, such as slow traffic jams on a motorway, and the person in the driver's seat may look away. But when the car asks, the person must take back control within a few seconds. A few carmakers have offered Level 3 traffic-jam systems in limited places at low speeds; Mercedes-Benz, for example, began offering one in Germany in 2022.","At Level 4, the car can drive itself completely within a certain area or set of conditions, and if something goes wrong it can bring itself to a safe stop without any human help. Today's driverless robotaxis are Level 4 vehicles: they work only in streets they have been carefully prepared for.","Level 5 would be a car that can drive anywhere a human could, in any weather, on any road, with no limits at all. Nobody has built one yet, and many experts think it is still a long way off.","Notice the big jump between Level 2 and Level 3. Below it, a human is always responsible for watching the road. Above it, in the right conditions, the machine is. That line is why it matters so much that people understand which kind of car they are sitting in."]},{"heading":"Robotaxis Arrive","paragraphs":["After the DARPA races, the internet company Google quietly hired several of the best competitors, including Sebastian Thrun. Its self-driving car project started in January 2009 inside Google's experimental lab and was revealed to the public in October 2010. Its first test cars were ordinary Toyota Prius and Lexus vehicles fitted with a spinning lidar on the roof, always with trained safety drivers on board. By 2012 the fleet had driven hundreds of thousands of miles on public roads.","In 2014 Google showed off a little round prototype, nicknamed 'Firefly' by the team, that looked a bit like a friendly cartoon bubble. In 2015, one of these cars gave a ride through Austin, Texas, to a blind man, with nobody else in the car and no police escort. It was described as the first fully driverless trip on a public road. In December 2016 the project became a separate company called Waymo, owned by Google's parent company, Alphabet.","Waymo began offering rides to members of the public in the Phoenix area of Arizona in December 2018, at first with safety drivers on board. In October 2020 it opened a fully driverless service to the public there: you could call a car with a phone app, and it would arrive with nobody in the driver's seat. Phoenix was chosen partly because its roads are wide, its streets are laid out in a neat grid, and it rarely snows. Later Waymo spread to San Francisco, Los Angeles, Austin, Atlanta and other cities. By 2026 the company reported giving hundreds of thousands of paid rides every week.","In China, too, robotaxis have grown quickly. Companies such as Baidu, with its Apollo Go service, and Pony.ai have run driverless taxis in several big cities, including Wuhan, where large fleets have carried passengers. Self-driving technology is also being tested for lorries on motorways, for delivery robots and for buses on fixed routes."]},{"heading":"Hard Questions on the Road Ahead","paragraphs":["Self-driving cars promise a lot. Most road crashes involve human mistakes: drivers who are tired, distracted, speeding or have been drinking. A computer never gets sleepy or checks its phone. Driverless cars could also give independence to people who cannot drive, such as many blind people, some older people and some people with disabilities. But the technology also raises difficult questions, and it is important to look at them honestly.","The first question is safety. In March 2018, in Tempe, Arizona, a test car run by the ride-hailing company Uber struck and killed a woman named Elaine Herzberg as she walked her bicycle across a road at night. It is widely described as the first pedestrian death caused by a self-driving test car. A safety driver was sitting in the car, but investigators found she had been looking at her phone, and they also criticised the company's weak safety culture. Uber halted its tests, and in 2020 it sold its self-driving division to another company.","In October 2023, in San Francisco, a woman crossing the street was hit by a car driven by a human, who drove away. She was thrown into the path of a driverless robotaxi run by Cruise, a company owned by General Motors. The robotaxi braked but hit her, and then, trying to pull over to the side of the road as it was programmed to do after a collision, it dragged her about 20 feet (6 metres). She was seriously injured. On 24 October 2023 California's Department of Motor Vehicles suspended Cruise's permit to run driverless cars, saying the company had not at first shown officials the full video of what happened. Cruise paused its driverless cars across the country, its chief executive resigned, and in December 2024 General Motors stopped funding robotaxis altogether. The lesson was that a robot car must handle not only ordinary driving but also rare, confusing situations, and that companies must be fully honest when things go wrong.","The second question is weather and surprises. Snow can hide lane lines, heavy rain and fog can confuse cameras and lidar, and a police officer waving traffic through a broken signal is very hard for a computer to understand. Roadworks, flooded streets and strange objects in the road are also difficult. That is one reason robotaxis started in sunny cities with well-mapped streets.","The third question is responsibility. When a human driver causes a crash, the law usually knows who is to blame. But if a driverless car crashes, who is responsible: the passenger, the owner, the company that built the car, or the company that wrote the software? Countries are still writing new laws to answer that question.","The fourth question is jobs. Millions of people around the world earn their living by driving taxis, lorries, buses and delivery vans. If robots take over much of that work, those people will need new jobs and new training. Some experts think the change will be slow, giving time to adjust; others worry it could be sudden.","Step back and look at how far we have come. In 1770 Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot's steam wagon rumbled through the streets of Paris at walking pace, and its driver had to wrestle with a heavy, awkward steering lever. About 250 years later, a car with nobody at the wheel can pick you up, find its way through a city and drop you at your door. So what will the next chapter of the automobile look like, and what can the whole long journey teach us? It is time to look back over the road so far, and then to look ahead."]}],"deepDive":"Why did self-driving cars take so long? The core problem is that driving is not one task but thousands. Following a well-marked motorway lane, which Dickmanns and Navlab largely solved in the 1990s, is mostly a geometry problem: find the lines, keep between them. City driving is a prediction problem about other minds. A robot car must estimate what pedestrians, cyclists and other drivers intend, often from tiny clues like a turned head. Engineers call the rare, odd situations 'edge cases' or the 'long tail': a mattress falling off a lorry, a person in a chicken costume, a traffic officer overriding a green light. Each is rare, but there are so many kinds that a car driving millions of miles will meet them constantly. Two broad technical philosophies compete. One, used by Waymo and most robotaxi firms, combines lidar, radar, cameras and detailed pre-made maps, and limits service to areas it has carefully mapped (Level 4). The other, championed by Tesla, relies on cameras and large neural networks trained on huge amounts of driving data, aiming to work anywhere without special maps; so far those systems sold to the public are classed as Level 2 and require a watching driver. Measuring safety is itself a debate. In the United States, human drivers have roughly one death for every 100 million miles or so driven, so proving a robot is safer in statistical terms requires many millions of miles, and comparisons must account for where and when each kind of vehicle drives: robotaxis mostly operate in cities, at lower speeds, in good weather. Companies publish their own safety studies, while regulators and independent researchers check them. Another argument concerns 'automation complacency': research on aircraft autopilots and car driver-assistance shows people quickly stop paying attention when a machine usually handles things, which makes Level 2 and Level 3 systems tricky, because the human must take over just when the situation is hardest. Finally there are questions about cybersecurity (could someone hack a car?), privacy (robotaxis record everything around them), and whether driverless cars will reduce traffic or create more by making car trips easier and cheaper.","funFact":"In the 2004 DARPA Grand Challenge, not a single robot finished, and the best one gave up after about 7.4 miles of a roughly 150-mile course, yet just 19 months later five robots completed the next race.","check":{"q":"Which robot car, led by Sebastian Thrun's Stanford team, won the 2005 DARPA Grand Challenge?","a":"Stanley","wrong":["Boss","Sandstorm","Navlab 5"]},"sources":[{"title":"History of self-driving cars","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_self-driving_cars"},{"title":"Houdina Radio Control","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houdina_Radio_Control"},{"title":"Ernst Dickmanns","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernst_Dickmanns"},{"title":"Navlab","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navlab"},{"title":"DARPA Grand Challenge","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DARPA_Grand_Challenge"},{"title":"Waymo","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waymo"},{"title":"Cruise (autonomous vehicle)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cruise_(autonomous_vehicle)"},{"title":"Self-driving car","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-driving_car"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/19-1.jpg","alt":"Stanford's robot car Stanley, a blue Volkswagen Touareg with sensors on its roof, winner of the 2005 DARPA Grand Challenge","file":"Stanley2.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanley2.JPG","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/19-2.jpg","alt":"A driverless Waymo Jaguar I-Pace robotaxi with its spinning lidar dome on the roof, in San Francisco","file":"Waymo Jaguar I-Pace - Front Quarter View 01.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waymo_Jaguar_I-Pace_-_Front_Quarter_View_01.jpg","author":"9yz","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/19-3.jpg","alt":"Boss, Carnegie Mellon's robot Chevrolet Tahoe covered in sensors, winner of the 2007 DARPA Urban Challenge","file":"CMU carbot (2328347458).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CMU_carbot_(2328347458).jpg","author":"Steve Jurvetson from Los Altos, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4340},{"n":20,"title":"The Road So Far, and the Road Ahead","when":"1769 – the future","summary":"The car was not invented by one person, but by thousands of clever people over 250 years, from a steam wagon slower than walking to robot taxis. Cars gave us amazing freedom, but also crashes, smog and traffic jams. Now it's your turn to imagine the car of the future!","sections":[{"heading":"Rewind: 250 Years in One Drive","paragraphs":["Let's take one last drive together, but this time through time instead of space. Imagine a road that begins in 1770 and ends today. Every few kilometres there is a signpost with a date, and beside each signpost stands a machine. Shall we go?","Our first stop is Paris in 1770. Here stands Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot's fardier, a huge three-wheeled steam wagon built for the French army to haul cannons. Its great copper boiler hangs out in front like the nose of a dragon. It moves at about 4 kilometres per hour, no faster than you walk, and it must stop every quarter of an hour or so to build up steam. But it moves by itself. For the first time, a full-size road vehicle is pushing itself along without a horse, an ox or a person pulling.","Next signpost: Mannheim, Germany, 1886. Karl Benz has just applied for patent number 37435 for his Patent-Motorwagen, a light three-wheeler with a small petrol engine of less than one horsepower. Unlike earlier inventors who bolted engines onto carriages, Benz designed the whole vehicle around its engine. People often call this patent the 'birth certificate' of the automobile.","Two years later, in August 1888, we spot Bertha Benz rattling down a country lane with her teenage sons Eugen and Richard, on the way to her mother's house in Pforzheim, about 106 kilometres away. She buys fuel at a pharmacy, fixes problems with whatever she has to hand, and arrives by dusk. Her journey proves to the world, and to her worried husband, that a car can make a real trip.","The road now crosses the Atlantic. On 1 October 1908, Ford's Model T goes on sale: tough, simple and high off the ground for bumpy country roads. In 1913, at the Highland Park factory in Michigan, Ford's team starts using a moving assembly line. Within about a year, the time to put together a Model T chassis drops from around twelve and a half hours to about an hour and a half. Prices tumble, and millions of ordinary families buy their first car. By 1927, more than 15 million Model Ts have been built.","Between those signposts the road itself changes beneath our wheels. Muddy tracks become tarmac. In 1932 Germany opens its first autobahn section between Cologne and Bonn, and in 1956 the United States begins building its giant Interstate highway system. Traffic lights blink at crossroads, petrol stations appear on street corners, and families pile into their cars for holidays, drive-in films and new homes in the suburbs. Racing drivers chase records on closed tracks such as Brooklands and Indianapolis, and the lessons they learn about brakes and tyres find their way into ordinary cars. But the road also grows more dangerous and more polluted, and by the 1960s people are demanding safer, cleaner cars.","Further along, in 1959, we pass a Volvo, and inside is a new kind of seatbelt designed by engineer Nils Bohlin: the three-point belt, which crosses both the hips and the chest. Volvo lets other carmakers use the design for free, and it goes on to save, by Volvo's estimate, more than a million lives.","Then the road fills with quiet humming. From 2008, when the Tesla Roadster appears, followed by the Nissan Leaf in 2010 and many others, electric cars make their great comeback, powered by lithium-ion batteries. Remember that electric cars were not new at all; around 1900 they were popular in many cities before petrol cars took over.","Our last signpost reads 2020. In the sunny suburbs of Phoenix, Arizona, a car pulls up with nobody in the driver's seat. Waymo's fully driverless taxis are open to the public. From walking pace in 1770 to robot taxis in 2020 took about 250 years. Along the way, the fastest car on Earth went from about 4 km/h to about 1,228 km/h, the speed reached by the jet-powered ThrustSSC in 1997 when it became the first car to break the sound barrier."]},{"heading":"Not One Inventor, but a Relay Race","paragraphs":["If someone asks you, 'Who invented the car?', what should you say? After twenty parts, you know the honest answer is: thousands of people. The car was not invented in one flash of genius. It was more like a relay race, with each runner carrying the baton a little further and then handing it on.","Cugnot showed that a machine could move a vehicle. Étienne Lenoir built the first commercially successful internal combustion engine in 1860. Alphonse Beau de Rochas described the four-stroke idea on paper in 1862, and Nicolaus Otto built a working four-stroke engine in 1876. Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach made engines light and fast enough to fit in a vehicle. Karl Benz built and patented the first practical automobile powered by an internal combustion engine. Bertha Benz proved it worked over a long distance.","Then came the problem-solvers. Robert William Thomson and John Boyd Dunlop gave us air-filled tyres, and the Michelin brothers put them on cars. Robert Bosch's company and engineer Gottlob Honold gave engines a reliable spark. Rudolf Diesel found a way to ignite fuel by squeezing air until it was hot. Charles Kettering's electric self-starter, first offered on the 1912 Cadillac, meant nobody had to risk a broken wrist turning a hand crank. Malcolm Loughead's hydraulic brakes helped cars stop safely.","Others built the world around the car. John Loudon McAdam and Edgar Purnell Hooley improved road surfaces. Traffic engineers, from John Peake Knight in 1868 London to the designers of electric signals in American cities, invented ways to control traffic. Garrett Morgan patented an improved traffic signal. Mary Anderson patented a windscreen wiper. Béla Barényi invented the crumple zone, and Nils Bohlin perfected the three-point seatbelt.","And still others changed how cars were made and sold. Ransom Olds built cars in large numbers. Ford's engineers, including Charles Sorensen, combined older ideas into the moving assembly line. Alfred Sloan at General Motors offered a car 'for every purse and purpose'. Taiichi Ohno at Toyota found ways to build cars with less waste. Scientists such as John Goodenough, Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino developed the lithium-ion battery, and computer scientists like Ernst Dickmanns and Sebastian Thrun taught cars to see.","These people came from France, Germany, Britain, Belgium, Austria, Hungary, Sweden, the United States, Japan and many other countries. Some were famous; many more were factory workers, mechanics, test drivers and inventors whose names nobody remembers. And many of them worked on just one small piece of the puzzle. That is how most big inventions really happen. A great idea is rarely a lone hero's triumph. It is a team effort stretched across time and across the world."]},{"heading":"The Great Trade-Off","paragraphs":["Cars have given people something wonderful: freedom. A family can visit grandparents in another town on a Sunday afternoon. A doctor can reach a farmhouse in the middle of the night. Fresh food, medicines and parcels arrive at shops and homes. In many countries, getting a driving licence feels like a ticket to growing up. Before cars, most people rarely travelled more than a day's walk or horse ride from home. Today, a car can carry you hundreds of kilometres between breakfast and lunch.","But every one of the stories in this Deep Dive also showed a cost, and we still argue today about how to balance the good and the bad.","The first cost is safety. According to the World Health Organization, road traffic crashes kill about 1.2 million people every year around the world, and they are the leading cause of death for children and young people aged 5 to 29. More than half of those killed are pedestrians, cyclists and motorcyclists, the people least protected by steel and airbags. About nine in ten road deaths happen in low- and middle-income countries, even though those countries have only around 60 percent of the world's vehicles. The good news is that safety ideas work. In many wealthy countries, the number of deaths for every kilometre driven has fallen enormously since the 1960s, thanks to seatbelts, crumple zones, airbags, better roads and laws against drink-driving. The United Nations has set the goal of cutting road deaths and injuries in half by 2030.","The second cost is pollution. Exhaust fumes cause smog and harm people's lungs, and burning petrol or diesel releases carbon dioxide, the main gas driving climate change. Cars and vans together produce roughly a tenth of all the world's energy-related carbon dioxide, and cars burn a large share of the world's oil. Leaded petrol was finally removed worldwide in 2021, a reminder that serious mistakes can be fixed, even if it takes a long time.","The third cost is space. There are now roughly one and a half billion motor vehicles in the world, and some estimates are even higher. Every one of them needs a place to park, by many estimates for more than 90 percent of the day, when it is just sitting still. In many cities, huge areas of land are covered by car parks, wide roads and motorway junctions, land that could have been parks, homes or playgrounds. Traffic jams waste time and fuel; drivers in big cities can lose days every year sitting in queues.","The fourth cost is harder to measure: what cars do to communities. When cities are built mainly for cars, shops spread out along busy roads, children have fewer safe places to walk or cycle, and people who cannot drive, because they are too young, too old, or cannot afford a car, can find it hard to get around.","None of this means cars are bad. It means that, like every powerful invention, they come with trade-offs. The interesting question for the future is not 'cars or no cars?' but 'how can we keep the freedom and lose as much of the harm as possible?'"]},{"heading":"Better Batteries and Cars That Get Reborn","paragraphs":["Let's start our look into the future with the part that matters most for electric cars: the battery. Today's electric cars use lithium-ion batteries. Inside each cell, tiny charged particles called lithium ions travel back and forth between two layers, through a liquid called the electrolyte. That liquid works well, but it can catch fire if the battery is badly damaged or overheats, and it limits how much energy can be packed in.","Many scientists and carmakers are working on solid-state batteries, which swap the liquid electrolyte for a solid one, such as a special ceramic or a solid plastic-like material. In theory, solid-state batteries could store more energy in the same weight, letting a car go further on a charge. Because most solid electrolytes do not burn, they could also be safer, and some designs might charge much faster. In 2023 the electronics company Panasonic reported a small solid-state prototype that could charge from 10 to 80 percent in about three minutes.","But making them is hard. Thin, spiky threads of lithium metal, called dendrites, can grow through the electrolyte and cause short circuits. The solid layers must stay pressed tightly together even as the battery swells and shrinks during charging. And manufacturing them cheaply in huge numbers has not yet been achieved. Toyota, which holds thousands of solid-state battery patents, has said it does not expect to use them in commercial cars before about 2027, and other carmakers such as Nissan, Honda, BMW and Hyundai are aiming for the late 2020s or the 2030s. Plenty of companies have announced breakthroughs; the real test is whether those batteries can be made by the million at a fair price.","Meanwhile, ordinary lithium-ion batteries keep improving. They have become far cheaper over the past fifteen years, and new chemistries, such as lithium iron phosphate (often shortened to LFP), avoid using cobalt, a metal whose mining has raised serious concerns about pollution and the treatment of workers. Some researchers are also working on sodium-ion batteries, which use sodium, the same element found in table salt, instead of scarcer lithium.","Batteries are not the only possible path. Some carmakers are still developing hydrogen fuel-cell cars, which make electricity on board by combining hydrogen with oxygen from the air and give off only water vapour. They can be refuelled in a few minutes, but hydrogen is costly to make cleanly and there are very few filling stations. Others are experimenting with fuels made from plants or waste, or even from captured carbon dioxide and clean electricity, which might one day keep older petrol cars, ships and aircraft running with less harm to the climate. Engineers do not yet agree on which mix of ideas will win; most likely, different solutions will suit different jobs.","The future car might also have a second life, and a third. Cars are already among the most recycled products in the world. When a car reaches the end of its life, workers drain its fluids and remove its battery, tyres and catalytic converter. Then the rest is crushed and shredded, and magnets and other machines sort out the metals. About three quarters of the material in a typical car can be recycled, and old cars supply millions of tonnes of steel to steelworks every year. Recycling metal uses far less energy than digging up and processing new ore.","Electric car batteries are the next big recycling challenge. An old battery that can no longer power a car well may still be useful for years storing electricity from solar panels, a so-called 'second life'. After that, recycling plants can recover valuable metals like lithium, nickel and cobalt to make new batteries. Engineers dream of a 'circular' car industry, where the materials in today's cars become the cars of tomorrow, over and over again."]},{"heading":"Shared Robot Taxis and Cities for People","paragraphs":["Now imagine a city street in the year 2050. What do you see? Nobody knows for certain, but here is one possible picture that many planners talk about.","Fewer people own their own cars. Instead, when you need a ride, you tap an app and a clean, quiet electric robotaxi arrives. It drops you off and drives away to pick up the next passenger, rather than sitting in a car park all day. If one shared car can do the work of several privately owned ones, a city might need far fewer parking spaces. Old car parks could become parks, homes, sports fields or gardens.","In this picture, streets look different too. Wide lanes are narrowed to make space for trees, benches, bicycle paths and wider pavements. Buses and trams run often and on time. Children can walk or cycle to school safely, as many did before city streets were filled with cars. Some cities, such as Pontevedra in Spain, have already turned much of their centres into areas mostly for people on foot, and cities including Paris and Amsterdam have built hundreds of kilometres of cycle lanes.","But there is another possible picture, and experts warn about it. If robotaxis become very cheap and comfortable, people might take more car trips, not fewer, instead of walking, cycling or catching a bus. Empty robot cars could drive around looking for passengers, adding to traffic. So whether self-driving cars make cities better or worse depends not just on the technology, but on the choices people and governments make: how roads are shared, how rides are priced, and how much space is given to public transport.","There are smaller changes coming as well. Cars are becoming computers on wheels, connected to the internet and updated with new software, a bit like a phone. Some may 'talk' to traffic lights and to each other, warning of an icy patch or a crash around the next bend. Electric cars could even help run the electricity grid, charging when there is lots of wind and solar power and, in some systems, sending power back when it is needed. All of these ideas have to be designed carefully to keep people's information private and to keep cars safe from hackers."]},{"heading":"Cars That Fly? Electric Air Taxis","paragraphs":["For about a hundred years, people have dreamed of flying cars, and cartoons and comics are full of them. They have always been held back by the same problems: they are noisy, expensive, need a lot of power and are hard to fly safely over crowded cities. Now a new kind of machine is getting close to carrying passengers: the eVTOL, short for 'electric vertical take-off and landing' aircraft.","An eVTOL is not really a car. It is more like a quiet electric helicopter with many small propellers, each spun by its own electric motor. Computers constantly adjust each propeller to keep the aircraft steady. Because electric motors are simple and there are many of them, if one fails the others can keep flying. Companies such as Joby Aviation and Archer Aviation in the United States, Volocopter in Germany and EHang in China have built eVTOLs and tested them in many flights. In October 2023, China's aviation authority gave EHang's EH216-S, a two-seat aircraft designed to fly without a pilot on board, a type certificate, a key step towards carrying passengers.","The idea is to use eVTOLs as air taxis, hopping over traffic jams from rooftops or special 'vertiports' to airports or across a city in a few minutes. But they face the same challenge as electric cars, only much harder. Batteries are heavy, and an aircraft has to lift its own weight into the air, so range is limited. Safety rules for aircraft are very strict, as they should be, and every new type must pass long certification tests. Cities will also have to decide where these aircraft may fly and land, and how much noise people will accept. Air taxis may become one more option for travel, but for a long time to come most journeys will still happen on the ground."]},{"heading":"Back to Bertha, and Forward to You","paragraphs":["Let's finish where so much of this story began: on a dusty country road in Germany, early one August morning in 1888.","Bertha Benz grips the steering tiller of the Motorwagen. Her sons are beside and behind her, ready to jump out and push on the hills. Nobody has ever driven a car this far before. There are no filling stations, no garages, no road signs for drivers, and no mechanics who have ever seen a machine like this. When the fuel runs low, she stops at a pharmacy in Wiesloch to buy a cleaning fluid called ligroin. When problems strike, according to the family's story, she uses a hatpin and a garter to fix them, and a shoemaker nails leather onto the brakes. By the end of the day, against all expectations, she has arrived. Today you can follow her route yourself: the Bertha Benz Memorial Route, opened in 2008, winds through the towns she passed, from Mannheim through Wiesloch and Bruchsal to Pforzheim.","Why does this scene matter so much? Because Bertha did not wait for everything to be perfect. She saw that the car worked well enough to try, and she went out and solved the problems one at a time as they appeared. When she got home, she told Karl what needed improving, such as an extra low gear for climbing hills, and the next version was better. That is how engineering works: try, learn, improve, try again.","The road ahead has big problems of its own. How do we make all the electricity for electric cars from clean sources like sun, wind and water? How do we bring road deaths closer to zero, especially for people walking and cycling? How do we make batteries without harming people or nature, and recycle every part of a car? How do we design cities where cars are useful but do not crowd out everything else? Who will make sure self-driving cars are safe and fair to everyone?","Nobody has fully solved these problems yet. That is exciting news, because it means there is work for the next generation of inventors, engineers, scientists, designers, city planners and lawmakers. Some of the people who will solve them are kids right now, reading about Cugnot's steam wagon and Bertha's daring drive and wondering, 'How could that be done better?'","Remember the patterns from this long story. Big inventions are built by many people, so it is fine to work on one small piece. Every invention has trade-offs, so think about who is helped and who might be harmed. Testing honestly matters, so never hide a problem. And curiosity, plus a bit of courage, can carry an idea a very long way, just as it carried Bertha all the way to Pforzheim.","So grab a pencil and a sheet of paper. What would your car of the future look like? Would it run on sunlight, fold up to fit in a cupboard, carry a whole football team, or not be a car at all? The road ahead is open, and the next chapter of this story is waiting for you to design it."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of technology often use the automobile as their favourite example of 'systems' thinking. A car on its own is useless; it needs fuel supplies, repair shops, paved roads, traffic laws, driving tests, insurance, finance, factories and a workforce trained to build it. The historian Thomas Hughes called these 'large technical systems', and they explain why the car took decades to spread even after the Benz patent of 1886: the system around it had to be built too. The same lesson applies to electric and self-driving cars today. Battery chemistry is only one piece; charging networks, grid capacity, mining and recycling, insurance law and new safety standards must all grow at the same time. Another idea historians stress is 'path dependence': choices made early can lock in later ones. The petrol engine won around 1910 partly because of cheap Texas oil, the electric starter and the low price of the Model T, not because it was best in every way. Once cities, petrol stations and repair skills were built around it, switching became very expensive, which is one reason the electric comeback took a century. Debates about the future often divide between technological optimists and those who emphasise behaviour and planning. Optimists point to the roughly 90% fall in battery prices since 2010 and to robotaxis already carrying paying passengers. Sceptics point to the 'rebound effect' (when something becomes cheaper and easier, people use more of it, so cheap robotaxis could increase total traffic) and to the fact that an electric car's climate benefit depends on how the electricity and the battery are made. Most studies find electric cars produce lower lifetime emissions than petrol cars in most countries, with the gap growing as grids get cleaner, but the size of that gap varies a lot from place to place. Finally, transport researchers note that the safest, cleanest trip is often the one not made by car at all: well-designed public transport, walking and cycling are part of any serious plan for healthier, lower-carbon cities. The future of the car will therefore be decided by engineers and also by voters, planners and everyday travellers.","funFact":"By many estimates, most privately owned cars spend more than 90 percent of their lives parked, which is why some planners think shared robotaxis could free up huge amounts of city land.","check":{"q":"In what year did Karl Benz apply for the patent often called the 'birth certificate' of the automobile?","a":"1886","wrong":["1770","1908","1959"]},"sources":[{"title":"Car","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Car"},{"title":"Road traffic injuries (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/road-traffic-injuries"},{"title":"Solid-state battery","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solid-state_battery"},{"title":"Vehicle recycling","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vehicle_recycling"},{"title":"eVTOL","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EVTOL"},{"title":"Bertha Benz Memorial Route","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha_Benz_Memorial_Route"},{"title":"Waymo","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waymo"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/automobile/20-1.jpg","alt":"Cugnot's steam fardier of 1770, with its big round boiler in front, at the Musée des Arts et Métiers in Paris","file":"Cugnot vehicle 2.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cugnot_vehicle_2.JPG","author":"Chatsam","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/automobile/20-3.jpg","alt":"The Volocopter 2X, an electric air taxi with 18 rotors, on display at the IAA car show in Frankfurt, 2017","file":"Volocopter 2X at IAA 2017 2.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volocopter_2X_at_IAA_2017_2.jpg","author":"Spielvogel","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3822}],"words":80771};
